window.TAPEOUT_VAULT = [{"id":"V001","k":"md-core-node-1-what-node-will-you-work-on","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"NODE.1 What node will you work on?","q":"NODE.1 What node will you work on?","a":"**Not confirmed. Don't claim one.** Public anchors: **Blackwell** is on a custom **TSMC 4NP** (FinFET) process; **Rubin** is reported on **TSMC 3nm-class** (FinFET) ([TechSpot](https://www.techspot.com/news/105852-nvidia-blackwell-ai-successor-rubin-moves-forward-six.html)); TSMC's **N2** moves to **nanosheet (GAA)** transistors. A library team may work on current products **and** evaluate future nodes. Prepare **FinFET** cell design first, and know enough about **GAA** to ask a smart question.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Guardrail</p>\n\nNever say \"I'll be working on 3nm.\" Ask: \"At a level you can share, is the work mostly established FinFET libraries or newer device platforms? More new-node bring-up or improving existing libraries?\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V002","k":"md-core-q1-tell-me-about-yourself-resume","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q1. \"Tell me about yourself.\"","q":"Q1. \"Tell me about yourself.\"","a":"**30-second version** (if he's clearly in a hurry, as in the July 2026 report):\n\n> I'm a Michigan MS grad in ICs and VLSI with a materials-science background. I've worked on both sides of a cell library: in the WICS lab I designed **ultra-low-power analog front-end blocks in TSMC 65nm** and validated them **across PVT**, and at Faraday I owned **chip-top DFT and STA on a 22nm tapeout**, where I was a **user of standard-cell libraries**. This role is where those meet, so I'm excited to go deep on it.\n\n**75-second version:** add the origin (from Vietnam, came to Michigan for engineering), the device side (materials → why transistors leak and vary), the Tcl/Perl automation at Faraday, and one human line (founded the Vietnamese student association, 3 → 90+ members).\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Device physics → circuit design (WICS) → library user (Faraday) → this role builds the library.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **Follow-up: \"Have you designed a production cell library?\"** \"No. My direct experience is **transistor-level analog design** and the **implementation flow that consumes libraries**. I can walk you through how I'd design, simulate and characterize a cell right now.\" Then do it.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V003","k":"md-core-q2-walk-me-through-your-most-relevant-project-wics-resume","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q2. \"Walk me through your most relevant project.\" (WICS)","q":"Q2. \"Walk me through your most relevant project.\" (WICS)","a":"> In Prof. Wentzloff's WICS lab I designed circuit blocks for a **fully implantable auditory prosthesis**: a **transimpedance amplifier** and an **active-RC filter** for the low-noise front end between the MEMS sensor and the ASIC, in **TSMC 65nm** with flip-chip packaging. The constraint was **power**: it's an implant, so I biased devices in **subthreshold**, where **gm/Id is highest**. The challenge was keeping **stability and noise** acceptable **across PVT corners**, because in subthreshold the current is **exponential in Vt**, so a small process or temperature shift moves everything. [verify: the corner that broke, what you changed, the number you hit.]\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Objective → my blocks → one decision → how I checked it → result.** Subthreshold = max gm/Id, but exponential sensitivity to Vt and temperature.\n\n</div>\n\n- **Bridge to the role:** \"That's the same physics that makes **low-voltage cells and ROM reads** hard: leakage, variation and temperature near threshold.\"\n- Full 3-deep defense: **Part RD.1**.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V004","k":"md-core-q3-what-did-you-do-at-faraday-tell-me-about-a-timing-violation-you-fixed-resume","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q3. \"What did you do at Faraday? Tell me about a timing violation you fixed.\"","q":"Q3. \"What did you do at Faraday? Tell me about a timing violation you fixed.\"","a":"> Quick framing of scope: on a **UMC 22nm shuttle**, my lane was **chip-top DFT and STA checks** and the **handoffs**: scan, **MBIST** integration, ATPG and ATE patterns, and **PrimeTime** timing debug, with fixes recommended to the physical-design team. I automated the check chain in **Tcl/Perl/Csh**. We taped out on the **1.5-month schedule**, and I got a **top-10% rating**. For a violation: find the failing path, decide **setup or hold**, trace the cause (**logic depth, weak drive, a constraint error**), recommend the least invasive fix (**resize, buffer, or fix the constraint**), and re-run all corners to make sure it didn't break a neighbor.\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Value to show</p>\n\n\"Every delay PrimeTime showed me came from the **.lib tables** this team builds. I've been the customer. I know what a wrong library costs downstream.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V005","k":"md-core-q4-what-is-hold-time-reported-jun-2025-t1","d":"seq","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"Repair a hold violation","q":"For a same-edge hold check with unchanged clock skew, which change directly improves negative hold slack?","a":"<div class=\"co co-analogy\"><p class=\"co-t\">Picture it</p>\n\nSetup and hold are a **photographer's shutter**: the subject (data) must be still a moment **before** the click (setup) and a moment **after** (hold). Taking photos less often (a slower clock) doesn't help if someone jumps into the frame right at the click.\n\n</div>\n\n![timing diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/timing.svg)\n\n> **Hold time** is the minimum time the data input must stay **stable after the capturing clock edge**, so the flop reliably captures the **old** value. A **hold violation** means **new data arrives too early**, through a path that's too fast, and corrupts the value being captured. **Setup time** is the mirror image: data must be stable **before** the edge.\n>\n> The key difference: the **hold check compares launch and capture on the same clock edge**, so the **clock period doesn't appear**. That's why you **can't fix hold by slowing the clock**. You fix it by **adding delay to the short data path** (buffers or slower cells), **reducing clock skew**, or using a flop with a smaller hold requirement. Setup, by contrast, gets better with a longer period.\n\n<div class=\"co co-eq\"><p class=\"co-t\">Hold and setup slack (S = capture clock arrival − launch clock arrival)</p>\n\n```latex\n\\text{Setup slack} = T + S - t_{cq,max} - t_{pd,max} - t_{setup}\n\\qquad\n\\text{Hold slack} = t_{cq,min} + t_{cd,min} - S - t_{hold}\n```\n**Remember:** setup races the **next** edge (T is in it); hold protects the **same** edge (no T). **Later capture clock (S > 0) helps setup, hurts hold.**\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Setup = old data arrives in time. Hold = new data stays away long enough. Period only helps setup.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **Follow-up: \"Where does hold come from inside the flop?\"** In a TG master-slave flop, the master's input gate closes a little **after** the clock edge (clock buffering, CLK/CLKB overlap). Data changing inside that window can leak through (Q7).\n- **Follow-up: \"Can setup or hold be negative?\"** Yes. They're measured at the **external pins**. If the internal clock is delayed relative to data, data can arrive after the edge and still be captured (negative setup), and vice versa.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Identify whether the constraint compares the same edge or successive edges.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Delay the capture clock relative to launch.","Reduce the data path’s contamination delay.","Increase the clock period.","Add delay to the short data path."],"ans":3,"why":["Positive capture skew increases the required hold window relative to launch.","A faster minimum path makes new data arrive earlier.","The clock period is absent from a same-edge hold check.","More minimum data delay keeps new data away from the capture aperture."],"ex":"Hold slack = minimum clock-to-Q + minimum data delay − capture-minus-launch skew − hold requirement.","oral":"Q4. \"What is hold time?\" (reported Jun 2025) T1"},{"id":"V006","k":"md-core-q5-size-cmos-gates-at-2-1-and-1-1-reported-t1","d":"cmos","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND2 matched-drive sizing","q":"Assume equal length and Rn=R/Wn, Rp=2R/Wp. Which NAND2 widths match the worst conducting path of an N1/P2 inverter?","a":"![gates diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/gates.svg)\n\n**The rule:** match each gate's **worst-case pull-up and pull-down resistance** to a reference inverter. **Series** transistors add resistance, so each must be **wider** (× number in series). **Parallel** transistors are sized for **one** conducting (worst case).\n\n\"2:1\" = PMOS is **2× weaker** than NMOS (hole mobility ≈ ½ electron mobility; classic planar). \"1:1\" = equal strength (closer to modern FinFETs, where PMOS is strained and nearly as strong).\n\n| Gate | NMOS (each) | PMOS at **2:1** | PMOS at **1:1** | Logical effort g (2:1) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Inverter | 1 | 2 | 1 | 1 |\n| NAND2 | **2** (2 in series) | **2** (parallel) | 1 | 4/3 |\n| NAND3 | 3 | 2 | 1 | 5/3 |\n| NOR2 | 1 (parallel) | **4** (2 in series) | 2 | 5/3 |\n| NOR3 | 1 | 6 | 3 | 7/3 |\n| AOI21 (Y = !(AB + C)) | A, B = 2; C = 1 | A, B, C = 4 | A, B, C = 2 | A, B: 6/3 = 2 · C: 5/3 |\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Series → multiply width by the stack count. Parallel → size for one. 2:1 means PMOS doubles.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **Follow-up: \"Which is better, NAND or NOR?\"** **NAND**: its slow PMOS are in **parallel**, and its series stack is **NMOS**, which is fast. NOR puts PMOS in series, so they must be huge: more area, more input capacitance (logical effort 5/3 vs 4/3). That's why libraries are **NAND-heavy** and NOR3+ is avoided.\n- **Follow-up: \"At FinFET nodes?\"** Widths are **quantized in fins**, so you choose legal fin counts (you can't use 1.5 fins) and compare the options in SPICE.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Size for the fewest parallel devices that can be on in a valid pull-up.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Each NMOS 1; each PMOS 4","Each NMOS 2; each PMOS 1","Each NMOS 2; each PMOS 2","Each NMOS 1; each PMOS 2"],"ans":2,"why":["The two unit NMOS sum to 2R; widening PMOS does not fix the pull-down.","The pull-down matches, but one width-1 PMOS has 2R.","Two R/2 NMOS resistances add to R; one width-2 PMOS alone supplies R.","The pull-up matches, but the series NMOS path remains 2R."],"ex":"Two R/2 NMOS resistances add to R; one width-2 PMOS alone supplies R.","oral":"Q5. \"Size CMOS gates at 2:1 and 1:1.\" (reported) T1"},{"id":"V007","k":"md-core-q6-draw-the-stick-diagram-of-a-nand2-reported-t1","d":"cmos","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND2 diffusion connectivity","q":"For a static CMOS NAND2 with common poly order A-B, which pair of diffusion-node sequences implements the intended networks? Here x is the internal series node.","a":"![stick diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/stick.svg)\n\n**How to draw any stick diagram (5 steps):**\n\n1. **Rails:** VDD (metal) along the top, GND (metal) along the bottom.\n2. **Diffusion:** a **p-diffusion** strip near VDD (PMOS), an **n-diffusion** strip near GND (NMOS).\n3. **Poly:** one **vertical poly line per input**, crossing **both** strips. Every crossing is a transistor.\n4. **Order the inputs with an Euler path:** find one input order that traces **every edge of both the pull-up and the pull-down graph once**. Then each diffusion strip is **unbroken**: shared source/drain, smallest area.\n5. **Contacts and metal:** connect the diffusion between/around the gates to VDD, GND or the output as the schematic says; tie the output from p-diff to n-diff with metal.\n\n**NAND2 with order A-B:** PMOS strip = **VDD | A | Y | B | VDD** (two PMOS in parallel share the output contact in the middle). NMOS strip = **Y | A | x | B | GND** (series: the middle node x has no contact).\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Rails outside, diffusion inside, one poly per input, Euler path for unbroken diffusion, output metal from p to n.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **Follow-up: \"AOI21?\"** Pull-down: (A series B) ∥ C. Pull-up: (A ∥ B) series C. Order **A-B-C** is an Euler path in both, so one unbroken strip each.\n- **Color convention (if drawing in color):** green = n-diffusion, yellow/brown = p-diffusion, red = poly, blue = metal, X = contact.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Trace the Y-to-rail path with only A asserted, then with both inputs asserted.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["PMOS: VDD-A-Y-B-VDD; NMOS: Y-A-x-B-GND","PMOS: VDD-A-Y-B-GND; NMOS: Y-A-x-B-GND","PMOS: VDD-A-Y-B-VDD; NMOS: Y-A-Y-B-GND","PMOS: VDD-A-x-B-Y; NMOS: Y-A-GND-B-Y"],"ans":0,"why":["The PMOS paths connect Y to VDD in parallel; the NMOS devices connect Y to GND in series through x.","A pull-up diffusion endpoint at GND creates the wrong network.","Tying the internal node back to Y bypasses A rather than making a two-device series path.","This makes the PMOS series and NMOS parallel, the NOR arrangement."],"ex":"A NAND output falls only when both NMOS devices conduct. Either PMOS must be able to pull it high.","oral":"Q6. \"Draw the stick diagram of a NAND2.\" (reported) T1"},{"id":"V008","k":"md-core-q7-draw-a-transistor-level-latch-and-a-d-flip-flop-reported-t1","d":"seq","lvl":1,"f":"short","title":"Q7. \"Draw a transistor-level latch and a D flip-flop.\" (reported)","q":"Q7. \"Draw a transistor-level latch and a D flip-flop.\" (reported)","a":"![latch diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/latch.svg)\n\n![dff diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/dff.svg)\n\n> **Latch (level-sensitive):** a **transmission gate** (TG) feeds an inverter pair. When the clock enables the input TG, the latch is **transparent**: Q follows D. When the clock flips, the input TG turns **off** and a **feedback TG** turns **on**, closing the loop, so the inverters **hold** the value.\n>\n> **Positive-edge D flip-flop = two latches back to back (master-slave).** The **master is transparent when CLK is low**, the **slave when CLK is high**. On the **rising edge** the master closes and holds D, and the slave opens and passes it to Q. So Q only changes at the rising edge.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Latch = TG in + two inverters + TG feedback. DFF = master (open on CLK low) + slave (open on CLK high). Captures at the rising edge.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **Where setup comes from:** D must get through the master's input TG and inverter and settle the master loop **before** the edge closes it.\n- **Where hold comes from:** the master's input TG doesn't close instantly (clock buffer delay, CLK/CLKB overlap), so D must stay put a little **after** the edge.\n- **Clk-to-Q:** slave TG + output inverter delay.\n- **Follow-up: \"Why use TGs instead of single NMOS pass gates?\"** An NMOS passes a **weak 1** (VDD − Vt); a TG passes **both levels fully**.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."]},{"id":"V009","k":"md-core-q8-why-is-pmos-slower-than-nmos-t1","d":"dev","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"Why PMOS devices are wider","q":"In a textbook planar model with equal geometry and mobility μn≈2μp, why is the PMOS made about twice as wide in a balanced inverter?","a":"> Holes have **lower mobility** than electrons, roughly **half** in silicon, so a same-size PMOS carries less current and has higher resistance. That's why the textbook inverter uses **Wp ≈ 2 Wn** to balance rise and fall. At advanced FinFET nodes, **strain engineering** (e.g., SiGe in PMOS) closes much of the gap, so the ratio is closer to 1:1, and you use the actual models.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Hole mobility ≈ ½ electron → PMOS ≈ 2× wider for equal drive (planar). FinFET strain narrows the gap.**\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Compare the mobility and width factors in the current model.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["To compensate for lower hole mobility and increase pull-up current","To make two parallel PMOS devices mandatory for a rise","To remove the PMOS gate capacitance from the input","To halve the PMOS threshold voltage by doubling width"],"ans":0,"why":["With other parameters comparable, doubling PMOS width compensates its lower mobility in the drive estimate.","An inverter has one PMOS; width compensation does not require parallel-input topology.","A wider PMOS adds gate capacitance rather than eliminating it.","Width changes drive and capacitance; it does not generally halve threshold voltage."],"ex":"With other parameters comparable, doubling PMOS width compensates its lower mobility in the drive estimate.","oral":"Q8. \"Why is PMOS slower than NMOS?\" T1"},{"id":"V010","k":"md-core-q9-how-would-you-reduce-power-timing-and-power-optimization-for-low-power-reported-t1","d":"pwr","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"Supply scaling and switching power","q":"Assume activity, capacitance and clock frequency remain fixed. Reducing VDD by 10% changes dynamic switching power by approximately how much?","a":"> First **separate the three components**: **dynamic switching** (αCV²f), **short-circuit** (both networks on during a slow edge), and **leakage** (static). Then attack each:\n\n| Lever | Cuts | Costs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Lower VDD** | Dynamic ∝ V² (−10% V ≈ −19% dynamic) | Speed; smaller margins |\n| **Clock gating** | Dynamic (stops clock toggling in idle logic) | ICG area, enable timing |\n| **Reduce activity / glitches** | Dynamic | Logic changes |\n| **Smaller cells off the critical path** | Dynamic + leakage (less C and width) | Delay on that path |\n| **Sharp input slews** | Short-circuit | Upstream drive |\n| **High-Vt cells off the critical path** | Leakage (exponential) | Slower |\n| **Longer channel length variants** | Leakage | Area, speed |\n| **Stacking / input-vector control** | Leakage (stack effect) | Design effort |\n| **Power gating (header/footer switch)** | Leakage when idle | Wake-up time, IR drop, state loss (needs retention) |\n| **Body biasing / DVFS** | Leakage or dynamic, adaptively | Complexity |\n\n> The rule: **optimize the critical path for speed and everything else for power.** Use LVT only where timing needs it, and HVT everywhere else.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Dynamic = αCV²f. Leakage is exponential in Vt. Speed where it matters, low power everywhere else.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **\"What's a footer cell?\"** An NMOS power switch between the logic's virtual ground and real ground; turning it off cuts leakage (header = PMOS switch on VDD).","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Use the exponent of VDD in the switching-power model.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["19% lower","10% lower","21% lower","Unchanged"],"ans":0,"why":["The voltage-squared factor becomes 0.9² = 0.81.","A 10% reduction would follow a linear voltage law.","The 21% figure corresponds to the increase from multiplying voltage by 1.1.","Fixed activity and frequency do not remove voltage dependence."],"ex":"With Pdyn = αCVDD²f, Pnew/Pold = 0.81. This estimate excludes any resulting frequency, activity or leakage change.","oral":"Q9. \"How would you reduce power? Timing and power optimization for low power.\" (reported) T1"},{"id":"V011","k":"md-core-q10-how-would-you-characterize-the-leakage-of-a-standard-cell-library-with-minimal-compute-t1","d":"char","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"Spend leakage-characterization compute wisely","q":"Which initial plan measures static, state-dependent library leakage with the least unnecessary simulation work?","a":"<div class=\"co co-analogy\"><p class=\"co-t\">Picture it</p>\n\nLeakage is a **photo, not a video**: you need one DC snapshot per input state, not a movie of the cell switching. That's why it's cheap if you set it up right.\n\n</div>\n\nThis is the closest thing to a real NVIDIA question for this role. **Clarify first, then give a plan.**\n\n> **First, I'd clarify the deliverable:** state-dependent leakage for the .lib (a value per input state, plus a default), at which **PVT corners**, for how many cells and Vt flavors, and at what accuracy.\n>\n> **Then the key insight: leakage is a DC quantity.** I don't need transient simulations at all. For each cell and each **input state**, one **DC operating-point solve** gives the supply current. That's orders of magnitude cheaper than timing characterization.\n>\n> **Plan, cheapest first:**\n>\n> 1. **Enumerate the states:** a combinational cell has 2ⁿ input vectors; a sequential cell also needs its **internal state** (Q = 0/1) and clock level. **Stack effect** makes leakage strongly state-dependent, so states matter.\n> 2. **Batch, don't launch:** put **many cells and states in one netlist**, or use parameter sweeps (`.alter` / `.data`), so model loading and license and job overhead happen once, not thousands of times.\n> 3. **Reuse proven equivalences only:** drive-strength variants (X1/X2/X4) are often parallel copies, so leakage scales with width. **Characterize one fully and spot-check** the others rather than assuming. Symmetric inputs can share results **only if the layout is symmetric**.\n> 4. **Be smart about corners:** leakage is exponential in temperature and Vt, so **worst case is fast/hot/high-voltage**. Run the corners the library actually requires. If intermediate temperatures are needed, interpolate in the **log domain**, validated on a sample.\n> 5. **A device-level shortcut (propose, then validate):** most leakage comes from **off transistors**. Characterize each device type's off-current once per corner, then **compute** cell leakage per state from which devices are off (and in what stack). Validate against SPICE on a representative sample; use SPICE for the outliers.\n> 6. **QA automatically:** flag leakage that **doesn't rise with temperature**, **X2 ≠ ~2× X1**, stacked states that leak **more** than single-off states, missing or zero values, and big changes from the previous library.\n>\n> **Pre- vs post-layout:** leakage is DC, so parasitic capacitance doesn't matter, but **layout-dependent effects** (stress, well proximity) shift Vt. So final numbers should come from the extracted netlist with those effects; pre-layout is fine for exploration.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Clarify → leakage is DC (op-point, not transient) → enumerate states → batch → reuse only proven equivalences → worst = FF/hot → validate shortcuts → automated QA.**\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Value to show</p>\n\nEngineering judgment about **compute cost** and **correctness at the same time**, plus automation (your Faraday strength). This question tests how you'd actually work, so structure beats trivia.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Ask what changes the steady-state off-device network.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Scale every drive variant from X1 without spot-checking topology or layout effects.","Solve DC operating points for required input/internal states and PVT corners, batching jobs and validating any reused equivalences.","Measure one input state per cell and reuse it for every state.","Run long switching transients for one random input vector at every corner."],"ans":1,"why":["Scaling can be a shortcut only after its accuracy is established.","Static supply current is a DC quantity, while state and corner coverage remain essential.","Off-device stacks change with input and stored state.","Switching activity is unnecessary for static leakage and one vector misses state dependence."],"ex":"Eliminate unnecessary transient work, not required state coverage. Sequential cells also require valid stored states and clock conditions.","oral":"Q10. \"How would you characterize the leakage of a standard-cell library with minimal compute?\" T1"},{"id":"V012","k":"md-core-q11-draw-a-6t-sram-cell-how-do-read-and-write-work-t1","d":"mem","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"SRAM read disturb","q":"During a conventional 6T SRAM read of Q=0, both bitlines start high. Which mechanism threatens the stored bit?","a":"![sram diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/sram.svg)\n\n> Two **cross-coupled inverters** store the bit on Q and QB. Two **NMOS access transistors (pass gates)**, gated by the **wordline**, connect Q to **BL** and QB to **BLB**.\n>\n> **Read:** precharge both bitlines high, raise WL. The side storing 0 discharges its bitline through **access + pull-down** in series. The **sense amp** fires once there's ~100-200 mV difference. **Danger:** the 0 node bumps up (voltage divider); if it crosses the other inverter's trip point, the cell **flips** (**read disturb**). So **pull-down must be stronger than access**.\n>\n> **Write:** drive one bitline to 0, raise WL. The **access transistor must overpower the pull-up** holding the node high. So **access must be stronger than pull-up**.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Read must not flip; write must flip. PD > PG > PU.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **\"How does a sense amp work?\"** A **cross-coupled latch** with an enable (SAE). Its two sides start balanced on the bitline difference; when SAE fires, **positive feedback** amplifies that small difference to a full 0/1. The bitline swing at SAE must beat the **sense-amp offset** (from mismatch).\n- Deeper: T1.4.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Trace current from a high bitline through the cell to ground.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The pull-up must overpower access to pull the precharged bitline low","The access transistor must overpower the pull-down to preserve Q=0","The access transistor lifts Q; the pull-down must keep that rise below a destructive level","The sense amplifier writes Q=0 before the wordline rises"],"ans":2,"why":["The pull-down, not the pull-up, discharges the bitline for a stored zero.","A stronger access path relative to pull-down increases the read bump and can upset the cell.","The access/pull-down divider raises the low storage node; read stability requires adequate pull-down strength.","The cell develops the read signal before sensing; the sense amplifier is not the normal pre-read writer."],"ex":"The access/pull-down divider raises the low storage node; read stability requires adequate pull-down strength.","oral":"Q11. \"Draw a 6T SRAM cell. How do read and write work?\" T1"},{"id":"V013","k":"md-core-q12-how-does-a-rom-read-work-what-does-the-keeper-do-t1","d":"mem","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"ROM keeper tradeoff","q":"In a precharged-high NOR ROM, connected selected cells discharge BL to represent 0. What is the principal risk of making the PMOS keeper stronger?","a":"> In a **NOR ROM**, each bit is one NMOS on the bitline, programmed by whether its **contact/via exists**. Precharge the bitline high, raise one wordline. **Connected** cell → bitline **discharges** (reads 0). **Not connected** → bitline **stays high** (reads 1).\n>\n> The **keeper** is a weak PMOS holding the bitline high against **leakage**. It creates **two opposite failure modes**:\n>\n> - **Reading a 1:** all the other connected cells on the bitline **leak** it down. A keeper that's **too weak** gives a **false 0**.\n> - **Reading a 0:** the one selected cell must discharge the bitline **against the keeper**. A keeper that's **too strong** gives a **late or missing 0**.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**The keeper saves the 1 and fights the 0. Check both, at the worst code pattern and corner.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **\"How many rows per bitline?\"** Limited by (N−1) off-cells' leakage vs one on-cell's current, worst at **fast/hot**. Fix with **hierarchical (shorter) bitlines**, low-leakage cells, or a better keeper/sensing scheme.\n- **\"Why does the code (data) matter?\"** It decides how many cells connect, which sets **leakage and bitline capacitance**. Verify the **worst pattern**, not a random one.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Compare keeper current with leakage during a 1 and cell current during a 0.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The stored code changes because the keeper creates a programming contact","Both 0 and 1 margins necessarily improve","An unselected 1 droops faster because the keeper sinks BL current","A selected 0 may discharge too slowly or fail to cross the sense threshold"],"ans":3,"why":["Programming connectivity is fixed independently of keeper strength.","Retention improves at the expense of discharge contention.","A PMOS keeper sources current to resist droop; it does not sink BL current.","The selected pulldown must overcome current supplied by the keeper."],"ex":"The keeper preserves a high bitline but opposes the selected discharge. Verify both read polarities at the relevant corners and code patterns.","oral":"Q12. \"How does a ROM read work? What does the keeper do?\" T1"},{"id":"V014","k":"md-core-q12b-what-is-a-rom-answer-it-like-an-engineer-talking-to-an-engineer-t1","d":"mem","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"ROM data changes electrical loading","q":"In a precharged NOR mask-ROM column, why can a new bit pattern change read margin even when the row and column counts stay fixed?","a":"<div class=\"co co-analogy\"><p class=\"co-t\">Picture it</p>\n\nThink of a **printed book**: the paper and binding (the array) are trivial; the hard part is making sure **every page is readable in every lighting condition** (corner), for **every edition size** (configuration), whatever text is printed (code pattern).\n\n</div>\n\nThe trap: a junior answers with the **definition**. An experienced engineer answers with **what it is physically, where the design problem actually is, and how you prove it works**. Answer in three layers and **let Bo pull you deeper**.\n\n**Layer 1: the one-liner (5 seconds).**\n\n> \"Non-volatile, read-only memory whose data is **fixed at manufacture by the layout itself**: a bit is a transistor that is or isn't connected to the bitline.\"\n\n**Layer 2: the engineer's answer (60-90 seconds). This is the default.**\n\n> \"Physically, a mask ROM is an **array of single transistors**. In the usual **NOR organization**, each cell's gate is a wordline and its drain either **has a contact or via to the bitline or doesn't**, so the data is literally the **presence or absence of one via**. That makes it the **densest and lowest-power** on-chip storage, and since software can't change it, it's where you put **boot and security code, microcode and constant tables**.\n>\n> But the bitcell is the easy part. **The design problem is the periphery and the margins.** A read is a **precharged, single-ended bitline**: one selected cell has to pull it down **against a keeper**, while every other connected cell on that bitline is **leaking it the same way** when you're trying to read a 1. So the key trade-offs are **rows per bitline versus leakage and speed, keeper strength, and when to fire the sense**, all at the worst corner, which is fast/hot for leakage and slow/cold at low VDD for discharge.\n>\n> And because a **compiler** generates every size for every chip, with **whatever code the firmware team hands you**, the real job is **verification**: proving that **every configuration and every code pattern** reads correctly at every corner, out to high sigma. That's the 'feasibility verification' in your job description.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**\"A bit is a via that's there or not. The cell is trivial; the design is the periphery; the job is verification across every size, code pattern, corner and sigma.\"**\n\n</div>\n\n**Layer 3: the \"wow\" details (use one or two, only when he engages).**\n\n| Say this | Why it lands |\n| --- | --- |\n| \"**The data is part of the circuit.** A column with many connected cells has **more leakage and more bitline junction capacitance**, so the ROM's speed and margin **depend on the code**. The worst case for reading a 1 is a column where **every other cell is connected**.\" | Shows you understand ROM is **data-dependent**, which most candidates miss |\n| \"Some ROMs **encode the data per column**, e.g., **store the inverse** when a column is mostly connected cells, with a flag bit, to **cut worst-case leakage and capacitance**.\" | A real design optimization; shows you think about architecture, not just the cell |\n| \"Adjacent rows typically **share a drain contact**, so one contact serves two cells. That's part of why NOR ROM is so dense.\" | Layout-level awareness |\n| \"**Via-programmed** ROMs put the code in an upper via layer, so a **late firmware change costs one mask**, not a full re-spin. **Diffusion- or implant-programmed** ROMs are denser, but changes hit early masks.\" | Connects circuit choice to **schedule and cost**, a senior-engineer concern |\n| \"Bitline length is set by **(N−1)·I_off vs I_on at fast/hot**. At advanced nodes the fix is **hierarchical bitlines** (short local bitlines + a global one) rather than a bigger cell, since the cell is **one fin**.\" | Shows the **deep-submicron** constraint and the architectural fix |\n| \"Sensing is often **single-ended** (a skewed inverter or domino-style stage), timed by a **replica or dummy bitline** that tracks PVT. Replica tracks **global** variation, not the **local** mismatch of the one cell being read, so you still need sigma margin.\" | Timing + variation nuance |\n| \"**Test is easy in one way:** the contents are known, so BIST reads every address and compresses it into a **MISR signature** to compare against the expected one.\" | Links to your **MBIST** experience |\n| \"There's a **security angle**: via-programmed bits can be read optically by delayering the chip, which is one reason **implant-programmed** ROMs exist for sensitive code.\" | Unexpected, thoughtful. Use only if the conversation goes there |\n| \"The figures of merit I'd track: **bits/µm², access time, energy per read, leakage per bit, and rows per bitline** at the worst corner.\" | Sounds like someone who has owned a macro |\n\n**How to deliver it.**\n\n1. Give **Layer 1**, then go straight into **Layer 2** without being asked. That's the answer.\n2. End with an **offer**: \"I can draw the column and walk through the read margin if that helps.\"\n3. Use **one** Layer-3 detail **per follow-up**, not all at once. Wow comes from **precision when pushed**, not from reciting everything.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Guardrail</p>\n\nDon't claim you've **built** a ROM compiler or used column-inversion encoding. Say \"**one technique I've read about** is…\" or \"**my understanding** is…\". Depth with honesty beats depth with a false claim, especially at NVIDIA.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Treat the programmed contacts as circuit elements.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Only connected-cell read-zero speed changes; the retained-high read is independent of unselected cells.","Replica timing exactly cancels both pattern-dependent loading and local selected-cell mismatch.","The number of connected cells changes bitline junction capacitance and aggregate off-state leakage.","Only the expected output changes; the electrical load is fixed entirely by row count."],"ans":2,"why":["Unselected connected cells can collectively discharge a bitline that should remain high.","A replica tracks selected common effects but cannot guarantee cancellation of every pattern and local mismatch.","The programmed connections are part of the electrical circuit.","Programmed diffusion connections and off-state paths can change the electrical load."],"ex":"For a retained-high read, unselected connected cells can collectively discharge the bitline; connected diffusion also loads the bitline.","oral":"Q12b. \"What is a ROM?\" (answer it like an engineer talking to an engineer) T1"},{"id":"V015","k":"md-core-q12c-what-is-a-standard-cell-same-layered-approach-t1","d":"char","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"Standard-cell deliverables","q":"Which delivery best describes a usable standard-cell library entry?","a":"**Layer 1:**\n\n> \"A pre-designed, pre-characterized logic or storage block, like an inverter, NAND or flop, at a **fixed height**, so place-and-route can **tile them in rows**.\"\n\n**Layer 2 (default):**\n\n> \"A standard cell is really a **contract**, not just a circuit. It's a **transistor-level design**, a **layout** that abuts cleanly with its neighbors (**fixed track height, shared power rails, legal pin access**), and a set of **views that must all agree**: the schematic, GDS, the **LEF** for place-and-route, the **Verilog** model, and the **.lib** that synthesis and STA trust for timing, power and noise. Each function comes in a **family**: several **drive strengths** and **Vt flavors**, so the tools can trade speed against leakage path by path.\n>\n> The design work is **transistor sizing and topology** against **delay, power, leakage, area and input capacitance**, then **characterizing** it across slew, load and PVT, and checking **EM, aging and variation**. The part people underestimate is that **a cell is only as good as its .lib**: if the model is optimistic, silicon fails what STA passed.\"\n\n**Layer 3 (\"wow\" follow-ups):**\n\n- \"**Pin access is a first-class metric** at advanced nodes. A fast cell the router can't reach is useless. NVIDIA Research has published on automated cell layout for routability (**NVCell**).\"\n- \"At FinFET nodes **width is quantized in fins**, so a family's drive strengths are really **fin-count choices**, and the **track height** sets how many fins fit. That's the core density-vs-drive trade.\"\n- \"**Flops dominate clock power**: they toggle every cycle through the clock pin, so **clock-pin capacitance** is one of the most valuable things to shave in a library.\"\n- \"**Multi-Vt** lets tools put LVT only on critical paths. The library's job is to make those swaps **footprint-compatible** so they're free for the router.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**\"A standard cell is a contract: circuit + layout + LEF + Verilog + .lib that all agree. A cell is only as good as its .lib.\"**\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Consider the information required by synthesis, place-and-route and STA.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A Liberty table without correspondence to the fabricated circuit","A fast schematic without legal row abutment or pin access","A DRC-clean GDS file with no characterized timing model","Consistent transistor, layout, physical-abstract, logic and timing/power views"],"ans":3,"why":["A model disconnected from the implemented device can predict behavior the cell does not have.","A circuit that cannot be placed and routed legally is not a usable standard-cell implementation.","Geometry alone cannot supply STA behavior or prove view consistency.","Implementation tools need agreeing physical, logical and electrical descriptions of the same cell."],"ex":"Implementation tools need agreeing physical, logical and electrical descriptions of the same cell.","oral":"Q12c. \"What is a standard cell?\" (same layered approach) T1"},{"id":"V016","k":"md-core-q13-what-s-the-difference-between-em-and-ir-how-do-you-know-about-it-t1","d":"int","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"Distinguish IR drop from EM","q":"Which statement correctly separates resistive supply drop from electromigration?","a":"> **IR drop** is **voltage lost now**: current through the resistance of the power grid and wires means the cell sees less than VDD, so it's **slower** and margins shrink. **Electromigration** is **damage over lifetime**: high current density pushes metal atoms along a wire or via until it **voids (opens) or forms hillocks (shorts)**. IR is a **performance** problem today; EM is a **reliability** problem after years.\n>\n> **How I learned it:** I first dug into EM/IR while **helping a friend prepare for a CAD EM/IR interview at Apple**. That was self-study, not signoff experience. Since then I've connected it to cell design: the **current waveform** through each wire and via, **average vs RMS vs peak** limits, and what a fix costs in timing and area.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**IR = voltage now (speed). EM = metal wear-out over years (reliability). Both start from the real current path.**\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Guardrail</p>\n\nOnly use the Apple-friend story if it's true, and say it plainly as learning. Never imply you worked at Apple or used its tools. Then **prove it with reasoning** (T1.8): Black's equation, avg/RMS/peak, where cells fail, how fixes trade off.\n\n</div>\n\n- **\"What would you check first on an EM violation?\"** Which **wire/via**, which **metric** (avg/RMS/peak), what **frequency, load and temperature** were assumed. Confirm it's a real stress, not a setup or mapping error, then fix the **cause**: more vias, wider metal, lower drive, or limiting the load/frequency the cell is allowed.\n- **\"Can average current be zero and still fail?\"** Yes. A **signal wire** carries current both ways: average ≈ 0, but **RMS (heating)** and **peak** are not.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Separate the circuit’s voltage equation from the physical failure mechanism.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Passing IR-drop checks guarantees that every wire also passes EM","IR drop reduces local voltage during operation; EM accumulates metal damage from current stress","IR drop requires atomic migration; EM is the instantaneous I×R voltage loss","IR drop depends only on average current; every EM rule uses only peak current"],"ans":1,"why":["The checks constrain different effects and have different limits.","The mechanisms are immediate electrical voltage loss versus cumulative material degradation.","This reverses the two mechanisms.","The applicable waveforms and reliability metrics must be specified; there is no universal peak-only EM rule."],"ex":"Both begin with current paths, but acceptable voltage loss does not certify long-term wire/via reliability.","oral":"Q13. \"What's the difference between EM and IR? How do you know about it?\" T1"},{"id":"V017","k":"md-core-q14-rc-charging-how-long-to-reach-50-90-t1","d":"dly","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"RC threshold-crossing time","q":"An ideal RC node charges from 0 to VDD after a step. What are its 50% and 90% crossing times?","a":"<div class=\"co co-eq\"><p class=\"co-t\">RC step response</p>\n\n```latex\nV(t) = V_{DD}\\,(1 - e^{-t/RC}) \\qquad t_{50\\%} = 0.69\\,RC \\qquad t_{63\\%} = RC \\qquad t_{90\\%} = 2.3\\,RC\n```\n**Remember:** \"0.7 to half, 1 to 63, 2.3 to 90.\" From 10% to 90% ≈ **2.2 RC**.\n\n</div>\n\n- **Charge sharing** (a classic, also asked at NVIDIA): a node of 10 fF at VDD connects to 5 fF at 0 V. Charge is conserved: V = VDD × 10/(10+5) = **0.67 VDD**. It can falsely flip a dynamic node.\n- **Series capacitors:** same **charge** on each; voltages split **inversely** to capacitance.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Invert the exponential step response rather than scaling time with voltage.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["RC and 2 RC","0.5 RC and 0.9 RC","0.693 RC and 2.303 RC","0.693 RC and 2.197 RC"],"ans":2,"why":["One time constant reaches about 63.2%, not 50%.","The voltage rise is exponential, not linear.","Use t = −RC ln(1 − fraction).","2.197 RC is the 10-90% interval, not the 0-90% crossing."],"ex":"At 50%, −ln(0.5) = 0.693; at 90%, −ln(0.1) = 2.303.","oral":"Q14. \"RC charging: how long to reach 50%? 90%?\" T1"},{"id":"V018","k":"md-core-q15-drc-and-lvs-are-clean-is-the-cell-done-t1","d":"flow","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"Beyond DRC and LVS","q":"A new cell passes DRC and LVS. Which work is still needed before declaring it production-ready?","a":"> No. **DRC** only says the layout is **manufacturable** (geometry rules). **LVS** only says it's **the circuit I intended** (connectivity and devices). I still need **extracted (post-layout) simulation** for timing, power and margins; **EM/IR** and reliability checks; **characterization** into the .lib; and a check that **all the views agree** (schematic, layout, LEF, .lib, Verilog) and that the **router can reach the pins**.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**DRC = legal shape. LVS = right circuit. Neither = works, fast enough, or reliable.**\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Separate geometric legality, circuit correspondence and electrical performance.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Only another schematic-level nominal truth-table simulation","Only pin renaming, because DRC already certifies routing access in every context","No electrical work, because LVS certifies timing and reliability","Extracted electrical characterization, reliability checks and agreement among integration views"],"ans":3,"why":["A nominal schematic test misses layout parasitics and corner-dependent failures.","DRC cleanliness alone does not prove all abutment and router pin-access contexts.","LVS does not certify delay, noise margin or lifetime.","DRC checks geometric rules and LVS checks extracted connectivity; timing, margins, reliability and integration remain separate evidence."],"ex":"DRC checks geometric rules and LVS checks extracted connectivity; timing, margins, reliability and integration remain separate evidence.","oral":"Q15. \"DRC and LVS are clean. Is the cell done?\" T1"},{"id":"V019","k":"md-core-q16-tell-me-about-a-tough-deadline-resume","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q16. \"Tell me about a tough deadline.\"","q":"Q16. \"Tell me about a tough deadline.\"","a":"> At Faraday I owned chip-top DFT and STA for a **22nm shuttle** on a **1.5-month schedule**. I **triaged** which checks actually gated tapeout, and built **Tcl/Perl automation** so the repeatable checks ran themselves, which kept my time for real problems like timing violations. We taped out on schedule, and I got a **top-10% rating**. **Lesson:** under a hard deadline, prioritize ruthlessly and automate the repeatable part.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V020","k":"md-core-q17-tell-me-about-a-mistake-resume","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q17. \"Tell me about a mistake.\"","q":"Q17. \"Tell me about a mistake.\"","a":"> During my move from the PhD track to the master's, I **took on too much** (research, teaching 400+ students, consulting) and my grades slipped. The real mistake was **not flagging it or cutting scope early**; I tried to grind through quietly. I fixed how I manage commitments, recovered that term, and finished strong. Now I **raise problems early**. [Better, if you have one: a **technical** miss you caught, e.g., a corner you hadn't simulated, and the check you added so it wouldn't recur.]\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Value to show</p>\n\nNVIDIA's value **Intellectual Honesty: \"learn from mistakes, share learnings.\"** Own it in two sentences, then the fix.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V021","k":"md-core-q18-tell-me-about-a-disagreement-or-teamwork-across-groups-resume","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q18. \"Tell me about a disagreement or teamwork across groups.\"","q":"Q18. \"Tell me about a disagreement or teamwork across groups.\"","a":"> At Faraday the flow crossed **synthesis, physical design and verification**, split between **Taiwan and Vietnam**. My checks sat in the middle, so I made every handoff explicit: what was checked, what was open, what I needed back. [Or the implant **chip-platform disagreement**: I stopped arguing and asked for their reasoning, found their real constraint, and reframed my proposal to de-risk it.] **Lesson:** most conflicts are an unspoken constraint; listen for it.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V022","k":"md-core-q19-why-nvidia-why-this-role-t1","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q19. \"Why NVIDIA? Why this role?\"","q":"Q19. \"Why NVIDIA? Why this role?\"","a":"> Everyone at the leading edge uses the same foundry process. The **standard-cell and memory libraries** are one of the few places a design company can get **more out of that same process** than its competitors. And every cell is used **billions of times** on chips where **power is the limit**, so a small gain in a flop's power or area is a real gain for the product. I want to work on that layer, and NVIDIA is where it matters most.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V023","k":"md-core-q19b-why-the-rom-team-and-standard-cells-or-rom-t1","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q19b. \"Why the ROM team?\" (and \"standard cells or ROM?\")","q":"Q19b. \"Why the ROM team?\" (and \"standard cells or ROM?\")","a":"> Honestly, I'm excited about both, for different reasons. **ROM** appeals to me because it's where the physics is most exposed: a ROM read is a **small-signal sensing problem with leakage fighting you**, and \"feasibility verification\" means proving that **every code pattern, every size the compiler can make, at every corner** still reads correctly. That's the kind of margin and verification work I did with PVT validation near threshold, and it uses my analog instincts directly. **Standard cells** appeal to me because of the **leverage**: one flop design gets used billions of times. I'd be happy on either side, and I'd like to learn both.\n\n- **If he says the seat is mostly ROM:** \"Great. The bitline-leakage-versus-keeper trade-off and compiler verification are what I'd want to get good at first.\"\n- **If mostly standard cells:** \"Great. Flops and level shifters are where I'd want to go deep: that's where the timing and low-voltage margins live.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Guardrail</p>\n\nDon't pick one so strongly that you sound disappointed by the other. Ask him which way the opening leans (Part P.4, question 1).\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Why a ROM team exists at NVIDIA (the one-liner)</p>\n\nEvery chip needs **fixed on-chip data**: boot code, firmware and microcode, constant tables. A **custom ROM** is **denser, faster and lower-power** than storing it in SRAM or logic, and a compiler lets every chip team generate the exact size it needs.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V024","k":"md-core-q19c-why-are-you-a-good-fit-for-this-role-t1","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q19c. \"Why are you a good fit for this role?\"","q":"Q19c. \"Why are you a good fit for this role?\"","a":"> Three reasons. **First, I understand the device under the cell.** Materials science and near-threshold analog design taught me why transistors **leak, vary and slow down cold at low voltage**, which is exactly what decides a cell's or a ROM's margin. **Second, I've been the library's customer.** At Faraday I signed off a **22nm chip in PrimeTime**, where every delay came from the .lib, and I integrated **MBIST** for the memories, so I know what users need from a library: **accurate models and testable memories**. **Third, I verify and automate.** I validated my WICS blocks **across every PVT corner**, and I built **Tcl/Perl automation** for the Faraday checks. That's the \"feasibility verification\" and \"new flows\" part of your job description. What I'm still building is production cell and ROM design itself, and I learn fastest by doing.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Device physics underneath + library customer downstream + verify-and-automate habit. Name the one gap honestly, then show you're closing it.**\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Map it to the job description (if he asks for specifics)</p>\n\n| JD asks for | Your evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Deep-submicron process issues | Materials BS; leakage and variation near threshold |\n| SPICE, adapt to new tools | Virtuoso/SPICE across PVT (WICS) |\n| Margin, variation simulations | Corner validation of the TIA/filter |\n| DRC/LVS debug | NTT block-level DRC/LVS |\n| Scripting, automation | Tcl/Perl/Csh flows at Faraday |\n| Teamwork, communication | Taiwan-Vietnam handoffs; GSI for 400+ students |\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V025","k":"md-core-q19d-why-go-from-analog-design-to-a-rom-team-t1","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q19d. \"Why go from analog design to a ROM team?\"","q":"Q19d. \"Why go from analog design to a ROM team?\"","a":"> Because a ROM is **much more analog than it looks**. The stored bit is trivial, but the **read path is an analog problem**: one selected cell has to **pull a precharged bitline down against a keeper**, while **hundreds of unselected cells leak** it the other way, and the **sense circuit** has to decide correctly at the right moment, at the **worst corner and code pattern**. That's the same thinking I used on the implant front end: **small signals, tight margins, subthreshold leakage, and checking every PVT corner**, not just typical. What changes is scale: my analog block was **one instance**; a ROM compiler generates **every size, for every chip**. I want that leverage, and I'd bring an analog designer's paranoia about margins to it.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**\"A ROM read is an analog problem in a digital box\": keeper vs leakage vs sense timing. Same margin thinking as my subthreshold front end, at far bigger scale.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **If pushed \"but you've never designed memory\":** \"True. I've been close to it: MBIST integration and NTT SRAM-block integration. And I can walk you through a ROM column's read margin right now.\" Then draw Q12.\n- **Same logic for standard cells:** \"A flop's setup time is a race between two analog paths; a level shifter is a contention problem.\" (Part D2.1)","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V026","k":"md-core-q19e-you-started-a-phd-why-did-you-leave-and-why-did-your-research-change-resume","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q19e. \"You started a PhD. Why did you leave, and why did your research change?\"","q":"Q19e. \"You started a PhD. Why did you leave, and why did your research change?\"","a":"**Why you'll get it:** your résumé shows a doctoral fellowship, an MS, and research that moved from an **RF receiver** (mixer-first, 2023) to an **implant front end** (2024-25). A circuit engineer will read that as \"is he committed to design?\" Answer calmly, briefly, and forward.\n\n> I started on the **PhD track in Prof. Wentzloff's WICS lab**, working on **ultra-low-power circuits**. My research moved from a **low-power RF receiver** to the **analog front end of an implantable auditory device**, which followed the lab's projects and pulled me deeper into **subthreshold design and margin work**. Over time I realized what I enjoy most is **designing circuits that ship**, where the result is used by a whole product, rather than spending five more years narrowing onto one research question. So I **completed my MS** and moved toward industry circuit design. The research wasn't a detour. It's where I learned to **design for margin across PVT and reason from device physics**, which is exactly what cell and ROM design needs.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**PhD track → research moved from RF to implant front end (followed the work, went deeper into low-power margins) → realized I want circuits that ship → completed the MS → industry circuit design. Not a detour: it's where the margin thinking comes from.**\n\n</div>\n\n- **\"Why not finish the PhD?\"** \"A PhD rewards going very deep on one question for years. I want to **build and ship circuits** that many designs depend on. Once I was sure of that, finishing the MS and moving to industry was the honest choice, not momentum.\"\n- **\"Why did your topic change from RF to the implant?\"** [verify: the real reason, e.g., funding or project needs in the lab, or your interest in the medical front end.] Frame: \"It **followed the lab's projects**, and it moved me toward the part I liked most: **low-power analog margins**.\"\n- **\"How did your advisor take it?\"** \"It was an honest conversation, and we're on good terms. He saw that my strengths are **hands-on and applied**, and that I'd do well in industry.\"\n- **\"Would you go back?\"** \"No. I'm building an industry career in circuit design.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Guardrails</p>\n\n1. **Never say \"I have a PhD.\"** Say \"I started on the PhD track and **completed my MS**.\"\n2. Never \"burned out,\" \"lost interest,\" or anything negative about the lab, the research or your advisor.\n3. Don't make **consulting** the reason you left, and don't imply it only started afterward (the dates overlap).\n4. Keep it to **~45 seconds**, then stop. Let him ask more if he wants.\n5. Bring up the grad-school overload/probation **only if he asks** about grades (Part A2, C3).\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V027","k":"md-core-q20-do-you-have-questions-for-me","d":"story","lvl":1,"f":"oral","title":"Q20. \"Do you have questions for me?\"","q":"Q20. \"Do you have questions for me?\"","a":"See **Part P.4** and the insider questions in **P.4b**. Ask two: **what a new grad owns first**, and **the transition from 65nm to your node**. If EM came up, ask about his EM flow work.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V028","k":"md-core-q21-write-a-spice-measurement-a-quick-script-reported-coding-spice-python-t1","d":"code","lvl":1,"f":"mcq","title":"Choose a SPICE falling-delay measurement","q":"For an inverter at VDD = 0.8 V, which trigger/target pair measures 50%-to-50% high-to-low propagation delay?","a":"```text\n* 50%-to-50% falling delay, input rising (VDD = 0.8 V)\n.meas tran tphl TRIG v(in)  VAL=0.4 RISE=1\n+               TARG v(out) VAL=0.4 FALL=1\n* 20%-80% output rise slew\n.meas tran trise TRIG v(out) VAL=0.16 RISE=1\n+                TARG v(out) VAL=0.64 RISE=1\n* leakage: hold one input state, let it settle, average supply current\n.meas tran ileak AVG i(vdd) FROM=5n TO=6n\n* (or a pure DC operating point per state: .op, then read i(vdd))\n```\n\n```python\n# parse measured delays; missing = failure, never zero\nimport re, sys\nvals = {}\nfor line in open(sys.argv[1]):\n    m = re.match(r\"\\s*(\\w+)\\s*=\\s*([-\\d.eE+]+)\", line)\n    if m: vals[m[1]] = float(m[2])\nfor k in [\"tphl\", \"trise\"]:\n    if k not in vals: sys.exit(f\"MISSING {k}\")  # fail loudly\nprint(vals)\n```\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Say the thresholds out loud (50% for delay, 20-80% for slew). A missing measurement is a failure, not a zero.**\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Distinguish propagation delay between pins from slew on one pin.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Output rising through 0.16 V; output rising through 0.64 V.","Input falling through 0.4 V; output rising through 0.4 V.","Input rising through 0.4 V; output falling through 0.4 V.","Input rising through 0.16 V; output falling through 0.64 V."],"ans":2,"why":["This measures 20-80% output rise slew.","This is the low-to-high propagation arc.","An input rise causes the inverter output fall; both thresholds are 50% VDD.","These unequal thresholds are not the specified 50%-to-50% delay."],"ex":"Choose event direction and threshold independently. A missing crossing must be reported as a failed measurement, not a zero delay.","oral":"Q21. \"Write a SPICE measurement / a quick script.\" (reported: \"Coding (SPICE/Python)\") T1"},{"id":"V029","k":"md-core-x1-1-compare-nor-type-and-nand-type-rom-arrays","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"NOR and NAND ROM topology","q":"For the contact-programmed NOR and series-string NAND ROM organizations described here, which tradeoff follows from their topology?","a":"<div class=\"co co-analogy\"><p class=\"co-t\">Picture it</p>\n\nNOR vs NAND ROM is **a row of parallel doors vs a hallway of doors in series**: parallel doors let you out fast through any one; a series hallway saves space but you must pass every door to get through.\n\n</div>\n\n![norvsnand diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/norvsnand.svg)\n\n\n**Short answer:** \"**NOR is fast; NAND is dense.** NOR puts every cell in **parallel** on the bitline, so a read is **one transistor pulling down**. NAND puts cells in a **series string**, which saves contacts and area but makes the read current tiny, so it's **slow**.\"\n\n| | **NOR ROM** | **NAND ROM** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Structure | One transistor per bit from bitline to ground; gate = wordline | 8-16 transistors in series between bitline and ground |\n| Read | Raise **one** WL; a connected cell discharges the BL | **All unselected WLs high**, selected WL **low**; the string conducts only if the selected cell is \"always on\" |\n| Programming | Contact/via present or absent | Selected cells made **always-on** (depletion implant or a shorting layer) |\n| Speed | **Fast**: single-device pull-down; pattern-independent path | **Slow**: series stack, small current, body effect; worse with longer strings |\n| Density | Lower: needs a drain contact per cell (shared by two rows) | **Higher**: no contacts inside the string |\n| Leakage | **High**: all connected cells leak onto the BL | Lower: a string is a series stack |\n| Layout | Simple and regular | Needs the programming implant/layer; string length vs speed |\n| Used for | **Speed-critical** on-chip ROM (the usual choice in logic processes) | **Density-critical**, slower storage |\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"In a logic process, NOR usually wins because **access time and compatibility with standard logic layers** matter more than the last bit of density. And in NOR, the **worst-case read-1 is data-dependent**: the more connected cells on a column, the more leakage.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Count series devices and contacts along the selected read path.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["NOR offers a short selected discharge path; NAND saves internal contacts but adds series-stack resistance","NOR eliminates unselected-cell leakage because cells are parallel","NAND offers a single-device discharge; NOR always discharges through every row","NAND string length improves current because more series devices add drive"],"ans":0,"why":["Parallel NOR cells permit a selected single-cell path; a NAND string can be denser but its series path reduces current.","Off cells in parallel can contribute aggregate bitline leakage.","The stated organizations have the opposite discharge topology.","More series devices increase path resistance and introduce internal-node effects rather than adding parallel drive."],"ex":"Parallel NOR cells permit a selected single-cell path; a NAND string can be denser but its series path reduces current.","oral":"X1.1 \"Compare NOR-type and NAND-type ROM arrays.\""},{"id":"V030","k":"md-core-x1-2-how-do-you-physically-program-a-mask-rom-bit-via-contact-vs-vt-implant-and-the-cycle-time-impact","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Late ROM code changes","q":"A ROM process supports late via programming, and wafers can be banked before that mask. Compared with early Vt-implant programming, why can a late firmware revision turn around faster?","a":"<div class=\"co co-analogy\"><p class=\"co-t\">Picture it</p>\n\nVia programming is **writing the message on the last page of the book**: easy to reprint late. Implant programming is **writing it into the paper itself**: harder to read, but a change means making the paper again.\n\n</div>\n\n**Short answer:** \"The bit is set by **one mask layer**. **Contact/via programming** decides whether the cell's drain connects to the bitline; **Vt-implant programming** makes some cells high-Vt so they never turn on. The big difference is **where that mask sits in the process**, which sets **how long a code change takes**.\"\n\n| | **Contact / via-programmed** | **Vt-implant (or diffusion) programmed** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| How a \"1\"/\"0\" is made | Contact or via **present or absent** between drain and bitline | Every cell is connected; selected cells get an extra **implant → very high Vt** (never conducts at read) |\n| Mask position | **Back end** (contact or an upper via layer) | **Front end** (early implant masks) |\n| Code-change cycle time | **Short**: wafers can be run through front-end steps and **held (\"banked\")**, then finished with the new via mask | **Long**: the change is near the start of the flow, so most of the **multi-month** fab cycle repeats |\n| Density | Slightly lower (programming via must fit rules) | **Densest** (uniform array, no programming via) |\n| Security | Bits can be read by **delayering and imaging** the via layer | **Harder to read** optically (implant is invisible in a normal image) |\n| Leakage/variation | Unconnected cells don't load the bitline | All cells connect → more junction capacitance; high-Vt cells still leak slightly |\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"If firmware is still changing late in the project, I'd push for **upper-via programming**: the code change becomes **one late mask on banked wafers** instead of a front-end restart. For **security-sensitive boot code**, implant programming is harder to reverse-engineer. It's a **schedule-vs-security** trade, not just density.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Think about which fabrication steps have already been committed.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The via mask makes the bit electrically erasable after fabrication","Via programming removes the need for code-pattern verification","The implant mask can be changed after all interconnect steps finish","The new code changes a late mask while already completed front-end processing is reused"],"ans":3,"why":["Mask programming is not post-fabrication electrical rewriting.","Different codes still change connectivity, loading and leakage.","An early implant change generally requires revisiting early process steps.","Banked wafers can finish using the revised late programming layer."],"ex":"The relevant scheduling lever is where the programming mask occurs in the process, not whether the data are logically different.","oral":"X1.2 \"How do you physically program a mask ROM bit? Via/contact vs Vt-implant, and the cycle-time impact.\""},{"id":"V031","k":"md-core-x1-3-draw-and-explain-the-rom-read-timing-how-do-you-design-precharge-and-the-decoder-to-minimize-access-time","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Sequence a dynamic ROM read","q":"Which ordering avoids a precharge-versus-read fight in a precharged NOR ROM?","a":"![romtiming diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/romtiming.svg)\n\n**Short answer:** \"**Precharge off → wordline on → bitline develops → sense enable → latch → wordline off → precharge on.** Access time is **decode + wordline RC + bitline development + sense**, so I attack the biggest term first.\"\n\n**The sequence (and the two rules):**\n\n1. CLK edge latches the address; **precharge turns off** (PCH_b high).\n2. **Decoder** fires one wordline. **Rule 1: precharge must be off before WL rises**, or you get crowbar current and a fight on the bitline.\n3. **Bitline develops**: falls for a connected cell, droops slightly (leakage) for an unconnected one.\n4. **SAE** fires, timed by a **replica/dummy bitline** so it tracks PVT; output latches.\n5. **WL falls, then precharge restores the bitline.** **Rule 2: WL must be off before precharge turns on.**\n\n**Precharge design:**\n\n- **Size the precharge PMOS** to restore the full bitline within the precharge window: roughly t ≈ C_BL·ΔV / I_pch. It sets **cycle time**, not access time.\n- **Keeper separate from precharge:** precharge is strong and brief; the keeper is weak and on during evaluate.\n- Keep the **precharge clock non-overlapping** with WL; watch the **simultaneous-precharge current burst** (all bitlines at once) for **IR/EM**; stagger by bank if needed.\n\n**Decoder design (minimize access time):**\n\n- **Predecode** (2→4 or 3→8) + a final NAND per row: splits a big fan-in into cheap stages.\n- **Size the path by logical effort**: the WL driver sees (columns × gate cap + wire), so the chain from address to WL uses ~FO4 stages.\n- **Wordline RC** grows with **length²**: put the decoder **in the middle** and drive both halves, or **segment the wordline** (global/local WL).\n- Speed-critical gates can be **skewed** (favor the rising WL edge).\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"I'd **break the access time into its terms** from a post-layout sim of the **largest configuration**: decode, WL RC to the far cell, BL development, SAE. The fix is different for each: logical effort for decode, **segmentation for WL RC, hierarchical bitlines for BL development**.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Track which devices source and sink the bitline in each phase.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Precharge off → sense → wordline on → precharge on.","Precharge off → wordline on → develop bitline → sense → wordline off → precharge on.","Precharge on → wordline on → sense → precharge off.","Wordline on → precharge off → sense immediately → wordline off."],"ans":1,"why":["Sensing precedes development and recharging begins with the wordline active.","It provides nonoverlap and development time before sensing.","The precharge device fights the selected discharge path.","The initial overlap can create contention, and immediate sensing lacks margin."],"ex":"Precharge and evaluation must not contend; sensing follows sufficient bitline development.","oral":"X1.3 \"Draw and explain the ROM read timing. How do you design precharge and the decoder to minimize access time?\""},{"id":"V032","k":"md-core-x1-4-what-sense-amplifier-would-you-use-for-a-dense-rom-bitline-how-do-you-handle-mismatch","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"ROM sense-margin budgeting","q":"A ROM latch sense amplifier compares a single bitline against a reference. Which condition should qualify sense-enable timing?","a":"**Short answer:** \"A NOR ROM column has **one bitline, no complement**, so it's **single-ended**. For density I'd use **short local bitlines with a simple single-ended stage** (a skewed inverter or domino-style sense). If speed matters, a **latch-type sense amp against a reference bitline**, which gives differential-style sensing with small swing.\"\n\n| Option | Pros | Cons | Use when |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Single-ended (skewed inverter / domino)** | Tiny, fits the column pitch, robust | Needs **large swing** → slower; sensitive to leakage and noise | Dense, lower-speed ROM; short local BLs |\n| **Latch SA vs reference bitline** (dummy column producing a mid-level reference) | **Small swing → fast**, lower energy | Area; needs a good reference; **offset** matters | Speed-critical ROM |\n| **Fully differential (two cells per bit)** | Most robust margin | **2× array area** | Rare: critical or very low-voltage cases |\n\n**Mismatch mitigation:**\n\n- **Bigger input devices**: offset σ ∝ 1/√(WL) (Pelgrom), at the cost of area and bitline load.\n- **Symmetric, common-centroid layout** and identical routing on both inputs.\n- **Budget swing for offset**: fire SAE when swing > offset at **5-6σ**; the **replica timing** margin must include it.\n- **Pre-amplify or offset-cancel** (auto-zero) if the swing budget is too tight.\n- **Aging:** a ROM's data never changes, so **some sense amps read the same value for years** → **asymmetric NBTI** → offset drifts. Check **aged offset**, and precharge/equalize both sides to balance stress.\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"In a ROM the offset problem gets worse **over life**: fixed data means **fixed asymmetric stress** on each sense amp. I'd sign off the offset **after aging**, not just fresh.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Ask what sets the sign of the initial regenerative imbalance.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The fresh typical corner passes, so aged mismatch need not be evaluated","The nominal signal reaches zero differential so regeneration starts symmetrically","The largest available latch gain makes offset irrelevant","The worst-case signal exceeds reference error, offset, noise and required yield margin"],"ans":3,"why":["Unequal stress can shift lifetime offset, so fresh typical results are insufficient.","Zero differential leaves offset and noise to decide the result.","Gain does not remove input-referred offset; it can amplify the wrong initial imbalance.","A regenerative latch amplifies both desired differential and input-equivalent errors; the signal must beat their budget."],"ex":"A regenerative latch amplifies both desired differential and input-equivalent errors; the signal must beat their budget.","oral":"X1.4 \"What sense amplifier would you use for a dense ROM bitline? How do you handle mismatch?\""},{"id":"V033","k":"md-core-x2-1-how-do-you-counteract-dibl-and-gate-oxide-tunneling-when-scaling-cells-to-3nm-2nm","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Device and designer leakage controls","q":"Which pairing correctly associates a device technology with the leakage mechanism it primarily addresses?","a":"**Short answer:** \"Mostly the **device** handles it: **FinFET and GAA** give the gate multi-sided control, which cuts **DIBL**; **high-k metal gate** keeps the physical oxide thick enough to limit **tunneling**. As a **cell designer**, I manage the rest through **device choice and topology**.\"\n\n- **Foundry side:** multi-gate electrostatics (fin/nanosheet) → lower DIBL and a steeper subthreshold slope; high-k/metal gate → same electrical thickness with a thicker physical oxide → much less gate tunneling.\n- **Designer side (what you control):**\n    - **Vt flavor**: HVT/UHVT where speed allows.\n    - **Channel-length variants** (longer L where offered) for leakage-critical devices (e.g., ROM bitcells, keepers).\n    - **Stacking**: a series off-stack lowers Vds on each device → **less DIBL** (the stack effect).\n    - **Reduce Vds on idle devices**: power-gate idle banks, lower retention voltage.\n    - **Avoid minimum devices** where leakage or mismatch dominates.\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"At 3nm/2nm, **GIDL and junction leakage** can matter as much as subthreshold at the hot corner, and body biasing is much weaker in FinFET than in planar or FD-SOI, so **Vt/L choice and topology** are the main levers left to the designer.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Separate short-channel electrostatics from transport through the gate insulator.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Series stacking primarily thins the oxide; high-k increases drain voltage to suppress leakage","Multi-gate control reduces DIBL; high-k permits thicker physical dielectric at similar electrical thickness to reduce tunneling","A cell designer directly changes the foundry gate dielectric to select HVT","Multi-gate control eliminates oxide tunneling; high-k mainly removes drain coupling by raising VDD"],"ans":1,"why":["Stacking redistributes device voltages; it does not thin oxide.","Gate electrostatics and dielectric tunneling are distinct controls described in the source.","Cell designers select offered devices; they do not change the fabrication dielectric.","The pairing confuses electrostatic barrier control with oxide transmission."],"ex":"Foundry architecture controls gate electrostatics and oxide properties. Cell design then uses allowed Vt/L variants and topology.","oral":"X2.1 \"How do you counteract DIBL and gate-oxide tunneling when scaling cells to 3nm/2nm?\""},{"id":"V034","k":"md-core-x2-2-wire-resistance-dominates-at-these-nodes-how-do-you-analyze-and-optimize-long-bitline-rc","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Scale a distributed bitline","q":"For the wire-only part of an otherwise unchanged uniform distributed RC bitline, doubling length approximately changes delay by what factor?","a":"<div class=\"co co-analogy\"><p class=\"co-t\">Picture it</p>\n\nA long bitline is **one endless street**: every extra block adds traffic (capacitance) and potholes (resistance), and the trip time grows with the **square** of the length. Hierarchical bitlines are **local streets feeding a highway**.\n\n</div>\n\n![hierbl diagram](assets/fig/diagrams/hierbl.svg)\n\n\n**Short answer:** \"Treat the bitline as a **distributed RC**: delay grows with **length squared**. Analyze on an **RC-extracted, full-length critical-path netlist** at the **far cell**, then **shorten the effective line**, mainly with **hierarchical bitlines**.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-eq\"><p class=\"co-t\">Distributed RC</p>\n\n```latex\nt_{50\\%} \\approx 0.38\\,R_w C_w \\;(+\\; 0.69\\,R_{drv} C_w) \\qquad R_w C_w \\propto L^2\n```\n**Remember:** \"**double the length → 4× the wire delay**.\" Halving a bitline into two segments cuts wire RC per segment by ~4×.\n\n</div>\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n- Extract **R and C** (not C-only); simulate a **critical-path netlist** with the **full distributed bitline and wordline**, the selected cell at the **far end**.\n- Separate **wire delay vs device delay** (the cell's current into the bitline's C) to know which to fix.\n- Check **bitline-to-bitline coupling**: a neighbor discharging couples onto a bitline that should stay high.\n\n**Optimization:**\n\n| Fix | Helps | Costs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hierarchical bitlines** (short local BL + global BL) | Cuts C, leakage and RC together | More sense stages and area |\n| **Sense in the middle / double-ended access** | Halves the effective length | Floorplan complexity |\n| **Wider or upper-layer metal** for global lines | Lower R | More C per length; track usage |\n| **Spacing / shielding** between bitlines | Less coupling | Density |","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Count the length dependence of both R and C.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["√2×","4×","1×","2×"],"ans":1,"why":["A square-root law does not describe distributed RC scaling.","Both resistance and capacitance double, making their product four times larger.","Wire parasitics do not remain constant with length.","This includes only one of the two length dependencies."],"ex":"With fixed cross section and environment, R ∝ L and C ∝ L, so wire delay ∝ L². Device and sense overhead can change total access-time scaling.","oral":"X2.2 \"Wire resistance dominates at these nodes. How do you analyze and optimize long bitline RC?\""},{"id":"V035","k":"md-core-x2-3-rom-code-is-permanent-and-the-hardware-must-last-the-gpu-s-life-how-do-you-design-wordline-drivers-against-nbti-hci-and-em","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Wordline-driver aging and reliability","q":"Which verification best supports wordline-driver lifetime signoff for a ROM with uneven row activity?","a":"**Short answer:** \"Simulate the **aged** circuit, not just the fresh one: apply a realistic **stress profile** to get each device's **ΔVt**, re-simulate at **end of life**, and check EM on the driver's output path at the **real access rate**.\"\n\n| Mechanism | Where in a WL driver | How you simulate it | Design against it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **NBTI** (PMOS, Vt rises under negative gate bias, hot) | The **pull-up PMOS** that drives the WL high | Aging-aware SPICE (e.g., reliability simulators) with **duty cycle per row** → ΔVt → end-of-life netlist | **Margin** on WL rise time; don't size to the edge; sense timing that tracks aged WL |\n| **HCI** (hot carriers during switching at high Vds) | Driver devices charging a **large WL capacitance**, esp. with **slow input slews** | Same flow, with **switching activity** | **Sharp input slews** into the driver; staged (tapered) drivers |\n| **EM** | **Driver output, vias and the WL metal**: peak/RMS current charging the WL | Current waveforms at the **max access rate** and **temperature** vs rule limits | **Via arrays** at the driver output, wider WL metal, **segmented WLs** (less charge per driver) |\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"ROM usage is **uneven**: boot code rows may be read heavily, others rarely. So aging and EM stress **differ by row**, and I'd simulate the **worst-used rows**, not an average. And the sense amps see **fixed data forever**, so their aging is **asymmetric**.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Distinguish stored data from access activity and device stress history.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Use average chip activity for every row and only fresh typical timing","Check DC leakage only because ROM data never changes","Increase all driver widths and assume both aging and EM are solved","Apply documented per-row stress assumptions to aged-device timing and waveform-based EM checks"],"ans":3,"why":["Average activity can hide heavily used rows and fresh models omit aging shifts.","Fixed contents do not mean wordlines stop switching or carrying charging current.","Upsizing changes capacitance, current and loading; it cannot replace lifetime and interconnect verification.","Bias duty, temperature and switching affect degradation, while actual current waveforms constrain metal/via reliability."],"ex":"Bias duty, temperature and switching affect degradation, while actual current waveforms constrain metal/via reliability.","oral":"X2.3 \"ROM code is permanent, and the hardware must last the GPU's life. How do you design wordline drivers against NBTI, HCI and EM?\""},{"id":"V036","k":"md-core-x3-1-for-an-aoi-oai-gate-how-do-you-size-for-symmetric-rise-fall-and-a-balanced-switching-threshold-vm","d":"seq","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"AOI21 matched-resistance sizing","q":"For Y = NOT(AB + C), use R proportional to 1/W, equal lengths, and a reference inverter with NMOS width 1 and PMOS width 2. Which widths match each worst-case conducting path to its reference?","a":"**Short answer:** \"Make the **worst-case pull-up path** as strong as the **worst-case pull-down path**. Each network's worst path is its **longest series stack**, so width scales with the stack depth, times the **mobility ratio β = μn/μp** for PMOS.\"\n\n**AOI21 (Y = !(AB + C)), β = 2:**\n\n- **Pull-down:** worst path = **A-B in series (2)** → A, B = **2**; C alone → **1**.\n- **Pull-up:** (A ∥ B) in series with C → worst path = **2 in series** → each PMOS = 2 × β = **4**.\n- Symmetric drive → rise ≈ fall for the **worst-case input**. Other input patterns will be faster.\n\n<div class=\"co co-eq\"><p class=\"co-t\">Switching threshold</p>\n\n```latex\nV_m = \\frac{V_{tn} + r\\,(V_{DD} - |V_{tp}|)}{1 + r} \\qquad r = \\sqrt{\\frac{k_p}{k_n}}\n```\n**Remember:** \"equal currents at V_m.\" Stronger PMOS → V_m moves **up**. For V_m ≈ VDD/2 you need k_p ≈ k_n (with |Vtp| ≈ Vtn).\n\n</div>\n\n- **Complex gates have more than one Vm:** it depends **which inputs switch** (e.g., A alone vs A and B together).\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"Symmetric rise/fall isn't always the goal. For **minimum average delay** of an inverter chain, the optimal Wp/Wn is closer to **√(μn/μp) ≈ 1.4** than 2, because the extra PMOS width adds input capacitance. And at FinFET the answer is **fin counts**, so I'd compare legal options in SPICE.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Write the series paths separately for the pull-up and pulldown.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["NMOS A/B/C = 2/2/2; PMOS A/B/C = 2/2/2","NMOS A/B/C = 1/1/2; PMOS A/B/C = 2/2/4","NMOS A/B/C = 1/1/1; PMOS A/B/C = 4/4/2","NMOS A/B/C = 2/2/1; PMOS A/B/C = 4/4/4"],"ans":3,"why":["A width-2 PMOS in each two-device stack leaves twice the reference pull-up resistance.","A/B, not C, form the NMOS series stack.","The NMOS series path is too weak, and the pull-up widths do not balance its two-device paths.","A-B is a two-device pulldown; each pull-up path also has two PMOS devices."],"ex":"NMOS A and B each contribute R/2, while C alone contributes R. Each width-4 PMOS contributes half the reference PMOS resistance.","oral":"X3.1 \"For an AOI/OAI gate, how do you size for symmetric rise/fall and a balanced switching threshold (Vm)?\""},{"id":"V037","k":"md-core-x3-2-how-do-you-calculate-logical-and-electrical-effort-for-a-custom-cell-and-optimize-it-for-fo4","d":"dly","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Size by stage effort","q":"A custom gate input has logical effort g = 2. For target stage effort gh = 4 and output load Cload, which input capacitance is required?","a":"**Short answer:** \"**g** = the cell's input capacitance divided by an inverter's that delivers the same output current; **h** = C_out/C_in. Stage delay **d = g·h + p**. Aim for **stage effort g·h ≈ 4**.\"\n\n- **Compute g per input** from the sized transistors: AOI21 at β=2 → input A: (2 + 4)/3 = **2**; input C: (1 + 4)/3 = **5/3**. (Inverter = 1 + 2 = 3.)\n- **Parasitic delay p** ≈ diffusion capacitance on the output node relative to an inverter (NAND2 ≈ 2, NOR2 ≈ 2, AOI21 higher).\n- **Optimize for a load:** for f = g·h = 4 on input A: h = 4/2 = 2 → **C_in = C_load/2** → pick the drive strength that gives that input cap.\n- **Path:** F = G·B·H, N ≈ log₄F, each stage effort F^(1/N).\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"Logical effort also tells you **which pin to give the critical signal**: the input with **lower g** (and nearer the output) is the faster arc. That's a real library decision: make the fast pin obvious in the .lib.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Solve for h before choosing a size.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Cload/4","2Cload","4Cload","Cload/2"],"ans":3,"why":["This gives h = 4 and stage effort 8.","This gives h = 0.5 and stage effort 1.","This gives h = 0.25 and stage effort 0.5.","h = 4/2 = 2, and Cin = Cload/h."],"ex":"Electrical effort h = Cload/Cin; logical effort multiplies, rather than replaces, this load ratio.","oral":"X3.2 \"How do you calculate logical and electrical effort for a custom cell and optimize it for FO4?\""},{"id":"V038","k":"md-core-x3-3-how-would-you-use-multi-vt-uhvt-to-lvt-inside-a-custom-rom-module","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Threshold assignment in ROM","q":"What is a defensible starting multi-Vt strategy for a leakage-dominated custom ROM?","a":"**Short answer:** \"Put **high-Vt where the transistor count is**, the **bitcells**, and **low-Vt only on the timing-critical periphery**. Leakage scales with device count; speed is set by a handful of devices on the access path.\"\n\n| Block | Vt choice | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Bitcell array** | **HVT / UHVT** (or long-L) | Most transistors, most leakage; also **improves I_on/I_off**, so **more rows per bitline** |\n| **Decoder, WL drivers, sense, output** (access path) | **LVT / SVT** | Few devices; they set access time |\n| **Keeper** | HVT or long-L | Weak on purpose; stable |\n| **Precharge** | SVT | Needs strength for cycle time, not access |\n| **Idle banks / non-critical control** | HVT + **power gating** | Leakage when not accessed |\n\n- **Check both sides of the trade:** HVT bitcells **lower I_on**, so the **read-0 discharge** slows. Verify slow/cold/low-VDD discharge **and** fast/hot read-1 leakage.\n- **Leakage math:** ~**10× per ~80-100 mV** of Vt, so a UHVT array can cut array leakage by an order of magnitude or more.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Separate the many idle devices from the few devices on the active timing path.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Evaluate HVT in the large array and reserve faster flavors for critical periphery, then verify both read margins","Use UHVT everywhere without rechecking discharge timing","Use LVT in every bitcell because lower read delay also guarantees minimum standby power","Choose only by transistor count and ignore the selected-cell current"],"ans":0,"why":["Array leakage motivates HVT, but reduced selected-cell current still needs read-zero and keeper-contention checks.","Slower periphery or bitcell discharge can violate access time even when leakage improves.","LVT generally trades higher leakage for speed, so standby power can worsen.","The read is driven by one selected path; its on-current remains essential."],"ex":"Array leakage motivates HVT, but reduced selected-cell current still needs read-zero and keeper-contention checks.","oral":"X3.3 \"How would you use multi-Vt (UHVT to LVT) inside a custom ROM module?\""},{"id":"V039","k":"md-core-x4-1-walk-through-a-monte-carlo-setup-for-margins-what-sigma-for-a-library-vs-a-memory-array-and-why","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"What zero Monte Carlo failures establishes","q":"Under independent identical trials, 1,000 Monte Carlo runs produce zero failures. Using the approximate 95% rule-of-three bound, what conclusion is justified?","a":"<div class=\"co co-analogy\"><p class=\"co-t\">Picture it</p>\n\nMonte Carlo is **rolling dice**. To see a one-in-a-billion roll you'd need about a billion rolls. **Importance sampling is fishing where the fish are**: sample mostly near the failure, then correct the odds.\n\n</div>\n\n**Short answer:** \"Define the **failure metric** first, run **local mismatch MC at each relevant global corner**, and switch to **high-sigma methods** when the target is beyond ~4σ. Memory needs **5-6σ per cell** because **millions of identical cells** must all work; a standard cell's variation is handled **statistically per path** in STA, so ~3σ cell characterization with **LVF** is typical.\"\n\n**Setup, step by step:**\n\n1. **Metric + failure:** e.g., ROM: bitline swing at SAE − SA offset < 0; flop: clk-Q pushout > 10%.\n2. **Global corner:** run local MC at **each** relevant corner (SS/cold for discharge, FF/hot for leakage), not only TT.\n3. **Mismatch models on**, correct devices and layout effects (post-layout netlist).\n4. **Sample size:** ~1,000s of runs resolve ~3σ. **Zero fails in n runs ≈ p < 3/n** only.\n5. **High sigma:** **importance sampling** / Solido-style methods to reach 5-6σ with thousands, not billions, of runs.\n6. **Check the tail shape:** don't extrapolate a Gaussian fit blindly; real tails are often non-Gaussian.\n\n| | Typical target | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Memory array (SRAM/ROM bitcell, sense)** | **5-6σ** | p_fail × N cells; 1 Mb at 99.9% yield → ~1e-9 per cell → ~6σ. **ROM usually has no redundancy**, so it needs the full margin |\n| **Standard cells** | ~3σ characterization + **LVF/POCV** | Timing variation is combined **statistically along each path** in STA; different failure model |\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"The sigma target comes from **N and the yield goal**, and from **repair**: SRAMs often have **redundancy**, which relaxes the per-cell target; a **ROM can't be repaired** because its content is fixed, so it needs the full sigma.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Use the sample count in the rule-of-three bound.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The failure probability is below 10⁻⁹ because every sample passed","The measured failure probability must be exactly 1/1,000","The failure probability is exactly zero","The failure probability has an upper bound of about 0.003; this does not establish a 10⁻⁹ tail"],"ans":3,"why":["A thousand clean trials do not probe a one-in-a-billion tail.","There were zero observed failures, and the confidence bound is not a point estimate of 1/n.","An unobserved failure can still have nonzero probability.","The approximate upper confidence bound is 3/n = 0.003."],"ex":"No observed failures is evidence with limited statistical resolution. Extremely rare-event targets need a justified tail method and model validation.","oral":"X4.1 \"Walk through a Monte Carlo setup for margins. What sigma for a library vs a memory array, and why?\""},{"id":"V040","k":"md-core-x4-2-static-vs-dynamic-ir-drop-in-a-standard-cell-layout-how-do-you-optimize-the-in-cell-rails","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Diagnose switching droop","q":"A supply is acceptable under average load but droops during simultaneous ROM precharge. Which investigation best targets the missing mechanism?","a":"**Short answer:** \"**Static IR** = average current × grid resistance, a DC shift. **Dynamic IR** = the **transient droop** when many cells switch at once: peak current through R, L·di/dt, minus what local decap supplies. Inside the cell, the levers are **rail width, rail-to-grid vias and where the current enters**.\"\n\n- **In-cell / row level:**\n    - **Rail width** on M0/M1 (within the track budget) and **rail via density** to the upper grid (\"power stapling\").\n    - Place **high-current devices near rail taps**; avoid supply current running along long thin internal wires.\n    - **Decap / filler-decap cells** near high-activity cells for dynamic droop.\n    - **Multi-finger** big drivers to spread current across rail contacts.\n- **Chip-level context:** grid strength, spreading simultaneous switching (clock skew scheduling), decap placement.\n- **ROM-specific:** **precharging every bitline at once** is a large current burst: stagger precharge by bank.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"The failure appears only when many loads switch together.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Use average current times DC resistance, since total charge per cycle is unchanged.","Analyze transient current, grid impedance and local decap delivery; consider staggered bank precharge.","Measure only the current peak, ignoring how long the pulse persists and where decap is placed.","Check only the package inductance, excluding local rail resistance and capacitance."],"ans":1,"why":["Identical charge per cycle can produce different transient droop when concentrated in time.","The burst excites resistive and inductive impedance, with local charge support affecting droop.","Droop depends on the waveform and distributed delivery network, not only its maximum.","Local impedance and charge support can contribute materially to device-level droop."],"ex":"Static IR is the average DC drop. Dynamic droop depends on the current waveform, R, L and decap, so inspect the event in time.","oral":"X4.2 \"Static vs dynamic IR drop in a standard-cell layout. How do you optimize the in-cell rails?\""},{"id":"V041","k":"md-core-x4-3-debug-a-drc-failure-for-dummy-gate-placement-and-an-lvs-failure-from-a-floating-bulk","d":"flow","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Array boundary and dummy cells","q":"A cell is DRC-clean alone but fails a dummy-gate rule when placed next to another cell. What is the most useful next step?","a":"**DRC: dummy gate / poly placement.**\n\n1. **Read the exact rule:** gate pitch, dummy poly at the diffusion edge, diffusion-break type, poly extension.\n2. **Check in context:** many of these errors appear **only when the cell abuts neighbors**. Run DRC **with neighbors or fill cells**, not just standalone.\n3. **Fix at the boundary:** add or align the **dummy gate at the cell edge**, use the correct **diffusion break**, keep **uniform gate pitch** across the boundary.\n4. **Re-run DRC in context, then re-extract**: dummy gates and diffusion breaks change **layout-dependent effects** (stress), so timing can shift.\n\n**LVS: floating bulk.**\n\n1. **Identify which device/net:** the report shows a well or substrate with no connection, or a 4-terminal device mismatch.\n2. **Check the tie strategy:** in standard-cell rows, **bulk is usually tied by separate tap cells**, not inside every cell. A standalone cell LVS may need the **tap or the right LVS option** for global bulk nets.\n3. **Check naming:** if the library has **separate bulk pins** (e.g., VNW/VPW for body bias) the **schematic and layout must use the same bulk nets**. A schematic tying bulk to VDD vs a layout on VNW is a classic mismatch.\n4. **Check the layers:** the well covers the devices, and the well/tap contacts exist.\n5. **Fix, re-run LVS, then re-extract.**\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Wow line</p>\n\n\"Half of cell DRC problems are **context problems**: the cell is clean alone and fails when abutted. I'd always check a cell **in a row with its neighbors and fill** before calling it clean.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"The failure appeared only after a new physical boundary was introduced.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Waive the error because standalone DRC already passed","Ignore the neighbor and only tighten SPICE time steps","Inspect the exact boundary rule and legal neighbor/fill context, repair it, then re-extract","Change the schematic Boolean function until LVS reports fewer devices"],"ans":2,"why":["Standalone checking does not cover the failing abutment context.","Transient numerical settings cannot repair a physical-rule violation.","Gate pitch and diffusion-break legality can depend on abutment; the repair can also change layout-dependent electrical effects.","Changing functionality does not address a boundary geometry rule and can break the design contract."],"ex":"Gate pitch and diffusion-break legality can depend on abutment; the repair can also change layout-dependent electrical effects.","oral":"X4.3 \"Debug a DRC failure for dummy-gate placement, and an LVS failure from a floating bulk.\""},{"id":"V042","k":"md-core-x5-1-the-rom-passes-at-tt-but-one-address-reads-0-instead-of-1-at-ff-125-c-debug-it","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"ROM false zero at fast/hot","q":"A precharged NOR ROM should retain BL high for a 1, but one column reads 0 at fast/hot. What is the most useful first electrical check?","a":"> \"A 1 reading as 0 at **fast/hot** says **leakage**. First I'd check **that column's code**: probably most other cells are connected, which is **max leakage and max bitline cap**. Then plot BL, keeper and SAE. If the bitline **droops below the sense threshold before SAE**, options are a **stronger or delayed keeper** (then re-check read-0 at slow/cold), **earlier SAE** if the read-0 still has margin, or **shorter bitlines** if no keeper size satisfies both. Last, check whether it's **one weak local device** by running mismatch MC on that column.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Identify which node was supposed to remain high and when it was sampled.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Plot that column’s BL, keeper current and sense-enable timing under its actual code pattern","Increase the clock period without inspecting the BL waveform","Strengthen the selected pulldown before checking the retained-high failure","Re-characterize only a slow-corner inverter delay table"],"ans":0,"why":["This tests whether code-dependent leakage pulls BL below the decision threshold before sensing.","A longer wait can worsen leakage droop and does not identify the cause.","The failure is a lost high; increasing discharge strength can aggravate it.","An unrelated inverter table cannot establish the column’s retention margin."],"ex":"First distinguish BL droop, sensing timing and local weakness. Any keeper fix must also be checked against selected-zero discharge.","oral":"X5.1 \"The ROM passes at TT but one address reads 0 instead of 1 at FF/125 °C. Debug it.\""},{"id":"V043","k":"md-core-x5-2-the-largest-configuration-misses-access-time-by-10-where-do-you-look","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Debug a slow large ROM configuration","q":"The largest ROM misses access time by 10%. What is the most informative first step?","a":"> \"**Break the path** on the post-layout netlist: decode → WL rise at the **far** column → BL development at the **far** row → SAE → output. Whichever term grew fastest with size is the target: **segment the wordline** if WL RC, **hierarchical bitlines** if BL development, **re-size the decode chain** if decode. Then re-verify that the fix didn't break read-1 leakage.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Different physical delay terms require different remedies.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Upsize every cell before identifying the dominant delay.","Measure decode, far-end wordline, bitline-development and sense/output contributions on the extracted path.","Move sense enable earlier without measuring bitline separation.","Reduce the keeper until access passes without checking retained-high reads."],"ans":1,"why":["Blanket upsizing adds load and may not address wire delay.","A stage-by-stage breakdown distinguishes decoder, wire and sensing limits.","Earlier sensing can trade a timing failure for a data error.","Keeper weakening can compromise leakage tolerance."],"ex":"Choose the fix from the measured bottleneck, then recheck both speed and read margins.","oral":"X5.2 \"The largest configuration misses access time by 10%. Where do you look?\""},{"id":"V044","k":"md-core-x5-3-why-not-just-make-the-keeper-stronger","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"ROM keeper contention","q":"Increasing a ROM bitline keeper improves retained-high margin. Which simultaneous risk must be checked?","a":"> \"It fixes read-1 and **hurts read-0**: the selected cell has to fight it, so discharge slows, contention current rises, and at slow/cold/low-VDD the 0 may not arrive in time. The keeper is a **window**, and if the window closes, the answer is **architecture**: shorter bitlines, not a bigger keeper.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Write net discharge current as selected-cell current minus opposing current.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Elimination of all precharge time because a stronger keeper stores no charge","Lower bitline capacitance because keeper width replaces metal load","Faster discharge because the keeper assists the selected pull-down","Slower or failed legitimate discharge because the selected cell fights more keeper current"],"ans":3,"why":["The bitline still requires recovery and charging time.","A wider keeper does not remove bitline metal or cell capacitance and can add parasitics.","The keeper pulls toward the retained-high state and opposes discharge.","Keeper current subtracts from the selected pull-down during contention; stronger retention can reduce read-zero margin."],"ex":"Keeper current subtracts from the selected pull-down during contention; stronger retention can reduce read-zero margin.","oral":"X5.3 \"Why not just make the keeper stronger?\""},{"id":"V045","k":"md-core-x5-4-what-happens-if-precharge-and-wordline-overlap","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Precharge/evaluate overlap","q":"A selected ROM pulldown turns on while the precharge PMOS is still enabled. What direct mechanism is created?","a":"> \"The precharge PMOS and the selected cell **fight**: **crowbar current**, a bitline that never discharges properly (wrong read), extra power, and **EM/IR stress** on the precharge path. That's why the two are **non-overlapping** by design, and I'd check that at the fast corner where edges shift.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Trace the conducting path through the precharge device and selected cell.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A series-off stack that reduces leakage and improves discharge","A VDD-to-ground contention path that wastes current and can prevent BL discharge","A larger sense differential with no extra supply current","An isolated floating bitline whose only current is junction leakage"],"ans":1,"why":["Both opposing paths are on, not stacked off.","Precharge sources BL while the selected cell sinks it.","Contention reduces useful discharge and draws current.","BL is connected to active pull-up and pulldown paths."],"ex":"Non-overlapping control avoids simultaneous precharge and evaluation. Verify control timing over corners.","oral":"X5.4 \"What happens if precharge and wordline overlap?\""},{"id":"V046","k":"md-core-x5-5-firmware-sends-a-code-change-two-weeks-before-tapeout","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Verify a late via-ROM code change","q":"A via-programmed ROM receives new code with unchanged dimensions. Which validation is required before accepting it?","a":"> \"If the ROM is **via-programmed**, it's a **re-encode on one mask**: regenerate, re-run **content verification**, and re-check **electrical margin for the new code**, since a column that became densely connected changes leakage and capacitance. If it's implant-programmed, this is a schedule conversation, not a circuit one.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Ask whether a column’s physical loading is independent of its contents.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Verify only the addresses whose expected output toggled.","Reuse all electrical results because only data, not geometry, changed.","Verify only that the binary file has the expected length.","Verify regenerated contents and recheck electrical margins for the new connectivity pattern."],"ans":3,"why":["Changed column loading can affect reads at otherwise unchanged addresses.","The code defines part of the geometry and connectivity.","Length does not prove contents or electrical behavior.","Code programming changes physical connections, loading and leakage."],"ex":"Late programmability reduces mask disruption; it does not remove content and circuit verification.","oral":"X5.5 \"Firmware sends a code change two weeks before tapeout.\""},{"id":"V047","k":"md-core-x5-6-a-new-flop-s-characterized-setup-time-is-negative-at-ss-is-that-a-bug","d":"char","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Negative external setup time","q":"A flop has a negative setup requirement measured at external pins. Which interpretation is technically sound?","a":"> \"Not necessarily. Setup is measured at the **external pins**; if internal clock buffering delays the capture, data can arrive after the external edge and still be captured. I'd check the **pass criterion** (e.g., 10% clk-Q pushout), the **waveforms at the boundary**, and that **hold** is still reasonable, since setup and hold trade against each other.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"External pin timing does not reveal the internal latch-closing instant directly.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It is necessarily a sign error because all setup values must be positive","It can be valid if internal clock delay permits later data arrival; inspect pushout and hold together","It permits an arbitrary data change after the external edge at every slew","It proves the flop requires no data-stability interval"],"ans":1,"why":["Negative external constraints can arise from real internal path delays.","Internal clock/data path timing can shift the external sampling window; waveform and hold checks validate the result.","The allowance is bounded and depends on characterized slew, arc and PVT conditions.","A shifted window still has a finite stability requirement."],"ex":"Internal clock/data path timing can shift the external sampling window; waveform and hold checks validate the result.","oral":"X5.6 \"A new flop's characterized setup time is negative at SS. Is that a bug?\""},{"id":"V048","k":"md-core-x5-7-em-passes-at-1-ghz-but-the-design-wants-to-run-the-cell-at-1-5-ghz","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Requalify EM after a frequency increase","q":"A cell passes EM at 1 GHz, but its required toggle rate becomes 1.5 GHz. Which response is justified without assuming a particular pulse shape?","a":"> \"RMS and average current scale **with frequency**, so the margin shrinks by roughly 1.5×. I'd re-run EM at 1.5 GHz with the real **load and slew**; if it fails, either **fix the cell** (vias, wider output metal) or **update the .lib EM limits** (max toggle rate / max load) so the tools keep designers in the safe region.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Which parts of the waveform change when events happen more often?","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Multiply every average, RMS and peak metric by exactly 1.5 without waveform analysis","Approve it because transistor geometry is unchanged","Treat the original 1 GHz result as a frequency-independent lifetime guarantee","Re-evaluate the actual current waveform and applicable EM metrics at the new load, slew and frequency"],"ans":3,"why":["Average, RMS and peak need not share the same frequency scaling.","Unchanged geometry can experience greater electrical stress.","The original operating condition does not certify a different one.","Waveform duty, overlap and stress metrics determine the new reliability margin."],"ex":"Re-run the qualified checks, then improve the current path or restrict legal operating conditions if needed. Qualification of the original discussion: average, RMS and peak current do not generally share one linear frequency multiplier. Their scaling depends on pulse shape, duration and overlap.","oral":"X5.7 \"EM passes at 1 GHz, but the design wants to run the cell at 1.5 GHz.\""},{"id":"V049","k":"md-core-x5-8-the-sense-amp-passes-fresh-but-fails-after-aging-simulation","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Explain aged sense-offset failure","q":"A ROM sense amplifier passes fresh simulations but fails after prolonged asymmetric data stress. Which mechanism should be checked first?","a":"> \"**Asymmetric stress:** in a ROM the data is fixed, so that sense amp reads the **same value for years** and one side's devices age more, shifting its **offset**. Fixes: **more swing budget** at SAE, **balance the stress** (precharge/equalize both sides between reads), or **larger devices**. And sign off the offset **aged**, at the worst-used column.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Every scenario: mechanism → the corner it lives at → the fix → what the fix costs → how you'd re-verify.** That's how an experienced engineer answers.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Consider whether both sides experience the same long-term bias.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A replica bitline necessarily tracks the aged differential offset of each individual sense amplifier.","A common-mode delay increase must be the only aging effect because both branches share a supply.","Unequal device aging shifts the sense amplifier’s input-referred offset.","Fresh local-mismatch Monte Carlo already includes every future stress-induced shift."],"ans":2,"why":["Replica timing does not directly measure each sense amplifier’s local aged offset.","Shared supply does not imply equal stress histories or equal aging.","Unequal stress can unbalance a differential sensing circuit.","Fresh process mismatch and aging are distinct modeling conditions."],"ex":"Compare aged offset with available bitline signal at sense enable. More swing, stress balancing or device changes each need revalidation.","oral":"X5.8 \"The sense amp passes fresh but fails after aging simulation.\""},{"id":"V050","k":"md-core-x6-1-your-library-s-lib-says-a-cell-is-8-faster-than-spice-at-one-corner-what-s-going-on","d":"char","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Reconciling Liberty and SPICE","q":"A Liberty delay is 8% faster than a separate SPICE result at one corner. What should be checked first?","a":"> \"First I'd check it's a **like-for-like comparison**: same netlist (pre- vs post-layout), same **slew and load point** (on-grid vs interpolated), same **thresholds** and **sensitization**. If it's an **interpolation** point, the table may be too coarse there: add indices. If it's **on-grid**, compare the characterization **deck** to my testbench: stimulus shape (ramp vs realistic waveform), receiver load, model version. **Optimistic** errors are the dangerous ones; I'd flag the cell until it's fixed.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Separate an on-grid discrepancy from an interpolated-point discrepancy.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Align netlist, PVT, sensitization, slew/load, thresholds and grid/interpolation conditions","Densify every table before checking whether both tests describe the same circuit","Add 8% to all cells at all corners without identifying the mismatch","Use the faster number because characterization results always override SPICE"],"ans":0,"why":["A like-for-like comparison distinguishes testbench mismatch from interpolation or characterization error.","Table refinement helps interpolation error, not mismatched extracted netlists or measurement definitions.","A blanket correction can hide the cause and distort unaffected arcs.","The disagreement needs investigation; file format does not establish physical correctness."],"ex":"A like-for-like comparison distinguishes testbench mismatch from interpolation or characterization error.","oral":"X6.1 \"Your library's .lib says a cell is 8% faster than SPICE at one corner. What's going on?\""},{"id":"V051","k":"md-core-x6-2-the-foundry-drops-a-new-pdk-version-mid-project-what-do-you-do","d":"story","lvl":3,"f":"oral","title":"X6.2 \"The foundry drops a new PDK version mid-project. What do you do?\"","q":"X6.2 \"The foundry drops a new PDK version mid-project. What do you do?\"","a":"> \"Read the **release notes**: which **models, rules or layers** changed. Rule changes → re-run **DRC/LVS**; model changes → re-simulate a **reference set** (inverter, NAND, flop, ring oscillator) old vs new to see the **size and direction** of the shift. If the shift is small and uniform, **re-characterize** and re-QA; if it hits a specific device (e.g., HVT leakage), target the cells that use it. Then tell users **which cells changed and by how much**.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V052","k":"md-core-x6-3-you-re-bringing-up-a-library-on-a-brand-new-node-what-do-you-build-first-and-why","d":"char","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Start a new-node cell library","q":"Before expanding many cell functions and drive strengths, which decision should a new-node library team settle?","a":"> \"The **minimum set that lets a design team start and lets us learn the node**: inverter/buffer, NAND/NOR, a basic flop, a clock buffer and ICG, tie/fill/tap/decap cells, plus **ring oscillators** to correlate with silicon early. Before that, I'd settle the **cell architecture** with layout and PD: **track height, fins per device, rail and pin-access strategy**, because every later cell inherits it. Then expand the family: drive strengths, Vt flavors, complex gates, then specialty cells like level shifters and multi-bit flops.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Which early choice will every cell inherit?","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Cell architecture: track height, device quantization, rails and pin access.","Schematic-only timing targets, treating layout-dependent effects as a later optional refinement.","The maximum-drive variants first, postponing small-cell pin-access checks.","A complete set of complex Boolean functions before testing physical integration."],"ans":0,"why":["These choices constrain every later cell and physical integration.","Layout legality, loading and access determine whether the family can actually be integrated.","Maximum-drive success does not establish the physical architecture for the rest of the library.","Function breadth before architecture risks a large amount of rework."],"ex":"Establish a viable shared cell architecture, then build a minimal useful set and correlation structures before expanding.","oral":"X6.3 \"You're bringing up a library on a brand-new node. What do you build first, and why?\""},{"id":"V053","k":"md-core-x6-4-how-do-you-pick-a-track-height-cell-height-for-a-new-library","d":"char","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Cell height and routed performance","q":"How should a proposed reduction in standard-cell track height be evaluated?","a":"> \"It's a **density vs drive vs routability** trade. Shorter cells are denser but fit **fewer fins** per device (weaker drive) and **fewer pin-access tracks**. Taller cells drive harder and route more easily but cost area. Libraries often ship **more than one height**: a dense one for most logic, a taller high-performance one for critical paths. I'd evaluate on **real blocks**: synthesize and route a benchmark with each option and compare area, timing and congestion.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"A library optimization must survive placement and routing, not just a cell screenshot.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Compare routed blocks for area, timing and congestion as well as available drive and pin access","Compare only schematic rise delay at one nominal load","Compare only isolated-cell area because routing does not depend on height","Assume fewer tracks improve both drive and routing flexibility"],"ans":0,"why":["Track height changes legal device capacity and routing access; block implementation exposes system-level tradeoffs.","One isolated arc misses placement, routing and corner-dependent costs.","A smaller cell can force routing detours or poor pin access.","Fewer tracks can limit fin count and access resources rather than improve them."],"ex":"Track height changes legal device capacity and routing access; block implementation exposes system-level tradeoffs.","oral":"X6.4 \"How do you pick a track height (cell height) for a new library?\""},{"id":"V054","k":"md-core-x6-5-a-design-team-says-your-flop-causes-hold-violations-everywhere-how-do-you-respond","d":"seq","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Customer reports widespread hold failures","q":"A design team reports hold failures involving your library flop. What should the library owner do first?","a":"> \"Reproduce **their** failing path first: which flop, **which corner**, what clock slew and skew. Then check the **model**: is the characterized hold too optimistic (criterion, clock slew range, aged vs fresh)? If the **model** is right and the flop just has a large hold time, the options are a **hold-friendlier variant**, or it's a **clock-tree/skew** issue on their side. Either way, I close the loop with data, not opinion.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Separate a bad model from a real constraint that the design violates.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Reduce the published hold constraint until STA reports no violations","Lower the clock frequency to prove hold is fixed","Declare the clock tree incorrect because the flop passed nominal simulation","Reproduce a failing path using the reported corner, clock slew and skew, then compare the constraint model with circuit behavior"],"ans":3,"why":["Changing the model without electrical evidence hides the failure.","Same-edge hold is not repaired merely by increasing the period.","Nominal simulation does not reproduce the customer conditions.","Reproduction separates characterization error, intrinsic hold demand and implementation timing."],"ex":"Start from a reproducible path, validate the model, and then choose a circuit variant or implementation fix.","oral":"X6.5 \"A design team says your flop causes hold violations everywhere. How do you respond?\""},{"id":"V055","k":"md-core-x6-6-how-do-you-validate-that-your-library-matches-silicon","d":"char","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Correlate library predictions with silicon","q":"Ring oscillators show a systematic frequency offset from simulation. Which comparison is the sound starting point?","a":"> \"**Test structures on a test chip or product**: ring oscillators built from library cells (per Vt and drive), leakage monitors, flop characterization circuits. Compare measured frequency and leakage to **SPICE and .lib predictions at the same voltage and temperature**. A **systematic offset** means a model/extraction issue, so re-center or re-guardband. **Scatter** tells you about variation. That feedback sets guardbands for the next release.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Control the experimental conditions before interpreting the residual.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Compare measured and predicted results at matched voltage and temperature, then inspect model/extraction bias separately from scatter.","Compare measured room-temperature frequency with an arbitrary simulated corner.","Treat every systematic offset as random mismatch and increase sample count only.","Change library timing without checking test conditions or extraction."],"ans":0,"why":["Matching conditions makes bias interpretable; scatter and mean offset reveal different issues.","Unmatched conditions confound the comparison.","More samples do not remove a systematic model bias.","An unexplained adjustment can conceal a measurement or modeling error."],"ex":"Correlation needs comparable conditions and multiple structures; systematic shifts and sample variation should not be conflated.","oral":"X6.6 \"How do you validate that your library matches silicon?\""},{"id":"V056","k":"md-core-x6-7-what-goes-into-a-multi-bit-flop-and-why-do-people-want-them","d":"seq","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Multi-bit flip-flop tradeoffs","q":"What is the primary benefit and corresponding integration cost of merging flops into a multi-bit cell?","a":"> \"**Two to eight flops sharing one clock buffer and layout**, so there are fewer clock pins and less **clock power and area** per bit. Costs: **pin access** gets harder, **placement flexibility** drops, and a multi-bit flop's **scan and timing** must be characterized per bit. Clock power is a big share of chip power, so the saving is real.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Identify what is shared and what remains a separate per-bit contract.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Packing bits eliminates the need for per-bit scan and functional verification","Each bit loses its clock input entirely, removing all clock timing checks","Sharing a clock automatically makes every bit’s data timing identical","Shared clock circuitry can reduce clock power per bit, but placement and pin access become more constrained"],"ans":3,"why":["Each storage bit still needs correct functional, timing and scan behavior.","The shared clock still drives storage elements and requires timing analysis.","Data paths and output loads can differ across bits despite a shared clock.","Sharing clock buffers reduces duplicated clock load, while the larger grouped footprint constrains physical choices."],"ex":"Sharing clock buffers reduces duplicated clock load, while the larger grouped footprint constrains physical choices.","oral":"X6.7 \"What goes into a multi-bit flop, and why do people want them?\""},{"id":"V057","k":"md-core-x6-8-how-would-you-speed-up-characterization-turnaround-for-a-2-000-cell-library","d":"char","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Incremental characterization","q":"Which change safely reduces repeated characterization work for a large library?","a":"> \"Make the flow **incremental**: re-characterize only cells whose **netlist, model or settings changed** (Make-style dependencies). **Batch** simulations, **reuse** results across drive strengths only where proven, run **pilot points** to catch setup errors before the full run, and make **failures loud** so missing points get rerun, not silently interpolated. Track runtime per cell to find outliers.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Ask what inputs determine whether an old result is still valid.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Reuse every previous table whenever the cell name is unchanged","Replace all missing simulation results with interpolated values automatically","Track netlist/model/settings dependencies and rerun invalidated cells, with pilot checks and explicit failed-point handling","Skip model-version tracking if the transistor count is unchanged"],"ans":2,"why":["A name can remain unchanged while models or settings change.","A failed point must be diagnosed; interpolation must not conceal it.","Dependency tracking identifies reusable work while preserving validity of changed inputs.","Model changes can invalidate results without changing device count."],"ex":"Incremental work is safe only when reuse is tied to the complete input contract and failed measurements remain visible.","oral":"X6.8 \"How would you speed up characterization turnaround for a 2,000-cell library?\""},{"id":"V058","k":"md-core-x6-9-when-would-you-use-a-pulsed-latch-or-a-tspc-flop-instead-of-a-tg-masterslave-flop","d":"seq","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Choose a pulsed latch tradeoff","q":"A pulsed latch is considered for time borrowing and small setup overhead. What must the timing plan explicitly accommodate?","a":"> \"**Pulsed latch:** when timing is tight and you want **tiny setup and time borrowing**, accepting a **larger hold** requirement and a pulse generator to verify across PVT. **TSPC:** fewer clock phases and a fast, compact flop, but sensitive to **slow clock slews** and charge sharing. **TG master-slave** stays the default for robustness. The choice is driven by the design's timing, power and robustness targets.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"A latch remains transparent for an interval, unlike an ideal instantaneous sampler.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A guarantee that a slower clock fixes every hold violation.","No dependence on pulse-generator behavior.","Pulse-width/PVT verification and potentially larger hold requirements.","Elimination of all minimum-delay checks."],"ans":2,"why":["Same-edge hold is not repaired by increasing the period.","The pulse generator defines the capture window.","The transparency window creates useful borrowing and a minimum-delay risk.","Data can race during the open pulse, so hold remains important."],"ex":"A wider effective transparency window can help maximum-delay paths while making minimum-delay protection harder.","oral":"X6.9 \"When would you use a pulsed latch or a TSPC flop instead of a TG master-slave flop?\""},{"id":"V059","k":"md-core-x6-10-how-do-aging-aware-libraries-work-and-when-do-you-sign-off-with-them","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Aging-aware characterization","q":"What makes an end-of-life Liberty library meaningful?","a":"> \"Characterize the cells with **aged device models**: ΔVt from **BTI/HCI** under an assumed **stress profile** (voltage, temperature, duty, lifetime), giving an **end-of-life .lib**. STA runs with it to make sure the design **still meets timing after years**. The key judgment is the **stress assumption**: too pessimistic wastes area and power; too optimistic risks field failures.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Ask which physical stress history the library is claiming to represent.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A smaller clock period chosen after characterization","Fresh models simulated only at a high temperature, treated as equivalent to all aging histories","A uniform arbitrary delay multiplier with no workload assumptions","Characterization with qualified aged models tied to an explicit voltage, temperature, duty and lifetime profile"],"ans":3,"why":["Changing the clock target does not produce aged device behavior.","Hot fresh behavior does not encode accumulated bias and switching degradation.","An unexplained multiplier cannot establish the applicable lifetime or mechanism.","Aged model shifts depend on assumed stress history; the library supports signoff only within that documented scenario."],"ex":"Aged model shifts depend on assumed stress history; the library supports signoff only within that documented scenario.","oral":"X6.10 \"How do aging-aware libraries work, and when do you sign off with them?\""},{"id":"V060","k":"md-core-x6-11-explain-lvf-and-why-it-matters-for-a-library","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"What LVF contributes","q":"What does Liberty Variation Format add to nominal timing characterization in the source’s explanation?","a":"> \"**Liberty Variation Format** adds **per-arc sigma tables**, for delay, transition and constraints, on top of the nominal tables. STA then combines variation **statistically along each path** (POCV) instead of applying a flat margin, which removes a lot of **pessimism**. For the library team, it means characterizing **local variation per arc**, which is expensive, so the **efficiency of the MC flow** matters.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Contrast a per-arc variation table with a blanket timing margin.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A replacement for nominal cell functionality","Arc-specific statistical variation information for delay, transition and constraints","A guarantee that all timing paths are independent","A single fixed percentage derate that ignores arc and operating point"],"ans":1,"why":["The logical and nominal timing models are still required.","Statistical tables support variation-aware path analysis.","Correlation assumptions require separate treatment.","A uniform derate discards the per-arc characterization that LVF provides."],"ex":"LVF supplies variation information to statistical timing analysis; it does not itself establish every path correlation or remove nominal characterization.","oral":"X6.11 \"Explain LVF and why it matters for a library.\""},{"id":"V061","k":"md-core-x6-12-how-do-you-decide-what-goes-in-the-library-at-all","d":"char","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Decide whether to add a library cell","q":"Which evidence best justifies adding a complex gate to a production cell library?","a":"> \"Driven by **users and data**: which functions and drive strengths the synthesis and PD tools actually pick, where they have to **stack cells** to make a function (a candidate for a new complex gate), what's **missing on critical paths** (a stronger drive, a better Vt), and power hot spots (**clock gating, multi-bit flops**). Every added cell has a **maintenance and characterization cost**, so it has to earn its place.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**The MS-level skill is analysis: model it, bound it statistically, verify it against silicon, and explain the trade-off with data. Answer every question from that stance.**\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Balance measurable user benefit against lifetime support cost.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Lower schematic transistor count alone, before comparing drive and routing.","A nominal delay improvement on one arc, without considering the other arcs or usage.","Repeated mapped-design bottlenecks where the cell yields useful PPA improvement large enough to justify characterization and maintenance.","Availability of unused layout width in the cell family, regardless of synthesis demand."],"ans":2,"why":["Transistor count omits parasitics, drive and physical usability.","An isolated nominal win may not benefit actual mapped designs.","Actual usage and measured benefit justify recurring library cost.","Spare geometric room is not evidence of a valuable function or strength."],"ex":"Use synthesis and physical-design evidence to select functions and strengths; a larger library is not automatically a better one.","oral":"X6.12 \"How do you decide what goes in the library at all?\""},{"id":"V062","k":"md-core-f1-3-what-is-mbist-what-faults-does-it-catch-in-your-zone-memory-relevant","d":"flow","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"MBIST and statistical yield","q":"What does passing a memory BIST pattern suite establish most directly?","a":"> **Memory built-in self-test**: an on-chip engine that tests the memories by writing and reading patterns (**march algorithms**). It catches **stuck-at** (bit stuck 0/1), **transition** (can't flip one way), **coupling** (writing one cell disturbs a neighbor), **address-decoder** (wrong cell accessed), and **retention** faults. My part was integrating it into the flow, not root-causing physical fails.\n\n- **Bridge to design:** \"Each fault maps to a design margin: read disturb shows up as a coupling or transition fault, a weak cell at low VDD as a retention fault. MBIST **finds** escapes; it **can't prove** 6σ margin. That's what design-time analysis is for.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Scope first → answer inside your zone → \"here's how I understand the rest.\" You never need to inflate Faraday.**\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Separate detection by a finite test from a statistical robustness claim.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A mathematical proof that every manufactured memory will retain data at every voltage","That any later failure must be outside the memory array","No detected failures for the tested conditions and exercised fault behaviors; it does not prove six-sigma design margin","Coverage of all analog margins solely because march patterns include writes"],"ans":2,"why":["Finite testing cannot guarantee every device and operating condition.","Untested conditions or defect mechanisms can still expose memory failures.","MBIST detects exercised faults under its tests, while statistical design margin requires separate analysis.","Pattern coverage does not directly measure all tail distributions or analog margins."],"ex":"MBIST detects exercised faults under its tests, while statistical design margin requires separate analysis.","oral":"F1.3 \"What is MBIST? What faults does it catch?\" (in your zone; memory-relevant)"},{"id":"V063","k":"md-core-d2-1-your-research-was-analog-why-digital-cells-and-rom","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.1 \"Your research was analog. Why digital cells and ROM?\"","q":"D2.1 \"Your research was analog. Why digital cells and ROM?\"","a":"> Standard cells and especially ROM are **analog design wearing digital clothes**. A flop's setup time is a race between two analog paths, a level shifter is a **contention** problem, and a ROM read is **small-signal sensing** with leakage fighting you. The skills from my TIA and filter work (**sizing for margin, PVT corners, noise and leakage at low bias**) are what a cell designer uses. The difference is scale: my analog block was one instance; a cell here is **billions**. That leverage is what draws me.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V064","k":"md-core-d2-2-you-haven-t-designed-memory-why-believe-you-can-do-rom","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.2 \"You haven't designed memory. Why believe you can do ROM?\"","q":"D2.2 \"You haven't designed memory. Why believe you can do ROM?\"","a":"> Fair. I haven't designed a memory array end to end. What I have is adjacent: I **integrated MBIST** at Faraday, and on the NTT course project I did the **physical integration of the SRAM blocks**. And the physics of a ROM read, **one weak cell against a keeper while every other cell on the bitline leaks at a hot, fast corner**, is the same subthreshold-leakage and variation physics I worked with near threshold. I'm happy to walk through a ROM column right now.\n\n<div class=\"co co-value\"><p class=\"co-t\">Then prove it</p>\n\nOffer to draw the NOR ROM column (Q12). An honest gap followed by immediate understanding beats claimed experience.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V065","k":"md-core-d2-3-why-did-you-leave-the-phd-track","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.3 \"Why did you leave the PhD track?\"","q":"D2.3 \"Why did you leave the PhD track?\"","a":"> It was a deliberate redirect. I went in wanting depth, and I got it, but I realized I want to be **close to real products and real silicon**, and a multi-year thesis narrows you onto one problem for a long time. So I **completed my MS** and moved toward industry circuit design. I'd rather make that correction than stay on the wrong track out of momentum.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Guardrail</p>\n\nSay \"**I started on the PhD track and completed my MS.**\" Never \"I have a PhD.\" Never badmouth research, your advisor or the lab; never \"burned out.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V066","k":"md-core-d2-4-you-did-consulting-will-you-leave-engineering-for-business","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.4 \"You did consulting. Will you leave engineering for business?\"","q":"D2.4 \"You did consulting. Will you leave engineering for business?\"","a":"> No. The consulting at miLEAD, **market research for four biotech startups**, was a **test I ran**. It made me a better communicator, and it clarified that I'm happiest **hands-on with the technology**. That's why I'm interviewing for a transistor-level role, not a strategy one.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V067","k":"md-core-d2-5-what-have-you-been-doing-since-you-graduated","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.5 \"What have you been doing since you graduated?\"","q":"D2.5 \"What have you been doing since you graduated?\"","a":"> [Fill in the true answer.] Shape: \"I finished my MS in [month] 2026 and have been **recruiting for full-time circuit roles**, and deliberately sharpening fundamentals for this work: [e.g., device physics, SRAM/ROM, characterization]. [One honest line about anything else.]\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-fill\"><p class=\"co-t\">Fill in</p>\n\nMake this true and specific. Keep side projects to one line.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V068","k":"md-core-d2-6-when-can-you-start-the-role-is-new-college-grad-2027","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.6 \"When can you start? The role is New College Grad 2027.\"","q":"D2.6 \"When can you start? The role is New College Grad 2027.\"","a":"> \"I've finished my MS, so I can start **earlier than the typical 2027 timeline** if the team needs it, and I'm flexible to fit the program.\" Detailed logistics go to Chanel.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V069","k":"md-core-d2-7-are-you-interviewing-elsewhere","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.7 \"Are you interviewing elsewhere?\"","q":"D2.7 \"Are you interviewing elsewhere?\"","a":"> \"I'm talking with a few companies, but this is the role I'm most excited about, because it sits exactly where my device and design background meet.\" Short, true, redirect.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V070","k":"md-core-d2-8-what-s-your-long-term-goal","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.8 \"What's your long-term goal?\"","q":"D2.8 \"What's your long-term goal?\"","a":"> \"To become someone the team relies on for the **hard cells and hard margins**: first owning a cell family or ROM end to end, then a **new-node library bring-up**, then deeper technical ownership of foundation IP.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V071","k":"md-core-d2-9-collaborate-or-lead","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D2.9 \"Collaborate or lead?\"","q":"D2.9 \"Collaborate or lead?\"","a":"> \"Either, depending on what the work needs. I get to know people's strengths, **come prepared** so I don't waste their time, and make the **next owner and next action** clear. At Faraday that meant clean handoffs between the Taiwan and Vietnam teams.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V072","k":"md-core-your-work-was-criticized-in-front-of-others-how-did-you-respond","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"Your work was criticized in front of others. How did you respond?\"","q":"\"Your work was criticized in front of others. How did you respond?\"","a":"> [verify: a real review.] \"I **thanked them, asked a clarifying question** so I understood the actual concern, and **didn't defend in the moment**. Afterward I checked it; [they were right / partly right], I fixed it, and I **followed up with the result** in the same forum. Public criticism is fast feedback; the worst response is to hide the fix.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V073","k":"md-core-what-does-excellent-work-look-like-to-you","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"What does excellent work look like to you?\"","q":"\"What does excellent work look like to you?\"","a":"> \"Work that's **right under the conditions that matter, not just nominal**, and that someone else can **trust and reuse** without asking me. At WICS that meant validating across **all PVT corners**, not only typical. Where I met my bar: [verify: e.g., the TIA/filter validation]. Where I didn't: NTT's full-chip closure. For a library, excellence is the same idea: every arc and corner correct, because thousands of designs will trust it.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V074","k":"md-core-if-you-could-work-on-anything-here-what-would-it-be","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"If you could work on anything here, what would it be?\"","q":"\"If you could work on anything here, what would it be?\"","a":"> \"**Energy-efficient cells and memories at low voltage**: flops, level shifters and ROM reads near threshold, where leakage and variation decide everything. That's exactly where my subthreshold background is useful, and at NVIDIA's scale, power is the limit on every product.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V075","k":"md-core-nvidia-is-intense-why-do-you-want-that","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"NVIDIA is intense. Why do you want that?\"","q":"\"NVIDIA is intense. Why do you want that?\"","a":"> \"I do my best work with **real ownership and a hard target**. The Faraday tapeout was a 1.5-month sprint, and I liked it. What I've learned is to **flag problems early and triage honestly** instead of grinding silently. That's how I'd keep up at NVIDIA's pace.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V076","k":"md-core-where-do-you-see-yourself-in-five-years","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"Where do you see yourself in five years?\"","q":"\"Where do you see yourself in five years?\"","a":"> \"Growing deep here. The first couple of years, getting really good at owning cells or a ROM end to end and supporting the teams that use them. By five years, I'd like to have been through a **new-node bring-up** and be someone the team relies on for **hard margin problems**, especially at low voltage.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V077","k":"md-core-technical-track-or-management","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"Technical track or management?\"","q":"\"Technical track or management?\"","a":"> \"**Technical first**, for a long time. I want real depth before I'd lead anyone. If leading a small technical team is where I add the most later, I'd be open to it, but depth comes first.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V078","k":"md-core-would-you-want-to-move-to-another-team-later","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"Would you want to move to another team later?\"","q":"\"Would you want to move to another team later?\"","a":"> \"My focus is getting deep **here**. Library work touches every design team, so I'd naturally learn a lot about the rest of the chip, but I'm not looking at this as a stepping stone.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V079","k":"md-core-would-you-go-back-for-a-phd-or-an-mba","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"Would you go back for a PhD or an MBA?\"","q":"\"Would you go back for a PhD or an MBA?\"","a":"> \"No. I'm focused on building an industry career in circuit design; that's the direction I've chosen.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V080","k":"md-core-what-do-you-want-to-learn-in-your-first-year","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"\"What do you want to learn in your first year?\"","q":"\"What do you want to learn in your first year?\"","a":"> \"Three things: **legal FinFET/GAA sizing and layout constraints**, the team's **characterization and QA flow**, and **high-sigma and reliability methodology**. And I'd like to see how the library is actually used by the physical-design teams.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Guardrails for the career cluster</p>\n\n1. **Keep the horizon inside NVIDIA and circuit design.** Don't volunteer longer-term ideas about strategy, consulting, a startup, or healthcare commercialization. They're real interests, but here they read as \"he'll leave.\"\n2. **No \"stepping stone\" language.** Never \"this will give me a foundation to…\"\n3. **Specific beats grand.** \"Own a flop family, then a node bring-up\" is stronger than \"become a leader in AI hardware.\"\n4. **Consistent with Part D2:** exploring is over; it converged on hands-on silicon.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Year 1: reproduce, then own a bounded change. Years 2-3: own a cell family or ROM. Year 5: new-node bring-up, go-to for hard margins. Year 10: deep technical ownership of foundation IP. Technical track first.**\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V081","k":"md-core-a1-why-nvidia","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"A1. \"Why NVIDIA?\"","q":"A1. \"Why NVIDIA?\"","a":"> Two reasons. First, NVIDIA is where the hardest chips are being built right now. The data-center GPUs are near the reticle limit and power is the ceiling, so every bit of efficiency at the transistor level matters at a scale nobody else has. Second, and more specific to me: at the leading edge everyone uses the same foundry process, and the libraries are one of the few places a design company can pull more out of that process than its competitors. I'd rather work on that layer at NVIDIA than anywhere else, because here it directly sets what the products can do.\n\n- **If they want more:** mention CUDA making the GPU a general computer, the move to full platforms (GPU, CPU, networking, systems), and that data center is now about 92% of revenue (Part N).","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V082","k":"md-core-a2-what-do-you-know-about-this-role","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"A2. \"What do you know about this role?\"","q":"A2. \"What do you know about this role?\"","a":"> It's a foundation-IP role: designing the transistor-level standard cells and custom ROMs that every NVIDIA chip is built from. Day to day that means designing and sizing cells, simulating them across corners and Monte Carlo, checking EM, aging and noise, getting layout DRC/LVS clean, and characterizing them into libraries the synthesis and timing tools use. On the ROM side, it's the array, decoder and sense path, verified with enough margin at high sigma, plus the flows that generate and check them. The customers are internal, the design teams, so quality checks and supporting them matter as much as the design itself.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V083","k":"md-core-a3-why-this-kind-of-circuit-work-and-not-analog-or-process-integration","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"A3. \"Why this kind of circuit work, and not analog or process integration?\"","q":"A3. \"Why this kind of circuit work, and not analog or process integration?\"","a":"> Because it's the circuit work closest to the device. Analog was where I learned to design for margin, but each block is one instance. Process integration is about making the device yield. Standard cells and ROM sit between them: transistor-level design that has to respect every device effect, deployed billions of times. That combination of physics and leverage is the thing I want to go deep on.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Guardrail</p>\n\nDon't dismiss analog or process work; you may be asked by someone who loves it. Frame this role as where your interests meet, not as escaping something.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V084","k":"md-core-a4-are-you-willing-to-relocate-and-work-on-site-in-santa-clara","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"A4. \"Are you willing to relocate and work on-site in Santa Clara?\"","q":"A4. \"Are you willing to relocate and work on-site in Santa Clara?\"","a":"> Yes, fully. I know it's an on-site role, and for this kind of work I'd want to be near the team anyway. The fastest way to learn a library flow is sitting next to the people who built it.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V085","k":"md-core-a5-where-do-you-see-yourself-in-five-years","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"A5. \"Where do you see yourself in five years?\"","q":"A5. \"Where do you see yourself in five years?\"","a":"> Growing deep here. The first couple of years, getting genuinely good at owning cells or a ROM end to end: design, margins, characterization, and supporting the design teams. Then a harder problem, ideally a new-node library bring-up where nothing exists yet. Five years out, I'd like to be the person newer engineers come to when a cell or a margin issue is hard.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V086","k":"md-core-a6-where-in-ten-years","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"A6. \"Where in ten years?\"","q":"A6. \"Where in ten years?\"","a":"> Far enough out that I'd rather give the direction than pretend I've mapped the title. The direction is deep technical ownership of foundation IP at the leading edge, with enough node transitions behind me to have real judgment. Whether that's a technical track or leading a team, I'll follow where I add the most.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Register note</p>\n\nRooted and specific, like TSMC. Not the expansive McKinsey energy. They're hiring an engineer to own cells, and they want someone who'll stay and get deep.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V087","k":"md-core-a7-nvidia-runs-at-a-very-fast-intense-pace-how-do-you-feel-about-that","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"A7. \"NVIDIA runs at a very fast, intense pace. How do you feel about that?\"","q":"A7. \"NVIDIA runs at a very fast, intense pace. How do you feel about that?\"","a":"> I do better with real ownership and pace than without it. The Faraday tapeout was a six-week sprint from RTL to GDSII, and I ran grad research, teaching 400-plus students and consulting at the same time. What grad school taught me is to flag problems early and triage honestly instead of grinding silently. That's how you keep up at a fast pace without dropping things.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true,"company":"NVIDIA"},{"id":"V088","k":"md-core-a8-what-makes-you-a-good-fit-why-should-we-hire-you","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"A8. \"What makes you a good fit? Why should we hire you?\"","q":"A8. \"What makes you a good fit? Why should we hire you?\"","a":"> Three things. I understand the device underneath a cell, from materials science and near-threshold design. I understand the flow that consumes the cell, from signing off a 22nm chip. And I'm honest and fast at learning what I don't know yet, like memory design, which I've been working through from first principles. Most candidates are strong in one of those. For a library team, which sits between the device and the design flow, having both sides is the fit.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V089","k":"md-core-b2-1-why-did-you-quit-the-phd","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"B2.1 \"Why did you quit the PhD?\"","q":"B2.1 \"Why did you quit the PhD?\"","a":"> It was a deliberate redirect, not a washout. I went in wanting depth, and I got it, but I realized I want to be close to real products and real silicon, and a five-year thesis narrows you onto one problem for a long time. Once I was honest with myself about that, the master's was the faster, better path to the work I care about. I'd rather make that correction than spend years on the wrong track out of momentum.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Do / Don't</p>\n\nDo: frame as clarity. Don't: badmouth research or your advisor, or say \"I burned out.\"\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V090","k":"md-core-b2-2-why-consulting-why-the-business-detour","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"B2.2 \"Why consulting? Why the business detour?\"","q":"B2.2 \"Why consulting? Why the business detour?\"","a":"> Curiosity. Alongside my research, I wanted to understand how technology becomes a product, so I consulted for biotech startups. I don't regret it: it made me better at communicating with non-engineers and at thinking about the \"why\" behind the technology. But it clarified the opposite of what I expected: I'm happiest with my hands on the technology, not advising it from a slide. That's a big part of why I'm here for a transistor-level role.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V091","k":"md-core-b2-3-why-circuit-design-now","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"B2.3 \"Why circuit design now?\"","q":"B2.3 \"Why circuit design now?\"","a":"> Because it's where the rest of my path pointed, and I finally know it. I've done device work, analog design and a digital signoff flow. A library role is the one place that uses all three. \"Why now\" is that I've tested the alternatives, deep research and the business side, so instead of wondering, I know what fits. That's a more durable reason to commit than if I'd never questioned it.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V092","k":"md-core-b2-4-your-interests-seem-to-be-everywhere-how-do-i-know-you-re-focused","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"B2.4 \"Your interests seem to be everywhere. How do I know you're focused?\"","q":"B2.4 \"Your interests seem to be everywhere. How do I know you're focused?\"","a":"> I understand why it looks that way on paper. The honest version is that I explored deliberately, research and then the business side, because I wanted to choose from experience, not assumption. What looks like scattered interests was actually me narrowing down. The exploring is over: it converged on hands-on silicon, which is why I'm here for this specific role and not still sampling.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V093","k":"md-core-b2-5-how-do-i-know-you-ll-stay-the-commitment-question","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"B2.5 \"How do I know you'll stay?\" (the commitment question)","q":"B2.5 \"How do I know you'll stay?\" (the commitment question)","a":"> The reasons I redirected before don't apply here. I left research because it was too narrow and too far from real products; I didn't pursue the business path because it was too far from the technology. This role is both technical and real, at a scale nobody else has. So it's not another way-station; it's where those earlier reasons point. And I don't make changes lightly: I went all in on the PhD, and it took real reflection to change course.\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Most important answer for you</p>\n\nRehearse B2.5 until it's calm and natural. It's rarer at NVIDIA than at TSMC, but if it comes, it decides the interview.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V094","k":"md-core-b2-8-would-you-ever-go-back-to-a-phd","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"B2.8 \"Would you ever go back to a PhD?\"","q":"B2.8 \"Would you ever go back to a PhD?\"","a":"> I'm focused on building an industry career. That's the direction I've chosen.\n\nShort, forward, closed. Don't elaborate.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V095","k":"md-core-skills-they-probe-this-which-of-these-can-you-go-deep-on","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"Skills (they probe this: \"which of these can you go deep on?\")","q":"Skills (they probe this: \"which of these can you go deep on?\")","a":"**EDA: Virtuoso, SPICE, Design Compiler, Innovus, PrimeTime, NCVerilog, Verdi.** \"Virtuoso and SPICE for transistor-level design and simulation; Design Compiler for synthesis; Innovus for place-and-route; PrimeTime for timing signoff.\"\n\n- **If asked \"pick one and go deep\":** for this role, **Virtuoso/SPICE** (your WICS work) or **PrimeTime** (Faraday). Don't claim depth on a tool you only touched.\n\n**Characterization: SEM, EDX, Hall, PPMS, evaporator, RTP.** \"On the device side I've run PVD deposition, RTP anneals, and characterized films with SEM, EDX, Hall and PPMS.\"\n\n- **Land:** \"I've measured device-level electrical behavior directly, so SPICE model corners aren't abstract to me.\"\n\n**Languages: Tcl, Perl, Python, C/C++, Verilog/SystemVerilog, MATLAB, Linux.** \"I automate flows. I built DFT/STA automation in Tcl and Perl at Faraday.\"\n\n- **Land:** the JD asks for Perl, Tcl, Make and automation. This is a direct match.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V096","k":"md-core-c2-why-is-your-grad-gpa-lower","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"C2. \"Why is your grad GPA lower?\"","q":"C2. \"Why is your grad GPA lower?\"","a":"> A few honest things. Graduate IC and VLSI coursework is a real step up, and I was carrying research, teaching and consulting at the same time. The bigger factor is that I changed direction mid-program, from the PhD track to the master's, and that transition was rough for a stretch. I owned it and pulled it back up; my most recent term is strong.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V097","k":"md-core-d3-how-do-you-handle-competing-priorities","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D3. \"How do you handle competing priorities?\"","q":"D3. \"How do you handle competing priorities?\"","a":"> I lived this in grad school: research in the WICS lab, teaching as a GSI for 400-plus students, and consulting, all at once. \"Work harder\" doesn't scale; triage does. Each week I'd be honest about what had a real deadline versus what felt urgent, protect blocks for deep work, and tell people early when something had to slip. It wasn't perfect, and that's part of what the tough stretch taught me, but it made me much better at prioritizing.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V098","k":"md-core-d9-how-do-you-make-sure-your-work-is-accurate","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D9. \"How do you make sure your work is accurate?\"","q":"D9. \"How do you make sure your work is accurate?\"","a":"> In the cleanroom, sloppy is expensive and invisible until later: a wrong anneal temperature doesn't show up until you characterize the film. So I logged every run's exact conditions and never trusted a single measurement. Same discipline in STA signoff at Faraday, where one missed corner can kill a chip. My habit: assume the failure is subtle, write things down so you can trace back, and verify rather than trust.\n\n- **Land:** \"For a library that billions of instances depend on, that's the habit you want.\"","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V099","k":"md-core-d11-how-do-you-handle-repetitive-high-stakes-verification","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D11. \"How do you handle repetitive, high-stakes verification?\"","q":"D11. \"How do you handle repetitive, high-stakes verification?\"","a":"> Validating circuits across PVT corners is repetitive and easy to zone out on, but one missed corner can sink the design. I don't rely on willpower: I build structure, a consistent checklist, and automate what can be automated so my attention goes to the anomalies. For library QA I'd do the same: scripts that flag non-monotonic tables or outliers across drive strengths, so judgment goes where it matters.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V100","k":"md-core-d18-what-are-you-most-proud-of","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D18. \"What are you most proud of?\"","q":"D18. \"What are you most proud of?\"","a":"> Technically, the Faraday tapeout: a real 22nm chip, signed off on a six-week clock, with a top-10% rating. Personally, VISA: building a community from three people to over ninety. Both are about owning something end to end.","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V101","k":"md-core-d19-what-s-your-greatest-weakness","d":"story","lvl":2,"f":"oral","title":"D19. \"What's your greatest weakness?\"","q":"D19. \"What's your greatest weakness?\"","a":"> I used to take on too much and try to power through quietly. That caught up with me during my grad-school transition. Now I scope early and raise problems as soon as I see them. On the technical side, memory design is the area I have least hands-on experience in, which is why I've been working through SRAM and ROM from first principles for this role.\n\n<div class=\"co co-why\"><p class=\"co-t\">Why this works</p>\n\nReal, not fake-humble, already being fixed, and the technical half pre-empts D2.3 honestly.\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"personal":true},{"id":"V102","k":"md-core-code-1-will-there-be-live-coding-how-to-respond-and-four-drills","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Missing delay measurements","q":"A script expects tphl and tplh, but a successful simulator process produces only tphl. How should the script handle the run?","a":"**Will there be live coding?** Unlikely in a 45-minute first round with a circuit engineer. The JD calls scripting \"a certain plus\"; only one intern report mentions \"Coding (SPICE/Python).\" The realistic version: \"**How would you script this?**\" in a shared doc.\n\n**How to respond:**\n\n1. **Say the logic first:** \"Loop over cells and corners, run the sim, parse the value, **flag anything missing instead of treating it as zero**, write a summary.\"\n2. **Then write simple code.** Syntax doesn't need to be perfect: \"I'd double-check the exact function name, but the structure is this.\"\n3. **Be honest, not apologetic:** \"I write practical automation, mostly Tcl/Perl for flows, and I look syntax up as I go. What I focus on is making the script **catch bad results**.\"\n4. **Be ready to explain your Faraday script** in plain words: input → what it checked → output → how it flagged failures.\n\n#### Drill 1: parse delays from a log, flag missing (Python)\n\n```python\nimport re\n\nneed = [\"tphl\", \"tplh\"]           # measurements we expect\nvals = {}\nfor line in open(\"sim.log\"):\n    m = re.match(r\"\\s*(\\w+)\\s*=\\s*([-\\d.eE+]+)\", line)\n    if m:\n        vals[m[1]] = float(m[2])   # name -> value\n\nmissing = [k for k in need if k not in vals]\nif missing:\n    raise SystemExit(f\"missing: {missing}\")  # fail loudly\nprint(vals)\n```\n\n#### Drill 2: sweep corners and loads, collect results (Python)\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\n\nrows = []\nfor corner in [\"ss\", \"tt\", \"ff\"]:\n    for load in [1e-15, 5e-15, 20e-15]:\n        deck = open(\"tb.sp\").read().format(corner=corner, load=load)\n        open(\"run.sp\", \"w\").write(deck)\n        subprocess.run([\"spice\", \"run.sp\", \"-o\", \"run.log\"], check=True)\n        d = parse_delay(\"run.log\")             # Drill 1 logic\n        rows.append((corner, load, d))         # None = missing\n\nbad = [r for r in rows if r[2] is None]\nprint(\"failed points:\", bad)                   # never average them in\n```\n\n#### Drill 3: the same loop in Tcl (read it, don't memorize it)\n\n```tcl\nset cells {INV_X1 NAND2_X1 NOR2_X1}\nforeach c $cells {\n    set f [open \"${c}.log\" r]\n    set found 0\n    while {[gets $f line] >= 0} {\n        if {[regexp {tphl\\s*=\\s*(\\S+)} $line -> d]} {\n            puts \"$c tphl = $d\"\n            set found 1\n        }\n    }\n    close $f\n    if {!$found} { puts \"MISSING $c\" }  ;# flag it\n}\n```\n\n#### Drill 4: SPICE parameter sweep (no script needed)\n\n```text\n.param cload=5f\nC1 out 0 {cload}\n.tran 1p 5n sweep cload list 1f 5f 20f\n.meas tran tphl TRIG v(in) VAL=0.4 RISE=1 TARG v(out) VAL=0.4 FALL=1\n```\n\n- **Make, in one sentence:** \"It reruns only the steps whose inputs changed, based on declared dependencies, so a big characterization flow doesn't redo everything.\"\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">Core memory</p>\n\n**Logic out loud first. Loop → run → parse → flag missing → summarize. Missing is a failure, never a zero.**\n\n</div>","src":"Circuit Design Interview Bible","hint":"Distinguish completion of a program from completion of the measurement contract.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Mark the run incomplete and report the missing tplh before summary statistics are computed","Drop the run silently and report the mean of the remaining points","Copy tphl into tplh because both measure propagation delay","Insert tplh = 0 because the simulator exit status was successful"],"ans":0,"why":["Process success does not establish that every required measurement exists.","Silent exclusion conceals missing coverage and biases summaries.","Rise and fall arcs can differ and need separate measurements.","Zero falsely represents an instantaneous measured transition."],"ex":"Validate the expected measurement set separately from process status. Missing data must remain a visible failure state.","oral":"CODE.1 Will there be live coding? How to respond, and four drills"},{"id":"V103","k":"md-drills-a1-the-cmos-inverter-vtc-region-by-region","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Find the inverter switching point","q":"For a long-channel square-law CMOS inverter, VDD = 0.9 V, Vtn = |Vtp| = 0.35 V and matched effective strengths βn = βp. What is VM, where Vin = Vout?","a":"Why they ask: every later question (noise margin, ratioed logic, keepers, SRAM stability) assumes you can draw this curve and say which device is in which region at each point.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nInverter VTC with the five operating regions, VM, VIL/VIH (slope −1 points), and the gain and crowbar-current peaks at VM.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Draw the VTC of a CMOS inverter and walk me through it.\"\n\n**Answer.** Start from KCL at the output: with no DC load, I_P = I_N at every point, so the VTC is the set of points where the two device currents match. Five regions:\n\n- **A** (Vin < Vtn): NMOS off, PMOS linear with ~0 current → Vout = VDD (**VOH = VDD**).\n- **B** (Vtn < Vin < VM): NMOS saturated, PMOS linear. Vout starts to drop.\n- **C** (Vin ≈ VM): **both saturated**. Two current sources fight, so the curve is nearly vertical. Gain is highest here and so is crowbar current.\n- **D** (VM < Vin < VDD − |Vtp|): NMOS linear, PMOS saturated.\n- **E** (Vin > VDD − |Vtp|): PMOS off → Vout = 0 (**VOL = 0**).\n\nDraw: a box from 0 to VDD on both axes and the dashed Vout = Vin diagonal. The curve stays flat at VDD, falls steeply through the diagonal at VM, and stays flat at 0. Mark the two slope −1 points (VIL and VIH) and label A-E under the axis.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Define VIL, VIH and the noise margins. Give me numbers for your plot.\"\n\n**Answer.** **VIL** is the largest input still read as a 0 and **VIH** is the smallest input still read as a 1. Both are taken at the **dVout/dVin = −1** points, because beyond them the gate amplifies noise (|gain| > 1). Then **NMH = VOH − VIH** and **NML = VIL − VOL**. In the model: VIL = 0.41 V, VIH = 0.49 V, VOH = 0.9, VOL = 0, so **NMH = NML = 0.41 V**. The margins are symmetric because VM = VDD/2. The undefined region VIH − VIL is only 78 mV wide because the gain is about 41.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Derive VM.\"\n\n**Answer.** At VM both devices are saturated and Vin = Vout = VM. Set the square-law currents equal:\n\n```latex\n\\frac{k_n}{2}\\,(V_M - V_{tn})^2 \\;=\\; \\frac{k_p}{2}\\,(V_{DD} - V_M - |V_{tp}|)^2\n\\;\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\\;\nV_M = \\frac{V_{tn} + r\\,(V_{DD} - |V_{tp}|)}{1 + r},\\qquad r = \\sqrt{\\frac{k_p}{k_n}}\n```\n\nHere kp and kn include W/L. With kn' = 2·kp' and Wp = 2·Wn we get r = 1, so VM = (0.35 + 0.55)/2 = **0.45 V**, which is exactly what the model gives. Velocity saturation changes the exponent (the currents become closer to linear in Vov), but the structure stays the same: VM is set by the **ratio of strengths**.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 (worked numeric problem).** \"VDD = 0.9, Vtn = |Vtp| = 0.35, kn' = 2kp'. What VM do you get with Wp = Wn? What Wp/Wn puts VM at 0.40 V? At 0.50 V?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- Wp = Wn: r = √(1/2) = 0.707, so VM = (0.35 + 0.707·0.55)/1.707 = **0.433 V** (the model gives 0.426).\n- VM = 0.40 requires r = (VM − Vtn)/(VDD − |Vtp| − VM) = 0.05/0.15 = 1/3. Then kp/kn = 1/9, so Wp/Wn = 2/9 = **0.22**.\n- VM = 0.50 requires r = 0.15/0.05 = 3, so kp/kn = 9 and Wp/Wn = **18**.\n\nThe lesson is that VM depends only weakly (square-root) on sizing. Doubling Wp moves VM by only about 20 mV (0.450 → 0.474 V in the model), and pushing VM toward a rail costs enormous width.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"What is the gain at VM, and why isn't it infinite?\"\n\n**Answer.** Use small-signal analysis at VM: both devices are saturated transconductors driving each other's output conductance.\n\n```latex\nA_v = -\\frac{g_{mn} + g_{mp}}{g_{dsn} + g_{dsp}},\\qquad g_m \\approx \\frac{2I_D}{V_{ov}},\\; g_{ds} \\approx \\lambda I_D\n\\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_v \\approx -\\frac{2/V_{ov,n} + 2/V_{ov,p}}{\\lambda_n + \\lambda_p}\n```\n\nWith Vov = 0.1 V and λ = 0.4 V⁻¹ the hand estimate is −(20 + 20)/0.8 = **−50**. The model gives **−41**, because at Vov = 0.1 V the device is in moderate inversion, where gm/Id = 14 V⁻¹ rather than 2/Vov = 20 V⁻¹. With ideal saturation (λ = 0) the gain would be infinite and the transition vertical. **Channel-length modulation** (plus DIBL) is the only thing keeping it finite. That is why short-channel inverters have softer VTCs.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"What if Wp is doubled or halved?\"\n\n**Answer.** Doubling Wp makes the PMOS stronger, so the NMOS needs more Vin to win. VM moves **right** (0.45 → 0.474 V) and the curve shifts without changing shape. NMH shrinks and NML grows. Halving Wp moves VM **left** (0.426 V). Show the family:\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: Wp = 1, 2, 4·Wn shifts VM by only about ±20 mV. Right: VTCs normalized to VDD for VDD = 0.9 V down to 0.06 V.\n\n</div>\n\nThe same physics is used deliberately in **skewed gates** (HI-skew or LO-skew inverters on dynamic outputs) and in **keeper feedback inverters**, where you shift VM to favor one transition.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6 - What if VDD drops toward and below Vtn + |Vtp| = 0.7 V?**\n\n**Answer.** Above 0.7 V there is a window around VM where both devices are strongly on (that window is region C, and it is where the crowbar current comes from). Below 0.7 V that window disappears. The square-law hand model then predicts a **dead band**: for some Vin both devices are off and the output would float, giving hysteresis. Real devices conduct **subthreshold** current, so the inverter keeps working as a ratio of two exponentials. The right panel shows clean, full-swing VTCs at 0.6 V, 0.4 V and even 0.25 V. Peak gain actually rises to about 69 at 0.4 V, because gm/Id is highest in weak inversion. It only degrades at a few kT/q: |gain| = 6 at 0.12 V and 1.6 at 0.06 V. The theoretical floor for gain > 1 is about 2·ln2·kT/q ≈ 36 mV (Swanson-Meindl/Meindl), and practical logic needs several times that. The price is **speed**: delay grows exponentially below Vt (see A6). Crowbar current also nearly disappears, which is one reason near- and sub-threshold logic is energy-efficient.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 7 - What if it is hot versus cold?**\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nInverter VTC at −40 °C and 125 °C for VDD = 0.9 V and 0.3 V, using a temperature-extended model (Vt −1 mV/K, µ ∝ T^−1.5, kT/q).\n\n</div>\n\n**Answer.** Three things move with temperature: **Vt falls** (about 0.5-1 mV/K), **mobility falls** (µ ∝ T^−1.5) and **kT/q rises**. The two mobility changes cancel in the ratio, and both thresholds shift by the same amount in this model, so **VM stays at 0.45 V**. What does change is the **steepness**. When cold, VM − Vt is only 0.03 V, so the devices sit near threshold, where gm/Id is high and |gain| ≈ 67. When hot, VM − Vt = 0.20 V, so gm/Id is lower and |gain| ≈ 25. Noise margins shrink a little when hot (NML goes from 0.425 to 0.389 V). In real silicon, Vtn and |Vtp| have different temperature coefficients, so VM drifts somewhat (added). Hot also means far more leakage (A5).\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"VM = VDD/2 because the inverter is symmetric.\" VM = VDD/2 only when the strengths are matched (kn·Wn = kp·Wp with equal |Vt|). Also, do not claim the gain is infinite because \"both are current sources\". λ (CLM) and DIBL set the gain.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nKCL (I_P = I_N) defines every point of the VTC. VM is set by the strength ratio through √(kp/kn), the slope at VM is set by gm/gds, and the curve still works below Vtn + |Vtp| because of subthreshold conduction, only slowly.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Use current balance, not a threshold voltage alone.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["0.55 V","0.45 V","0.35 V","0.90 V"],"ans":1,"why":["This is VDD − |Vtp|, not the switching point.","Equal saturation currents give equal overdrives, placing VM at half the supply here.","This is the NMOS threshold, not the equal-current point.","At the high input rail the NMOS wins and the output is low."],"ex":"Equate βn(VM − Vtn)²/2 and βp(VDD − VM − |Vtp|)²/2. The half-supply result requires these matched strengths and thresholds.","oral":"A1. The CMOS inverter VTC, region by region"},{"id":"V104","k":"md-drills-a2-the-swapped-inverter-nmos-on-top-pmos-on-the-bottom","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Swapped CMOS transistor types","q":"An NMOS connects OUT to VDD and a PMOS connects OUT to ground, with both gates driven by IN. Under the supplied source-follower model, what distinguishes this swapped structure from a CMOS inverter?","a":"Why they ask: this is the reported \"what-if\" question. It tests whether you reason from **which terminal is the source** instead of from memorized pictures.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nNormal inverter compared with the swapped version: in the swapped circuit both devices have their source at the output, so each is a source follower.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Same inverter, but the NMOS connects the output to VDD and the PMOS connects the output to GND. Gates are both tied to Vin. Sketch Vin-Vout.\"\n\n**Answer.** First find the sources. For the NMOS on top, the drain is at VDD, so the **source is the output**. For the PMOS on the bottom, the drain is at GND, so its **source is also the output**. Both are **source followers**:\n- The NMOS can pull the output up only while Vgs > Vt, so Vout ≤ Vin − Vtn (and body effect makes this worse, because Vsb = Vout).\n- The PMOS can pull the output down only to Vout ≥ Vin + |Vtp|.\n\nThe result is a **non-inverting** gate with **gain < 1** and no regeneration. Between those two limits there is a band about Vtn + |Vtp| ≈ 0.7 V wide where **both devices are nearly off**. Inside that band the output is set by leakage balance and by history.\n\nDraw: a positive-slope line, not a falling step. It starts at roughly |Vtp| or below at Vin = 0 and ends at roughly VDD − Vtn(body) or above at Vin = VDD, inside a shaded band bounded by Vin − Vtn and Vin + |Vtp|. Overlay the normal inverter for contrast.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nSwapped-device DC transfer curve: a line with slope about 1/n from 0.12 V to 0.78 V inside a dead band, against the normal inverter.\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why is the DC curve that straight line, and why is its slope 1/n?\"\n\n**Answer.** With no load, the only steady state is where both devices are in **subthreshold** and their leakage currents match. In weak inversion I ∝ exp((V_G − Vt − n·V_S)/(n·U_T)), with bulk-referenced voltages. Set the NMOS current (source = out, bulk = 0) equal to the PMOS current (source = out, bulk = VDD) with matched strengths:\n\n```latex\nV_{out} = \\frac{V_{DD}}{2} + \\frac{1}{n}\\left(V_{in} - \\frac{V_{DD}}{2}\\right)\n```\n\nWith n = 1.35 this gives 0.117 V at Vin = 0 and 0.783 V at Vin = VDD. The model gives **0.12 → 0.78 V with slope 0.73**. The slope is the **source-follower gain 1/n < 1**, and the 1/n comes from body effect. If the strengths are unmatched, the line shifts by (U_T/2)·ln(βn/βp): only a few mV per 2×.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Is that DC value what you'd actually see?\"\n\n**Answer.** Usually not, because the line is reached only through **subthreshold creep**, which is very slow. Take Vin = VDD. If the output starts at 0, the NMOS pulls it up quickly to about (Vin − Vt)/n ≈ 0.4 V (the strong-inversion limit in this model) and then creeps: 0.26 V at 50 ps and still only 0.47 V at 2 ns. If the output starts at 0.9 V, the PMOS has Vsg ≈ 0 and nothing pulls it down, so it stays at **0.90 V**. Same input, different output: the node behaves like a **soft, history-dependent dynamic node**.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: with Vin = VDD the output depends on its initial state; right: a chain of swapped stages decays toward VDD/2 while inverters restore full levels.||A_swapped_toggle.png|Toggling the swapped gate gives a tiny in-phase swing, while a normal inverter swings rail to rail.\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"So can it be used as a logic buffer? What happens in a chain?\"\n\n**Answer.** No. A logic gate must **regenerate**: it needs |gain| > 1 around the switching point, so that noise shrinks stage by stage. Here gain ≈ 1/n ≈ 0.74 everywhere, so every stage **compresses** the swing toward the fixed point. Even in the best DC case a 0.9 V input decays as 0.90 → 0.78 → 0.69 → 0.63 → 0.58 → 0.55 → **0.52 V after 6 stages** (the deviation from VDD/2 shrinks by about 1/n per stage). When toggled at 1 GHz the output barely moves (0.43-0.45 V in the model). With a weaker body effect than this model's, the first-cut swing is |Vtp| ↔ VDD − Vtn ≈ 0.35 ↔ 0.5 V: still well below rail-to-rail, and it never restores.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 - What if you build a ring oscillator out of these?**\n\n**Answer.** It **does not oscillate**. Oscillation needs a net **inverting** loop with loop gain > 1 at the frequency where the phase adds to 360°. Each stage here is non-inverting with gain < 1, so an odd count does not invert and the loop gain is below 1. The nodes settle to a leakage-defined DC point (near VDD/2 here). This is the cleanest way to show that inversion and gain are both required (see A7).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if Vin swings beyond the rails: VDD + Vtn and −|Vtp| (bootstrapped)?**\n\n**Answer.** If the gate goes to VDD + Vtn(body), the NMOS stays on with Vgs > Vt even when its source is at VDD, so the output reaches the **full VDD**. Likewise, driving the gate to −|Vtp| lets the PMOS reach full 0. You then have a full-swing **non-inverting** buffer, but the input swing is still larger than the output swing (gain < 1). This is exactly the reason for **boosted wordlines** (VPP) in DRAM and boosted pass gates: an NMOS can pass a full 1 only if its gate is about one Vt above the level being passed.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Is this structure ever used?\"\n\n**Answer.** Yes, as a follower rather than as logic:\n- It is a **complementary source follower** (a class-B push-pull output stage). Its dead band is exactly **crossover distortion**, which is why analog designers bias such stages into class-AB.\n- Single **source followers** are used as analog buffers and level shifters.\n- An **NMOS pull-up** gives a natural **VDD − Vt precharge** or clamp, used in some ROM, DRAM and sense-amp bitline schemes to limit swing and save power.\n- NMOS-only pass-gate logic has the same weak-1 behavior (A3).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 7.** \"Speed?\"\n\n**Answer.** Speed is poor and gets worse as the output moves. A source follower's drive is ∝ (Vin − Vt − n·Vout)². It shrinks as Vout rises, so the output approaches its final value roughly hyperbolically rather than exponentially, with no regeneration to sharpen the edges. In a normal inverter, Vgs = VDD for the whole transition.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nDrawing a normal inverter VTC flipped upside down, or claiming \"it's still an inverter, just weaker\". Both devices are followers (the source is the output), so the gate is **non-inverting**, has **gain < 1** and cannot reach either rail. Also, do not quote a single exact output value. Inside the dead band the output is set by leakage and history.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nFind the source first. NMOS-up/PMOS-down makes two source followers: Vout tracks Vin with slope about 1/n, is confined between Vin − Vtn and Vin + |Vtp|, and has no gain and no inversion, so it cannot restore logic levels.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Identify the source terminal of each device before reasoning about its conduction.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Its dead band guarantees an output exactly at VDD/2 for every input and initial state","It remains inverting but has a longer propagation delay","It is non-inverting with gain below one and history-dependent weakly driven output regions","It becomes a rail-to-rail regenerative buffer for ordinary rail-limited input"],"ans":2,"why":["Leakage balance and initial charge matter; a universal midrail voltage is not justified.","Swapping rail connections changes terminal behavior and polarity, not just speed.","Both device sources are at OUT, so source-following limits drive and does not restore logic levels.","Rail-limited gates lose overdrive near the rails and the follower gain does not regenerate."],"ex":"Both device sources are at OUT, so source-following limits drive and does not restore logic levels.","oral":"A2. The swapped inverter: NMOS on top, PMOS on the bottom"},{"id":"V105","k":"md-drills-a3-pass-transistors-and-transmission-gates","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Pass-chain Elmore delay","q":"Model eight identical series pass devices as an RC ladder, each R = 10 kΩ with C = 2 fF at each downstream node. Using τ = RC·N(N+1)/2, what is the Elmore time constant?","a":"Why they ask: ROM and mux paths are full of pass devices. The weak-1/weak-0 rules, Vt drops and static current in the receiver are daily concerns in custom design.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nPassing a 1 and a 0 through an NMOS, a PMOS and a transmission gate into 2 fF: only the TG passes both values fully.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"What does an NMOS pass transistor do to a 1 and to a 0? What about a PMOS and a TG?\"\n\n**Answer.** Whichever terminal is lower in voltage is the NMOS source.\n- **Passing a 0:** the source is the output node. Vgs = VDD the whole way, so the NMOS discharges the node fully: a **strong 0**.\n- **Passing a 1:** the source becomes the output. As it rises, Vgs = VDD − Vout shrinks until the device cuts off at Vout ≈ **VDD − Vtn(Vsb)**: a **weak 1**. The model reaches 0.32 V at 100 ps and 0.44 V at 1 ns, then creeps.\n- **PMOS** is the mirror image: a strong 1 and a weak 0 that stalls near |Vtp| or above (0.46 V at 1 ns in the model).\n- A **TG** (NMOS and PMOS in parallel, complementary gates) passes **both values fully**, because one device is always strong at the end of the swing.\n\nDraw: output waveforms. The NMOS rises fast and then flattens below VDD − Vt. The PMOS falls fast and then flattens above |Vtp|. The TG goes rail to rail.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why is the 1 even weaker than VDD − Vt0? Calculate it.\"\n\n**Answer (worked).** **Body effect.** The output node is the source, so Vsb = Vout and the threshold rises as the node charges:\n\n```latex\nV_t(V_{SB}) = V_{t0} + \\gamma\\left(\\sqrt{2\\phi_F + V_{SB}} - \\sqrt{2\\phi_F}\\right),\\qquad V_{out} = V_{DD} - V_t(V_{out})\n```\n\nTake Vt0 = 0.35, γ = 0.3 √V, 2φF = 0.8 V. Solving the self-consistent equation gives **Vout = 0.479 V** with Vt = 0.421 V, not 0.55 V. The model, whose body effect is stronger, gives 0.44-0.47 V. Strictly speaking, after cutoff the node keeps creeping up through subthreshold current, so the result is also time-dependent.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2 - What if the weak 1 drives the gate of another pass transistor, rather than its source/drain?**\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: a degraded node drives the next pass gate's gate (a second Vt drop) or its source/drain (no new drop); right: inverter static current against its input voltage.\n\n</div>\n\n**Answer.** At a **gate**, the next device's gate sits at about VDD − Vt. It can then pass at most (VDD − Vt) − Vt, so the **drops accumulate**: 0.15 V in the model. At a **source/drain** of a device whose gate is at full VDD, that device can still pass up to VDD − Vt, so a level of about VDD − Vt passes through with **no extra drop** (0.45 V). Rule: **never drive a gate from an un-restored pass output**. Series pass chains lose only one Vt.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"What's wrong with feeding VDD − Vt into a CMOS inverter?\"\n\n**Answer.** The inverter's PMOS sees Vsg = VDD − Vin ≈ Vt, so it is **not fully off**, while the NMOS is on. That gives a **static crowbar current** plus a degraded VOL. With the numbers above (Vin = 0.479, so Vsg = 0.421 and the PMOS Vov = 0.071 V), square law with Wp = 2 gives kp·Wp/2·Vov² = 100 µ·2/2·0.071² ≈ **0.5 µA**. The model gives **1.03 µA at 0.47 V** and **0.24 µA at 0.55 V**, against **22 pA** with a full-VDD input: 4-5 orders of magnitude. Across thousands of mux outputs this becomes a static-power bug, and the input sits near VM, so noise margin is lost as well.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"How do you fix the weak 1 without a TG?\"\n\n**Answer.** Use a **level restorer (keeper)**: a PMOS from VDD to the pass node, with its gate driven by the output of the receiving inverter. Once the node rises past the inverter's VM, the output goes low, the PMOS turns on and pulls the node the rest of the way to VDD. The static current disappears. The cost is a **ratio constraint**: to write a 0, the pass NMOS (often in series with the driver) must pull the node below the inverter VM **while fighting the keeper**. The keeper must therefore be weak (long channel) - the same ratioed problem as A4. Other fixes: a TG, a boosted gate (VDD + Vt), or a low-Vt pass device.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if you lower VDD?**\n\n**Answer.** The weak 1, about VDD − Vt(body), shrinks faster than VDD. At VDD = 0.6 V the first-cut output is 0.6 − 0.35 − (body) ≈ 0.2 V, which is below the receiver's VM. The logic fails. That is why NMOS-only pass-transistor logic disappeared as VDD scaled and TGs or restorers became standard.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6 (worked numeric problem).** \"Eight NMOS pass gates in series, each R = 10 kΩ, node capacitance C = 2 fF. Delay? How do you improve it?\"\n\n**Answer.** Use the Elmore delay of an RC ladder:\n\n```latex\n\\tau = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} i\\,RC = RC\\,\\frac{N(N+1)}{2}\n```\n\nRC = 20 ps and N = 8, so τ = 20 ps × 36 = **720 ps**. Delay grows as **N²**. Insert a buffer in the middle (2 segments of 4): 2 × 20 ps × 10 = **400 ps** plus two buffer delays. Buffers also restore the weak level, so the practical rule is to buffer every 3-4 pass devices.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying an NMOS passes \"VDD − Vt\" and stopping there. Mention body effect (the true value is lower), mention that the drops compound only through gates, and mention the static current in the receiver.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nNMOS passes a strong 0 and a weak 1, PMOS the opposite, and a TG passes both. A weak 1 costs a body-effect-enlarged Vt and static current in the next inverter, and Vt drops add up whenever a degraded node drives a gate.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Count how many resistors each downstream capacitor charges through.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["20 ps","720 ps","1,280 ps","160 ps"],"ans":1,"why":["20 ps is only one isolated RC section.","RC is 20 ps and the weighted sum is 8×9/2 = 36.","1,280 ps uses N² rather than the triangular weighting.","160 ps counts each capacitor once and ignores upstream resistances."],"ex":"τ = (10 kΩ)(2 fF)×36 = 720 ps. This is the stated ladder time constant, not automatically a measured 50% propagation delay.","oral":"A3. Pass transistors and transmission gates"},{"id":"V106","k":"md-drills-a4-ratioed-logic-pseudo-nmos","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Why pseudo-NMOS has a nonzero low","q":"In a pseudo-NMOS inverter with an always-on PMOS load, why is VOL generally above zero while the input is high?","a":"Why they ask: keepers on ROM bitlines and dynamic nodes, and the SRAM read ratio, are all the same ratioed fight. The pseudo-NMOS VTC is the cleanest way to show it.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nPseudo-NMOS VTC for several PMOS widths against CMOS (VOL > 0 and VM pushed up), and the static current paid when the output is low.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Draw a pseudo-NMOS inverter's VTC and compare it with CMOS.\"\n\n**Answer.** The PMOS gate is tied to **GND**, so the PMOS is always on as a load, and the NMOS pull-down is the only input device.\n- Vin = 0: the NMOS is off, so VOH = VDD.\n- Vin = VDD: KCL requires the NMOS to sink the PMOS current, so it needs **Vds > 0**. Therefore **VOL ≠ 0** and **static current** flows whenever the output is low.\n\nCompared with CMOS: VM moves up (0.56-0.71 V depending on Wp), gain is lower (about 9-12 compared with 41), NML is smaller, and the low-to-high edge is slow because the PMOS is weak. The benefit is one transistor per input (N + 1 instead of 2N) and lower input capacitance.\n\nDraw: the curve starts at VDD, falls later than the CMOS curve and flattens at a nonzero VOL. Add the CMOS curve as a dashed reference.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1 (worked numeric problem).** \"VDD = 0.9 V, Vt = 0.35 V, kn' = 200 µA/V², kp' = 100 µA/V², Wn/L = 1. Size the PMOS for VOL = 0.1 V. Static power?\"\n\n**Answer.** At VOL the PMOS is **saturated** (Vsd = 0.8 V > Vsg − |Vtp| = 0.55 V) and the NMOS is **linear**:\n\n```latex\n\\frac{k_p'}{2}\\frac{W_p}{L}(V_{DD}-|V_{tp}|)^2 = k_n'\\frac{W_n}{L}\\left[(V_{DD}-V_{tn})V_{OL} - \\frac{V_{OL}^2}{2}\\right]\n```\n\n- Right-hand side: 200 µ × (0.55·0.1 − 0.005) = **10 µA**.\n- Solve for the PMOS: Wp/L = 10 µ / (50 µ × 0.3025) = **0.66**, which is about 1/1.5 of the NMOS width.\n- Check in the model: Wp = 0.66 gives VOL = 97 mV.\n- Static power = 10 µA × 0.9 V = **9 µW per gate while low**. A million such gates, half of them low, would burn about **4.4 W**. That is why pseudo-NMOS survives only in a few wide NOR, PLA or ROM structures.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2 - What if the PMOS is too strong?**\n\n**Answer.** VOL rises and VM moves toward VDD. At Wp = 1.5, VOL = 0.26 V. At **Wp = 4, VOL = 0.75 V**, the |gain| peaks at only 0.64 and the stage cannot drive the next gate low. It is no longer logic. A ratioed gate works only if the pull-down **wins the fight by a ratio**, typically βn/βp ≈ 3-4 or more.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 - What if the PMOS is too weak?**\n\n**Answer.** VOL is excellent (47 mV at Wp = 0.33) and static current halves (5 µA), but **tpLH** grows: the rise current is just the weak PMOS current, so tpLH ≈ C·VDD/(2·Ip). The output also has poor noise immunity when high, because the pull-up holds the node only weakly against coupling. Sizing therefore trades VOL and static power against rise delay.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"Where else do you see this exact fight?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **ROM or dynamic bitline keepers.** The bitline is precharged high and a weak PMOS keeper holds it against the leakage of all the off cells. A selected cell (an NMOS, often in a series stack) must pull the bitline down **while fighting the keeper**, just like the pseudo-NMOS pull-down. Example with model leakage values (Vds = 0.9 V, W = 1): 256 off cells × 19 pA ≈ **5 nA** at 25 °C, but 256 × 2.3 nA ≈ **0.59 µA** at 125 °C. The keeper must source more than the hot leakage, yet stay weak enough that one on-cell (tens of µA) overrides it quickly at the slow-cold corner. Size the keeper at **hot/fast leakage** for holding and at **slow/cold** for overpowering.\n- **SRAM read stability.** During a read, the access NMOS (saturated, from the bitline at VDD) fights the pull-down NMOS (linear) at the 0 node, so the node bumps up. The **cell ratio** CR = β_pd/β_ax keeps that bump below the other inverter's trip point.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 (worked numeric problem).** \"Compute the SRAM read bump for CR = 1, 2, 3. Use VDD = 0.9 V, Vt = 0.35 V and ignore body effect.\"\n\n**Answer.** Set the saturated access current equal to the linear pull-down current:\n\n```latex\n\\frac{\\beta_{ax}}{2}(V_{DD}-V_t-V)^2 = CR\\cdot\\beta_{ax}\\left[(V_{DD}-V_t)V - \\frac{V^2}{2}\\right]\n```\n\nThe read bump is **V = 0.161 V at CR = 1**, **0.101 V at CR = 2** and **0.074 V at CR = 3**. Each of these must stay well below the opposite inverter's VM (about 0.45 V), with margin for variation. That is why CR ≈ 1.5-2.5 is typical. It is the same KCL ratio calculation as the pseudo-NMOS VOL.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Why does pseudo-NMOS still appear in fast wide NORs?\"\n\n**Answer.** A wide NOR in CMOS needs a **series PMOS stack**, which is very slow and very large. Pseudo-NMOS has only parallel NMOS devices and one PMOS load, so input capacitance is low and the falling edge is fast. Its static power is acceptable if the gate is clock-gated or rarely low. Dynamic logic (precharge plus keeper) gets the same advantage without the static current.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"VOL is 0 because the NMOS is on.\" KCL says the NMOS must carry the PMOS current, so it needs a nonzero Vds. VOL is set by the β ratio, not by the NMOS alone.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nRatioed logic is KCL with two devices on at once. VOL is set by the β ratio, static current is the price, and the same calculation sizes ROM keepers and the SRAM cell ratio.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Apply KCL to a node with both pull-up and pull-down conducting.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The output capacitor fixes the final DC voltage regardless of sizing.","The output is isolated from both supply rails.","The NMOS needs nonzero VDS to sink the PMOS’s static current.","The PMOS is off whenever the NMOS is on."],"ans":2,"why":["DC current balance, not the initial capacitor charge, sets VOL.","There is a conducting pull-up and pull-down path.","At exactly zero VDS the NMOS cannot carry the finite pull-up current.","The PMOS gate is held low, so it remains on."],"ex":"The pull-down must win a ratioed current fight. A stronger pull-up raises VOL and static power but can improve the rising transition.","oral":"A4. Ratioed logic: pseudo-NMOS"},{"id":"V107","k":"md-drills-a5-mosfet-fundamentals-under-the-hood","d":"dev","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Temperature inversion","q":"Near threshold, why can a cold CMOS corner be slower than a hot corner?","a":"Why they ask: every inverter answer eventually reduces to Id(Vgs, Vds, Vsb, T). Leakage, keepers, ROM sensing and corner selection all depend on it.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: log Id-Vgs at two Vds showing the subthreshold slope and DIBL; right: Id-Vgs at −40 °C and 125 °C showing the temperature-inversion crossover (temperature dependence added explicitly to the model).\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Sketch Id versus Vgs on a log scale and tell me what sets each part.\"\n\n**Answer.** There are two regimes.\n- **Subthreshold** (a straight line on log scale). Current is **diffusion** over a source barrier that the gate lowers by ΔVgs/n, giving I ∝ exp((Vgs − Vt)/(n·kT/q)).\n- **Strong inversion**: the curve bends over into roughly square-law, or nearly linear once velocity saturation sets in.\n\nThe slope of the straight part is the **subthreshold swing**:\n\n```latex\nS = n\\,\\frac{kT}{q}\\,\\ln 10,\\qquad n = 1 + \\frac{C_{dep}}{C_{ox}}\n```\n\nWith n = 1.35 at 300 K, S = **80 mV/dec**, and the model measures 83. The ideal limit (n = 1) is 60 mV/dec. Real planar devices are about 80-100 mV/dec. FinFETs, with better gate control, are about 65-75 mV/dec.\n\nDraw: log Id against Vgs with a straight subthreshold line, a knee near Vt and saturation above it. Mark Ioff at Vgs = 0.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"How is Vt actually defined? It's not a cliff.\"\n\n**Answer.** Vt is a convention:\n- **Constant-current Vt**: the Vgs at which Id = e.g. 100 nA·W/L. This is fast to measure, and the fab and SPICE models commonly report it.\n- **Linear extrapolation**: extrapolate the linear-region Id-Vgs at its maximum-gm point down to the axis.\n- **Physical (textbook) definition**: surface potential ψs = 2φF, i.e. the inversion charge equals the background doping.\n\n```latex\nV_t = V_{FB} + 2\\phi_F + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 q \\varepsilon_{si} N_A (2\\phi_F)}}{C_{ox}}\n```\n\nThe definitions differ by tens of mV, so always ask which one a number refers to. In the left plot the constant-current Vt at 100 nA is **0.330 V at Vds = 0.05 V** and **0.239 V at Vds = 0.9 V**.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"What is DIBL and what does it do to your circuits?\"\n\n**Answer.** **Drain-induced barrier lowering**: in a short channel the drain field reaches the source barrier and lowers it, so Vt falls as Vds rises (ΔVt ≈ −η·Vds). In the plot the curve shifts left by 91 mV, which is **≈ 107 mV/V** measured at constant current. Consequences:\n- **Ioff rises** about 11× from Vds = 0.05 V to 0.9 V (15 pA → 167 pA).\n- Output conductance rises, so **intrinsic gain and VTC steepness fall**.\n- **Stacking** helps, because the upper off device sees a smaller Vds and the lower one gets a negative Vgs.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 (worked numeric problem).** \"Body effect: Vt0 = 0.35 V, γ = 0.3 √V, 2φF = 0.8 V. What is Vt at Vsb = 0.3 V and 0.5 V? Where does this matter?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- Vt(0.3) = 0.35 + 0.3·(√1.1 − √0.8) = **0.396 V**.\n- Vt(0.5) = 0.35 + 0.3·(√1.3 − √0.8) = **0.424 V**.\n\nIt matters for the upper devices in an NMOS stack (NAND or ROM series cells), whose sources sit above ground, for pass-gate weak levels (A3) and for source followers (A2). Reverse body bias is a leakage knob for the same reason. FinFETs have weak body effect, which removes much of this and also most of the body-bias knob.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"How does temperature change Id?\"\n\n**Answer.** Three effects compete:\n- **Vt decreases** by about 0.5-1 mV/K, which raises current.\n- **Mobility decreases** (µ ∝ T^−1.5, phonon scattering), which lowers current.\n- **kT/q increases**, which raises S: about 62 mV/dec at −40 °C, 80 at 27 °C and 107 at 125 °C (n = 1.35).\n\nAt high Vgs the mobility term wins, so hot is slower. Near Vt the threshold term wins, so hot is faster. The curves therefore **cross**: this is **temperature inversion**. With the explicit extension used here (Vt −1 mV/K, µ ∝ T^−1.5, kT/q), the crossover is at **Vgs ≈ 0.75 V**. At 0.9 V cold gives 34 µA and hot 28 µA. At 0.5 V hot gives 4.3 µA and cold 1.3 µA.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if VDD sits near or below the crossover?**\n\n**Answer.** Near the crossover (the **zero-temperature-coefficient** point) delay barely changes with temperature, which is pleasant. **Below** it, the **cold** corner becomes the slowest corner. In modern low-VDD FinFET designs, timing therefore has to be signed off at **SS/cold** as well as SS/hot, and the worst corner can swap between the high-voltage and low-voltage (DVFS) operating points. The point to state in an interview: \"worst-case timing is not always hot\".\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6 - What if it's hot: what happens to leakage?**\n\n**Answer.** Leakage is exponential in −Vt/(n·kT/q), and **both** Vt and kT/q move in the direction that increases it. In the model, Ioff goes **91 fA (−40 °C) → 19 pA (25 °C) → 2.3 nA (125 °C)**: about **120× from 25 to 125 °C** and 25,000× across the full range (real-silicon ratios depend strongly on the leakage mix and Vt flavor (added)). Hot is therefore the worst case for **keepers, ROM bitline droop, dynamic-node retention and standby power**. This is a design point, not a footnote, for a ROM with hundreds of cells per bitline.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"Hot is always the slow corner.\" That holds only above the temperature-inversion crossover. Another common mistake: calling S \"60 mV/dec\". That is the ideal-n room-temperature limit. Real devices are worse, and S scales with T.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nSubthreshold current is diffusion over a gate-controlled barrier (S = n·kT/q·ln10). Vt is a convention that DIBL, body bias and temperature all move, and because Vt and mobility move in opposite directions with T, hot is faster below the crossover and slower above it.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Compare the competing changes in threshold and mobility instead of assuming one always dominates.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Subthreshold current becomes independent of threshold at low VDD","The threshold increase at cold can outweigh the mobility improvement","The thermal voltage rises as temperature falls","Mobility always decreases when temperature decreases"],"ans":1,"why":["Threshold sensitivity is especially strong near and below threshold.","Near threshold, current is very sensitive to overdrive; lower cold mobility scattering may not compensate the higher threshold.","Thermal voltage is proportional to absolute temperature and falls at cold.","The usual mobility trend improves at cold; that alone would predict faster operation."],"ex":"Near threshold, current is very sensitive to overdrive; lower cold mobility scattering may not compensate the higher threshold.","oral":"A5. MOSFET fundamentals under the hood"},{"id":"V108","k":"md-drills-a6-inverter-delay-and-sizing","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Why width cannot remove intrinsic delay","q":"In the model tp(W) = 0.69(R0/W)(Cd0·W + Cext), what remains as W tends to infinity while Cext is fixed?","a":"Why they ask: standard-cell design means sizing for delay, input capacitance and load. They want tp = 0.69·R·C from first principles and the optimal-chain argument.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: inverter transient with a 20 ps input ramp into 4 fF, with 50% delay points; right: step-input tpHL against VDD from the model, with an α-power fit.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Where does tp = 0.69·R·C come from, and what is R?\"\n\n**Answer.** If the switching device is modeled as a resistor R discharging C, then V(t) = VDD·e^(−t/RC). V reaches VDD/2 at t = RC·ln 2 = **0.69·RC**. A real transistor is not a resistor: during the 50% transition it is mostly a current source. **R_eq** is defined as the average of Vds/Id over the swing from VDD to VDD/2, which is about (3/4)·VDD/Idsat. In the model, the unit NMOS Idsat is 30.5 µA, so R_eq ≈ 22 kΩ. For C = 4 fF, 0.69·R·C = **61 ps**, against **63 ps** from the exact step-input integral and **69 ps** with a 20 ps input ramp (tpHL ≈ tpLH because the strengths are matched). Rise and fall times (10-90 %) are about **128 ps**.\n\n```latex\nt_p = \\int_{V_{DD}/2}^{V_{DD}} \\frac{C\\,dV}{I_D(V)} \\;\\approx\\; 0.69\\,R_{eq}\\,C,\\qquad R_{eq}\\approx \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{V_{DD}}{I_{Dsat}}\n```\n\nDraw: input ramp and output RC-like edge, with arrows between the 50 % crossings.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"What's self-loading, and why doesn't making the inverter wider make it infinitely fast?\"\n\n**Answer.** The load is C_ext plus the inverter's **own drain capacitance**, which is proportional to W. With tp = 0.69·(R₀/W)·(γ·C₀·W + C_ext): widening W shrinks the C_ext term, but the self-loading term 0.69·R₀·γ·C₀ = **tp0** is independent of W. That is the **intrinsic delay**, and no amount of width removes it. Widening also raises the input capacitance, which slows the **previous** stage.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"What is FO4 and why do we use it?\"\n\n**Answer.** **FO4** is the delay of an inverter driving four copies of itself: tp = tp0·(1 + 4/γ). It is a process-normalized unit that cancels most technology dependence, so designs can be compared across nodes. Worked example: R = 5 kΩ, Cin = 1.5 fF and Cd = 1.5 fF (γ = 1) gives FO4 = 0.69 × 5 kΩ × (1.5 + 6) fF = **25.9 ps**. Gate delays are often quoted in FO4s, and a clock period is commonly ~15-25 FO4.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 (worked numeric problem).** \"Drive C_L = 4096·Cin of a unit inverter. How many stages and what ratio? γ = 1, tp0 = 5.2 ps.\"\n\n**Answer.** With N stages of fanout f each, f^N = F = 4096 and total delay = N·tp0·(1 + f/γ).\n\n- N = 1: f = 4096 → 4097·tp0 (= 21.2 ns)\n- N = 3: f = 16 → 51·tp0\n- N = 5: f = 5.28 → 31.4·tp0\n- **N = 6: f = 4.00 → 30.0·tp0 (= 155 ps)**\n- N = 7: f = 3.28 → 29.97·tp0\n- N = 8: f = 2.83 → 30.6·tp0\n\nThe optimum satisfies ln f = 1 + γ/f, which gives **f ≈ 3.6** for γ = 1 (e for γ = 0). The minimum is **very flat**, so engineers use f ≈ 4. N = 6 or 7 are within 0.1 %. Adding an inverter for polarity costs almost nothing near the optimum.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 - What if you double the width, or double the load?**\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Double W** of one inverter with a fixed external load: R halves and self-load doubles, so the intrinsic term is unchanged and the external term halves. Delay drops but not by 2×. The previous stage now sees 2× input capacitance, so the path may not improve. Only the full chain tells you.\n- **Double the load**: only the external term doubles. For FO4 with γ = 1 that is 5·tp0 → 9·tp0, or **+80 %**, not +100 %, because self-loading does not change.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if you lower VDD?**\n\n**Answer.** tp ∝ C·VDD/Ion, and with the **α-power law** Ion ∝ (VDD − Vt)^α:\n\n```latex\nt_p \\;\\propto\\; \\frac{C\\,V_{DD}}{(V_{DD}-V_t)^{\\alpha}}\n```\n\n- The model has no velocity saturation, so the fit gives α ≈ **2.1**. From 0.9 to 0.7 V delay grows **2.0×** (63 → 127 ps), and at 0.5 V it is **7.5×**.\n- Real short-channel devices have α ≈ 1.2-1.5. With α = 1.3, 0.9 → 0.7 V would give only 1.4×.\n- Below Vt the formula breaks down. The current becomes exponential, so delay explodes: **3.6 ns at VDD = Vt** and **28 ns at 0.25 V**, which is 57× and 440× the 0.9 V value.\n\nThis is the energy-delay tradeoff behind DVFS.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Why does a slow input slew increase delay, and how big is the effect?\"\n\n**Answer.** While the input is still ramping, the pull-down is only partly on and the pull-up has not switched off, so the two fight (crowbar current). The 50 %-to-50 % delay grows. In the model, a stage driven by a realistic self-similar edge (~128 ps 10-90 %) has **122 ps** delay, against 63 ps for a step: almost 2×. This is why timing libraries are indexed by **input slew and output load**, and why max-transition rules exist.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"Make it twice as wide and it's twice as fast.\" Self-loading caps the gain, and a bigger input capacitance slows the previous stage. Another trap is quoting tp = RC without the ln 2, or treating R as Vds/Id at one bias point.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nDelay is charge over current (0.69·R_eq·C). Self-loading sets an intrinsic floor, the optimum fanout per stage is about 4 (flat minimum), and delay scales as VDD/(VDD − Vt)^α until it goes exponential below Vt.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Distribute R0/W across the two capacitance terms.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Zero delay, since resistance tends to zero","The external-load term 0.69R0Cext unchanged","An unbounded delay proportional to W","The finite intrinsic term 0.69R0Cd0"],"ans":3,"why":["Own drain capacitance grows with W and leaves a nonzero floor.","The external-load term falls as 1/W.","The growing capacitance is multiplied by a falling resistance.","The W factors cancel in the self-capacitance contribution."],"ex":"Expanding gives tp = 0.69R0Cd0 + 0.69R0Cext/W. Upsizing also increases the load seen by the previous stage.","oral":"A6. Inverter delay and sizing"},{"id":"V109","k":"md-drills-a7-ring-oscillator","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Recover stage delay from a ring","q":"A 101-stage inverter ring oscillates at 250 MHz. Assuming equal average propagation delay per stage, what is that delay?","a":"Why they ask: it combines inversion, gain and delay, and it is the standard silicon process monitor. Expect \"why odd?\", \"what if even?\" and \"how do you gate it?\".\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nFive-stage ring oscillator and its node waveforms (C = 4 fF per node): period about 1.22 ns, so tp is about 122 ps per stage.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"What sets the frequency of a ring oscillator, and why must N be odd?\"\n\n**Answer.** An edge must travel around the loop **twice** for one full period: once to flip every node, and once more to flip them back. So:\n\n```latex\nf = \\frac{1}{2\\,N\\,t_p}\n```\n\nThe loop needs **net inversion** so that it has **no stable DC solution**. With N odd, assuming all nodes are logic levels leads to a contradiction, and the only DC solution is every node at VM. That point is **unstable** because the small-signal loop gain is |A|^N ≫ 1. Model: N = 5 with 4 fF per node gives T = **1224 ps** → f = 0.82 GHz → **tp ≈ 122 ps**.\n\nDraw: N inverters in a loop and staggered square-ish waveforms, each node delayed by tp from the previous one.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"What if N is even?\"\n\n**Answer.** The loop is then non-inverting, so it has **two stable states** (alternating 0/1/0/1 and its complement). It **latches** into one of them and never oscillates. N = 2 is exactly the cross-coupled pair at the core of an SRAM cell or latch. The VM point still exists but is a metastable saddle. A noise kick sends it to one of the stable states (metastability).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"What's the minimum stage gain required? What about N = 1?\"\n\n**Answer.** Use the Barkhausen condition with single-pole stages. The N stages must add 180° of phase on top of the DC inversion, so each provides 180°/N, and the loop gain must be ≥ 1 at that frequency. This gives |A₀| ≥ 1/cos(π/N): **2.0 for N = 3**, **1.24 for N = 5** and **1.11 for N = 7**. An inverter (|A| ≈ 41) passes easily. The swapped structure in A2 (gain < 1, non-inverting) fails both tests. For **N = 1**, a single inverter with output tied to input settles at **VM**. That is a self-biased amplifier (used to bias inverter-based amplifiers or crystal oscillators), not an oscillator, because one pole cannot give 180° of phase.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 (worked numeric problem).** \"31-stage ring, tp = 15 ps. Frequency? Silicon measures 250 MHz on a 101-stage ring: what's tp? What does a 5 % slower tp do to the 31-stage ring?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- f = 1/(2·31·15 ps) = **1.075 GHz**.\n- tp = 1/(2·101·250 MHz) = **19.8 ps**.\n- tp = 15.75 ps gives f = **1.024 GHz**, a 4.8 % drop. Frequency is directly proportional to device drive, which is why rings make good monitors.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"How are rings used as process monitors?\"\n\n**Answer.** f ∝ Ion/(C·VDD), so ring frequency on scribe-line or on-die monitors bins **fast/slow corners**, tracks **VDD droop and temperature**, and supports adaptive voltage scaling. Making the ring **NMOS-sensitive** or **PMOS-sensitive** (stages with stacked NMOS or PMOS, NAND versus NOR rings, or skewed sizing) separates the two devices, so FS and SF corners can be identified. Rings with wire loads or extra fanout separate **device** from **interconnect** (RC) variation, and leakage-sensitive variants track Ioff. The ring includes realistic input slews, so its tp (122 ps here) is a \"loaded, real-slew\" delay rather than the 63 ps step-input number.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if one stage is a NAND2 with an enable input?**\n\n**Answer.** With **EN = 1** the NAND behaves as an inverter (Y = ¬A), so the ring oscillates. f falls slightly because a NAND stage, with its series NMOS stack, is slower: for example 30 inverters at 15 ps plus one NAND at 20 ps gives 1/(2·470 ps) = **1.064 GHz**. With **EN = 0** the NAND output is forced to **1**, the loop is broken and every node settles to a defined level. There is no floating node and no crowbar current, so the ring is cleanly **stopped**. This is how test rings are gated and how counters read them out. NAND (enable active-high, parks the output high) or NOR (parks it low) is chosen by the required idle state.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6 - What if VDD or temperature changes?**\n\n**Answer.** f follows the device current. Lowering VDD lowers f sharply (A6), and near or below threshold it falls exponentially, which is why rings make good on-die VDD sensors. Temperature has the effect described in A5. Above the inversion crossover hot is slower, below it hot is faster, and near the zero-temperature-coefficient point the ring is nearly temperature-independent. The same ring therefore reads differently at different supply voltages, which matters when calibrating it as a thermal sensor.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nWriting f = 1/(N·tp). The factor of 2 is needed because each node must switch twice (rise and fall) per period. Other traps: saying \"even rings oscillate too, just at a different frequency\" (they latch), and forgetting the per-stage gain requirement.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nA ring needs odd inversion and enough per-stage gain (|A| > 1/cos(π/N)). It runs at 1/(2·N·tp), latches if N is even, and its frequency maps directly onto Ion/(C·VDD), which makes it a process, voltage and temperature monitor.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Each node makes two transitions in one period.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["1.98 ps","3.96 ns","19.8 ps","39.6 ps"],"ans":2,"why":["This is a factor-of-ten arithmetic error.","This is roughly the entire ring period, not one stage delay.","tp = 1/(2 × 101 × 250 MHz).","This omits the two traversals per full period."],"ex":"One oscillation includes rising and falling propagation around the loop: T = 2Ntp = 4 ns.","oral":"A7. Ring oscillator"},{"id":"V110","k":"md-drills-b1-nand2-from-the-transistors-up","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND input arrival order","q":"A NAND2 has A on the upper NMOS and B on the lower NMOS. For a falling output, why can placing the late rising input on A help?","a":"Why they ask: NAND2 is the workhorse of every standard-cell library. Pin order, sizing and the internal node turn up directly in cell layout and in the pin-dependent timing arcs of the .lib.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nNAND2 with 2:1 sizing; the late-arriving input A drives the NMOS nearest the output, so node X is already discharged when A arrives\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Draw a two-input NAND at transistor level, size it, and tell me which input you'd connect to which transistor.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Pull-up:** two PMOS in **parallel** (either input low → Y = 1). **Pull-down:** two NMOS in **series** (both high → Y = 0). The two networks are duals, so Y = ¬(A·B).\n- Sizing in a 2:1 mobility process: a unit inverter is N = 1, P = 2. Each series NMOS is doubled to **N = 2** so that the stack matches one unit NMOS. Each PMOS is **P = 2**, because the worst-case pull-up has only one PMOS on. Input capacitance per pin is 2 + 2 = 4 units against 3 for the inverter, so **g = 4/3**.\n- **Pin order:** connect the **latest-arriving input to the NMOS nearest the output** (MNA). The early input has already discharged the internal node X through MNB, so the late edge only has to discharge C_out.\n- Draw: the schematic above. Then the stick diagram: a p-diffusion strip VDD | A | Y | B | VDD and an n-diffusion strip Y | A | X | B | GND, with poly A and B in the same order in both. That gives one Euler path, no diffusion breaks, and shared diffusion on Y and X.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Which input pattern gives the worst-case fall, and which gives the worst-case rise? Give me numbers.\"\n\n**Answer.** I'd use Elmore delay with W&H units: an NMOS of width 1 has resistance R, a PMOS of width 2 has R, and diffusion is C per unit width. The output carries 6C of parasitic (2C + 2C from the PMOS drains, 2C from MNA), node X carries 2C, and the load is h fan-out NAND2 inputs at 4C each.\n\n**Worked numeric problem.** R = 10 kΩ, C = 0.15 fF (RC = 1.5 ps), fan-out h = 3, so C_out = 6C + 12C = 18C.\n\n| Transition | Who conducts | Elmore | Delay |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fall, A (top) arrives last, X already at 0 | MNA + MNB (R/2 + R/2) | R·18C = 18RC | **27.0 ps** |\n| Fall, B (bottom) arrives last, X precharged | adds (R/2)·C_X | 18RC + (R/2)(2C) = 19RC | **28.5 ps** (worst fall) |\n| Rise, A falls (X isolated by MNA) | one PMOS (R) | 18RC | 27.0 ps |\n| Rise, B falls with A = 1 (X recharged through MNA) | adds (R + R/2)·2C | 21RC | **31.5 ps** (worst rise) |\n| Rise, A and B fall together | two PMOS in parallel (R/2) | 9RC | 13.5 ps (best) |\n\n```latex\nt_{pdf,\\,worst} = \\tfrac{R}{2}\\,(2C) + \\left(\\tfrac{R}{2}+\\tfrac{R}{2}\\right)(6+4h)C = (7+4h)\\,RC\n```\n\nThe worst fall happens when the bottom input arrives last. The worst rise happens when only one PMOS turns on **and** X has to be recharged as well. The simultaneous-falling case is the fastest rise.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"What voltage is the internal node X at when A = 1 and B = 0? How much charge does it hold?\"\n\n**Answer.** Y is high, MNA has V_G = V_DD and MNB is off. MNA acts as a source follower that charges X toward V_DD − V_t. Because X is MNA's source, **body effect** raises V_t, so X reaches only about 0.4 V within roughly 100 ps in the model. After that MNA is in subthreshold and X keeps **creeping up**. The true DC limit is the point where MNA's subthreshold current equals MNB's I_off, about 0.66 V in the model (no DIBL). With C_X = 1 fF at about 0.45 V, X holds **Q ≈ 0.45 fC**. That charge matters in three ways:\n1. It produces the extra (R/2)·C_X term in the worst fall.\n2. Delay becomes **history-dependent**, which is why .lib timing arcs are per pin and sometimes per state.\n3. In dynamic logic the same charge causes **charge sharing** (B5). In a static NAND the on PMOS restores the output, so it only shows up as a small dip.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nInternal node X charging through MNA, which acts as a source follower: fast to about 0.4 V (body-effected V_t), then a slow subthreshold creep toward the DC limit\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"Does the VTC depend on which input switches?\"\n\n**Answer.** Yes. At V_M, KCL sets the PMOS current equal to the stack current.\n- **Both inputs switching together:** the two PMOS are in parallel (twice the pull-up) against the full series stack, so V_M rises: **0.477 V** in the model.\n- **One input switching:** one PMOS against a stack whose other device is fully on: about **0.43 V**. That is slightly below the reference N1/P2 inverter (0.450 V), because two width-2 NMOS in series are a bit stronger than one width-1 device. The bottom transistor sits in the linear region with only a small V_DS.\n- **Top (A) versus bottom (B) switching:** when A switches, its source is at V_X > 0 (degeneration plus body effect), so it is weaker and V_M is slightly higher: **0.431 V against 0.427 V**. The model's gap is small. In bulk silicon with a stronger body effect it is larger (added).\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nNAND2 VTCs for the three switching cases; simultaneous switching shifts V_M up by about 50 mV, and top versus bottom differ by a few mV\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"Why does the 'both switch together' case matter for timing sign-off?\"\n\n**Answer.** The .lib is characterized with **single-input switching**: one pin toggles and the others are held at non-controlling values. When both inputs fall together the rise is about 2× faster (9RC against 18RC), so single-input-switching numbers are pessimistic for max delay and **optimistic for hold/min delay**. When both rise together the stack sees only one edge. This is the **multi-input switching** effect, and some flows add derates for it (added).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if** \"you put the late-arriving input on the **bottom** transistor instead?\"\n\n**Answer.** Every late fall now pays for discharging X: 19RC against 18RC, which is **+1.5 ps (+5.6%) at h = 3** and +10% at h = 1 (11RC against 10RC). The late rise also pays 3RC to recharge X. Rule: the critical input goes **nearest the output**. The gate is logically symmetric but **not electrically symmetric**, and library cells publish different arcs for pins A and B.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6 - What if** \"this is a 1:1 FinFET-like process (μ_n ≈ μ_p)?\"\n\n**Answer.** The unit inverter becomes N = 1, P = 1 with C_in = 2. NAND2 becomes N = 2, P = 1, so C_in = 3 and **g = 3/2**. That is *worse* than the 4/3 of the 2:1 process, because the inverter it is compared against got cheaper. NOR2 becomes N = 1, P = 2, also **g = 3/2**, so the NAND advantage disappears in logical-effort terms. In FinFET, widths are **quantized to whole fins**, so \"N = 2\" means 2 fins. Ratios like 1.5 cannot be built, which is one reason libraries use skewed and multi-fin variants.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 7.** \"In layout, why does the ordering matter for area?\"\n\n**Answer.** With the same poly order (A, B) in the PMOS and NMOS rows there is a common **Euler path**: each diffusion row is one unbroken strip with shared source/drain contacts. Merged diffusion also reduces C_X, since X needs no contact at all and is only a shared diffusion between two gates. A different order would need a diffusion break, which costs a poly pitch and adds capacitance.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"the worst-case rise is when both inputs fall\". Two PMOS in parallel is the *fastest* rise. The worst is one PMOS on plus recharging X. A second trap is treating A and B as interchangeable: they have different delays, VTCs and leakage.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nNAND2 = parallel PMOS (P = 2) plus series NMOS (N = 2), g = 4/3. Put the late input nearest the output, and remember the internal node X: it holds charge, makes delay history-dependent, and moves V_M.\n\n</div>\n\n---","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Draw the internal-node voltage before the last input switches.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["An already-high B can discharge the internal node before A arrives","The output then falls through two PMOS in parallel","The upper transistor bypasses the lower transistor during discharge","A’s Boolean role is stronger than B’s in the NAND truth table"],"ans":0,"why":["Pre-discharging the internal capacitance reduces extra stored charge on the late critical transition.","PMOS devices form the pull-up, not the falling-output discharge path.","Both series NMOS still conduct in the output pull-down path.","NAND inputs are logically symmetric; the difference comes from physical internal-node history."],"ex":"Pre-discharging the internal capacitance reduces extra stored charge on the late critical transition.","oral":"B1. NAND2 from the transistors up"},{"id":"V111","k":"md-drills-b2-nor-vs-nand-complex-gates-and-logical-effort-with-real-numbers","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND versus NOR logical effort","q":"Use equal lengths, a width-1 NMOS/width-2 PMOS reference inverter, and input capacitance proportional to total gate width. After matching worst-case drive, what are NAND2 and NOR2 per-input logical efforts?","a":"Why they ask: choosing cells and sizing a path is daily work in a standard-cell team, and logical effort is the fastest way to show you can reason about it without a simulator.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLogical effort vs fan-in; in a 2:1 process NOR grows twice as fast as NAND, and in a 1:1 process they coincide||B_le_path.svg|Worked path: inverter, NAND2, NOR2 with a branch of 2 driving 120 units\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Why do people prefer NAND over NOR?\"\n\n**Answer.** In a NOR the **PMOS are in series**, and the PMOS is already the weak device (μ_p ≈ μ_n/2 in bulk). Matching unit drive in a NOR2 takes P = 4 and N = 1, so C_in = 5 and **g = 5/3**. A NAND2 needs N = 2 and P = 2, so C_in = 4 and **g = 4/3**. The NOR has more input capacitance, larger PMOS area and more PMOS leakage, and with large series PMOS stacks a slower rise. Both gates have the same transistor count; the difference comes entirely from which device ends up stacked.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Give me g and p for NAND/NOR with 2-4 inputs.\"\n\n**Answer.** p is the parasitic delay: output diffusion capacitance in units of the inverter's.\n\n| Gate | g (2:1 process) | g (1:1 process) | p |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NAND2 / NOR2 | 4/3 / 5/3 | 3/2 / 3/2 | 2 |\n| NAND3 / NOR3 | 5/3 / 7/3 | 2 / 2 | 3 |\n| NAND4 / NOR4 | 2 / 3 | 5/2 / 5/2 | 4 |\n\n```latex\ng_{\\mathrm{NAND}n} = \\frac{n+2}{3}, \\qquad g_{\\mathrm{NOR}n} = \\frac{2n+1}{3}, \\qquad g_{1:1} = \\frac{n+1}{2}\n```\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Size an AOI21, Y = ¬(A·B + C), and give its logical effort.\"\n\n**Answer.** Pull-down: A and B in series, in parallel with C. Pull-up: (A ∥ B) in series with C, which is the dual. Size each network so its worst path matches a unit inverter (N = 1, P = 2).\n\n| Input | NMOS width | PMOS width | C_in | g |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 2 (series) | 4 (two-stack) | 6 | **2** |\n| B | 2 (series) | 4 | 6 | **2** |\n| C | 1 (alone) | 4 | 5 | **5/3** |\n\nPut the C PMOS at the output and the A ∥ B pair toward VDD. The output then sees NMOS 2 + 1 and PMOS 4, a total of 7, so **p = 7/3**. Putting A ∥ B at the output would give p = 11/3. Pin C is the fast pin, so route the late signal to C.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 - worked path problem.** \"Inverter → NAND2 → NOR2 → 120 units of load. The NAND2 output also drives an identical NOR2 off the path. C_in = 10. Size it and give the delay.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n\n```latex\nG = 1\\cdot\\tfrac{4}{3}\\cdot\\tfrac{5}{3} = \\tfrac{20}{9},\\quad B = 2,\\quad H = \\tfrac{120}{10} = 12,\\quad F = GBH = 53.33\n```\n\n```latex\n\\hat f = F^{1/3} = 3.764,\\qquad D = 3\\hat f + P = 3(3.764) + (1+2+2) = 16.29\\,\\tau\n```\n\nSize backward from the load using C_in,i = g_i·b_i·C_out,i / f̂:\n- NOR2 input = (5/3)(120)/3.764 = **53.1**, giving N = 53.1/5 = 10.6 and P = 42.5.\n- NAND2 input = (4/3)(2·53.1)/3.764 = **37.6**, giving N = P = 18.8.\n- Inverter input = 1·37.6/3.764 = 10.0 ✓ (closes the loop).\n\nEvery stage then has f = 3.764. With τ = 3 ps, D ≈ 16.3τ ≈ **49 ps**. The optimal stage count is N̂ = log₄F = 2.87, so 3 stages is correct.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 - What if** \"the load doubles to 240?\"\n\n**Answer.** F = 106.7, f̂ = 4.74, D = 3(4.74) + 5 = **19.2τ**. Now N̂ = log₄F = 3.37. A 4-stage path would give 4·F^¼ + 6 = **18.9τ**, about 2% faster, but it inverts the logic. Keeping polarity takes 5 stages, which gives 19.7τ (worse). The right answer is to keep 3 stages and upsize, or to restructure (for example, absorb an inversion into the logic). The delay curve is very flat near the optimum.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if** \"you build a NOR3 in a 1:1 FinFET-like process?\"\n\n**Answer.** P = 3 fins per PMOS in a series stack and N = 1 fin, so C_in = 4 and **g = 2**, exactly the same as NAND3 (N = 3, P = 1, also g = 2). p = (3 + 3)/2 = **3** for both. The classic \"avoid NOR\" rule is mostly a 2:1-mobility argument. In a balanced process the NOR penalty shrinks to secondary effects:\n- the three-high PMOS stack has internal-node capacitance and series contact/via resistance;\n- fins are quantized;\n- PMOS performance depends on strain, which can make the effective ratio non-unity (added).\n\nBulk body effect is largely gone in FinFET, because undoped fins have weak body coupling (added), so stacking costs mostly resistance and capacitance.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Why not just build a NAND8?\"\n\n**Answer.** Stack delay grows **quadratically**. Each of the n series devices is sized n× (R/n), and each internal node carries about nC. The Elmore delay is then:\n\n```latex\nt_{stack} \\approx \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\frac{iR}{n}\\,(nC) + R\\,C_{out} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\,RC + R\\,C_{out}\n```\n\nThe internal-node terms are 1, 3 and 6 RC for n = 2, 3, 4, while g grows linearly. On top of that, body effect weakens the upper devices in bulk, there are more internal nodes for charge sharing, and slews degrade. Practical limits are about **4 NMOS and 3 PMOS** in series. Beyond that, decompose into a tree (NAND4 = NAND2 → NOR2 gives the same function in a shallower form).\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"NOR is slower because it has more transistors\" (it has the same number), or quoting g = 5/3 for NOR2 without saying that it assumes 2:1 mobility. In a 1:1 process NAND2 and NOR2 both have g = 3/2.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nNAND is preferred because series NMOS is cheaper than series PMOS when μ_n ≈ 2μ_p. Logical effort (F = GBH, f̂ = F^(1/N), D = Nf̂ + P) turns path sizing into arithmetic.\n\n</div>\n\n---","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Normalize each input’s N+P width to the reference inverter.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["NAND2: 5/3; NOR2: 4/3","Both: 2","NAND2: 4/3; NOR2: 5/3","Both: 4/3"],"ans":2,"why":["This swaps the cost of series PMOS and NMOS.","Transistor count alone does not determine normalized input capacitance.","NAND2 uses N2+P2 = 4 per input; NOR2 uses N1+P4 = 5; reference capacitance is 3.","NOR2 requires larger PMOS devices to compensate its series stack."],"ex":"The NOR penalty here follows from the stated 2:1 reference widths and the PMOS series stack; it is not a universal technology-independent rule.","oral":"B2. NOR vs NAND, complex gates, and logical effort with real numbers"},{"id":"V112","k":"md-drills-b3-power-dynamic-short-circuit-leakage-and-the-energy-delay-trade-off","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Supply scaling and switching power","q":"At fixed capacitance, activity and frequency, VDD falls from 0.90 V to 0.81 V. What happens to dynamic switching power?","a":"Why they ask: standard-cell libraries carry power tables (internal power, leakage), and a designer must know which knob moves which term and by how much.\n\n**Opening question.** \"Where does the power go in a CMOS gate, and which term dominates?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n1. **Dynamic (switching):** P = α·C·V_DD²·f. This usually dominates in active logic.\n2. **Short-circuit:** both devices are on during an input transition. It is typically under about 10% if slews are controlled.\n3. **Static leakage:** subthreshold (dominant), gate tunneling, junction/GIDL. It dominates when idle and grows exponentially with temperature.\n\nDraw: an inverter with C_L, arrows for charge from VDD into C_L and from C_L to GND, and a dashed arrow from VDD straight to GND labeled \"short-circuit\".\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Derive the CV²f. Where does the energy go?\"\n\n**Answer.** On a 0→1 output transition the supply delivers charge Q = C·V_DD at voltage V_DD, so it supplies **E = C·V_DD²**. Half of that, ½CV², ends up stored in C. The other half is dissipated in the PMOS **regardless of its resistance**. On 1→0 the stored ½CV² is dissipated in the NMOS. Each full charge/discharge cycle therefore costs CV² from the supply. With α the probability of a 0→1 transition per cycle:\n\n```latex\nP_{dyn} = \\alpha\\, C\\, V_{DD}^2\\, f\n```\n\nExample: a 2 fF node draws 1.62 fJ per 0→1 transition at 0.9 V, and 0.81 fJ of that is stored.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2 - worked numeric problem.** \"A block switches 1 nF in total with α = 0.1 at 2 GHz and 0.9 V. Power? What if V_DD drops 10%? What if activity doubles?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- P = 0.1 × 1 nF × 0.81 V² × 2 GHz = **162 mW**.\n- **What if V_DD −10% (0.81 V)** at the same f: P scales as 0.9² = 0.81, giving **131 mW (−19%)**. The gate also slows: with the α-power law (α = 1.3, V_t = 0.35 V), delay ∝ V/(V − V_t)^α grows **×1.135**. If f is lowered to match, P = 162 × 0.81 / 1.135 ≈ **116 mW (−29%)**. Energy per operation is −19% and EDP is ×0.92.\n- **What if activity doubles** (for example, glitching or poor clock gating): P is linear in α, so **324 mW**. Glitches count as real transitions. Clock gating and balanced paths cut α. The clock net has α = 1 (two edges per cycle) and is often 30-40% of dynamic power (added).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"When does short-circuit current flow, and how does it depend on input slew?\"\n\n**Answer.** It flows only while V_tn < V_in < V_DD − |V_tp| **and** the output has moved enough to put voltage across the device that is turning off. With a rising input and C_L = 0.2 fF in the model, the supply current is pure short-circuit current, because the PMOS is only turning off. Results:\n- Input t_r = 20 ps: E_sc ≈ **0.4%** of C_L·V_DD².\n- Input t_r = 200 ps: E_sc ≈ **11.5%**.\n\nSweep: 10 / 20 / 50 / 100 / 200 / 400 ps gives 0.1 / 0.4 / 1.7 / 4.6 / 11.5 / 27% of C_L·V_DD².\n\nWith a 2 fF load at 100 ps it is only about 0.1%. The output barely moves during the input window, so the PMOS has almost no V_SD. The design rule is to **keep input and output slews comparable**; library max-transition limits partly enforce this.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nInverter with a rising input; supply current is pure short-circuit current, and a 10× slower input gives about 30× more short-circuit charge here\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 - What if** \"V_DD < V_tn + |V_tp| (here 0.7 V)?\"\n\n**Answer.** There is no input voltage at which both devices are above threshold, so strong-inversion short-circuit current disappears and only subthreshold overlap current remains. That is one reason near-threshold designs see negligible short-circuit power. The cost is that the VTC becomes subthreshold-shaped, delay gets much worse, and **leakage energy per operation rises** because each operation takes longer.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"Leakage: give me a number for a block, and say what moves it.\"\n\n**Answer.** A 10M-gate block at about 100 pA average per gate leaks 10M × 100 pA × 0.9 V ≈ **0.9 mW at room temperature**. At 125 °C the model's subthreshold leakage is ×44, giving about **40 mW**. In standby leakage dominates, which is why there are power gating, HVT cells, stacking and input-vector control (see B4). Leakage grows exponentially as V_t falls (60-100 mV/decade) and with temperature, and roughly linearly to exponentially with V_DD through DIBL.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Where is the energy-delay optimum?\"\n\n**Answer.** Energy ∝ V², and delay ∝ V/(V − V_t)^α. So EDP ∝ V³/(V − V_t)^α, and its minimum is at:\n\n```latex\n\\frac{d}{dV}\\ln\\frac{V^3}{(V-V_t)^\\alpha}=0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; V_{opt} = \\frac{3V_t}{3-\\alpha} = \\frac{3(0.35)}{1.7} \\approx 0.62\\ \\text{V}\n```\n\nThe curve is flat around the optimum, so running at 0.9 V costs only about +20% EDP and buys about 1.7× speed. A square-law model (α = 2) would put the optimum at 3V_t ≈ 1.05 V. Including leakage energy pushes the optimum **up**, because slow operation integrates leakage for longer.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nNormalized energy, delay and EDP vs V_DD with the α-power model; the EDP minimum is at about 0.62 V\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"the PMOS resistance determines how much energy is lost while charging\". It does not: half of CV² is lost in any resistive charging. Another trap is saying \"dropping V_DD 10% saves 10%\". It saves 19% at the same f because power scales with V².\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nP = αCV²f + short-circuit + leakage. V² is the strongest knob, slew control keeps short-circuit power small, and leakage is the exponential term that dominates when idle and hot.\n\n</div>\n\n---","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Square the ratio of the two supply voltages.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It decreases by 9%.","It decreases by 10%.","It decreases by 19%.","It decreases by 34%."],"ans":2,"why":["This does not apply the squared voltage ratio.","Ten percent is the voltage decrease, not the power decrease.","Power scales as VDD², giving 0.9² = 0.81 of the original.","A 34% reduction would require additional changes beyond the stated fixed terms."],"ex":"Pdynamic = αCVDD²f. Timing and leakage need separate checks; they are not included in this fixed-frequency calculation.","oral":"B3. Power: dynamic, short-circuit, leakage, and the energy-delay trade-off"},{"id":"V113","k":"md-drills-b4-leakage-in-gates-stack-effect-state-dependence-temperature-and-how-to-characterize-it-cheaply","d":"char","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Off-stack leakage suppression","q":"For NAND2 inputs 00 in a model dominated by subthreshold leakage, why can leakage be far below a one-off-device state?","a":"Why they ask: every cell in the library needs state-dependent leakage numbers (Liberty `leakage_power` with `when` conditions). Bo will want to hear the physics of the numbers and an efficient way to compute them.\n\n**Opening question.** \"Your NAND2 is idle. Does its leakage depend on the input state, and why?\"\n\n**Answer.** Yes, by up to about 10× in a NAND2. Leakage flows through whichever network is **off**:\n- inputs 11: the output is low, and the two parallel PMOS leak with full V_DS;\n- 01 / 10: one NMOS in the stack is off;\n- 00: **both NMOS are off in series**, and this is the minimum because of the **stack effect**.\n\nThe intermediate node rises a little, which gives the top device V_GS < 0, body effect, and less DIBL on the bottom device.\n\nDraw: the NMOS stack with node X at a few tens of mV, annotated \"top: V_GS = −V_X, V_DS = V_DD − V_X; bottom: V_GS = 0, V_DS = V_X\".\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Derive the intermediate node voltage for two off NMOS in series.\"\n\n**Answer.** Use subthreshold current in EKV form: the gate term is divided by n, and the source term enters at full strength, which includes body effect. DIBL is η·V_DS. Apply KCL at X (top current = bottom current):\n\n```latex\nI = I_0\\,e^{\\frac{V_G - V_t + \\eta V_{DS}}{nU_T}}\\,e^{-\\frac{V_S}{U_T}}\\left(1-e^{-\\frac{V_{DS}}{U_T}}\\right)\n```\n\n```latex\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\eta (V_{DD}-V_X)}{nU_T} - \\frac{V_X}{U_T}}_{\\text{top}} = \\underbrace{\\frac{\\eta V_X}{nU_T} + \\ln\\!\\left(1-e^{-V_X/U_T}\\right)}_{\\text{bottom}}\n```\n\nIf V_X ≫ U_T, the log term vanishes and the closed form is:\n\n```latex\nV_X \\approx \\frac{\\eta V_{DD}}{n + 2\\eta}\n```\n\nWith η = 0.08, n = 1.35, V_DD = 0.9: the closed form gives **V_X ≈ 48 mV**, the exact equation gives **51 mV**, and the full model (including λ) gives **57 mV**. The leakage reduction I_single/I_stack ≈ e^{η(V_DD − V_X)/(nU_T)} is about 7×; the exact value is **8.1×** and the model gives **10.2×**. Without DIBL the model gives only **2.4×**, so **DIBL removal on the bottom device is the main mechanism**. Body effect and negative V_GS on the top device set V_X.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2 - worked numeric.** \"Give me the leakage per state for NAND2 and NAND3.\"\n\n**Answer.** Model at 27 °C, 2:1 sizing (NAND2: N = 2, P = 2; NAND3: N = 3, P = 2). States are listed top → bottom of the NMOS stack.\n\n| NAND2 state | Leaking path | I_leak |\n|---|---|---|\n| 00 | 2-stack, V_X = 57 mV | **33 pA** (best) |\n| 01 | top off, full V_DS, source at 0 | 341 pA (worst, tie) |\n| 10 | bottom off; X passes up to ~0.64 V through the on top device, so V_DS is smaller and DIBL lower | 175 pA |\n| 11 | two PMOS off in parallel (each W = 2 at half mobility = one unit NMOS) | 341 pA (worst, tie) |\n\nThe NAND2 average over equiprobable states is 222 pA. For NAND3 the range is **25 pA (000)** to **511 pA (011 and 111)**, about 20×, and the average is 213 pA. 001/010 give 50 pA (two-stack) and 100 gives 45 pA.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nState-dependent leakage of NAND2 and NAND3; the all-off NMOS stack is lowest, and a single off device with full V_DS (or the parallel PMOS) is highest\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"How do you use that?\"\n\n**Answer.** (a) Liberty stores leakage per `when` state, and power tools weight the states by signal probability. (b) **Input-vector control**: in sleep mode, park the inputs in the minimum-leakage state (for example, drive NAND inputs to 0). (c) Pin order affects leakage as well as delay, as the 01/10 asymmetry shows. (d) **Stack forcing**: replace one off-prone device of width W with two series devices of width W/2 each. Input capacitance stays the same and leakage falls several-fold, at the cost of delay, so it suits non-critical paths.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 - What if** \"the die runs at 125 °C instead of 25 °C?\"\n\n**Answer.** Three effects stack up:\n1. The subthreshold slope degrades: S = n·U_T·ln10 goes from **80.5 mV/dec to 107 mV/dec**.\n2. V_t drops by about 0.8 mV/K.\n3. Mobility falls as T^−1.5. This partly offsets the first two, and it also slows I_on (×0.83 in the model).\n\nThe model gives **×44** for a single off device, which is about a **doubling every 18 °C**. The two-stack goes up ×72, and the HVT device ×97, because a higher-V_t device has more exponent to lose. Temperature inversion and self-heating make leakage sign-off at the hot corner (added).\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nSubthreshold leakage vs temperature on a log axis for a single device, a two-stack and an HVT device\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if** \"you swap to HVT (+100 mV)?\"\n\n**Answer.** Leakage falls by e^{0.1/(nU_T)} = **17.5×** (the model agrees), while I_on drops to **0.70×** in the model. Real libraries typically quote about 10× less leakage and about 15-30% more delay per Vt step (added), because the model's square-law-like strong inversion overstates the I_on loss. The use is **multi-Vt optimization**: swap non-critical cells to HVT after timing closure, and keep LVT/SVT on critical paths.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"How would you characterize leakage for a whole library with minimal compute?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n1. **DC operating point only.** Leakage is static, so each state needs one `.op`, not a transient. That is milliseconds per state.\n2. **Enumerate states sparingly.** 2ⁿ states per cell, but you can **exploit symmetry**: equivalent pins with identical stacks share results, and states where the off network is identical give the same leakage (in NAND3, 001 and 010 are both two-stacks with identical numbers here).\n3. **Batch.** Put all states of a cell, or many cells, in one netlist as independent instances, or use `.alter`/`.data` sweeps over corner and temperature. One simulator launch then covers hundreds of op points, which amortizes the parse and model-load overhead that dominates such short runs.\n4. **Exploit structure.** Leakage = the sum over off sub-networks. Characterize **primitives** (single off device at each V_DS, 2-stack, 3-stack, PMOS parallel) once per Vt/corner/temperature, then compose cell leakage from lookup. A full simulation is needed only for cells with odd topologies such as transmission gates and feedback.\n5. **Fit temperature and V_DD.** Simulate 3-4 temperatures and fit log(I) against T (it is smooth) instead of simulating every point.\n6. **Validate.** Full-sim a random sample of cells and states at the extreme corners (FF, 125 °C, high V_DD) and compare against the composed values (for example, within 5%). Also check that gate leakage and GIDL are included, because primitive composition misses gate tunneling from on devices.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"two stacked off transistors leak half as much\". It is 5-10× less, because the intermediate node gives the top device V_GS < 0 and body effect, and the bottom device loses its DIBL. A second trap is forgetting the PMOS network in the 11 state.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nLeakage is state-dependent: an off stack is about 10× lower thanks to V_X of a few tens of mV and DIBL. It is exponential in temperature (about 2× per 18 °C here) and in V_t (about 17× per 100 mV). Characterize it with batched DC op points and composed primitives.\n\n</div>\n\n---","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Treat the intermediate node as an unknown voltage fixed by current balance.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Two off devices simply divide a fixed leakage current by two","The floating stack node changes VGS, body bias and VDS, suppressing the off-stack currents","The PMOS pull-up removes the need to satisfy KCL in the NMOS stack","The output voltage becomes zero, eliminating all drain bias"],"ans":1,"why":["Subthreshold leakage depends exponentially on terminal biases, not a fixed two-resistor division.","The intermediate-node equilibrium reduces effective drive and DIBL; leakage reduction is nonlinear.","Steady-state current continuity still determines the stack-node voltage.","With both inputs low the pull-up holds the NAND output high, not zero."],"ex":"The intermediate-node equilibrium reduces effective drive and DIBL; leakage reduction is nonlinear.","oral":"B4. Leakage in gates: stack effect, state dependence, temperature, and how to characterize it cheaply"},{"id":"V114","k":"md-drills-b5-domino-logic-precharge-evaluate-keeper-window-charge-sharing","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Domino keeper sizing window","q":"At the evaluation decision voltage, I_on = 33 µA and keeper current is 13.2 µA per unit width. At the retained-high voltage, keeper current is 5 µA per unit width and disturbance current is 1.21 µA. Require I_on ≥ 3Ikeeper during evaluation. Which width satisfies both bounds?","a":"Why they ask: custom datapath and ROM/register-file read paths use dynamic nodes. Bo works next to the custom ROM team, and keeper sizing and charge sharing are the classic drill topics.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nFooted domino AND2 with a weak PMOS keeper driven from the output; X is the charge-sharing node\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Draw a domino gate and walk me through one clock cycle.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **CLK = 0 (precharge):** the precharge PMOS pulls the dynamic node to V_DD and the foot NMOS is off. OUT = 0 after the static inverter.\n- **CLK = 1 (evaluate):** the precharge is off and the foot is on. If the pull-down network conducts (A·B = 1), the dynamic node discharges and OUT rises. Otherwise the node **floats high**, held only by its capacitance and the keeper.\n- The node can only fall during evaluate, so each domino output makes **at most one 0→1 transition per cycle**. Monotonic-rising outputs are what make cascading legal.\n\nDraw: the schematic above plus the waveform below.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nTwo cycles: in cycle 1 only A rises, so charge sharing with X dips the dynamic node; in cycle 2 A and B rise and the node evaluates\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why is it fast, and what does it cost?\"\n\n**Answer.** Fast because:\n- there is no PMOS in the logic network, so input capacitance is only the NMOS (lower logical effort);\n- the output inverter can be **HI-skewed** (strong PMOS) to fire early;\n- only one edge type has to be fast.\n\nCosts:\n- **clock load** and α = 1 on precharged nodes, hence high power;\n- **noise sensitivity** (floating node);\n- **charge sharing**;\n- the **monotonicity** requirement;\n- a **keeper** is needed.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2 - keeper window, worked numeric.** \"How do you size the keeper?\"\n\n**Answer.** The keeper is a weak PMOS whose gate is driven by OUT. It must satisfy **I_on(pull-down) > I_keeper > n·I_off (plus noise)**.\n\nSetup: an 8-input domino OR, pull-down NMOS W = 2, foot W = 4. Model numbers:\n- One on path at V_dyn = 0.45 V (the trip point of the output inverter): **I_on = 33.0 µA**.\n- Keeper per unit W: 13.2 µA at V_dyn = 0.45 V, 5.0 µA at V_dyn = 0.8 V.\n- 8 off inputs at V_dyn = 0.8 V: n·I_off = 0.34 nA (clean), 23.7 nA (0.15 V input noise), **1.21 µA (0.3 V input noise)**.\n\nBounds:\n- **Lower bound** (hold V_dyn ≥ 0.8 V against 0.3 V noise): W_k ≥ 1.21 µA / 5.0 µA = **0.24**.\n- **Upper bound** (contention rule, I_on ≥ 3·I_k at V_M): W_k ≤ 33.0 / (3 × 13.2) = **0.83**.\n- Pick **W_k = 0.3**: I_on/I_k = 8.3, and evaluate delay is 56 ps against 52 ps with no keeper.\n\nPure leakage alone would need only W_k ≈ 10⁻⁴. **Noise and the hot corner set the floor**, not leakage at 27 °C (leakage at 125 °C is ×44).\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: hold with noisy off inputs; no keeper or an undersized keeper lets the node cross V_M, while W_k = 0.3 holds at 0.82 V. Right: evaluate; a stronger keeper slows the fall and W_k = 3 never switches\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 - What if** \"the keeper is too weak or too strong?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Too weak** (W_k = 0.05): the node crosses V_M at **2.7 ns** (1.5 ns with no keeper), so OUT glitches high. That is a functional failure, and in domino it is irreversible within the cycle.\n- **Too strong:** W_k = 1.2 (beyond the 3:1 rule) evaluates in 78 ps instead of 52 ps (+50%). At **W_k = 3**, I_k ≈ 40 µA exceeds I_on, so the node settles near 0.6 V and **never evaluates**.\n\nReal designs use a **conditional or two-stage keeper**, or a keeper with a long channel or stacked device to get very small W without a minimum-width limit (added).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 - charge sharing, worked numeric.** \"In cycle 1, A goes high but B stays low. What happens to the dynamic node?\"\n\n**Answer.** Charge on C_dyn redistributes onto C_X through MNA. MNA cuts off once V_X reaches V_DD − V_t, which splits the problem into two cases:\n\n```latex\n\\text{Case 1 } (\\Delta V < V_t):\\quad \\Delta V = \\frac{C_X}{C_{dyn}}\\,(V_{DD}-V_t)\n```\n\n```latex\n\\text{Case 2 } (\\Delta V \\ge V_t):\\quad V_{final} = V_{DD}\\,\\frac{C_{dyn}}{C_{dyn}+C_X}\n```\n\n- **C_dyn = 10 fF, C_X = 3 fF:** case 1 gives ΔV = 0.3 × 0.55 = **0.165 V**, so V_dyn = **0.735 V**.\n- **C_X = 10 fF:** case 1 would need ΔV = 0.55 ≥ V_t, so case 2 applies: V_dyn = **0.45 V**, right at the inverter trip point, which is a failure.\n\nThe model transient gives 0.77 V and 0.50 V at 1 ns, slightly higher because body effect raises MNA's effective V_t (X stalls near 0.4 V). Redoing case 1 with V_t,eff ≈ 0.48 V gives 0.9 − 0.3 × 0.42 ≈ 0.77 V, which matches.\n\nFixes: precharge the internal node with a small PMOS on X; make C_dyn larger relative to C_X (put the larger-capacitance input lower in the stack); add a keeper; reorder inputs.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nCharge sharing with no keeper: C_X/C_dyn = 0.3 costs about 0.13 V, while C_X = C_dyn drops the node close to the inverter trip point\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if** \"the inputs are non-monotonic, for example a glitch high then low during evaluate?\"\n\n**Answer.** The node discharges during the glitch and **cannot recover**: there is no pull-up during evaluate apart from the weak keeper, which turns off once OUT flips. The output stays wrong for the rest of the cycle. Consequences:\n- one dynamic gate cannot drive another directly. Its output falls during evaluate, which looks like a 1→0 glitch at the next gate's input, so you need the inverter (that is what makes it **domino**);\n- **domino cannot implement inverting logic**. Use dual-rail domino, or push inversions to the start or end of the domino chain;\n- static logic inside a domino chain must be **non-inverting** and hazard-free.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6 - What if** \"you remove the foot (footless domino)?\"\n\n**Answer.** You save one series device, so the pull-down is faster (lower logical effort) and the clock load is smaller. But during precharge, if any input is still high, there is a **direct path from the precharge PMOS through the pull-down to GND**. That causes crowbar current and a degraded precharge. So footless stages need inputs that are **guaranteed low during precharge**. Normally that means a footed first stage followed by footless stages, with **delayed precharge** (each stage's precharge waits until its inputs have reset) (added).\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"make the keeper as strong as possible for robustness\". It fights evaluation and at W_k = 3 the gate never switches. A second trap is quoting the case-2 charge-sharing formula when the pass device cuts off (case 1).\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nDomino means precharge high, conditionally discharge once, monotonic inputs only. Size the keeper inside I_on > I_k > n·I_off + noise, and check charge sharing with C_X/C_dyn and V_DD − V_t.\n\n</div>\n\n---","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Evaluate the retention lower bound and contention upper bound independently.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Wk = 3.00","Wk = 0.10","Wk = 1.00","Wk = 0.30"],"ans":3,"why":["3.00 gives 39.6 µA keeper current itself at the evaluation point, exceeding the pulldown.","0.10 supplies only 0.50 µA, below the 1.21 µA disturbance.","1.00 requires 39.6 µA for the 3:1 condition, above 33.","0.30 supplies 1.50 µA for retention and gives evaluation contention 3×3.96 = 11.88 µA below 33."],"ex":"The feasible interval is Wk ≥ 1.21/5 = 0.242 and Wk ≤ 33/(3×13.2) = 0.833. Both voltages must be checked.","oral":"B5. Domino logic: precharge/evaluate, keeper window, charge sharing"},{"id":"V115","k":"md-drills-b6-crosstalk-miller-factor-coupling-noise-and-fixes","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Worst coupling direction for delay","q":"With equal, aligned, full-swing transitions and the simple Miller model, which aggressor behavior gives the largest capacitive load to a rising victim?","a":"Why they ask: signal integrity is part of every timing and noise sign-off, and a custom-circuit designer must know why dynamic nodes and sense amplifiers are the most exposed.\n\n**Opening question.** \"Two long parallel wires, an aggressor and a victim. What does the aggressor do to the victim?\"\n\n**Answer.** Two effects through the coupling capacitance C_c:\n1. **Delay change.** When both switch, the effective coupling capacitance is **MCF·C_c**: 0 if they switch in the same direction, 1 if the aggressor is quiet, 2 if they switch in opposite directions.\n2. **Noise glitch.** When the victim is quiet, the aggressor injects charge and the victim bumps. This is worst when the victim is floating, as in dynamic nodes.\n\nDraw: two wires, C_c between them, C_g from each to ground, a driver R_v on the victim and R_a on the aggressor.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1 - worked numeric.** \"R_v = 1 kΩ, C_g = 50 fF, C_c = 50 fF. Delay for MCF 0, 1, 2?\"\n\n**Answer.** t₅₀ = 0.69·R_v·(C_g + MCF·C_c):\n- **34.5 ps** (same direction);\n- **69.0 ps** (quiet neighbor);\n- **103.5 ps** (opposite direction).\n\nThat is a 3× spread. Max-delay sign-off must use MCF = 2 (opposite switching), and **hold/min-delay must use MCF = 0** (same direction). The \"×2\" comes from the voltage across C_c swinging 2·V_DD.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nVictim rising with the neighbor switching the same way, quiet, or opposite; the coupling capacitance counts 0, 1 or 2 times\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2 - worked numeric.** \"Victim quiet. What noise bump do you get if the victim is floating, and if it is driven?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Floating:** pure capacitive divider, ΔV = V_DD·C_c/(C_c + C_g) = 0.9 × 50/100 = **0.45 V**. That is half the supply and above typical noise margins.\n- **Driven:** the victim driver bleeds the charge away. Weste-Harris approximation:\n\n```latex\n\\Delta V_{victim} = \\frac{C_c}{C_{g,v}+C_c}\\cdot\\frac{V_{DD}}{1+k},\\qquad k = \\frac{\\tau_{aggr}}{\\tau_{victim}} = \\frac{R_a\\,(C_{g,a}+C_c)}{R_v\\,(C_{g,v}+C_c)}\n```\n\nWith R_a = 500 Ω, every C_g = 50 fF and C_c = 50 fF:\n- R_v = 1 kΩ: k = 0.5, so **ΔV = 0.30 V** (linear RC simulation: 0.24 V).\n- R_v = 250 Ω: k = 2, so **ΔV = 0.15 V** (simulation: 0.12 V).\n\nThe formula is about 25% pessimistic here. The scaling is right: **a stronger victim holder means less noise**.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nVictim glitch for a floating victim (permanent 0.45 V shift) vs driven victims (a transient bump that decays with τ_victim)\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 - What if** \"you double the spacing?\"\n\n**Answer.** Sidewall coupling falls roughly as 1/s, so take C_c 50 → 25 fF, while C_g rises slightly (say 55 fF) as fringing goes to ground instead.\n- Floating noise: 0.9 × 25/80 = **0.28 V** (down from 0.45).\n- Driven (R_v = 1 kΩ): k = (500·80f)/(1k·80f) = 0.5, so the formula gives **0.19 V** (simulation 0.14 V).\n- Delay range: 0.69·1k·(55 + {0, 25, 50}) fF = **38 / 55 / 72 ps**, against 34.5-103.5 ps before. The spread shrinks from 69 ps to 34.5 ps.\n\nThe cost is routing tracks (a non-default rule).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 - What if** \"you insert a grounded shield wire between them?\"\n\n**Answer.** The coupling to the aggressor drops to about 0, so the noise is about 0. The victim's coupling capacitance now goes to a **quiet** line, so C_g ≈ 50 + 50 = 100 fF and the MCF variation disappears. Delay is a fixed **69 ps**, equal to the old MCF = 1 case: deterministic but not faster. You trade a track, and some speed compared with the best case, for predictability. Shields are standard on clocks, dynamic nodes and long analog/sense lines.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"Why are dynamic nodes and sense amplifiers the most sensitive?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- A **dynamic node** is floating or held only by a weak keeper, so a coupling bump is **not restored**. If it crosses the inverter trip point it is an irreversible logic error, as in B5.\n- A **sense amp / bitline** resolves a small differential (tens of mV in SRAM/ROM). Coupling of a few tens of mV between adjacent bitlines, or from wordlines, is comparable to the signal and can flip the resolved value. Hence twisted bitlines, shielding, and symmetric layout (added).\n- Static CMOS nodes are actively driven, so their noise decays with τ_victim, and the next gate's **noise immunity** (it needs both amplitude and width to propagate) filters short glitches.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"List the fixes in order of cost.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n1. Upsize the victim driver (raises k).\n2. Slow the aggressor slew, if timing allows (lowers k).\n3. Insert repeaters (shorter coupled length).\n4. Spacing (NDR).\n5. Shielding.\n6. Move to a different layer, or route orthogonally on adjacent layers.\n7. Timing-window analysis: aggressors that cannot switch in the victim's window are filtered out, which removes most pessimism (added).\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"coupling only matters for noise\" and forgetting MCF = 0 in hold analysis. A second trap is computing driven-victim noise with the floating formula: a driven victim recovers, a floating one does not.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nCoupling scales the effective C_c by 0/1/2 for delay and injects ΔV = V_DD·C_c/(C_c + C_g)·1/(1 + k) of noise. Floating dynamic nodes (k → 0) take the full divider.\n\n</div>\n\n---","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Look at voltage change across the capacitor, not only at the victim.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The aggressor remains at a constant voltage.","The aggressor falls at the same time.","The aggressor rises at the same time.","The aggressor is removed and coupling becomes zero."],"ans":1,"why":["A quiet neighbor contributes approximately one coupling capacitance.","The voltage across the coupling capacitor changes by twice the signal swing.","Equal same-direction swings give nearly zero coupling-voltage change.","No coupling contributes no Miller load."],"ex":"The idealized effective coupling factors are 2 for opposite transitions, 1 for a quiet neighbor and 0 for matched same-direction transitions.","oral":"B6. Crosstalk: Miller factor, coupling noise, and fixes"},{"id":"V116","k":"md-drills-b7-wires-elmore-delay-and-repeaters","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Distributed-wire Elmore delay","q":"A uniform unloaded RC wire has total R=500 Ω and C=200 fF. In the continuous distributed-line limit, what is its Elmore first moment?","a":"Why they ask: past a few hundred µm, wires dominate delay. An engineer who worked on EM flows will also want to see how wire sizing ties delay to current density.\n\n**Opening question.** \"How do you estimate the delay of a long on-chip wire, and how do you fix it?\"\n\n**Answer.** Model the wire as **distributed RC**. Elmore delay is the first moment of the impulse response: for each capacitor, multiply by the resistance it shares with the path from the source, and sum. For a uniform line that gives **RC/2**; the 50% delay is about 0.38RC. Because R and C both scale with L, delay ∝ **L²**. The fix is **repeaters**: split the wire into N segments so each segment's delay is ∝ (L/N)², which makes total delay **linear in L**. Also consider wider or upper-layer metal.\n\nDraw: a driver, an RC ladder with R/N and C/N, a load, and arrows for \"R upstream × C at node\".\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1 - worked numeric.** \"A 1 mm wire with r = 0.5 Ω/µm and c = 0.2 fF/µm. Elmore delay as an N-segment ladder?\"\n\n**Answer.** R = 500 Ω, C = 200 fF, RC = **100 ps**. For an L-section ladder (R/N then C/N), node i has upstream resistance iR/N:\n\n```latex\nt_{Elmore} = \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{iR}{N}\\cdot\\frac{C}{N} = RC\\,\\frac{N+1}{2N}\n```\n\nN = 1 gives 100 ps, N = 2 gives 75 ps, N = 4 gives **62.5 ps**, N = 10 gives 55 ps, and N → ∞ gives **50 ps (RC/2)**. The real 50% step delay is about 0.38RC = **38 ps**. Elmore is an upper bound on the 50% delay in RC trees (added).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Now add a driver and a load.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n\n```latex\nt = R_d\\,(C_w + C_L) + R_w\\left(\\tfrac{C_w}{2} + C_L\\right)\n```\n\nThe driver sees the whole capacitance. The wire resistance sees half its own capacitance plus the full load. The cross term R_w·C_L is why you put **large receivers** at the near end and why the load end matters.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 - worked numeric, repeaters.** \"Unit inverter R = 10 kΩ, C = C_p = 0.15 fF, same wire. Optimal repeater spacing, size and delay per mm?\"\n\n**Answer.** Minimize the per-length delay of one segment, t_seg = (R/W)(W·C_p + c·l + W·C) + r·l(c·l/2 + W·C):\n\n```latex\nl_{opt} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2R\\,(C+C_p)}{rc}} = 245\\ \\mu\\text{m},\\quad W_{opt} = \\sqrt{\\frac{R\\,c}{r\\,C}} = 163,\\quad \\frac{t}{l} = \\left(2+\\sqrt{2\\left(1+\\tfrac{C_p}{C}\\right)}\\right)\\sqrt{RC\\,rc} = 49\\ \\text{ps/mm}\n```\n\nA numeric optimizer gives the same 245 µm, 163 and 49.0 ps/mm. Examples:\n- **1 mm:** 4 segments, **49 ps** repeated against 77.5 ps unrepeated (one size-163 driver).\n- **5 mm:** **245 ps** against **1375 ps**.\n\nRepeaters pay off beyond about **0.38 mm** for this driver. The optimum is flat: ±30% in spacing costs only a few percent of delay, so designers use fewer, smaller repeaters to save power and area.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nElmore delay vs length: unrepeated is quadratic, optimally repeated is linear; wider wire and upper metal reduce the slope as √(rc)\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 - What if** \"you double the wire width?\"\n\n**Answer.** **r halves**, but c does **not** double: the area component doubles, while fringe and sidewall coupling stay about the same. Say c rises ×1.3. Repeated delay ∝ √(rc), so it scales by √(0.5 × 1.3) = **0.806**, giving 39.5 ps/mm. Optimal spacing grows to 304 µm. The unrepeated 1 mm delay goes from 77.5 to 57.5 ps.\n\nThe EM view: a 2× wider wire carries the same current at half the current density, which roughly helps EM lifetime per Black's equation, MTTF ∝ J^−n. Wide wires are therefore common on clocks and long buses, at the cost of tracks.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 - What if** \"you move the route to upper metal?\"\n\n**Answer.** Upper metal is thicker and wider, so r might be about 10× lower (0.05 Ω/µm) with similar c. Repeated delay ∝ √(rc) falls ×√10 to **15.5 ps/mm** (×0.32), and repeaters are spaced about **775 µm** apart. Costs:\n- via-stack resistance going up and down (several vias, each a few Ω or more);\n- limited tracks, and blockage of the layers below by the via stacks;\n- repeaters still sit in the device layer;\n- on very wide, fast upper-layer lines, **inductance** starts to matter, so the RC-only model becomes optimistic (added).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Why does wire delay not scale down with technology like gate delay does?\"\n\n**Answer.** Shrinking width and thickness raises r per µm roughly as 1/(w·t). Surface and grain-boundary scattering plus barrier layers make copper resistivity worse still at small widths. c per µm stays roughly constant. A *local* wire shrinks in length too, but a *global* wire spans the die, which does not shrink, so its RC grows while gate delay falls. Hence more repeaters, more metal layers, and the upper-metal \"fat wire\" strategy.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"wire delay is RC\". The Elmore delay of a distributed line is **RC/2** (the 50% delay is about 0.38RC), and it is quadratic in length. A second trap is saying \"doubling the width halves the delay\": c rises too, so repeated delay improves only about √(0.65) ≈ 0.8×.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nDistributed RC gives Elmore delay RC/2 ∝ L². Repeaters at l_opt = √(2R(C + C_p)/(rc)) make delay linear (49 ps/mm here), and every wire-geometry what-if scales that slope as √(rc).\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Keep the first moment distinct from the voltage crossing delay.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["38 ps","100 ps","25 ps","50 ps"],"ans":3,"why":["About 0.38RC estimates the 50% crossing, not the Elmore first moment requested.","RC=100 ps treats all capacitance as a far-end lump and is not the distributed-line limit.","RC/4 does not follow from the uniform-line integral.","The first moment is RC/2 = 500×200 fF/2 = 50 ps."],"ex":"The first moment is RC/2 = 500×200 fF/2 = 50 ps.","oral":"B7. Wires, Elmore delay, and repeaters"},{"id":"V117","k":"md-drills-c1-static-latch-and-tg-latch-bistability-and-the-write-condition","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Ratioed latch write condition","q":"A storage node X is held high by an always-enabled feedback PMOS. To write 0 through an input path, what must happen before regeneration completes the write?","a":"Why they ask: every flop, ICG and SRAM bit is \"two inverters in a loop plus a way in\", and the write fight is the first sizing rule a standard-cell designer owns.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nPositive TG latch: input TG on when CLK = 1, feedback TG on when CLK = 0; X is the storage node.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Draw a static latch with transmission gates and walk me through how it works.\"\n\n**Answer.** A latch is a **bistable loop** (INV1 → INV2 → back) plus a gate that lets data in. With CLK = 1 the input TG is on and the feedback TG is off, so X follows D and Q follows D: the latch is **transparent**. With CLK = 0 the input TG turns off and the feedback TG turns on, closing the loop: two inverters back to back hold whatever X was at the closing edge. The value is captured at the **closing** edge (here, the falling edge of CLK). It is static because the loop restores the level actively, so it holds forever with no refresh, only leakage power.\nDraw: D → TG(CLK) → X → INV1 → QB; QB → INV2 → TG(CLKB) → back to X; label which TG is on in each phase.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why does a loop of two inverters store a bit? Show me graphically.\"\n\n**Answer.** Plot INV1's VTC (QB vs Q) and INV2's VTC mirrored on the same axes: the **butterfly**. Where the curves cross, both inverters agree, so those are the equilibria. There are three: (0, VDD) and (VDD, 0) are **stable** because the loop gain there is ≪ 1 (each inverter sits on a flat part of its VTC, so a disturbance shrinks going round). The middle crossing at (V_M, V_M) is **metastable**: the loop gain there is |A|² ≫ 1, so any tiny disturbance grows until the loop hits a rail. Bistability needs loop gain > 1 at the middle point. The **static noise margin** is the side of the largest square that fits in a lobe of the butterfly; it is how much series noise voltage the loop can take before it flips.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nButterfly of two identical inverters (illustrative model): two stable points, one metastable point at V_M = 0.45 V, hold SNM about 378 mV.\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Suppose the feedback is not gated, just a weak inverter always on. What is the write condition?\"\n\n**Answer.** This is the **ratioed** (jam / keeper) latch. During a write the input path and the feedback inverter are both driving X. Say X holds 1 and we write 0: INV2's PMOS has its gate at QB = 0, so it is fully on and pulling X up; the input path pulls X down. Write by KCL at X: the input path must pull X below the forward inverter's **switching threshold V_M** (with margin), and then INV1 flips QB, which turns the keeper PMOS off and the loop finishes the job. So the rule is: **the driver plus pass device must overpower the feedback device** at X = V_M. In practice the keeper is minimum width with a long L, and the writer is a normal-strength device.\n\n**Worked numeric problem (hand, square law).** k′n = 200 µA/V², k′p = 100 µA/V², |V_t| = 0.35 V, VDD = 0.9 V. X holds 1, keeper PMOS gate at 0; we write 0 through an NMOS (gate at VDD, source at 0). Require X ≤ 0.3 V (0.15 V below V_M = 0.45 V). How strong must the writer be relative to the keeper?\n\nKeeper: V_SD = 0.6 V > V_SG − |V_t| = 0.55 V, so it is saturated.\n\n```latex\nI_P = \\tfrac{1}{2}k'_p\\left(\\tfrac{W}{L}\\right)_p (V_{DD}-|V_t|)^2 = 50\\,\\mu \\cdot 0.55^2 \\left(\\tfrac{W}{L}\\right)_p = 15.1\\,\\mu\\text{A}\\cdot\\left(\\tfrac{W}{L}\\right)_p\n```\n\nWriter: V_DS = 0.3 V < V_GS − V_t = 0.55 V, so it is linear.\n\n```latex\nI_N = k'_n\\left(\\tfrac{W}{L}\\right)_n\\left[(0.55)(0.3) - \\tfrac{0.3^2}{2}\\right] = 200\\,\\mu \\cdot 0.120 \\left(\\tfrac{W}{L}\\right)_n = 24.0\\,\\mu\\text{A}\\cdot\\left(\\tfrac{W}{L}\\right)_n\n```\n\n```latex\nI_N \\ge I_P \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\frac{(W/L)_n}{(W/L)_p} \\ge \\frac{15.1}{24.0} = 0.63\n```\n\nWith a long-L keeper of W/L = 1/2, the writer needs W/L ≥ 0.32, which is easy. With a keeper of W/L = 2, the writer needs W/L ≥ 1.26. Writing 1 against the keeper's NMOS is the dual case, and it is harder if the pass device is NMOS-only, because the NMOS passes a weak 1 (it stops at VDD − V_t).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"What if the feedback is too strong? Where does it fail first?\"\n\n**Answer.** X stalls above V_M, INV1 never flips, and the write fails. If it is only marginal, the write still completes but slowly, which pushes out D→Q and setup. It fails first at the corner where the **writer is weak and the keeper is strong**. For writing 0 against a PMOS keeper, that is **SF (slow NMOS, fast PMOS)**, and it gets worse at **low VDD**, because the writer's overdrive shrinks while the keeper's V_SG stays at VDD. The inverter's V_M also moves at that corner (a fast PMOS raises it), so you check the ratio against the corner's own V_M. In the model below, the keeper/TG width ratio that breaks the write falls from 2.0 at TT/0.9 V to 1.12 at SF/0.9 V and 0.90 at SF/0.7 V. A ratio that is \"2× safe\" at TT has almost no margin at SF and low voltage.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nX during a write-0 through a TG against an always-on keeper PMOS; the write fails where X crosses the forward inverter's V_M (illustrative model).\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"What if instead you clock the feedback inverter (C²MOS style) or use the feedback TG like in your drawing?\"\n\n**Answer.** Then there is **no contention**: the feedback is cut off, or tri-stated, during the transparent phase, so the writer only has to charge X's capacitance. Sizing is no longer a ratio, the write is robust at every corner and at low VDD, and the cell is faster. The costs are extra transistors (a clocked inverter adds 2 stacked devices), **more clock-pin load** (more gates toggling every cycle means more clock power, which dominates flop power), and a stacked, slower feedback inverter. There is also a brief floating window: around the edge where both the input path and the feedback are off, X is dynamic for a few ps, so clock overlap and skew between CLK and CLKB matter. That leads into C2. Standard-cell flops usually use clocked or TG-gated feedback for this reason; ratioed keepers show up in register files and SRAM-like structures where clock load matters most.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"Why is the latch transparent but a flop is not? And why do we care about the closing edge?\"\n\n**Answer.** A latch passes D to Q for the whole active phase, so a fast path can race through two latches in the same phase. Its timing reference is the **closing edge**: setup and hold are measured there, because the bit must have got into the loop and set it past V_M before the input TG shuts. A flop is two latches in opposite phases (C2), so at no instant is there a transparent path from D to Q, which gives edge-triggered behavior. Latch-based design can use **time borrowing** (data arriving late in the transparent phase just passes through), but it makes hold checks and the timing graph more complex.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6 (first principles).** \"Why must the loop gain at V_M exceed 1? What happens at very low VDD?\"\n\n**Answer.** Near the metastable point, a small deviation v goes round the loop and comes back as A²·v. If A² > 1 it grows, and the two outer points are the only stable states. If A² < 1 (both VTCs too shallow), the middle point becomes stable and the butterfly collapses to a single crossing, so the cell stores nothing. At very low VDD the inverter's gain falls, because when VDD is only a few thermal voltages the devices cannot stay saturated (subthreshold drain current depends on V_DS through 1 − e^{−V_DS/U_T}). Variation also skews the two VTCs, so the **SNM shrinks** and a mismatched pair can lose one lobe. That sets the **data-retention voltage** of latches and SRAM.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"make the feedback inverter weak\" without stating against what and at which corner. The write condition is a KCL ratio evaluated at X = V_M, at the corner with a slow writer and fast keeper (SF for a write-0 against a PMOS) and at minimum VDD. A ratio that works at TT can fail there.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nA latch is a gain > 1 loop with three equilibria; a write must push the storage node past V_M against the feedback, so either size the writer to win at the worst skewed corner or gate the feedback so there is no fight.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Trace when the feedback stops opposing the desired value.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["X need only move slightly below VDD, regardless of the inverter threshold","The clock period must be increased while leaving a failing DC current ratio unchanged","The feedback pull-up must become stronger than the input path at the switching point","The input path must pull X below the forward inverter’s switching region while overcoming the feedback pull-up"],"ans":3,"why":["A small disturbance can return to the original stable state.","Additional time cannot overcome a static equilibrium that never crosses the trip region.","A stronger opposing keeper prevents the required crossing.","Crossing the inverter threshold changes feedback polarity so the loop reinforces the new value."],"ex":"Writeability is a current-balance problem at the relevant internal switching voltage and worst writer/keeper corner.","oral":"C1. Static latch and TG latch: bistability and the write condition"},{"id":"V118","k":"md-drills-c2-masterslave-tg-flip-flop-phases-internal-setup-and-hold-clock-overlap","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Clock overlap inside a TG flip-flop","q":"A positive-edge TG master-slave flop has late CLKB, leaving both latches partly conductive near the edge. Which failure becomes more likely?","a":"Why they ask: this is the standard-cell DFF. They want to hear where setup and hold physically come from inside the cell, and what breaks when the two clock phases are not ideal.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nPositive-edge TG master-slave flop: master open when CLK = 0, slave open when CLK = 1.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Draw a positive-edge-triggered master-slave flop and explain the phases.\"\n\n**Answer.** Two latches in opposite phases. **CLK low**: the master input TG is on, so the master tracks D (master node = D̄), while the slave input TG is off and the slave loop holds the old Q. **CLK rises**: the master input TG closes and the master loop locks the value it had; the slave TG opens and passes the locked value to Q. **CLK high**: D can do anything, because the master is closed. So Q updates only at the rising edge. Each TG needs CLK and CLKB, which are generated by a local clock inverter (or two) inside the cell.\nDraw: D → TG(open on CLK = 0) → INV → TG(open on CLK = 1) → INV → Q, each with a gated feedback inverter; mark the local CLK → CLKB inverter.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Where does the setup time come from inside this cell?\"\n\n**Answer.** At the rising edge the master must already hold the new value **strongly enough that its loop regenerates the right way** when the input TG shuts. That needs D to have propagated through the input TG and the master's forward inverter, and the feedback node to have followed, before the TG closes. So setup ≈ delay D → master storage node (TG + INV1 + feedback node) minus the delay from CLK to the moment the master TG actually turns off (through the local clock inverter). If the clock path inside the cell is slow, setup shrinks. Data arriving just in time leaves the master near V_M, so it regenerates slowly, which is the clk→Q **pushout** (C3, C5).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"And hold?\"\n\n**Answer.** After the rising edge, the master input TG does not shut instantly. CLKB is created by an inverter, so the TG's NMOS (gate = CLKB) stays on until CLKB falls one inverter delay later, and the TG is fully off only after both gates have crossed their thresholds. If D changes inside that window, the new value leaks into the master and corrupts the captured bit. So hold ≈ (CLK → TG fully off) − (D → master node delay). If the internal data path is slower than the internal clock path, **hold is negative**: D can change slightly before the clock edge and still be safe. Many library flops have small or negative hold for this reason.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 (what if).** \"What if CLK and CLKB are skewed, so CLKB arrives late?\"\n\n**Answer.** Then there are **overlap** windows where both latches are partly transparent. At the rising edge CLK = 1 while CLKB is still 1 (a **1-1 overlap**): the master NMOS (gate CLKB) and the slave NMOS (gate CLK) are both on, so a D that changes just after the edge can **race through** both latches to Q in the same cycle. That is a hold failure inside the cell. At the falling edge CLK = 0 while CLKB is still 0 (a **0-0 overlap**): both PMOS devices are on, so again there is a transparent path. Condition for safety: the D → Q path delay through both latches must exceed the overlap time, i.e. internal hold ≥ t_overlap − t_D→Q,internal. Fixes: generate CLKB locally with a short, well-sized inverter so the overlap is a few ps; use **clocked inverters** or **non-overlapping clocks** in custom designs; or make the flop's internal path slower (adds setup). CLK slew matters too: a slow edge widens the region where both devices conduct.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nCLKB late by 14 ps: during the 1-1 overlap both TGs conduct and a D change just after the edge races to Q.\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 (what if).** \"What if you remove the slave's feedback to save area and clock load, making it a dynamic flop?\"\n\n**Answer.** While CLK is low, the slave TG is off and the slave input node is **floating**: it keeps its value only as charge on the node capacitance. Leakage (subthreshold through the off TG, junction leakage, gate leakage) drains it. If the clock stops or runs slowly, the node droops past the next inverter's threshold and Q flips (or the next inverter burns crowbar current while the node is near V_M). So a dynamic flop has a **minimum clock frequency**, it is unsafe for clock gating, scan shift at low speed, or test with a stopped clock, and it is noise-sensitive (coupling onto a floating node). Standard-cell libraries keep it static for this reason.\n\n**Worked numeric problem.** Slave node C = 1 fF, stored 1 at 0.9 V. Fail when it droops to 0.6 V (keep noise margin). The leakage at the start, from the model: 33 pA at 25 °C and 2.5 nA at 105 °C. Estimate the minimum clock frequency.\n\n```latex\nt_{hold} \\approx \\frac{C\\,\\Delta V}{I_{leak}} = \\frac{1\\,\\text{fF}\\cdot 0.3\\,\\text{V}}{33\\,\\text{pA}} = 9.1\\,\\mu\\text{s}\\;(25^\\circ\\text{C}),\\qquad \\frac{0.3\\,\\text{fC}}{2.54\\,\\text{nA}} = 118\\,\\text{ns}\\;(105^\\circ\\text{C})\n```\n\nThe node floats for half a period (CLK low), so f_min = 1/(2·t_hold). The transient model gives slightly longer times (14 µs and 177 ns), because leakage falls as V_DS shrinks. That gives f_min ≈ 36 kHz at 25 °C and **≈ 2.8 MHz hot**. Hot leakage is about 80× larger, so the hot corner sets the spec.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nFloating 1-fF node leaking through an off TG; hot leakage cuts the safe floating time from about 14 µs to about 177 ns (illustrative model).\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"What sets clk→Q of this flop?\"\n\n**Answer.** It is the delay from CLK, through the local clock inverter (to get CLKB), to the slave TG opening, then through the slave TG and the output inverter(s) driving the load. If the master was only marginally set (late data), add the regeneration time of the master loop. In the .lib it is a table versus CLK input slew and output load. Sizing the output inverter trades clk→Q against clock-pin and D-pin capacitance.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Why do most library flops buffer CLK inside instead of taking CLK and CLKB from outside?\"\n\n**Answer.** Local generation keeps CLK-CLKB skew tiny and fixed (one inverter, characterized with the cell), it presents a **single small clock pin load** to the clock tree, and it makes the internal setup and hold independent of how the tree was routed. The cost is that the internal inverter adds to clk→Q and burns power every cycle even when D is idle, which is why flop clock power is a big share of chip dynamic power and why clock gating (C6) matters.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying \"setup and hold are properties of the data path.\" They are properties of the cell's internal race: data path to the storage node versus clock path to the TG actually turning off. That is why hold can be negative, and why CLK/CLKB overlap is a hold problem inside the flop.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nA master-slave flop is two opposite-phase latches; setup is \"data must reach and set the master before it shuts\", hold is \"data must not leak in before it is fully shut\", and clock overlap is the window where both are open.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Ask whether there is a temporary conducting path through both storage stages.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A larger clock period removes the overlap race without changing phase timing.","New data races through during the overlap, creating an internal hold failure.","Late CLKB improves hold because delaying any clock-related signal gives the data more time.","Only output load changes; setup and hold remain properties of the external logic path."],"ans":1,"why":["The overlap is a local same-edge event, so more cycle time does not remove it.","Overlap weakens isolation between input and output at capture.","Internal phase delay can keep the master path conducting after capture.","The cell’s internal clock/data race determines its setup and hold requirements."],"ex":"Setup and hold arise from internal data-versus-clock races. Finite phase skew and slow slew can extend transparency beyond the intended edge.","oral":"C2. Master-slave TG flip-flop: phases, internal setup and hold, clock overlap"},{"id":"V119","k":"md-drills-c3-setup-hold-characterization-the-clkq-pushout-curve","d":"char","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Setup-time pushout criterion","q":"A characterization flow defines setup at 10% clock-to-Q pushout. Nominal clock-to-Q is 45 ps. Which trial passes its delay criterion, assuming the correct output settles?","a":"Why they ask: a library team owns these numbers. They want the curve, the pass criterion, the search algorithm, and how slews change the result.\n\n**Opening question.** \"How do you characterize the setup time of a flop?\"\n\n**Answer.** Sweep the data edge toward the clock edge and watch clk→Q. Far from the edge, clk→Q is **flat** (t_cq0). As data gets closer, the master is only partly set when it closes, its regeneration starts from a smaller imbalance, and clk→Q **rises**, roughly logarithmically. Closer still, the **wrong value is captured** (or Q never transitions). Setup time is defined as the data-to-clock offset where clk→Q has **pushed out by a fixed criterion, typically 5-10 %** of t_cq0, not the hard failure point. Hold is the same sweep with the data changing after the edge. You find each point by bisection in SPICE, per (data slew, clock slew) pair, per arc (rise/fall), per corner.\nDraw: x-axis = setup offset, y-axis = clk→Q; flat, then a knee rising, then a vertical asymptote/fail region; a dashed line at 1.1·t_cq0; mark where they cross.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nBehavioral flop model: clk→Q vs data offset with a 10 % pushout criterion for nominal, slow data slew and slow clock slew (illustrative model).\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why use a pushout criterion and not the point where it fails?\"\n\n**Answer.** Near the failure point clk→Q goes to infinity (it is the metastable point), so the \"fail\" offset is not a usable number, and operating near it means clk→Q is huge and variable. Static timing assumes **clk→Q = the table value** whenever setup is met, so setup must be defined at a point where clk→Q is still close to nominal. The 5-10 % criterion couples the two: meeting setup guarantees the clk→Q that STA uses. Some flows instead use an **interdependent setup/clk→Q** optimization (trading a little extra clk→Q for less setup), but the principle is the same.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Walk me through the bisection procedure.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n1. Measure t_cq0 with data far from the edge (e.g. 1 ns before). Target = 1.1·t_cq0.\n2. Bracket: lo = offset that is known to fail or exceed the target (e.g. −20 ps), hi = known pass (e.g. +80 ps).\n3. Run at mid = (lo + hi)/2. If clk→Q > target or Q is wrong, set lo = mid; else set hi = mid.\n4. Stop when hi − lo < resolution (e.g. 0.05-0.5 ps). Report hi (the safe side).\n\nEach iteration halves the bracket, so a 100-ps bracket at 0.05-ps resolution takes log₂(100/0.05) ≈ 11 runs. My model converged in exactly 11. Watch the pass/fail definition: also check that Q reaches the **correct** value and settles, not only the delay. A glitch that recovers can look like a pass. Repeat for each slew pair, each arc, and each corner, which is why characterization farms run millions of SPICE jobs.\n\n**Worked numeric problem (behavioral model).** Master node X is driven by D through an input stage of delay d_in = 20 ps, then an RC with τ_RC = 12 ps. The master closes δ = 12 ps after the clock edge. Regeneration: clk→Q = t_cq0 + τ_reg·ln((V_DD/2)/ΔV₀), with t_cq0 = 45 ps, τ_reg = 8 ps, ΔV₀ = X(δ) − V_DD/2, V_DD = 0.9 V. Find setup and hold at 10 % pushout (step input).\n\n10 % pushout means 4.5 ps extra:\n\n```latex\n\\Delta V_0 = 0.45\\,e^{-4.5/8} = 0.256\\,\\text{V} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; X(\\delta) = 0.706\\,\\text{V} = 0.785\\,V_{DD}\n```\n\nSetup (D rises): X = V_DD(1 − e^{−t/τ_RC}) = 0.785·V_DD needs t = 12·ln(1/0.215) = 18.4 ps of settling before closure:\n\n```latex\nt_{su} = t + d_{in} - \\delta = 18.4 + 20 - 12 = 26.4\\,\\text{ps}\n```\n\nHold (D falls after the edge): X = V_DD·e^{−y/τ_RC} ≥ 0.785·V_DD allows y = 12·ln(1/0.785) = 2.9 ps of decay before closure:\n\n```latex\nt_{h} = \\delta - d_{in} - y = 12 - 20 - 2.9 = -10.9\\,\\text{ps}\n```\n\nWith a 10-ps ramp instead of a step, the simulation gives 27.0 ps and −10.8 ps. Hard failure (X(δ) = V_DD/2) is at 16.3 ps and −16.3 ps. Note that **setup + hold ≈ 16 ps** is the width of the sampling window; d_in − δ only slides the window.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"Explain negative hold time. Is it a bug?\"\n\n**Answer.** No. Hold is negative when the cell's **internal data path (d_in) is slower than its internal clock path (δ)**. In the example, D needs 20 ps to reach the master node but the master shuts 12 ps after the edge, so D can change up to about 11 ps **before** the clock edge and the new value still arrives too late to disturb the captured one. Setup gets correspondingly larger: the window just slides later. Negative hold is helpful in STA (hold is easier to meet with short paths and clock skew), and designers sometimes add input delay deliberately for that, paying setup.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 (what if).** \"What if the input data slew is larger?\"\n\n**Answer.** The data reaches the storage node later and less sharply. The input stage's delay grows with input slew, and a slow ramp crosses the internal threshold later relative to its own 50 % point. So **setup increases**. In my model, a 60-ps data slew instead of 10 ps pushes setup from 27 to 54 ps. Hold moves much less here (−10.8 to −9.8 ps), because the later arrival inside is partly offset by the ramp starting earlier. In real cells hold often decreases with data slew, but the direction is cell-specific (added). That is exactly why it is characterized rather than guessed. The window (setup + hold) also widens, from 16 to 44 ps, because a slow edge spends longer in the ambiguous region.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 (what if).** \"What if the clock slew is larger?\"\n\n**Answer.** The internal clock buffer and the TG turn-off happen later relative to the clock pin's 50 % point (and the TG spends longer half-on). The closure point δ moves later, so data gets more time: **setup decreases and hold increases**. In the model, a 60-ps clock slew gives setup 27 → 14.5 ps and hold −10.8 → +1.7 ps. The window stays about 16 ps; it just slides. This is why a degraded clock slew at a flop (weak clock buffer, long route) can create **hold** violations that STA catches only if the hold table is indexed by clock slew. It is also why clock trees have tight max-transition limits.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"How does this end up in the .lib?\"\n\n**Answer.** As constraint arcs on the D pin, related to CLK: `timing_type : setup_rising` and `hold_rising`, with `rise_constraint` (for D rising) and `fall_constraint` (for D falling) tables. Each is a 2-D lookup on a `lu_table_template`, typically **index_1 = related_pin_transition (clock slew)** and **index_2 = constrained_pin_transition (data slew)**, e.g. 5×5 or 7×7 points, one table per PVT corner. The STA tool interpolates using the actual slews at the pins. The clk→Q tables (index: CLK slew × output load) are characterized consistently with the same pushout criterion. For advanced nodes, statistical/LVF variants add σ tables for setup and hold so OCV can be applied per arc.\n\n```\npin(D) { timing() { related_pin : \"CLK\"; timing_type : setup_rising;\n  rise_constraint(setup_tmpl) { index_1(\"0.01,0.05,0.2\"); index_2(\"0.01,0.05,0.2\"); values(\"...\") }\n  fall_constraint(setup_tmpl) { ... } } }\n```\n\n**▸ Follow-up 7.** \"Setup and hold are characterized independently. Is that a problem?\"\n\n**Answer.** They interact: a data pulse that just meets setup at the leading edge and just meets hold at the trailing edge may fail when both are tight together, because the master gets less total drive. Characterizing each with the other side relaxed can be optimistic. Flows handle it with margin, with a minimum-pulse-width check on D, or with interdependent characterization. Also, clk→Q at the setup point is already 10 % slower, which STA must use consistently. Either use the pushed-out clk→Q or define setup tightly enough that the difference is covered by margin.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nDefining setup as \"the point where the flop fails\". At that point clk→Q is unbounded. Setup is defined at a small clk→Q pushout (5-10 %), so the clk→Q that STA assumes stays valid whenever setup is met.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nCharacterize by bisecting the data offset to the 5-10 % clk→Q pushout point, per slew pair, arc and corner; setup + hold is the sampling window, and internal clock versus data delay only slides it, which is why hold can be negative and clock slew trades setup for hold.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Calculate the pushout limit before looking at functional pass/fail.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["54.0 ps","49.0 ps","Any finite clock-to-Q value","50.0 ps"],"ans":1,"why":["54.0 ps is 20% above nominal, beyond the allowed 10%.","The allowed value is at most 1.10×45=49.5 ps, so 49.0 ps passes.","Functional eventual resolution alone does not meet the bounded-delay criterion.","50.0 ps exceeds the stated 49.5 ps bound."],"ex":"The allowed value is at most 1.10×45=49.5 ps, so 49.0 ps passes.","oral":"C3. Setup/hold characterization: the clk→Q pushout curve"},{"id":"V120","k":"md-drills-c4-timing-budget-setup-hold-skew-and-jitter-with-numbers","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Compute setup and hold slack","q":"For a same-edge flop path, T=1000 ps, capture-minus-launch skew=+30 ps, setup uncertainty=50 ps, hold uncertainty=10 ps, tcq max/min=60/45 ps, path max/min=780/5 ps, setup=40 ps and hold=15 ps. Which slack pair is correct?","a":"Why they ask: they want to see you write both inequalities without hesitation, sign skew correctly, and know that only setup depends on frequency.\n\n**Opening question.** \"Write the setup and hold constraints for a flop-to-flop path including clock skew and jitter.\"\n\n**Answer.** Let skew = t_clk,capture − t_clk,launch (positive means the capture clock arrives later). Setup (the **max** path must arrive before the **next** capture edge):\n\n```latex\nt_{cq,max} + t_{pd,max} + t_{su} \\;\\le\\; T + t_{skew} - t_{jitter}\n```\n\nHold (the **min** path launched by an edge must not disturb the data captured by the **same** edge at the receiver):\n\n```latex\nt_{cq,min} + t_{pd,min} \\;\\ge\\; t_{h} + t_{skew} + t_{unc,hold}\n```\n\nPositive skew helps setup and hurts hold. Cycle-to-cycle jitter enters setup because the launch and capture edges are different edges. For hold the same edge is involved, so jitter mostly cancels; only a small uncertainty term remains.\nDraw: launch CLK, capture CLK shifted by skew, the D2 window from min to max arrival, the setup window before the next capture edge, the hold window after the same capture edge.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nWorked example 1: max arrival at 840 ps leaves 100 ps setup slack; the min arrival at 65 ps falls inside the 55-ps-wide hold window after the skewed capture edge.\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Do worked example 1.\"\n\n**Answer.** **Given:** T = 1000 ps (1 GHz), t_cq = 60 ps max / 45 ps min, t_pd = 780 ps max / 5 ps min, t_su = 40 ps, t_h = 15 ps, skew = +30 ps, jitter (setup) = 50 ps, hold uncertainty = 10 ps.\n\n```latex\n\\text{setup slack} = (1000 + 30 - 50) - (60 + 780 + 40) = 980 - 880 = +100\\,\\text{ps}\n```\n\n```latex\n\\text{hold slack} = (45 + 5) - (15 + 30 + 10) = 50 - 55 = -5\\,\\text{ps}\\;\\;\\text{(violation)}\n```\n\nMax frequency from setup: T_min = 60 + 780 + 40 − 30 + 50 = 900 ps, so f_max = 1.11 GHz.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2 (what if).** \"The chip fails. Just slow the clock to 800 MHz?\"\n\n**Answer.** That fixes only setup. At T = 1250 ps the setup slack becomes 1250 + 30 − 50 − 880 = **+350 ps**, but the hold inequality has **no T in it**: hold slack is still **−5 ps** at any frequency. A hold violation is a functional failure that no clock frequency fixes (only a different skew, or more delay on the short path). That is why hold is signed off at the fast corner (min delays, FF/hot or cold) and must be clean before tapeout.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"How do you fix the −5 ps hold?\"\n\n**Answer.** Options, in rough order of preference:\n1. **Insert delay on the short path only**: a hold buffer or delay cell (e.g. +20 ps gives slack +15) near the capture flop, where it does not sit on any long path. If it is on a segment shared with the 780-ps path, setup slack drops from 100 to 80 ps, which is still fine.\n2. **Reduce the positive skew**: rebalance the clock tree so the capture clock is not 30 ps late (that trades setup slack).\n3. Use a flop with **smaller or negative hold** or slower clk→Q min (a library choice).\n4. Upsizing logic does not help; that makes the min path faster.\n\nNever fix hold by slowing the clock.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 (what if).** \"Same path, but the skew is −30 ps (capture clock early).\"\n\n**Answer.**\n\n```latex\n\\text{setup} = 1000 - 30 - 50 - 880 = +40\\,\\text{ps},\\qquad \\text{hold} = 50 - (15 - 30 + 10) = +55\\,\\text{ps}\n```\n\nNegative skew eats setup and gives hold. So \"skew is bad\" is wrong as a blanket statement: skew moves slack between setup and hold. **Useful skew** deliberately delays capture clocks on critical paths.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"Do a second example: 2 GHz, capture clock early.\"\n\n**Answer.** **Given:** T = 500 ps, t_cq = 50, t_pd max/min = 400/20, t_su = 35, t_h = 20, skew = −40, jitter = 30, hold unc = 5.\n\n```latex\n\\text{setup} = 500 - 40 - 30 - (50+400+35) = 430 - 485 = -55\\,\\text{ps}\n```\n\n```latex\n\\text{hold} = (50+20) - (20 - 40 + 5) = 70 + 15 = +85\\,\\text{ps}\n```\n\nSetup fails, with plenty of hold slack. Fix with **useful skew**: delay the capture clock so skew = +40. Then setup = 500 + 40 − 30 − 485 = **+25 ps** and hold = 70 − (20 + 40 + 5) = **+5 ps**. Both pass, but hold is now thin, and the next stage's launch is also delayed (its own setup must be rechecked: skew borrowed here is paid back downstream).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Negative hold time in the library: plug it in.\"\n\n**Answer.** Same as example 1 but with t_h = −10 ps: hold slack = 50 − (−10 + 30 + 10) = **+20 ps**. The violation disappears without a buffer. This is why cell designers care about the flop's internal clock-versus-data delay (C3).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 7 (first principles).** \"Why does jitter cancel for hold but not for setup? And where does duty cycle enter?\"\n\n**Answer.** Hold compares a launch and a capture caused by **the same source edge**: if the PLL edge comes 10 ps early, both flops see it early (apart from the small part of jitter that accumulates differently along the two tree branches, which is why an uncertainty term remains). Setup compares edge n (launch) to edge n + 1 (capture), so **period jitter** directly shortens the available time. Duty cycle enters for **half-cycle paths**: a positive-edge to negative-edge path gets only T_high (minus duty-cycle distortion), and so do latch-based designs and ICG enables in some styles (C6).\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"We'll fix the hold violation by lowering the frequency.\" Hold has no T term: it is a race between paths launched by the same edge, so frequency does not help, and the silicon fails at every speed.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nSetup: max path + t_su ≤ T + skew − jitter. Hold: min path ≥ t_h + skew + uncertainty, with no T term. Positive skew trades hold slack for setup slack, and hold is fixed with delay on the short path, never with the clock.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Write required and arrival times for next-edge setup and same-edge hold separately.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Setup +100 ps; hold −5 ps","Setup +100 ps; hold +995 ps","Setup +40 ps; hold +55 ps","Setup +150 ps; hold +5 ps"],"ans":0,"why":["Setup is 1000+30−50−60−780−40 = 100; hold is 45+5−15−30−10 = −5.","The clock period does not belong in the same-edge hold constraint.","Those values result from using −30 ps skew instead.","This omits the stated setup and hold uncertainties."],"ex":"Positive capture skew adds setup time and consumes hold margin. The hold failure remains if only the period is lengthened.","oral":"C4. Timing budget: setup, hold, skew and jitter with numbers"},{"id":"V121","k":"md-drills-c5-metastability-regeneration-clkq-blow-up-mtbf","d":"cdc","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Metastability per decade","q":"A regenerative latch has τ = 15 ps. How much extra resolution time is required when the initial differential is ten times smaller, at the same final target?","a":"Why they ask: it connects the latch's small-signal loop gain (C1) to a system reliability number, and it tests whether you know characterization cannot remove it.\n\n**Opening question.** \"What is metastability and why can't a flop resolve instantly?\"\n\n**Answer.** If data changes inside the flop's sampling window, the master loop is left near its **metastable point** (V_M, V_M) when it closes, with a small imbalance ΔV₀. Around that point the cross-coupled pair is a positive-feedback amplifier. Linearize with g_m and node capacitance C:\n\n```latex\nC\\frac{d\\Delta V}{dt} = (g_m - g_{ds})\\,\\Delta V \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\Delta V(t) = \\Delta V_0\\, e^{t/\\tau},\\quad \\tau \\approx \\frac{C}{g_m}\n```\n\nThe time to resolve to a valid level V_s is t_res = τ·ln(V_s/ΔV₀). It is **logarithmic in ΔV₀** and unbounded as ΔV₀ → 0. Since ΔV₀ is proportional to how close the data arrived to the exact balance point, clk→Q grows by τ·ln10 per decade of closeness.\nDraw: exponential curves of ΔV vs t for decreasing ΔV₀, each reaching the rail later by a fixed step per decade.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: regeneration from smaller initial imbalances takes linearly longer per decade (τ = 15 ps). Right: clk→Q vs distance from the metastable point; each decade closer adds τ·ln10 ≈ 35 ps (illustrative model).\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Put numbers on that.\"\n\n**Answer.** With τ = 15 ps and resolving to V_s = 0.45 V:\n- ΔV₀ = 0.1 V gives 15·ln 4.5 = 23 ps\n- ΔV₀ = 1 mV gives 92 ps\n- ΔV₀ = 10 µV gives 161 ps\n- ΔV₀ = 10 nV gives 264 ps\n\nEach decade adds 15·ln10 = 34.5 ps. If the node slews at about 0.03 V/ps through the aperture, data 0.1 ps from the balance point leaves ΔV₀ = 3 mV and clk→Q ≈ 45 + 75 = 120 ps. At 10⁻⁵ ps it is 258 ps. There is no offset at which clk→Q is infinite with certainty, but there is also no bound.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Give me the MTBF formula and do a two-flop synchronizer.\"\n\n**Answer.** The probability that an asynchronous data edge lands in the effective window is T₀·f_data per clock, and the probability that it is still unresolved after t_r is e^{−t_r/τ}:\n\n```latex\n\\text{MTBF} = \\frac{e^{t_r/\\tau}}{T_0\\, f_{clk}\\, f_{data}}\n```\n\n**Given:** τ = 15 ps, T₀ = 20 ps, f_clk = 1 GHz, f_data = 100 MHz. In a **two-flop synchronizer**, the first flop gets almost a full cycle to resolve before the second samples it: t_r = T − t_cq − t_su = 1000 − 60 − 40 = 900 ps.\n\n```latex\n\\text{MTBF} = \\frac{e^{900/15}}{20\\,\\text{ps}\\cdot 10^9\\cdot 10^8} = \\frac{e^{60}}{2\\times 10^{6}} = \\frac{1.14\\times 10^{26}}{2\\times 10^6} = 5.7\\times 10^{19}\\,\\text{s} \\approx 1.8\\times 10^{12}\\,\\text{years}\n```\n\nWith only a single flop feeding logic, with 100 ps left over: e^{6.67}/2×10⁶ = 786/2×10⁶ = **0.39 ms**. It fails thousands of times per second.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nMTBF grows exponentially with resolution time; doubling τ halves the exponent and turns 10¹² years into about 62 days.\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 (what if).** \"What if τ doubles to 30 ps, for example at low VDD or the cold-slow corner?\"\n\n**Answer.** The exponent halves: e^{30}/2×10⁶ = 1.07×10¹³/2×10⁶ = **5.3×10⁶ s ≈ 62 days**. From 10¹² years to two months. τ = C/g_m degrades badly at low VDD (g_m collapses near threshold), so **synchronizers must be signed off at the slowest τ corner**, often low-V and SS. Fix: a third flop. With t_r = 1800 ps at τ = 30 ps, MTBF is back to e^{60}/2×10⁶ = 1.8×10¹² years. Or use a flop designed for small τ (high g_m/C in the master loop, minimal extra load on the storage nodes).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 (what if).** \"What if the clock doubles to 2 GHz?\"\n\n**Answer.** The penalty is twofold: t_r shrinks to 500 − 100 = 400 ps, and f_clk doubles in the denominator. e^{26.7}/(20 ps·2×10⁹·10⁸) = 3.8×10¹¹/4×10⁶ = **9.5×10⁴ s ≈ 26 hours**. The exponent dominates: losing 500 ps of resolution time cost 33 e-folds; the factor 2 in f_clk is negligible by comparison. For 10-year MTBF at these rates you need t_r ≥ τ·ln(10 yr·T₀·f_clk·f_data) ≈ 511 ps at τ = 15 ps, or 1022 ps at τ = 30 ps.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"Can't we just characterize setup and hold conservatively and guarantee it never happens?\"\n\n**Answer.** No. Setup/hold characterization guarantees behavior **only when the data meets the window**. A synchronous path in STA does. An **asynchronous** input has no fixed phase relationship to the clock, so its edges land uniformly over the period, and a fraction T₀/T of them land in the window no matter how the window is defined. Making setup/hold larger just moves where you draw the line; the physics (a continuous input mapped to a binary output in finite time) guarantees that some inputs resolve arbitrarily slowly. You can only make failure **improbable** (more t_r, smaller τ), and you handle it architecturally: synchronizers on single bits, gray-coded pointers or handshakes for buses, and async FIFOs.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"What is T₀ physically, and how do you measure τ?\"\n\n**Answer.** T₀ is the effective width of the window in which an input causes a slow resolution, normalized so that the formula fits. It is about the aperture width scaled by the ratio of the regeneration time constant to the input slope. You get both τ and T₀ by SPICE: sweep the data offset very finely around the balance point (bisection down to fs or below), record clk→Q versus log|Δt|, and fit. The slope of clk→Q vs ln|Δt| is τ, and the intercept gives T₀. τ is about 1-2 FO4-like inverter time constants of the loop, so anything that loads the storage nodes (big output inverter, scan mux, extra keeper) increases τ.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"A two-flop synchronizer eliminates metastability.\" It makes the probability astronomically small for a given τ, t_r and data rate. MTBF is exponential in t_r/τ, so a slow corner (τ doubles) or a faster clock can turn 10¹² years into days.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nA near-balanced latch regenerates as e^{t/τ}, so clk→Q grows by τ·ln10 per decade of closeness and is unbounded; MTBF = e^{t_r/τ}/(T₀·f_clk·f_data) is managed with resolution time and small τ, never eliminated by characterization.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Subtract the two logarithmic resolution-time expressions.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 150 ps","About 15 ps","About 34.5 ps","No extra time"],"ans":2,"why":["Resolution time grows logarithmically, not linearly, with the amplitude ratio.","One τ corresponds to a factor e, not a factor ten.","The increment is τ ln(10) = 34.54 ps.","A smaller initial imbalance takes longer to regenerate."],"ex":"From tres = τ ln(Vtarget/ΔV0), dividing ΔV0 by ten adds τ ln 10.","oral":"C5. Metastability: regeneration, clk→Q blow-up, MTBF"},{"id":"V122","k":"md-drills-c6-clock-gating-cell-latch-and","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Glitch-free clock gating","q":"An AND-type integrated clock gate uses a latch for its enable. Which transparency phase prevents enable changes from creating partial high pulses?","a":"Why they ask: the ICG is in every standard-cell library and is the main clock-power lever. They want to know why the latch is there, what its timing check is, and the active-low variant.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nIntegrated clock gate: a negative-level latch (open when CLK = 0) feeding an AND with CLK.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Why not just AND the clock with an enable?\"\n\n**Answer.** Because EN is produced by logic clocked by the same clock, so it changes **after the rising edge, while CLK is high**. With a plain AND, an EN rise during the high phase creates a **runt pulse** on GCLK (a partial high pulse that can clock some flops and not others), and an EN fall during the high phase **truncates** a pulse. Both are glitches on a clock, which is catastrophic. The ICG puts a **latch that is transparent when CLK is low** in front of the AND. EN can only pass to the AND input while CLK = 0, when the AND output is forced low anyway, and it is frozen while CLK = 1. So GCLK is always either a full clock pulse or nothing.\nDraw: EN → latch (enable = CLKB) → AND with CLK → GCLK; then the waveforms: CLK, EN changing mid-high, AND-only GCLK with a runt, latched EN changing only at the falling edge, clean GCLK.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nEN changes twice while CLK is high: the plain AND produces a runt and a truncated pulse; the latch defers EN to the low phase, so the ICG emits one clean full pulse.\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why is the latch transparent when CLK is low, not high?\"\n\n**Answer.** When CLK is low the AND output is 0 regardless of its other input, so any change at that input is invisible. That is the safe time to let EN through. When CLK goes high, the AND passes CLK, so its other input must be **stable for the whole high phase**: the latch must be closed. A latch transparent when CLK is high would pass EN changes straight into the AND during the high phase, which is the glitch we wanted to remove. Put the other way: the latch's **closing edge is the rising edge of CLK**, which is exactly when GCLK starts its pulse.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"So what are the setup and hold of EN on an ICG?\"\n\n**Answer.** EN must be stable before the latch closes, at the **rising edge** of CLK: that is the **setup check** (clock-gating setup), referenced to the rising edge. It must not change until the latch is fully closed after that edge: the **hold check**, also at the rising edge. Because EN usually comes from flops clocked by the previous rising edge, the setup path gets a full cycle. There is a catch, though: the ICG sits **upstream** in the clock tree, so its clock arrives earlier than the leaf clocks that launch EN.\n\n**Worked numeric problem.** T = 1000 ps. The ICG is 150 ps of insertion delay above the leaf flops (so a leaf flop's clock arrives 150 ps after the ICG's clock). EN path: t_cq = 60 ps, logic = 650 ps, ICG setup = 50 ps. Slack?\n\n```latex\n\\text{slack} = T - t_{insertion} - t_{cq} - t_{logic} - t_{su,ICG} = 1000 - 150 - 60 - 650 - 50 = +90\\,\\text{ps}\n```\n\nThe 150 ps of tree below the ICG comes straight out of the enable path's budget. This is why enable paths to high-level ICGs are often critical, and why tools clone ICGs closer to the leaves.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"Why does it have to be one cell instead of a latch and an AND from the library?\"\n\n**Answer.** The latch output to AND input path and the CLK-to-latch versus CLK-to-AND relationship are an **internal race**. The latch must close before the AND's CLK input rises enough to pass a pulse, and the latch output must not glitch at its opening (falling) edge while the AND's CLK input is still not fully low. As a single characterized cell, the internal skew is fixed and verified, and the .lib describes it as a clock-gating cell (`clock_gating_integrated_cell : \"latch_posedge\"`) with clock-gating setup/hold arcs. A place-and-route tool could separate a discrete latch and AND, routing different delays to them, and break the guarantee. ICGs usually also include a **test enable** (EN OR TE) so scan can force the clock on.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 (what if).** \"What if the downstream flops are negative-edge triggered, or the clock idles high? Show the OR-based gate.\"\n\n**Answer.** For a clock whose **active edge is falling** (or which must idle high), use an **OR** gate: GCLK = CLK + EN̄_latched. When disabled, GCLK is held **high**. The latch is now transparent while **CLK is high** (when the OR output is forced to 1, so changes are invisible) and closes at the **falling edge**. Setup and hold of EN are then referenced to the falling edge. The rule is symmetric: the latch is open during the phase in which the gate's output is forced to its idle value, and closes at the edge that starts an active pulse.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5 (what if).** \"What if you use a flop instead of a latch to sample EN?\"\n\n**Answer.** A positive-edge flop would update EN_q right after the rising edge, while CLK is high, which recreates the glitch at the AND. A negative-edge flop works functionally (EN_q changes only at the falling edge, in the low phase), but the enable gets only the time until the falling edge, which is half a cycle. The latch is better: it is transparent through the whole low phase, so EN can arrive anytime up to the rising edge (**time borrowing**), giving a full-cycle budget with fewer transistors.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"How much power does clock gating save, and what does it cost?\"\n\n**Answer.** Clock power is P = α·C·V²·f with α = 1 for the clock (it toggles every cycle) on the tree and on every flop's clock pin. Gating a group of N flops for a fraction g of cycles removes about g of the power of that subtree (wires, buffers, flop internal clock nodes), less the ICG's own power and the always-toggling CLK pin of the ICG. Costs: the enable timing above, an extra cell delay in the clock path (more insertion delay and on-chip variation between gated and ungated branches, which hurts skew), and DFT needing the test-enable bypass. It pays off when the group is large enough and idle often enough.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying the latch \"synchronizes EN\". Its job is to freeze EN during the half-cycle in which the AND passes the clock (CLK high for an AND-type ICG), so the gated clock can only be a whole pulse or nothing. A latch transparent on the wrong phase, or a positive-edge flop, brings the glitch back.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nICG = a latch open while the gate's output is forced idle, plus an AND (or OR for idle-high clocks); EN is checked for setup/hold at the edge that starts the pulse, and the ICG's early clock eats into the enable budget.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Find the clock phase during which the AND output is insensitive to enable.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Latch transparent while CLK=0 and closed while CLK=1","Latch replaced by a rising-edge flop without internal timing checks","Latch permanently transparent with extra output capacitance","Latch transparent while CLK=1 and closed while CLK=0"],"ans":0,"why":["During CLK=0 the AND output is forced low, so enable may change safely before being held throughout the high phase.","A rising-edge update can change the AND input after CLK has risen, recreating a partial pulse.","Capacitance does not create a reliable glitch-free clock contract.","This passes enable changes precisely when the AND can create or truncate a high pulse."],"ex":"During CLK=0 the AND output is forced low, so enable may change safely before being held throughout the high phase.","oral":"C6. Clock gating cell: latch + AND"},{"id":"V123","k":"md-drills-c7-level-shifter-dcvs-cross-coupled-pmos-contention-vddl-limits-corners","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Low-to-high level-shifter contention","q":"In a cross-coupled PMOS low-to-high level shifter, an NMOS driven by VDDL must pull down a node opposed by a PMOS powered from VDDH. Which combination most directly worsens that initial current fight?","a":"Why they ask: every multi-voltage chip (SRAM periphery, ROM wordlines, IO, retention) needs low-to-high shifting. It is a ratioed contention circuit like C1, and they expect you to reason from \"which device is on\" to a minimum-VDDL number.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nDCVS level shifter: inputs swing 0…VDDL, cross-coupled PMOS on VDDH; N1 must overpower P1 to flip it.\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Draw a low-to-high level shifter and walk through a transition step by step.\"\n\n**Answer.** Two NMOS pull-downs driven by A and Ā (0…VDDL, Ā from a VDDL inverter) and two **cross-coupled PMOS** loads on VDDH. Start: A = 0, Ā = VDDL, so N2 is on and OUT (X̄) = 0, which turns P1 fully on and holds X = VDDH, keeping P2 off. Now A rises to VDDL:\n1. N1 turns on with V_GS = VDDL, but P1 is still fully on (its gate is OUT = 0, V_SG = VDDH). This is **contention**: N1 must sink more current than P1 sources to pull X down.\n2. Ā falls, so N2 turns off.\n3. As X falls below VDDH − |V_tp|, P2 turns on and starts charging OUT.\n4. As OUT rises, P1's V_SG drops and it weakens, X falls faster, and P2 gets stronger. This is **positive feedback**, and it finishes with X = 0 and OUT = VDDH.\n5. Static state: one side has its NMOS on and PMOS off, so there is **no static current** and the output swings full rail to VDDH.\n\nDraw: the schematic, then a waveform where X sags slowly during the contention phase and then snaps down as OUT rises.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"What is the design condition? Make it quantitative.\"\n\n**Answer.** Same idea as the latch write in C1: by KCL at X, N1 with V_GS = **VDDL** must out-sink P1 with V_SG = **VDDH**, around X ≈ VDDH/2 (enough for P2 to turn on and the feedback to take over). A first-order criterion:\n\n```latex\nI_{N1}(V_{GS}=V_{DDL},\\,V_{DS}=V_{DDH}/2) \\;>\\; I_{P1}(V_{SG}=V_{DDH},\\,V_{SD}=V_{DDH}/2)\n```\n\nThe NMOS overdrive is VDDL − V_tn, while the PMOS overdrive is VDDH − |V_tp|, so N1 must be **much wider** than P1 (and P1 often has a long L) when VDDL ≪ VDDH. In the model (VDDH = 0.9 V, W_P = 1), I_P1 = 13.2 µA. N1 reaches that at VDDL = 0.74 V for W_N = 1, 0.62 V for 2, 0.54 V for 4 and 0.48 V for 8.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: N1 current at its gate = VDDL vs the fully-on P1 current; the crossings are the DC minimum VDDL for each NMOS width. Right: transient delay (C = 2 fF) ends where the shifter fails to flip within 5 ns, including an SF cold corner (illustrative model).\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2 (what if).** \"What happens as VDDL approaches V_t? Will a wider NMOS keep saving you?\"\n\n**Answer.** No, there are diminishing returns. Above threshold, current rises roughly quadratically or linearly with VDDL − V_t, so doubling W buys a decent VDDL step. Near and below V_t, N1 is in **subthreshold**, so its current is exponential in VDDL. Doubling W then only buys n·U_T·ln2 ≈ 1.35 × 25.9 mV × 0.693 ≈ **24 mV** of VDDL. In the transient model the minimum working VDDL is 0.70, 0.60, 0.525 and 0.475 V for W_N = 1, 2, 4, 8: each doubling buys 100, 75 and 50 mV, shrinking toward that limit. Below about V_t + 100 mV, the delay also explodes (0.9 V: 83 ps at W_N = 4; 0.525 V: over 1 ns), and wider N1 adds input capacitance on the VDDL driver. For deep low VDDL, change topology instead: a **current-limited PMOS** (a series PMOS gated by the input, or a header that weakens P1 during the transition), **Wilson current-mirror** shifters, or a two-stage shifter.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3 (what if).** \"Which PVT corner is worst for this level shifter, and why?\"\n\n**Answer.** The corner with the **weakest N1 relative to P1**: **slow NMOS / fast PMOS (SF, NMOS letter first)**, at **cold** temperature, with **minimum VDDL and maximum VDDH**. Cold matters because V_t rises at low temperature, and at low VDDL the NMOS is near threshold, so its current is set by VDDL − V_t and drops sharply. Meanwhile the strongly-on PMOS gains from higher mobility in the cold. Maximum VDDH makes P1 stronger. In the model (SF: V_tn +50 mV and k −10 %, |V_tp| −50 mV and k +10 %; −40 °C: +50 mV on both |V_t|, +20 % mobility, U_T = 20 mV), W_N = 4 needs VDDL ≥ 0.65 V instead of 0.525 V at TT. That is 125 mV lost, purely from the corner. Naming conventions differ: your 627 exam wrote the PMOS letter first, so it called this same corner **FS**. In the interview, say the physics: slow NMOS, fast PMOS.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4 (what if).** \"Why not just drive a VDDH inverter directly with the VDDL signal?\"\n\n**Answer.** When the input is a VDDL \"1\", the VDDH inverter's PMOS sees V_SG = VDDH − VDDL. If that exceeds |V_tp|, the PMOS is **on** and fights the NMOS: there is a large static crowbar current and V_OUT may not even reach a valid low. Even if VDDH − VDDL < |V_tp|, the PMOS is only in **subthreshold**, and leakage is exponential in V_SG, so static current is orders of magnitude above normal leakage. Model (VDDH = 0.9 V, W_N = 1, W_P = 2): input at 0.9 V gives 22 pA (normal leakage); at 0.7 V, 6.0 nA (270×); at 0.6 V, 78 nA (about 3500×); at 0.5 V, 0.61 µA with V_OUT = 24 mV. Below about 0.45 V the inverter does not even switch (input below its V_M). Multiply by thousands of crossings and the IDLE power is ruined. The DCVS shifter avoids this because no device ever has a partially-on gate in steady state: the PMOS gates see VDDH-domain full-swing signals.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nStatic supply current of a VDDH inverter whose input \"1\" is only VDDL: the PMOS is never fully off, and below about 0.45 V the output does not switch (illustrative model).\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"What about high-to-low shifting?\"\n\n**Answer.** Usually it needs **nothing**: a VDDL-domain inverter driven by a 0…VDDH signal works fine. For a \"1\" at VDDH, the NMOS is overdriven (stronger) and the PMOS gets V_GS = +(VDDH − VDDL), i.e. more strongly off, so there is no static current. For a \"0\" it is a normal inverter. The cautions: gate-oxide reliability if VDDH exceeds the VDDL devices' rating (use thick-oxide input devices), and the input threshold is set by VDDL. Libraries still often provide a high-to-low \"shifter\" cell, mainly as a buffer with an **isolation** function and a clean domain-crossing boundary for the power-intent (UPF) checks.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Draw the timing and power issues: delay asymmetry, and what if VDDL is powered off?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Delay is asymmetric.** OUT rising is two steps in series: N1 must first win its fight against P1 to pull X down, and only then does P2 charge OUT. OUT falling is one step: N2 pulls OUT down directly, against P2 (also a contention, but with no second stage). So the rise is usually slower, and both scale with the N/P ratio. The .lib therefore has separate rise and fall tables, and delay depends strongly on VDDL, so multi-voltage STA must use the VDDL-specific characterization.\n- **Short-circuit energy.** During the contention phase N1 and P1 conduct together, so switching energy is above C·V².\n- **VDDL powered off.** A and Ā float or both go low. If both float to mid-rail, both NMOS are partly on and the cross-coupled pair can sit at an undefined state with static current. If both are 0, the latch holds its last state. So level shifters that receive a switchable domain include an **enable/isolation clamp** (force a known output, cut the NMOS legs) controlled from the always-on domain. That is the \"enable level shifter\" cell in UPF flows.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"Just upsize N1 until it works.\" Near V_t, NMOS current is exponential in VDDL, so each doubling of width buys only about n·U_T·ln2 ≈ 24 mV while the input load grows. The worst case is slow-N/fast-P at cold, minimum VDDL and maximum VDDH, and the fix there is topology (limit P1's current), not width alone.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nA DCVS level shifter is a ratioed latch write across domains: N1 at VDDL overdrive must beat P1 at VDDH overdrive, which sets a minimum VDDL that rises sharply at slow-N/fast-P cold; driving a VDDH gate directly with VDDL leaks orders of magnitude more, and high-to-low needs no shifter.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Compare the two devices’ available overdrives.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Weaker NMOS, stronger PMOS, lower VDDL and higher VDDH","Stronger NMOS, weaker PMOS, higher VDDL and lower VDDH","Stronger NMOS together with lower VDDH","Weaker PMOS together with higher VDDL"],"ans":0,"why":["These changes weaken the writer while strengthening the opposing pull-up.","Every listed change helps the initial pulldown relative to the keeper.","Both changes favor the desired discharge.","Both changes help the NMOS win."],"ex":"The first transition is ratioed: a low-domain gate voltage controls a device fighting a high-domain pull-up. State device strengths rather than relying on ambiguous corner abbreviations.","oral":"C7. Level shifter (DCVS cross-coupled PMOS): contention, VDDL limits, corners"},{"id":"V124","k":"md-drills-d1-6t-sram-read-the-read-bump-and-cell-ratio","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Which SRAM mismatch hurts read stability?","q":"During a 6T SRAM read of a stored zero, which relative strength change most directly increases the storage-node read bump?","a":"Why they ask: it is the classic \"which transistor fights which\" problem. It checks KCL, operating regions and sizing intuition in one question.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\n6T cell during a read with Q = 0, plus the AX/PD divider that sets the read bump on Q\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Walk me through a 6T SRAM read. Which node is at risk, and how do you size against it?\"\n\n**Answer.** Both bitlines are **precharged to V_DD**, then WL goes high. On the side that stores '1', BLB and QB are both at V_DD, so nothing happens there. On the side that stores **'0'**, the access NMOS (AX) connects BL (at V_DD) to Q, while the pull-down (PD, gate = QB = V_DD) holds Q at ground. The two devices form a **resistive divider**, and Q rises by a **read bump** ΔV. If ΔV gets near the **trip point of the opposite inverter**, the cell flips. That is a **destructive read** (read upset). The fix is to make PD stronger than AX. The **cell ratio** CR = (W/L)_PD / (W/L)_AX is typically about 1.5-2.5. Draw: the 6T cell, the I_read arrow from BL through AX into PD, and next to it the two-transistor divider (AX on top, PD below, Q in between).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Which region is each device in? Write the KCL for V_Q.\"\n\n**Answer.** AX has gate = drain = V_DD and source = Q, so V_DS = V_GS. That means V_DS > V_GS − V_t and AX is **saturated** (it acts like a diode-connected device). PD has V_GS = V_DD and V_DS = V_Q, which is small, so PD is **linear**. Set I_AX = I_PD with the square law:\n\n```latex\n\\frac{\\beta_{AX}}{2}\\,(V_{DD}-V_Q-V_t)^2=\\beta_{PD}\\Big[(V_{DD}-V_t)V_Q-\\tfrac{V_Q^2}{2}\\Big]\n```\n\n**Worked numeric problem.** Take V_DD = 0.9 V and V_t = 0.35 V.\n- CR = 1: (0.55 − V)²/2 = 0.55V − V²/2, which gives V² − 1.1V + 0.15125 = 0, so **V_Q = 161 mV**.\n- CR = 2: 3V² − 3.3V + 0.3025 = 0, so **V_Q = 101 mV**.\n\nThe EKV model gives **135 mV (CR = 1)** and **89 mV (CR = 2)**. The model's numbers are lower because it includes the body effect and the n factor on AX: AX's source rises, so it weakens.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nRead bump vs CR from the illustrative model, with the trip point of the opposite inverter at 0.9 V and 0.6 V\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Why compare against the trip point of the other inverter? Is that DC check enough?\"\n\n**Answer.** Q is the input of the QB inverter. If Q crosses that inverter's V_M, QB starts to fall, and positive feedback finishes the flip. In this model V_M ≈ **0.40 V**. It sits below V_DD/2 because that inverter's NMOS (W = 2) is stronger than its PMOS. So the nominal bump (89 mV) has about 310 mV of DC headroom. The DC check is **necessary but not sufficient**, for three reasons:\n- The real metric is **read SNM**, which perturbs both halves at once (see D2).\n- **Mismatch** moves both the bump and V_M.\n- The cell has to hold at the **6σ tail cell**, not at the nominal cell.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3. What if CR = 1?** \"Same-size PD and AX. Does the cell still work?\"\n\n**Answer.** The bump grows from 89 to **135 mV** (model). The nominal cell still holds in DC, and read SNM drops only from 216 to 193 mV in this model. But the mismatch tail is now closer: at CR = 1, a 3σ AX/PD skew gives a **183 mV** bump. With CR < 1, AX starts to win, the bump climbs toward V_M, and the read becomes destructive. The trade-off is **area vs stability**: a bigger PD makes the cell larger and adds node capacitance, so foundry cells settle near CR ≈ 1.5-2.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4. What if V_DD drops to 0.6 V?**\n\n**Answer.** The absolute bump shrinks to **41 mV** at CR = 2, which looks safer. It is not, for two reasons:\n- V_M falls to **0.27 V** and read SNM falls from 216 to **149 mV**.\n- σ_Vt does **not** scale with V_DD.\n\nSo the margin measured in σ shrinks. This is why SRAM sets the chip's **V_min**. The cell is the first block to fail as V_DD drops, because its margin is a fixed number of mV competing against fixed mismatch.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5. What if there is local mismatch: PD Vt +3σ, AX Vt −3σ (σ = 25 mV)?**\n\n**Answer.** This is the worst read skew: a weak PD and a strong AX. At CR = 2 the bump goes from 89 to **124 mV**. In the butterfly, read SNM falls from 216 to **128 mV**, and the lobes become asymmetric. The other side's V_M also moves with its own mismatch. Corners do not catch this, because a corner shifts AX and PD together while the failure depends on their **difference**. That is why bitcells are verified with local Monte Carlo at high sigma (see D7).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6. What if you underdrive the WL (0.8 V instead of 0.9 V)?**\n\n**Answer.** AX gets weaker, and the bump drops from 89 to **63 mV**. This is a **read assist**. Its cost is lower I_read, so the BL develops slower and SAE has to fire later. It also hurts **write**, which needs a strong AX. Other read assists:\n- lower BL precharge\n- a higher cell supply than the periphery supply\n- the **8T cell**, whose separate read port (two stacked NMOS) never touches the storage nodes, so read SNM ≈ hold SNM\n\n**▸ Follow-up 7.** \"Besides stability, what else does the read path set?\"\n\n**Answer.** **Read current.** AX in series with PD discharges C_BL. The **weakest** cell's current sets how long you wait before SAE (see D4). Upsizing AX speeds the read but raises the bump, and upsizing PD costs area. That is the whole 6T sizing triangle in one sentence: read stability needs PD > AX, writability needs AX > PU (see D2), and speed needs a strong AX.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nSaying the '1' node is the one at risk, or that \"the bitline gets disturbed\". The disturbed node is the **'0' node**, lifted by the AX/PD divider, and the failure is it crossing the **other** inverter's trip point. A second trap is quoting only the nominal bump. The design case is the 6σ mismatched cell at low V_DD.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nDuring a read, the '0' node sits on an AX(saturated)/PD(linear) divider. Keep PD stronger than AX (CR ≈ 1.5-2) so that the bump stays well below the opposite inverter's trip point even at the mismatch tail and V_min.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Identify the device injecting charge and the device holding the zero.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A weaker access NMOS and stronger pull-down NMOS.","A stronger access NMOS and weaker pull-down NMOS.","A lower wordline voltage with unchanged pull-down strength.","A lower bitline precharge voltage with unchanged devices."],"ans":1,"why":["This makes the restoring pull-down win more strongly.","The access path injects more current while the restoring path removes less.","Wordline underdrive weakens the disturbing access device.","Lower precharge generally reduces the read disturbance in this model."],"ex":"The zero node is lifted by the access/pull-down divider. Read stability depends on their relative strengths and the opposite inverter’s response.","oral":"D1. 6T SRAM read: the read bump and cell ratio"},{"id":"V125","k":"md-drills-d2-butterfly-curves-snm-write-trip-and-assists","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"SRAM static noise margin","q":"For a 6T SRAM butterfly plot, which quantity is the conventional static noise margin?","a":"Why they ask: SNM is the standard currency of bitcell robustness. They want to see you derive it graphically, then show that read and write pull the sizing in opposite directions.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nButterfly curves from the illustrative model in hold and read, with the largest inscribed squares (SNM)\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Define static noise margin for an SRAM cell and draw the butterfly for hold and read.\"\n\n**Answer.** Plot V_QB = f(V_Q) for one inverter, then plot the other inverter's VTC **mirrored** about y = x. The two curves form a \"butterfly\" with two lobes, one per stable state. **SNM** is the side of the **largest square that fits inside the smaller lobe**. It equals the largest DC noise voltage, applied in series with both inverter inputs in the worst-case polarity, that the cell survives with two stable states. In **read**, AX pulls each low output up to the read bump, which lifts the low tail of each VTC and shrinks the lobes. Model (CR = 2, V_DD = 0.9 V): **hold SNM ≈ 366 mV, read SNM ≈ 216 mV**. Draw: two S-curves, one mirrored, then a square in each lobe, with the read curves' low ends lifted.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why a square, and why the smaller lobe?\"\n\n**Answer.** Noise sources that shift both inputs by the same V_n move one curve right and the other up by the same amount. The cell loses a state when the shifted curves touch, leaving only one crossing. The largest shift that keeps three crossings is the side of the largest inscribed square. A cell is only as robust as its weaker state, so take the smaller lobe. Numerically: rotate the axes by 45°, find the largest separation of the curves along the diagonal, and divide by √2. That is how the plot's squares were computed.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"How does a write work, and what sizing condition does it need?\"\n\n**Answer.** Suppose Q = 1 and we want to write 0. Drive **BL = 0** (BLB stays at V_DD) and raise WL. AX now has to pull Q down **against the pull-up PU** (gate = QB = 0, so PU is on). Q must fall below the trip point of the QB inverter. The condition is **AX stronger than PU**, i.e. a small **pull-up ratio** PR = (W/L)_PU/(W/L)_AX. The PMOS's lower mobility helps here. Strength order: **PD > AX > PU**. In the model, with PU = AX = 1 and PD = 2, the full 2-node cell becomes monostable at **WL = 0.67 V**. The **write margin** is therefore 0.9 − 0.67 = **230 mV** of WL headroom.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nLeft: write butterfly at WL = VDD, where only the written state survives. Right: WL voltage at which the cell flips vs pull-up width, with and without a negative bitline\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3. What if the pull-up is made stronger (W_PU = 3)?**\n\n**Answer.** The WL flip voltage rises from 0.67 to **0.835 V**, so write margin falls from 230 mV to **65 mV**. A few σ of PU-strong / AX-weak mismatch then gives **write failures**, and these fail worst at the slow-N/fast-P (**SF**) corner. Hold SNM and the '1' level improve slightly, which is why designers are tempted. The core tension: read wants a weak AX, write wants a strong AX; PU strength only helps hold and only hurts write.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"How do you measure write margin?\"\n\n**Answer.** Three common definitions:\n1. **WL sweep**: hold the BLs at write values, ramp WL, and find the WL where the cell flips. Margin = V_DD − WL_trip. This is the one plotted.\n2. **BL sweep**: hold WL at V_DD and lower BL from V_DD. The BL voltage at the flip is the margin; a higher value is better.\n3. **Write butterfly / write SNM**: with WL at V_DD, the old state's lobe must vanish so only one crossing remains (left panel).\n\nAll three rank cells the same way. Pick one and quote it at a σ target.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5. What if you add a negative-bitline assist (BL = −0.1 V)?**\n\n**Answer.** AX's V_GS and V_DS both grow by 0.1 V, so it beats PU more easily. WL trip moves from 0.67 to **0.56 V** (margin about **340 mV**). Even with W_PU = 3 it moves from 0.835 to **0.735 V**. Costs:\n- A coupling-cap boost circuit whose depth varies with BL capacitance.\n- **Oxide stress**: AX sees gate-source = 0.9 − (−0.1) = 1.0 V, above nominal V_DD.\n- Unselected rows on the same BL see AX with gate = 0 and source = −0.1 V, i.e. V_GS = +0.1 V, which multiplies their subthreshold leakage. This is a slow disturb risk for cells storing '1' there.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"What's half-select, and does an 8T cell fix it?\"\n\n**Answer.** With a **column mux** (bit-interleaving, used so that one particle strike's multi-bit upset lands in different words), WL rises across the whole row while only some columns are written. The **unselected columns in the selected row** have WL high and precharged BLs, so they are doing a **read**. A write therefore still needs **read stability** on the half-selected cells, and a strong write assist on WL (overdrive) makes their read disturb worse. 8T separates the **read** port, but the write still uses the 6T access devices on a shared WL. So 8T does **not** fix write half-select. Fixes:\n- read-modify-write (write the whole row back)\n- no column interleaving on the write path\n- column-selected WL / cross-point writes\n\n**▸ Follow-up 7.** \"Name the other write assists and their side effects.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Cell-V_DD collapse** during write: weakens PU. It must not drop below retention voltage for the other cells on that supply.\n- **WL overdrive**: makes AX stronger, but half-selected cells suffer.\n- **Negative BL**: covered above.\n\nEvery write assist makes some cell weaker, which is why assists are column- or row-local and timed.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"Upsize AX to help write.\" That fixes write and breaks read, because CR drops and the bump rises. Another trap is calling SNM the gap between the curves at V_DD/2. SNM is the side of the **largest inscribed square in the smaller lobe**.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nStrength order PD > AX > PU: read SNM shrinks because AX lifts the '0' node, write needs AX to overpower PU, and assists (WL under/overdrive, negative BL, V_DD collapse) buy margin on one side at a cost on the other.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"A cell must retain either polarity, including the less robust one.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The horizontal distance between curves only at VDD/2","The diagonal length of the largest square in the larger lobe","The side length of the largest square fitting in the smaller lobe","The read bitline differential when sense-enable rises"],"ans":2,"why":["A single midrail slice does not find the maximal tolerable paired DC perturbation.","Using the larger state or the square diagonal overstates the limiting margin.","The smaller lobe represents the weaker stored state; its largest inscribed square gives the DC noise tolerance.","Bitline sensing margin and storage-node static noise margin are different quantities."],"ex":"The smaller lobe represents the weaker stored state; its largest inscribed square gives the DC noise tolerance.","oral":"D2. Butterfly curves, SNM, write trip and assists"},{"id":"V126","k":"md-drills-d3-nor-rom-bitline-i-on-vs-n1-i-off-keeper-window","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"ROM keeper and sensing time","q":"In a constant-current ROM model, CBL=50 fF, desired BL drop=0.1 V, selected-cell current=20 µA and keeper current=18 µA. Ignore other leakage during the selected read. How long does the drop take?","a":"Why they ask: this is the core custom-ROM question for a standard-cell/ROM team. Every number you can derive from C·dV/dt = I, and the hot-corner leakage twist separates a memorized answer from one you can actually derive.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nNOR ROM column: precharge PMOS, feedback keeper, sense inverter, and the three cell cases (selected 0, leaking unselected, unprogrammed 1)\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"You have a NOR ROM column with N cells on a bitline. Walk me through a read and tell me what limits N.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n1. **Precharge** BL to V_DD (PRE_b low), then release it.\n2. Raise one WL.\n3. If the selected cell **has a transistor connected** to BL (contact or via present), it discharges BL with I_on, and we read **\"0\"**. If not, BL should stay high, and we read **\"1\"**.\n\nIn the read-1 case, though, all **N−1 unselected cells** that have transistors leak I_off each. So sensing is a **race**: read-0 must develop ΔV before read-1 droop does. N is limited by **I_on / ((N−1)·I_off)** at the hot corner, and C_BL ∝ N slows read-0 at the same time. A weak **keeper** can replace the leakage, but it also fights read-0, so it must sit inside a window. Draw: the column with precharge, keeper, sense inverter, one ON cell, leaking cells, and a no-contact cell.\n\n**Worked numeric problem.** Given N = 256, C_BL = 50 fF, I_on = 20 µA (slow cell), I_off = 2 nA at 25 °C and 60 nA hot (×30). Sense at ΔV = 100 mV.\n\n```latex\nt_{0}=\\frac{C_{BL}\\,\\Delta V}{I_{on}}=\\frac{50\\,\\text{fF}\\cdot 0.1\\,\\text{V}}{20\\,\\mu\\text{A}}=250\\ \\text{ps}\n\\qquad\n\\Delta V_{1}(t_0)=\\frac{(N-1)I_{off}\\,t_0}{C_{BL}}\n```\n\n- Worst-case read-0 assumes **no** other cell on the column leaks. Leakage would only help it, so the worst case is data-dependent: all other cells unprogrammed. That gives **250 ps** to reach 100 mV.\n- Worst-case read-1 assumes all 255 others are programmed and leak.\n  - **Cold:** 255 × 2 nA = 0.51 µA. Droop at 250 ps is **2.6 mV**, which is fine.\n  - **Hot:** 255 × 60 nA = **15.3 µA**. Droop at 250 ps is **76.5 mV**, leaving only 23.5 mV of differential against the 100 mV read-0. Read-1 itself crosses the 100 mV sense level at **327 ps**. The column fails.\n- **Keeper window:** I_on > I_keeper > (N−1)·I_off. Here the window ratio is 20/15.3 = **1.31**. A keeper of 18 µA (1.2× the leakage) leaves I_on − I_k = 2 µA for read-0, so t = 50 fF × 0.1 V / 2 µA = **2.5 ns**, ten times slower. (The plot shows about 2 ns because its keeper ramps in over the first 20 mV of droop.)\n- **Hierarchical fix:** use a local BL with N = 64 and C = 15 fF. Hot leakage is 63 × 60 nA = 3.78 µA. A 5 µA keeper leaves 15 µA net for read-0, so t = 15 fF × 0.1 V / 15 µA = **100 ps**, and read-1 is held.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nBL(t) for worst-case read-0 and read-1 at cold and hot: no keeper, keeper sized for N = 256, and a 64-cell local bitline\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why precharge at all? Why not a static PMOS pull-up?\"\n\n**Answer.** A static pull-up (pseudo-NMOS) is **ratioed**: V_OL ≠ 0 and every '0' column burns DC current for the whole cycle. Precharge-evaluate has **no static current**, uses only the fast NMOS for evaluation, and lets you sense a **small swing** instead of waiting for full rail. Costs:\n- a precharge phase in the cycle\n- vulnerability to leakage droop and coupling (that is why the keeper exists)\n- precharge current peaks when all BLs recharge at once (an IR/EM concern, see D6)\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Why does I_off grow 10-30× or more from cold to hot?\"\n\n**Answer.** Subthreshold current goes as I_off ∝ exp(−V_t/(n·kT/q)). With temperature:\n- **V_t drops**, by roughly 0.5-1.5 mV/K.\n- kT/q rises (25.7 → 32.6 mV from 25 to 105 °C).\n- DIBL adds more when V_DS = V_DD.\n\nA simple model with dV_t/dT = −1 mV/K over 80 K gives about **80×**, so ×30 is a moderate assumption. I_on, by contrast, is roughly flat or even falls at hot because mobility drops. So the ratio I_on/I_off collapses at **hot**, which makes hot the ROM sensing corner.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3. What if N doubles to 512?**\n\n**Answer.** C_BL rises to about 90 fF, so read-0 takes **450 ps**. Hot leakage is 511 × 60 nA = **30.7 µA**, which is **greater than I_on**. Read-1 now droops faster than read-0 falls (I_on/leak = 0.65). The window is **inverted**, so no keeper size works. You must split the bitline (hierarchy) or cut leakage per cell.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4. What if V_DD drops from 0.9 to 0.7 V?**\n\n**Answer.** Use I_on ∝ (V_DD − V_t)^1.3 as a rough alpha-power law. I_on falls to **0.56×**, so read-0 is **1.8× slower** (450 ps). I_off falls much less, mainly through DIBL. So **I_on/I_off worsens** and the droop accumulates over a longer sensing time. Low V_DD plus hot is the ROM's worst corner, and both effects compound.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"List the fixes.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Hierarchical bitlines**: short local BLs of 16-64 cells and a global BL. This cuts N and C together.\n- **HVT or long-L cells**: I_off drops exponentially and I_on drops only polynomially.\n- **Data encoding**: store each word inverted when that leaves fewer programmed transistors, plus one polarity bit. This bounds the worst-case leakers per column.\n- **Negative WL** on unselected rows, or **raising the source line** of unselected cells. Either gives a negative V_GS and less I_off.\n- A **feedback keeper**: weak, long-L, gate driven by the sense-inverter output. It turns off once BL falls past the trip point, so it only fights at the start.\n- **Replica/self-timed sensing** from a dummy column with worst-case cells, so SAE tracks PVT.\n- **Reference-based differential sensing** (against a dummy BL at about half I_on) instead of a skewed inverter.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Via (contact) programming vs implant programming. What's the difference?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Contact/via programming:** every site has a transistor, and a \"1\" simply has **no drain contact or via** to the BL. Code sits on a late mask (contact or an upper via layer), so **turnaround is fast** and a code change is cheap. A side effect is that unconnected cells add **no junction cap**, so C_BL depends on the data.\n- **Implant (V_t) programming:** \"1\" cells get a high-V_t implant so they never turn on. Every cell is identically connected, which gives a **denser** array with shared contacts. But the mask comes early, so turnaround is **long**. Every junction loads the BL. The code is also not visible in metal, so it is harder to read optically (added).\n- Diffusion (active-layer) programming is a third, front-end option.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 7.** \"Why is the sense inverter skewed, and why is the keeper long-L?\"\n\n**Answer.** A **high-V_M** (skewed) inverter trips after a small droop, so read-0 is detected early. It is single-ended, though, so its V_M variation and noise eat the margin; that is why bigger arrays move to an SA with a reference. A long-L keeper is weak per unit area and has **lower σ_Vt** (larger WL, Pelgrom), so its strength sits more predictably inside the narrow window.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nComputing read-1 with just one leaking cell, or at room temperature. The worst case is **all N−1 others programmed, at hot**. The read-0 worst case is the opposite data pattern (nobody else leaks) with a slow cell. A second trap is \"just add a bigger keeper\": the keeper must stay below I_on, so if (N−1)·I_off ≈ I_on there is no window left.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nA NOR ROM read is a race between I_on·t/C (read-0) and (N−1)·I_off·t/C (read-1). Size N so that I_on ≫ (N−1)·I_off at hot, put the keeper inside that window, and use hierarchy, HVT/long-L cells or encoding when it closes.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Apply charge divided by net current, with the keeper’s direction explicit.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["250 ps","132 ps","2.5 ns","25 ns"],"ans":2,"why":["250 ps ignores the opposing keeper.","132 ps approximately adds the keeper current to the pulldown.","Net discharge is 2 µA, so t=50 fF×0.1 V/2 µA = 2.5 ns.","25 ns is a factor-of-ten unit error."],"ex":"Use the net current leaving the bitline. A keeper sized near the selected-cell current can greatly slow evaluation.","oral":"D3. NOR ROM bitline: I_on vs (N−1)·I_off, keeper window"},{"id":"V127","k":"md-drills-d4-sense-amplifier-offset-required-v-and-sae-timing","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Budget sense-enable timing","q":"Use constant weak-cell current 20 µA, bitline capacitance 60 fF and required differential 100 mV. What minimum development time follows from this model?","a":"Why they ask: the SA links variation (σ_offset) to timing (when to fire SAE), and both come straight from first principles.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nBitline differential vs time for a nominal and a weak cell, against the 5σ offset band and required ΔV, with the SAE window\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"Why do memories use a sense amplifier, and what determines when you fire it?\"\n\n**Answer.** C_BL is large (tens of fF) and the cell current is small (tens of µA). Full swing would take ns. A **latch-type SA** (cross-coupled inverters, enabled by SAE through a tail NMOS) amplifies a small ΔV of about **50-150 mV** to full rail through **positive feedback**. Fire SAE when the **weakest cell's** ΔV = I_cell·t/C_BL exceeds the **SA offset at kσ** plus noise and coupling margin. Draw: the cross-coupled latch with an input pair on BL/BLB (or BL-connected latch with isolation PMOS), the tail device on SAE, and ΔV(t) ramps against an offset band.\n\n**Worked numeric problem.**\n- **Offset:** A_VT = 1.5 mV·µm and an input pair of W/L = 0.4/0.06 µm give σ_Vt = 1.5/√0.024 = **9.7 mV** per device. The pair offset is √2 × 9.7 = **13.7 mV**. Adding latch and load contributions, take **σ_off = 15 mV**.\n- **Sigma target:** with 10⁴-10⁵ SAs per chip at 99.9 % yield, the per-SA probability is 10⁻⁷-10⁻⁸, which is **5.2-5.6σ**. Use 5σ = **75 mV**, plus 25 mV for noise and coupling, giving a **100 mV** requirement.\n- **Timing:** C_BL = 60 fF. The weak tail cell (20 µA) develops 0.33 mV/ps and reaches 100 mV at **300 ps**. The nominal cell (30 µA) gets there at 200 ps. Firing at the nominal time is the mistake.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"How fast does the latch resolve?\"\n\n**Answer.** Once enabled, the latch's differential grows as ΔV(t) = ΔV₀·e^(t/τ), with τ ≈ C/g_m.\n\n```latex\nt_{res}\\approx\\tau\\ln\\frac{V_{DD}}{\\Delta V_0}\n```\n\nWith τ = 10 ps: ΔV₀ = 100 mV gives **22 ps**, and ΔV₀ = 10 mV gives **45 ps**. A small input is slow, and an input near zero (or one cancelled by offset) is metastable.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Where does offset come from, and how do you reduce it?\"\n\n**Answer.** Sources:\n- **V_t mismatch** of the input pair (dominant)\n- β mismatch\n- latch PMOS/NMOS mismatch\n- **asymmetric parasitics** from layout and coupling\n- SAE feedthrough\n\nRemedies: by Pelgrom, 4× the area halves σ. You can also use common-centroid/symmetric layout, offset-cancelling SAs, or redundancy. Limits: the SA must fit the **column-mux pitch**, and a BL-connected SA adds C_BL.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3. What if SAE fires 50 ps early (250 ps)?**\n\n**Answer.** The weak cell has only **83 mV**. After 25 mV of noise, 58 mV is left for offset, which is **3.9σ**. The failure probability is about **5 × 10⁻⁵** per weak-cell read per SA, which multiplied over millions of reads is a yield or field failure. **What if it fires late?** Reliability improves, but cycle time grows and BL energy grows linearly. One column at 100 mV costs C·V_DD·ΔV = **5.4 fJ**, compared with **48.6 fJ** at full swing.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"How do you generate SAE so that it tracks the bitline?\"\n\n**Answer.** Use **replica-bitline self-timing**. A dummy column with **m** always-on replica cells discharges a replica BL. When that replica BL crosses an inverter trip (≈ V_DD/2), it fires SAE. The ratio is set so the replica reaches 0.45 V when the real BL reaches 100 mV, i.e. the replica must slew **4.5× faster**. You get that by using m cells or less replica capacitance. Because it uses the **same bitcell devices**, it tracks PVT, especially at low V_DD, where cell current and logic delay diverge (an inverter-chain delay tracks badly). Using m > 1 cells also averages the replica's own mismatch by σ/√m.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5. What if aging shifts the offset by +10 mV over life?**\n\n**Answer.** **NBTI** (PMOS), **PBTI**/**HCI** (NMOS) shift V_t. An SA that keeps reading the same value stresses one side more, so its offset **drifts** in one direction. With the same 100 mV budget: (100 − 25 − 10)/15 = **4.3σ** instead of 5σ. Fixes:\n- design to the **end-of-life** σ\n- alternate input polarity (input swapping or data scrambling) to balance stress\n- periodic recovery\n- extra SAE delay margin\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6. What if V_DD is lowered?**\n\n**Answer.** Offset (in mV) does **not** scale down, but cell current falls steeply, so the time to reach the same required ΔV rises as 1/I_cell. The latch τ also grows. So the read slows by more than the logic around it, which is another reason replica timing is needed.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nTiming SAE from the **nominal** cell and a **1σ** offset. The design point is the weak tail cell versus the offset at **5-6σ** plus noise. Another trap is claiming a bigger SA always helps: area and pitch limits, plus extra BL loading, push back.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nRequired ΔV = k·σ_offset + noise. Fire SAE when the weakest cell reaches it (t = C_BL·ΔV/I_weak), and generate SAE with a replica bitline so that it tracks the cells across PVT and aging.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Compute charge first, then divide by the weak read current.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["300 ps","3 ns","30 ps","200 ps"],"ans":0,"why":["t = CΔV/I = 60 fF × 0.1 V / 20 µA.","This overestimates it by ten.","This underestimates the required charge time by ten.","200 ps would correspond to 30 µA, the stronger nominal cell."],"ex":"The required charge is 6 fC. At 20 µA it takes 300 ps; sense-enable timing must use the weak-cell budget, with offset and noise already included in the required differential.","oral":"D4. Sense amplifier: offset, required ΔV and SAE timing"},{"id":"V128","k":"md-drills-d5-array-organization-aspect-ratio-column-mux-banking-decoder","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"ROM folding and address allocation","q":"A 4K×32 memory is organized as 512 physical rows by 256 columns. How many column choices per output bit and how many row-address bits are needed?","a":"Why they ask: it tests whether you can turn \"4K×32\" into rows, columns, a mux and a decoder with numbers, and whether you see the delay and energy consequences.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nFor 128 Kb, BL development delay grows with rows and WL RC with columns squared; their sum has a minimum near 256-512 rows\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"You need a 4K×32 memory (128 Kb). How do you organize the array?\"\n\n**Answer.** Not 4096 rows × 32 columns. A 4096-cell BL is about 780 fF and takes about 3 ns to develop 100 mV. Instead, **fold** the words with a **column mux** so the array is closer to square: 512 rows × 256 columns (mux 8) or 256 × 512 (mux 16). Then weigh:\n- **BL C ∝ rows**, which sets read time\n- **WL R and C ∝ columns**, so WL RC ∝ columns²\n- mux ratio, which sets SA pitch\n- decoder size\n- energy\n\nGo to **banks** when the capacity grows. Draw: the array, the row decoder on the left, the column mux and SAs at the bottom, I/O, and the control block.\n\n**Worked numeric problem.** Per cell: BL 0.19 fF, WL 0.3 fF and 2 Ω. I_cell = 25 µA and ΔV = 100 mV.\n\n| rows × cols | mux | C_BL | t_BL (100 mV) | WL 0.5·R·C |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4096 × 32 | 1 | 778 fF | 3.1 ns | 0.3 ps |\n| 1024 × 128 | 4 | 195 fF | 778 ps | 4.9 ps |\n| 512 × 256 | 8 | 97 fF | 389 ps | 19.7 ps |\n| 256 × 512 | 16 | 49 fF | 195 ps | 79 ps |\n| 128 × 1024 | 32 | 24 fF | 97 ps | 315 ps |\n\nIn this model the delay sum is lowest near **256 × 512**. Many designs still choose **512 × 256**, because of energy, SA pitch and layout aspect (next follow-up).\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Where does the energy go, and does the mux ratio matter?\"\n\n**Answer.** Every cell on the selected row turns on, so **all columns swing**, not just the 32 you read. Energy per access = cols × C_BL × V_DD × ΔV = bits × c_cell × V_DD × ΔV ≈ **2.2 pJ**, regardless of aspect. The **useful fraction is 32/cols**: mux 16 wastes 15/16 of it. To cut energy you need a **divided/segmented WL** (only the needed columns' local WL goes high) or **banking** (only one bank is active), not a different aspect ratio.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"Size the row decoder with logical effort.\"\n\n**Answer.** Take a 9:512 decoder (512 rows × 256 columns).\n- Each address line (true or complement) drives half of the 512 ANDs, so **B = 256**.\n- WL load = 256 × 0.3 fF = 76.8 fF. With address C_in = 2 fF, **H = 38.4**.\n- A 9-input AND built as NAND3 + NOR3 has **G = (5/3)(7/3) = 3.89**.\n\n```latex\nF=G\\,B\\,H=3.89\\cdot256\\cdot38.4\\approx3.8\\times10^{4},\\qquad N=\\log_4F\\approx7.6\\Rightarrow 8,\\qquad f=F^{1/8}\\approx3.74\n```\n\nDelay ≈ N·f + P ≈ 29.9 + 8 ≈ **38 τ** (with p ≈ 1 per stage; N = 8 beats 7 or 9). **Predecoding** (three 3:8 predecoders whose outputs are ANDed at each row) cuts the branching per address bit and the number of large gates. The final WL driver is a large inverter chain sized for the WL RC.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"Why does WL delay go as columns², and how do you fix it?\"\n\n**Answer.** R and C both grow with length, so the distributed RC ∝ L². The Elmore value is 0.5·R·C (the 50 % point is about 0.38·RC). Fixes:\n- **strap** the poly WL with metal\n- a **divided WL**: a global WL plus local WL drivers per segment, which also saves energy\n- drive the WL from the middle (halves L, quartering the RC)\n\nYou cannot put repeaters inside the cell pitch, so segmentation is the way to \"repeat\".\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4. What if the spec were 1K×128 instead of 4K×32 (same 128 Kb)?**\n\n**Answer.** Physically it can be the **same 512 × 256 array**:\n- **4K×32**: 9 row bits + 3 column bits, **mux 8**, 32 SAs.\n- **1K×128**: 9 row bits + 1 column bit, **mux 2**, **128 SAs** and I/Os.\n\nThe wide word has **4× the bandwidth** per access and wastes fewer columns (1 of 2 instead of 7 of 8 in each mux group), so energy per useful bit is lower. The costs are 4× the SAs and I/O, an SA pitch of only **2 columns** (tight), and higher peak current per access.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5. What if capacity grows 16× (2 Mb)?**\n\n**Answer.** Don't stretch one array. Use **banks** of about 128 Kb, plus bank decode, an H-tree or balanced global data and address routing, and activate only one bank per access. **Leakage ∝ total bits** no matter how you organize it, so add retention or power-gating of idle banks. For ROM, rows per local BL also set the **(N−1)·I_off** leakage (D3), which is another reason for hierarchy.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6.** \"Any other reason to use a column mux besides aspect ratio?\"\n\n**Answer.** **Soft errors**: interleaving bits of different words means one multi-cell upset hits different words, so per-word **ECC** (SECDED) can correct each one. The cost is half-select during writes (D2).\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"Make it square for minimum delay\" without mentioning that every column on the row swings (energy), SA pitch, or the mux's effect on half-select. Another trap is forgetting that BL delay is set by the **number of rows**, not by total capacity.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nRows set C_BL and read time, columns set WL RC (∝ cols²), and mux ratio sets SA pitch and wasted energy. Fold the words to balance these, then use banks and a logical-effort-sized decoder (here about 8 stages, ≈ 38 τ).\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Split total word addresses into row selection and word selection within a row.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["8 choices per bit and 12 row bits","8 choices per bit and 9 row bits","32 choices per bit and 5 row bits","16 choices per bit and 8 row bits"],"ans":1,"why":["Twelve is the total word-address width, not the row-address width after folding.","256/32=8 selects per output bit and log2(512)=9 row bits; the remaining three address bits select columns.","Five row bits address only 32 rows and do not describe this array.","That corresponds to a different 256-row, 512-column organization."],"ex":"256/32=8 selects per output bit and log2(512)=9 row bits; the remaining three address bits select columns.","oral":"D5. Array organization: aspect ratio, column mux, banking, decoder"},{"id":"V129","k":"md-drills-d6-em-and-ir-rails-droop-decap-black-s-equation","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Average and RMS pulse current","q":"An ideal current is 2 mA for 10% of each period and zero otherwise. What are its average and RMS values?","a":"Why they ask: the interviewer has EM-flow background. They want to see that you separate the voltage problem (IR) from the reliability problem (EM), and that you can do the numbers.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nStatic IR along a 100 µm, 200 Ω rail carrying 0.5 mA spread uniformly, fed from one end vs both ends\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"What's the difference between IR drop and EM, and how would you check a standard-cell power rail?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **IR drop** is voltage lost across grid resistance, so cells see a lower V_DD. That means slower timing and less SRAM/ROM margin. It has a **static** part (average current) and a **dynamic** part (L·di/dt and resistive drop during switching bursts, buffered by decap).\n- **EM** is a **wear-out** mechanism: the electron wind transfers momentum to metal atoms, which grows **voids** (opens, rising resistance) and **hillocks** (shorts). It depends on **current density J** and **temperature**, and it is checked per wire and via against foundry limits for average, RMS and peak current.\n\nSo: IR is a voltage at time zero, EM is a lifetime. Draw: a rail with tap currents and the V(x) parabola.\n\n**Worked numeric problem (static IR).** The rail is 100 µm long and 0.1 µm wide, with R_s = 0.2 Ω/□. That is 1000 squares, so R = **200 Ω**. A total current I = 0.5 mA is spread uniformly along it.\n\n```latex\nV(x)=V_{DD}-\\frac{IR}{L^2}\\Big(Lx-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\Big)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\Delta V_{max}=\\frac{IR}{2}\\ \\text{(one end)},\\qquad \\frac{IR}{8}\\ \\text{(both ends)}\n```\n\n- Fed from one end: 0.5 mA × 200 Ω / 2 = **50 mV**.\n- Fed from both ends: **12.5 mV**.\n- With **straps every 20 µm** (each segment 40 Ω, 0.1 mA, both ends fed): **0.5 mV**.\n- Doubling the width halves R, so the one-end drop becomes 25 mV.\n\nThe lesson: IR drop goes as **I·R/2 for distributed current**, not I·R, and strap pitch is the strongest lever.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"What is dynamic droop, and what does decap do?\"\n\n**Answer.** Model the package and bump path as series R and L feeding the on-die decap C, and apply a load step I. The first droop is roughly I·√(L/C), followed by ringing at f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)) with Q = √(L/C)/R. **Numbers:** L = 20 pH, R = 2 mΩ, C = 200 nF, 10 A step:\n- √(L/C) = 10 mΩ, so the estimate is about 100 mV; the simulation gives **105 mV**.\n- f₀ = **80 MHz**, Q = 5.\n\n**What if decap is 4× (800 nF)?** The droop **halves** to about **55 mV** (∝ 1/√C) and f₀ halves to 40 MHz. The DC level is still 0.9 − 10 A × 2 mΩ = 880 mV. Decap only buys **time** for the regulator and package; it cannot fix the resistive (DC) drop.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nSimulated RLC response to a 10 A load step with 200 nF vs 800 nF on-die decap\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2.** \"For EM, which current do you check: average, RMS or peak?\"\n\n**Answer.** Take a 2 mA pulse with 10 % duty cycle:\n- **I_avg = 0.2 mA**\n- **I_rms = 2 × √0.1 = 0.63 mA**\n- **I_peak = 2 mA**\n\n**Power rails** carry mostly unidirectional current, so the **average** J limit applies (Black's equation). **Signal wires** are **bidirectional**: they charge and then discharge, so the average is about 0, and damage partly heals when current reverses. Their limits are **RMS** (Joule self-heating, which raises temperature and accelerates EM in nearby wires) and **peak**. For bidirectional ±2 mA pulses of 10 % each, I_rms = 2√0.2 = **0.89 mA**.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nAverage, RMS and peak current of one pulse train: each maps to a different EM/self-heating rule\n\n</div>\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3. What if current doubles, or temperature rises 20 °C?**\n\n**Answer.** Black's equation:\n\n```latex\n\\text{MTTF}=A\\,J^{-n}\\exp\\!\\Big(\\frac{E_a}{kT}\\Big),\\qquad n\\approx 1\\text{-}2\n```\n\n- **J × 2 with n = 2** gives MTTF ÷ **4** (with n = 1, ÷2).\n- **105 → 125 °C** with E_a = 0.9 eV: exp[(E_a/k)(1/398 − 1/378)] = **0.25**, also ÷4.\n- **Both together** give ÷16 (0.062).\n\nThis is why EM limits are quoted at a temperature (often 105-125 °C), and why self-heating (RMS) feeds back into the average-current EM rule.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4.** \"How do vias come in?\"\n\n**Answer.** Vias are EM weak points: current crowds at the via edge, and the via-to-line interface is where voids nucleate. Each via has its own current limit. With an illustrative 0.1 mA per via, a 1.2 mA rail tap needs **≥ 12 vias**, so use **via arrays**. Arrays also add **redundancy** (one voided via doesn't open the path) and improve yield. **Blech effect**: if J·L is below a critical product, back-stress halts EM. Short segments between vias are therefore effectively immune, which EM tools exploit.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"List the fixes for IR and EM.\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Wider / parallel / stacked rails**: R ↓ and J ↓, which fixes both.\n- **More straps and vias**.\n- **Decap** near hot spots, for dynamic droop only.\n- Place high-current cells near straps.\n- Spread switching activity: stagger precharge, skew clock edges.\n- Power-switch sizing.\n- For signal EM: non-default width rules, a smaller driver (slower edge, lower peak), or splitting the load.\n\nFor memories, **all BLs precharging at once** gives a large peak current, so stagger precharge or use segmented arrays.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 6. What if temperature rises 20 °C: does IR drop change too?**\n\n**Answer.** Yes. Cu resistivity rises about 0.4 %/K, so R goes up about 8 % and IR drop goes up about 8 % (50 → 54 mV in the example) (added). Hot is also the leakage corner, so static current rises as well. Hot is therefore bad for IR and EM together.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\nChecking a signal wire's EM with average current (≈ 0, so it \"passes\") and missing the RMS/peak limits. Or treating EM as a voltage-drop problem. EM is lifetime, ∝ J^−n·e^(E_a/kT), so 2× current or +20 °C each cost about 4× MTTF.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nIR = I·R/2 for distributed load (I·R/8 fed from both ends); droop ≈ I·√(L/C), so decap scales as 1/√C and can't fix DC drop. EM uses average current for rails and RMS/peak for signals, and Black's equation makes 2× J or +20 °C roughly a 4× lifetime hit.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Average current and average squared current are different operations.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Average about 0.63 mA; RMS 0.20 mA","Average 2.0 mA; RMS 2.0 mA","Average 0.20 mA; RMS 0.20 mA","Average 0.20 mA; RMS about 0.63 mA"],"ans":3,"why":["This reverses the two definitions and violates RMS ≥ absolute average.","Those values apply to a constant 2 mA current.","This incorrectly applies linear duty scaling to RMS.","Average uses duty D; RMS uses sqrt(D)."],"ex":"Iavg=2×0.1=0.20 mA; Irms=2√0.1≈0.632 mA. Use the metric required by the applicable reliability or heating check.","oral":"D6. EM and IR: rails, droop, decap, Black's equation"},{"id":"V130","k":"md-drills-d7-variation-and-high-sigma-pelgrom-yield-math-importance-sampling","d":"var","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"What do zero Monte Carlo failures prove?","q":"For 1,000 independent trials with zero observed failures, what is the approximate one-sided 95% upper bound on failure probability?","a":"Why they ask: every memory margin above (bump, SNM, write trip, SA offset, ROM leakage) is meaningful only at a sigma target. They want the yield arithmetic and why brute-force Monte Carlo can't reach it.\n\n<div class=\"co co-sketch\"><p class=\"co-t\">Sketch it yourself</p>\n\nPelgrom scaling: σ(ΔVt) = A_VT/√(WL)||D_tail.png|Normal tail with 3, 4.5 and 6σ markers, and array yield vs per-cell sigma for 128 Kb, 1 Mb and 16 Mb\n\n</div>\n\n**Opening question.** \"How do you verify that a bitcell is robust? Why aren't corners enough?\"\n\n**Answer.**\n- **Global variation** (die-to-die and wafer; the SS/FF/SF/FS corners) moves **all** devices together.\n- **Local random mismatch** (random dopant fluctuation, line-edge roughness, metal grain) is **independent per transistor**, with σ_Vt = A_VT/√(WL).\n\nBitcells use **minimum-size** devices, so their σ is large, and there are **millions** of cells. A cell fails on **differences** (AX vs PD, left vs right) that corners never create. So you verify global corners **plus** local Monte Carlo at **about 6σ** per cell, which needs special statistical methods. Draw: a normal curve with the tail beyond kσ shaded.\n\n**Worked numeric problem.**\n- **Pelgrom**, with A_VT = 1.5 mV·µm:\n  - 0.1 × 0.05 µm gives σ_Vt = 1.5/√0.005 = **21.2 mV**.\n  - 0.2 × 0.1 µm (4× the area) gives **10.6 mV**, half as much.\n- **Yield:** a 1 Mb array (N = 2²⁰ cells) at 99.9 % yield with no repair:\n\n```latex\nY=(1-p)^N\\approx e^{-Np}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ p\\le\\frac{-\\ln Y}{N}=\\frac{1.0005\\times10^{-3}}{1\\,048\\,576}=9.5\\times10^{-10}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ k=\\Phi^{-1}(1-p)\\approx 6.0\\sigma\n```\n\nFor reference, the tails are: 3σ → 1.3 × 10⁻³, 4.5σ → 3.4 × 10⁻⁶, 6σ → 9.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ (one-sided). Even 3σ, in 1 Mb, means about **1400** failing cells.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 1.** \"Why can't you just run Monte Carlo?\"\n\n**Answer.** Estimating p ≈ 10⁻⁹ to ±10 % needs about 100 failures, i.e. **100/p ≈ 10¹¹ simulations**. Running 1000 samples with zero failures only shows p < 3 × 10⁻³, which is about **2.7σ**. **Importance sampling** fixes this: sample from a distribution **shifted toward the failure region** (centred near the most-probable failure point in V_t space), then weight each sample by the likelihood ratio f(x)/g(x). That gives an unbiased p estimate from about 10³-10⁴ simulations. Related methods: statistical blockade, and minimum-norm (most-probable-point) search.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 2. What if the array is 16× larger (16 Mb)?**\n\n**Answer.** p ≤ 1.0 × 10⁻³ / 2²⁴ = 6.0 × 10⁻¹¹, which is **6.44σ**: only **+0.44σ** for 16× the bits. The tail is so steep that capacity costs little extra sigma. **Redundancy** (spare rows/columns) and **ECC** relax the requirement much more. For example, with SECDED on 39-bit codewords (32 data + 7 check) in a 1 Mb array, a word fails only with ≥ 2 bad bits. p_word ≈ C(39,2)·p², and 32768 words × 741 × p² ≤ 10⁻³ gives p ≤ 6.4 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ **4.4σ** per cell. In practice ECC is usually reserved for soft errors, so this is an upper bound on the benefit.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 3.** \"How does σ map into the memory margins we discussed?\"\n\n**Answer.** Each metric has a mean and a spread, and you need roughly μ − kσ > 0:\n- **Read SNM**: a 3σ AX/PD skew already took read SNM from 216 to 128 mV (D1/D2).\n- **SA**: required ΔV = 5σ × 15 mV + noise (D4).\n- **ROM**: I_off is **lognormal**. One σ_Vt (21 mV) changes I_off by exp(0.0212/(1.35 × 0.0259)) ≈ **1.8×**, so a 6σ low-V_t leaker is huge. Summed over N−1 cells it averages out somewhat, but the weak-I_on read-0 cell sits at the far tail.\n\nMetrics like SNM are **nonlinear** in V_t, so sample in V_t space. Do not fit a Gaussian to SNM and extrapolate it.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 4. What if you combine global corners with local variation?**\n\n**Answer.** Run local high-sigma MC **at** the worst global corner for each failure mode. Read stability is typically worst at **FS** (fast NMOS AX, slow PMOS) and write at **SF** (slow NMOS AX, fast PMOS). ROM read-1 is worst **hot** (leakage) and read-0 is worst **slow / low V_DD** (I_on). Low V_DD makes all of these worse, because σ_Vt stays fixed while the margins shrink.\n\n**▸ Follow-up 5.** \"What breaks the Gaussian assumption?\"\n\n**Answer.** V_t mismatch is close to Gaussian, but the far tail is not guaranteed. Random telegraph noise and aging (BTI) add time-dependent shifts, and layout-dependent effects add systematic offsets. So high-sigma signoff often includes **aging-aware** and **end-of-life** margins, plus silicon V_min correlation.\n\n<div class=\"co co-guard\"><p class=\"co-t\">Trap</p>\n\n\"We ran 1000 Monte Carlo runs and none failed, so it's fine.\" That shows only about 2.7σ. A 1 Mb array needs about 6σ per cell, which takes importance sampling or a similar method. Another trap is using global corners alone for a bitcell, whose failures come from **mismatch between neighbouring devices**.\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"co co-core\"><p class=\"co-t\">One-line takeaway</p>\n\nσ_Vt = A_VT/√(WL) makes bitcells the worst-matched devices on the chip. N cells at yield Y need p ≤ −ln Y/N per cell (about 6σ for 1 Mb, +0.44σ for 16×), which you verify with importance sampling and recover with redundancy or ECC.\n\n</div>","src":"Drill Book","hint":"Use a confidence bound rather than replacing the unknown probability with the observed count.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["0","10⁻⁹","10⁻⁶","3 × 10⁻³"],"ans":3,"why":["No observed failures does not prove zero failure probability.","A billion-to-one claim needs far stronger evidence.","One thousand trials cannot establish a part-per-million bound.","The rule of three gives approximately 3/n."],"ex":"The exact bound is 1 − 0.05^(1/1000) ≈ 0.002991. This does not certify the roughly 10⁻⁹ per-cell target of a large unrepaired array.","oral":"D7. Variation and high sigma: Pelgrom, yield math, importance sampling"},{"id":"V131","k":"md-eecs427-in-a-nand2-with-a-on-the-top-nmos-what-is-the-internal-node-voltage-when-a-1-b-0-and-why","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND internal-node voltage","q":"In a NAND2, upper NMOS A is on, lower NMOS B is off and the output is high. In the first-order pass-transistor model, where does the internal node charge toward?","a":"→ V_DD − V_th. The top nMOS conducts from Y = V_DD down to the internal node, but an nMOS stops passing once V_GS falls to V_T. This stored charge is the reason the next falling transition is slower when B arrives last.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Write VGS for the upper device as the internal node rises.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Ground, because the lower device is off","About VDD−Vth, because the upper NMOS loses gate overdrive as its source rises","VDD+Vth, because the gate bootstraps the node","Exactly VDD immediately, independent of threshold and history"],"ans":1,"why":["An off lower NMOS does not provide a strong clamp to ground.","The upper NMOS acts as a source follower; body effect and leakage refine this approximate level.","There is no boosted gate drive or bootstrap in the stated circuit.","Finite overdrive, capacitance and later leakage equilibration preclude this immediate ideal behavior."],"ex":"The upper NMOS acts as a source follower; body effect and leakage refine this approximate level.","oral":"In a NAND2 with A on the top nMOS, what is the internal node voltage when A = 1, B = 0, and why?"},{"id":"V132","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-a-transmission-gate-tristate-called-nonrestoring-and-when-is-it-acceptable","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Nonrestoring transmission-gate logic","q":"Why is a transmission-gate path called nonrestoring, and where is its use most defensible?","a":"→ Its output is a resistive copy of its input with no gain (unity VTC slope), so noise passes through. It is fine inside a cell when a restoring inverter follows. Avoid long TG chains.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Ask whether the path has a steep logic transfer curve or merely passes voltage.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It inverts the signal with high gain; add another TG to cancel the inversion","It removes input noise by regeneration; cascade indefinitely without buffers","It provides no voltage gain to reject noise; use a restoring stage after a bounded pass path","It cannot conduct a logic 1 under any condition; use only constant-zero signals"],"ans":2,"why":["A TG does not invert or provide inverter-like gain.","Regeneration requires active gain and feedback or restoring logic, not passive transmission.","A resistive pass path transmits noise rather than regenerating logic levels.","A complementary TG can pass both rails."],"ex":"A TG can be useful inside a cell when followed by a restoring inverter; long unbuffered chains accumulate resistance and transmit noise.","oral":"Why is a transmission-gate tristate called nonrestoring, and when is it acceptable?"},{"id":"V133","k":"md-eecs427-size-a-nor3-for-a-2-1-reference-inverter-and-give-its-logical-effort","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Size a NOR3 for matched drive","q":"Using R ∝ 1/W and a reference inverter with Wn = 1, Wp = 2, how should a NOR3 be sized to match worst-case pull-up and pull-down resistance, and what is each input’s logical effort?","a":"→ Three series pMOS of width 6 each and three parallel nMOS of width 1 each. Cin = 7, g = 7/3.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Identify which network has three devices in series.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Each NMOS 3, each PMOS 2; g = 5/3.","Each NMOS 1, each PMOS 6; g = 7/3.","Each NMOS 3, each PMOS 6; g = 3.","Each NMOS 1, each PMOS 2; g = 1."],"ans":1,"why":["This sizes the wrong transistor stack.","Three series PMOS devices each need three times the reference width; one NMOS can discharge.","The NMOS network is unnecessarily tripled and its input effort is not the matched-drive minimum.","The PMOS stack would have three times the reference pull-up resistance."],"ex":"Cin per NOR3 input = 1 + 6 = 7 width units; the reference input is 1 + 2 = 3.","oral":"Size a NOR3 for a 2:1 reference inverter, and give its logical effort."},{"id":"V134","k":"md-eecs427-same-nand2-and-nor2-but-referenced-to-a-1-1-inverter","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Logical effort with equal-strength devices","q":"With a reference inverter N1/P1 and each network matched to its own reference resistance, what is the logical effort of NAND2 and NOR2 under the linear-width capacitance model?","a":"→ NAND2: nMOS 2, 2 and pMOS 1, 1. NOR2: pMOS 2, 2 and nMOS 1, 1. Both have Cin = 3 and g = 3/2 relative to the 1:1 inverter (Cin = 2).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Normalize each gate’s input capacitance to the specified inverter, not a memorized one.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Both are 3/2","NAND2 is 4/3 and NOR2 is 5/3","Both are 1","NAND2 is 2 and NOR2 is 1/2"],"ans":0,"why":["NAND2 uses N2/P1 and NOR2 N1/P2, so each input has 3 units versus 2 in the reference.","Those are the common N1/P2-reference results, not the stated N1/P1 case.","Each two-input gate needs greater per-input capacitance to match its series path.","Neither value follows from the stated input-capacitance ratios."],"ex":"NAND2 uses N2/P1 and NOR2 N1/P2, so each input has 3 units versus 2 in the reference.","oral":"Same NAND2 and NOR2 but referenced to a 1:1 inverter?"},{"id":"V135","k":"md-eecs427-find-a-common-euler-path-for-ab-c","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Common Euler ordering for AOI21","q":"For NOT(AB+C), the NMOS graph has A:OUT-x, B:x-GND, C:OUT-GND. The PMOS graph has A and B:VDD-y, and C:y-OUT. Which proposed traversal pair uses each edge once with common input order A-B-C?","a":"→ Pull-down edges: A from OUT to x, B from x to GND, C from OUT to GND. Pull-up (dual, (A ∥ B) in series with C): A from V_DD to y, B from V_DD to y, C from y to OUT. The order **A, B, C** works in both: nMOS OUT -A- x -B- GND -C- OUT, and pMOS y -A- V_DD -B- y -C- OUT. That gives one unbroken strip per network.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Check both endpoints of every named transistor edge.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["NMOS OUT-A-x-B-GND-C-OUT; PMOS VDD-A-y-B-OUT-C-VDD","NMOS OUT-A-x-B-GND; PMOS y-A-VDD-B-y-C-OUT","NMOS OUT-A-x-B-GND-C-OUT; PMOS y-A-VDD-B-y-C-OUT","NMOS OUT-A-x-C-GND-B-OUT; PMOS y-A-VDD-B-y-C-OUT"],"ans":2,"why":["B connects y to VDD, not y to OUT.","The NMOS traversal omits C.","Each step follows a defined edge and both sequences use A, B and C once.","There is no C edge from x to GND or B edge from GND to OUT."],"ex":"A common Euler order supports continuous diffusion in both networks; verify connectivity rather than input order alone.","oral":"Find a common Euler path for ¬(A·B + C)."},{"id":"V136","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-the-antenna-ratio-and-name-two-fixes","d":"flow","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Repair an antenna-rule violation","q":"A gate-connected conductor accumulates fabrication charge and violates an antenna rule. Which pair of remedies directly targets this risk?","a":"→ (A_poly + A_M1 + …)/A_gate_ox. Fix with a metal jumper to a higher layer near the gate, or with an antenna (reverse-biased) diode on the net.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"The damage mechanism occurs during fabrication, before normal operation.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A larger output capacitor and a weaker driver to slow the net during operation.","An extra receiving gate with unverified oxide area and no foundry antenna-rule recheck.","An approved antenna diode and a process-appropriate metal jumper near the gate.","A lower functional supply and a longer setup-time allowance."],"ans":2,"why":["Operational slew is distinct from fabrication charge collection.","Adding gate area may affect a ratio, but it is not an approved blanket fix without rule and functional verification.","The diode supplies a discharge path; the jumper changes conductor exposure during processing.","Fabrication exposure is not removed by changing the later operating conditions."],"ex":"Antenna checks relate exposed conductor geometry to sensitive gate oxide under foundry-specific rules; use process-approved fixes.","oral":"What is the antenna ratio, and name two fixes."},{"id":"V137","k":"md-eecs427-what-causes-latch-up-and-what-layout-practices-prevent-it","d":"var","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Well taps and latch-up","q":"How do nearby well/substrate taps and guard rings reduce latch-up susceptibility in bulk CMOS?","a":"→ The parasitic PNP/NPN thyristor is triggered when current through R_well/R_sub forward-biases a junction. Prevent it with frequent well and substrate taps, guard rings and higher substrate doping. It is especially important near I/O.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Focus on the well/substrate voltage produced by injected current.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["They remove the need for complementary PMOS and NMOS devices","They reduce well/substrate resistance and collect injected carriers before parasitic bipolar feedback sustains conduction","They reduce clock frequency so the parasitic thyristor cannot trigger","They increase well resistance so less current reaches the supply"],"ans":1,"why":["The protection does not alter CMOS into a single-device-type logic family.","Lower resistive voltage drops and carrier collection reduce junction forward bias that can trigger regenerative parasitic conduction.","Latch-up is a parasitic electrical condition, not a clock-frequency correctness property.","Higher resistance can increase local voltage drop and make triggering easier."],"ex":"Lower resistive voltage drops and carrier collection reduce junction forward bias that can trigger regenerative parasitic conduction.","oral":"What causes latch-up, and what layout practices prevent it?"},{"id":"V138","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-poly-need-to-extend-past-the-active-region","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Poly extension beyond active","q":"What failure does extending a polysilicon gate past the active region help prevent?","a":"→ Mask misalignment could otherwise leave a diffusion path around the gate end, shorting source to drain.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Visualize shifting the gate mask sideways relative to active.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Source and drain becoming disconnected because the gate conductor is too resistive","An intentional extra series transistor appearing in the metal rail","The body contact being electrically converted into a gate contact","Mask misalignment leaving an ungated diffusion path between source and drain"],"ans":3,"why":["The relevant concern is uncovered active, not gate resistance disconnecting diffusion.","Extension beyond active does not create a device without an active crossing.","Poly extension does not change contact type.","The gate must cover the full active width despite alignment tolerances."],"ex":"An uncovered path around a gate end can bypass gate control and short the intended source/drain regions.","oral":"Why does poly need to extend past the active region?"},{"id":"V139","k":"md-eecs427-derive-t-p-0-69rc","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Identify the RC delay derivation","q":"For Vout(t) = VDD(1 − exp(−t/RC)), which equation correctly finds the 50% propagation time?","a":"→ For a step into RC, V_out = V_DD(1 − e^(−t/RC)). Set it equal to V_DD/2: e^(−t/RC) = ½, so t = RC·ln 2 ≈ 0.69RC.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Substitute Vout = VDD/2 before taking the logarithm.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["exp(−t/RC) = 2, giving t = −RC ln 2.","t/RC = 2, giving t = 2RC.","1 − t/RC = 1/2, giving t = RC/2 exactly.","exp(−t/RC) = 1/2, giving t = RC ln 2."],"ans":3,"why":["The residual fraction is one-half, not two.","Two time constants reaches about 86.5%, not 50%.","A linear approximation is not the exact exponential crossing.","Setting the output to half the final value leaves half the initial error."],"ex":"Propagation delay here means a specified voltage crossing, not the full settling time.","oral":"Derive t_p = 0.69RC."},{"id":"V140","k":"md-eecs427-estimate-the-fo1-delay-of-a-320-160-nm-inverter-in-130-nm","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Inverter RC delay","q":"Use tpd≈0.69RC with R=10 kΩ and total self-plus-load capacitance 1.26 fF. What delay follows?","a":"→ C_total = 0.42 + 0.21 (self) + 0.42 + 0.21 (load) = 1.26 fF. With R ≈ 10 kΩ, t_pd = 0.69 × 10 kΩ × 1.26 fF ≈ 8.7 ps.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Include both the driver’s own capacitance and its fanout load.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["4.35 ps","8.69 ps","12.6 ps","86.9 ps"],"ans":1,"why":["This halves the stated total capacitance a second time.","0.69×10000×1.26e−15 seconds is 8.694 ps.","12.6 ps is RC before the 0.69 crossing factor.","This is a factor-of-ten unit error."],"ex":"0.69×10000×1.26e−15 seconds is 8.694 ps.","oral":"Estimate the FO1 delay of a 320/160 nm inverter in 130 nm."},{"id":"V141","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-doubling-an-inverter-s-width-not-halve-its-delay","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Doubling inverter width","q":"Assume R scales as 1/W, intrinsic drain capacitance as W, and external load is fixed. Doubling one inverter’s width has which first-order effect?","a":"→ t_p = 0.69RC_p + 0.69(R/S)C_L. The intrinsic term does not depend on S, and the larger input cap also slows the previous stage (self-loading).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Expand the RC model into intrinsic and external-load contributions.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Its intrinsic term doubles while the external delay term stays fixed","Its own delay is guaranteed to halve and the preceding stage is unaffected","Its external-load delay term halves, its intrinsic term stays fixed, and its input capacitance increases","Both intrinsic and external delay terms halve while input capacitance stays fixed"],"ans":2,"why":["Resistance also changes and reduces the external term.","Self-loading and the upstream capacitance penalty invalidate that guarantee.","The resistance and own-capacitance changes cancel only in the intrinsic product.","Intrinsic delay includes increasing self-load, and the gate capacitance grows."],"ex":"Sizing must be evaluated across the path, not just by the resized transistor’s resistance.","oral":"Why does doubling an inverter's width not halve its delay?"},{"id":"V142","k":"md-eecs427-for-a-nand2-which-input-should-the-critical-signal-drive-and-why","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Assign a late NAND input","q":"For a NAND2 falling transition, the early input has already risen and settled. Which series NMOS should the late critical input control to benefit from a pre-discharged internal node?","a":"→ The transistor nearest the output. Its internal node is already discharged by the earlier input, so only C_L has to be discharged.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Ask which early-on device provides an internal-node discharge path.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The NMOS nearest ground.","Either device, because equal transistor widths guarantee identical internal-node history.","The NMOS nearest the output.","Whichever input has the fastest rising-output arc, without checking the falling-output history."],"ans":2,"why":["The internal node may remain charged until the late ground-side device turns on.","Equal widths do not make initial internal charge the same.","The early ground-side NMOS can discharge the internal node before the late top device turns on.","The requested falling transition depends on the series pull-down history, not just a different arc’s speed."],"ex":"Pin assignment matters because the early input can prepare internal charge before the critical transition arrives.","oral":"For a NAND2, which input should the critical signal drive, and why?"},{"id":"V143","k":"md-eecs427-size-nand3-and-nor3-to-match-a-2-1-inverter-give-the-worst-case-r-in-each-direction","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND3 and NOR3 sizing","q":"Under Rn=R/Wn and Rp=2R/Wp, which NAND3/NOR3 widths match the worst-path resistance of an N1/P2 inverter?","a":"→ NAND3: nMOS 3, 3, 3 and pMOS 2, 2, 2. NOR3: pMOS 6, 6, 6 and nMOS 1, 1, 1. All worst-case paths equal R.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Identify the series network in each gate before applying the stack count.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["NAND3: each N1/P6; NOR3: each N3/P2","NAND3: each N1/P2; NOR3: each N1/P2","NAND3: each N3/P2; NOR3: each N1/P6","NAND3: each N3/P6; NOR3: each N3/P6"],"ans":2,"why":["This applies series compensation to the wrong networks.","Uncompensated three-device series paths have three times the reference resistance.","Three series NMOS need width 3 each; three series PMOS need width 6 each; parallel networks size for one device on.","It unnecessarily strengthens the parallel networks and no longer matches each worst path to R."],"ex":"Three series NMOS need width 3 each; three series PMOS need width 6 each; parallel networks size for one device on.","oral":"Size NAND3 and NOR3 to match a 2:1 inverter; give the worst-case R in each direction."},{"id":"V144","k":"md-eecs427-write-the-elmore-delay-for-the-nand3-pull-down-with-internal-nodes-1-5c-and-an-output-of-9-5f-c","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND3 Elmore sum","q":"A NAND3 pulldown has three series resistors R/3. Internal capacitors 1.5C sit behind cumulative resistances R/3 and 2R/3; output capacitance (9+5f)C sits behind R. What is the Elmore time constant?","a":"→ (R/3)(1.5C) + (2R/3)(1.5C) + R(9 + 5f)C = (10.5 + 5f)RC.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Weight internal nodes by cumulative resistance, not by the final path resistance.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["(10.5 + 5f)RC","(4 + 5f/3)RC","(9 + 5f)RC","(12 + 5f)RC"],"ans":0,"why":["The internal terms sum to (0.5+1)RC and the output contributes (9+5f)RC.","This effectively gives the output only R/3 instead of all three resistors.","This omits both internal-node contributions.","This weights both internal capacitors by the full R."],"ex":"Sum each capacitance multiplied by the resistance shared with its discharge path: 0.5+1+9+5f = 10.5+5f.","oral":"Write the Elmore delay for the NAND3 pull-down with internal nodes 1.5C and an output of (9 + 5f)C."},{"id":"V145","k":"md-eecs427-two-nand2-layouts-output-on-the-middle-p-diffusion-versus-on-both-outer-ones-which-is-better","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Reduce NAND output diffusion","q":"Two functionally identical NAND2 layouts differ only in PMOS output diffusion: one shared middle region versus two contacted outer regions. Which tends to lower intrinsic delay?","a":"→ Middle (shared) output. It has one contacted diffusion on Y instead of two, so lower output cap and lower intrinsic delay.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Count diffusion attached to the switching output node.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Use two outer output diffusions.","Use the shared middle output diffusion.","Choose solely by the gate input capacitance, which is unchanged.","Add both outer regions to the shared output region."],"ans":1,"why":["Two output-connected regions increase the capacitive load under the stated comparison.","One output diffusion region generally lowers output junction loading.","Output capacitance is the variable being changed here.","Extra output-connected diffusion adds capacitance."],"ex":"At fixed drive, reducing output diffusion capacitance lowers the intrinsic delay term.","oral":"Two NAND2 layouts: output on the middle p-diffusion versus on both outer ones. Which is better?"},{"id":"V146","k":"md-eecs427-why-might-a-designer-choose-1-5-instead-of-2","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"PMOS sizing and average delay","q":"In an inverter optimization where average delay includes width-dependent self-loading, why might Wp/Wn below the equal-drive ratio be useful?","a":"→ Average delay is minimized near √(μn/μp). A smaller pMOS cuts input cap and diffusion cap more than it slows the rising edge. β = 2 is kept where symmetric rise and fall matter (clocks, noise margins).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Include the cost of the device’s added gate and diffusion capacitance.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Less PMOS capacitance can reduce overall average delay despite a slower pull-up","It removes the effect of mobility ratio on drive","It always improves both noise margins and every transition","It guarantees equal rise and fall delay for every load"],"ans":0,"why":["A sizing optimum balances drive improvement against the capacitance added by a wider device.","Mobility still affects current; the optimization trades that against loading.","Smaller PMOS can worsen rise delay and alter the switching point, so benefits are not universal.","Equal-drive sizing and minimum-average-delay sizing are different objectives."],"ex":"A sizing optimum balances drive improvement against the capacitance added by a wider device.","oral":"Why might a designer choose β ≈ 1.5 instead of 2?"},{"id":"V147","k":"md-eecs427-derive-g-and-p-for-a-nand2-sized-2-2-from-t-p-0-69r-c-self-c-out","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND2 logical effort and parasitic delay","q":"For a matched-drive NAND2, Cin=4C and Cself=6C; normalize delay by the inverter unit 0.69R·3C and define h=Cout/Cin. Which d=gh+p follows?","a":"→ C_in = 4C, C_self = 6C. d = (6C + C_out)/(3C) = 2 + (4/3)h, so g = 4/3 and p = 2.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Substitute Cout = h·4C before normalizing.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["d = (4/3)h + 6","d = 2h + 4/3","d = (3/4)h + 2","d = (4/3)h + 2"],"ans":3,"why":["The parasitic capacitance must be normalized by 3C.","This swaps the load coefficient and parasitic term.","Logical effort is gate input capacitance divided by reference capacitance, not its inverse.","Cout/(3C)=(4/3)h and Cself/(3C)=2."],"ex":"d=(6C+Cout)/(3C)=2+(4/3)h; therefore g=4/3 and p=2.","oral":"Derive g and p for a NAND2 sized 2/2 from t_p = 0.69R(C_self + C_out)."},{"id":"V148","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-the-fo4-delay-in-and-roughly-in-ps-at-130-nm","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"FO4 in the simple logical-effort model","q":"For an inverter with g = 1 and parasitic delay p = 1, what is the normalized delay when it drives four identical inverter inputs?","a":"→ 5τ, about L/3 ≈ 43 ps (measured: about 7.4 × 6.3 ≈ 47 ps with p_inv ≈ 3.4).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Add the parasitic term after computing stage effort.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["5τ","4τ","7.4τ","3τ"],"ans":0,"why":["d = gh + p = 1 × 4 + 1 = 5.","This omits parasitic delay.","That requires a different parasitic value, such as p = 3.4.","This is below the electrical-effort contribution alone."],"ex":"FO4 specifies h = 4. Its delay in picoseconds depends on the process and loading model; 5τ belongs to the stated p = 1 model.","oral":"What is the FO4 delay in τ, and roughly in ps at 130 nm?"},{"id":"V149","k":"md-eecs427-a-path-has-g-2-b-8-h-9-6-how-many-stages-what-stage-effort-and-what-delay","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Path effort and stage delay","q":"For a fixed three-stage path with G=2, B=8, H=9.6 and total parasitic P=6, use D=3(GBH)^(1/3)+P. What normalized delay results?","a":"→ F = 153.6, N = 3, f̂ = 5.35, D = 3·5.35 + P (= 6 for INV-NAND4-INV) = 22.1τ.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Calculate path effort, take the cube root, then add stage effort and parasitics.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 159.6τ","About 22.1τ","About 10.5τ","About 16.1τ"],"ans":1,"why":["Adding path effort directly to P confuses multiplicative effort with summed stage delay.","F=153.6, equal effort is about 5.355, and D≈3×5.355+6=22.1τ.","10.5τ does not include the three-stage effort sum under the stated formula.","16.1τ omits the total parasitic delay."],"ex":"F=153.6, equal effort is about 5.355, and D≈3×5.355+6=22.1τ.","oral":"A path has G = 2, B = 8, H = 9.6. How many stages, what stage effort and what delay?"},{"id":"V150","k":"md-eecs427-three-nand2s-c-in-4-c-l-108-give-the-sizes","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Size a three-NAND path","q":"Three NAND2 stages each have logical effort 4/3 and no branching. The first input capacitance is 4 units and the final load is 108 units. Which sequence equalizes stage effort?","a":"→ F = 64, f̂ = 4, input caps 4 → 12 → 36. The last gate's transistors are 18 each. D = 18τ.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Find path effort, then divide equal stage effort by gate logical effort.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Input capacitances 4 → 9 → 27","Input capacitances 4 → 16 → 64","Input capacitances 4 → 12 → 36","Input capacitances 4 → 36 → 108"],"ans":2,"why":["The first two and final load ratios are not equal.","Electrical efforts are 4, 4 and 108/64, so effort is unequal.","Each electrical effort is 3 and each stage effort is (4/3)×3=4.","The first ratio is 9 while the final ratio is 1."],"ex":"F=(4/3)³(108/4)=64, so optimal stage effort is 4. The electrical ratio is 4/(4/3)=3.","oral":"Three NAND2s, C_in = 4, C_L = 108: give the sizes."},{"id":"V151","k":"md-eecs427-nand2-and-nor2-logical-effort-with-a-2-1-reference-versus-a-1-1-reference","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND versus NOR logical effort","q":"With a 2:1 PMOS:NMOS reference inverter and matched worst-case resistance, what are g for NAND2 and NOR2 respectively?","a":"→ 2:1: 4/3 and 5/3. 1:1: both 3/2 (NAND2 nMOS 2, pMOS 1; NOR2 pMOS 2, nMOS 1).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"The PMOS series stack starts from a wider reference device.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["4/3 and 5/3","2 and 2","3/2 and 3/2","5/3 and 4/3"],"ans":0,"why":["NAND2 input width is 2 + 2; NOR2 is 1 + 4; reference is 3.","Fan-in alone is not logical effort.","Those are the values for a 1:1 sizing reference in the simplified model.","This swaps the PMOS-series and NMOS-series costs."],"ex":"Normalize each gate’s per-input capacitance to an inverter with equal output drive.","oral":"NAND2 and NOR2 logical effort with a 2:1 reference versus a 1:1 reference?"},{"id":"V152","k":"md-eecs427-you-are-unsure-between-3-and-4-stages-which-error-is-cheaper","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Comparing stage-count penalties","q":"A normalized stage-count study reports delay penalties 1.26× at twice the optimum count and 1.51× at half the optimum count. Within that stated model, which conclusion follows?","a":"→ Too many. At N/N̂ = 2 the penalty is 1.26, versus 1.51 at N/N̂ = 0.5, and near the boundary the difference is a few percent.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Compare the supplied penalties; do not turn a local model comparison into a universal rule.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Underestimating stage count is less costly because fewer parasitics always win","Overestimating stage count by this factor is less costly than underestimating it","The data prove adding any number of stages always improves delay","Both errors have the same cost because they are equally far in ratio"],"ans":1,"why":["The stated numbers already include the model’s competing effort and parasitic costs.","The reported 1.26 penalty is smaller than 1.51 for these two modeled deviations.","Both penalties exceed one, so either deviation is worse than the optimum.","The delay-versus-stage-count curve is not symmetric in these ratios."],"ex":"The reported 1.26 penalty is smaller than 1.51 for these two modeled deviations.","oral":"You are unsure between 3 and 4 stages. Which error is cheaper?"},{"id":"V153","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-the-branching-effort-8-in-the-4-16-decoder","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Decoder branching effort","q":"In a 4-to-16 decoder, one true address line drives eight identical NAND4 input pins, and only one lies on the timed path. What branching effort applies at that address node?","a":"→ Each true or complement address line drives 8 of the 16 NAND4 decoders (half the words have that bit equal to 0), but only one is on the timed path.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Count loads driven by one polarity of one address bit.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["8","16","1","4"],"ans":0,"why":["Total load is eight pin loads while on-path load is one.","A true or complement line drives half the word decoders, not all sixteen.","Seven off-path loads still load the address driver.","The number of address bits is not the branch-load ratio."],"ex":"Branching effort is total capacitive load divided by the on-path capacitive load.","oral":"Why is the branching effort 8 in the 4:16 decoder?"},{"id":"V154","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-the-best-stage-effort-larger-than-4-in-the-measured-130-nm-process","d":"dly","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Why optimal stage effort shifts","q":"In a logical-effort path model, a measured inverter parasitic delay is much larger than the usual p = 1 approximation. What trend should this favor?","a":"→ p_inv ≈ 3.4, not 1. Each extra stage costs more parasitic delay, so the optimum shifts to fewer, higher-effort stages (f ≈ 5-6).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"What cost is paid once for every added stage?","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["No buffering at any electrical effort.","Fewer stages with greater effort per stage.","More stages with smaller effort per stage regardless of load.","Exactly the same optimum stage effort in every process."],"ans":1,"why":["Large loads can still justify buffers despite added parasitics.","Each added stage now incurs more fixed parasitic cost.","Extra stages become more costly, not less.","The optimum depends on the parasitic term as well as path effort."],"ex":"Minimize the sum of effort and parasitic delay over candidate stage counts instead of treating effort four as a universal optimum.","oral":"Why is the best stage effort larger than 4 in the measured 130 nm process?"},{"id":"V155","k":"md-eecs427-why-can-p-a-b-be-used-for-carry-computation-but-not-for-the-sum","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Carry propagate definitions","q":"Why can carry logic use P=A OR B in Cout=G OR(P AND Cin), with G=A AND B, while the sum still needs XOR?","a":"G + (A+B)Ci = G + (G + A⊕B)Ci = G + PCi, so the extra A = B = 1 case is absorbed by G. The sum needs (A⊕B)⊕Ci; OR would give the wrong sum when A = B = 1.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Check the one input combination where OR and XOR differ.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["When A=B=1, G already forces carry high, but the sum still depends on the parity A XOR B","Using OR in the sum is correct if carry is generated elsewhere","The carry ignores Cin whenever A differs from B","OR and XOR are identical for every input combination"],"ans":0,"why":["The extra OR-propagate case is absorbed by G in carry; it would change sum parity when both inputs are one.","A separate carry output does not repair the wrong sum bit.","For differing inputs, carry propagates Cin.","They differ at A=B=1, which is exactly the relevant case."],"ex":"The extra OR-propagate case is absorbed by G in carry; it would change sum parity when both inputs are one.","oral":"Why can P = A + B be used for carry computation but not for the sum?"},{"id":"V156","k":"md-eecs427-in-a-carry-bypass-adder-why-must-the-block-propagate-use-xor","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Why carry bypass uses XOR propagate","q":"A carry-bypass mux passes Cin when every bit claims propagate. Why is Pi=Ai XOR Bi appropriate, whereas Pi=Ai OR Bi can select a wrong bypass?","a":"With OR, a block with A = B = 1 in some bit would claim \"propagate\" and the mux would pass Cin, but that bit actually generates; the carry-out would be wrong when Cin = 0.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Test the input pair 11 with a zero incoming carry.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Ai=Bi=1 generates a carry rather than merely propagating Cin, but OR would mark that bit as propagate","OR always has larger electrical delay than XOR, regardless of implementation","XOR and OR differ only when Cin=1, so neither can implement bypass","Ai=Bi=0 generates a carry, but XOR would mark it as propagate"],"ans":0,"why":["With Cin=0, bypassing a generating bit can discard the carry it should create.","The issue is logical correctness, not a universal delay ordering.","Their relevant difference is the input pair 11, independent of Cin.","Both inputs zero kill carry and both XOR and OR are zero."],"ex":"Bypass must mean the output carry equals the input carry. XOR identifies exactly the one-high input condition that has this property.","oral":"In a carry-bypass adder, why must the block-propagate use XOR?"},{"id":"V157","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-on-the-mirror-adder-s-critical-node-and-how-is-it-minimized","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Optimize a mirror-adder carry path","q":"A ripple path is limited by the mirror adder’s carry node. Which sizing strategy most directly protects that path from avoidable capacitance?","a":"Node Co: 4 diffusion caps, 2 internal gate caps and 6 gate caps of the next cell. Keep Ci transistors closest to the output, size only the carry stage, keep the sum stage minimum size.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Follow the capacitances seen by the propagating carry.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Choose sizes using only carry-node diffusion, omitting next-stage input loading.","Prioritize carry devices and keep noncritical sum circuitry small.","Keep all stages identically sized because the repeated Boolean function implies identical timing criticality.","Upsize every sum transistor because any sum improvement must speed the carry path."],"ans":1,"why":["Next-stage gates contribute real carry-node capacitance.","Carry-focused sizing avoids loading the critical node unnecessarily.","Arrival times, fanout and path roles determine criticality even in repeated logic.","Larger noncritical circuitry can add load without shortening the propagating carry dependency."],"ex":"Optimize the actual carry node, including diffusion, internal gate capacitance and next-stage fanout.","oral":"What is on the mirror adder's critical node and how is it minimized?"},{"id":"V158","k":"md-eecs427-write-the-critical-path-delay-of-a-linear-carry-select-adder-and-a-square-root-carry-select-adder","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Carry-select delay model","q":"For equal-sized M-bit blocks in the stated linear carry-select model, which expression captures ripple within a block and selection across N/M blocks?","a":"t = tsetup + M·tcarry + (N/M)·tmux + tsum; square root: t = tsetup + 2tcarry + √(2N)·tmux + tsum, from N ≈ n²/2.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Separate the number of bits per block from the number of blocks.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["tsetup + N·M·tcarry + tmux + tsum","tsetup + N·tcarry + N·tmux + tsum","tsetup + (N/M)·tcarry + M·tmux + tsum","tsetup + M·tcarry + (N/M)·tmux + tsum"],"ans":3,"why":["Multiplying N by M double-counts ripple depth across precomputed blocks.","Carry-select avoids serially rippling through every bit before all selection.","This swaps block size with block count.","The first resolved block contributes M carry steps, followed by about N/M selection stages in this model."],"ex":"The first resolved block contributes M carry steps, followed by about N/M selection stages in this model.","oral":"Write the critical-path delay of a linear carry-select adder and a square-root carry-select adder."},{"id":"V159","k":"md-eecs427-rank-kogge-stone-brent-kung-and-sklansky-on-logic-levels-fanout-and-wiring","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Prefix-adder tradeoffs","q":"Which qualitative comparison matches the source’s conventional prefix-adder structures?","a":"KS: low levels, low fanout, high wiring. BK: high levels (2log2N − 1), low fanout, low wiring. Sklansky: low levels, low wiring, high fanout.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Identify what Kogge-Stone spends to keep both depth and fanout low.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Kogge-Stone: greatest depth/lightest wiring; Brent-Kung: least depth/heaviest wiring","Sklansky: low depth with uniformly low fanout; Kogge-Stone: inherently high fanout","All three have identical depth, fanout and routing demand","Kogge-Stone: low depth/low fanout/heavy wiring; Brent-Kung: greater depth/low fanout/lighter wiring"],"ans":3,"why":["This reverses the principal KS/BK tradeoff.","Sklansky’s high fanout is one of its characteristic costs.","The structures trade these physical and logical properties differently.","KS spends wiring for shallow low-fanout computation; BK reduces interconnect at extra logic depth."],"ex":"Prefix topology is a speed/fanout/wiring tradeoff. A nominal logic-depth advantage must still survive physical implementation.","oral":"Rank Kogge-Stone, Brent-Kung and Sklansky on logic levels, fanout and wiring."},{"id":"V160","k":"md-eecs427-how-does-the-inversion-property-speed-up-a-ripple-adder","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Use full-adder inversion symmetry","q":"How can alternating polarity stages reduce delay in a ripple-carry adder when full-adder inversion symmetry is used correctly?","a":"S and Co of inverted inputs are the inverted outputs, so alternate cells take inverted A, B and ¬C, and the carry inverter is removed from every stage of the chain.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Separate logic polarity from the existence of a carry dependency.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["They turn the serial carry dependency into a logarithmic prefix tree without changing interconnect.","They avoid final polarity bookkeeping because complemented sum bits represent the same binary word.","They can remove a carry-restoring inverter from each stage of the chain.","They remove carry delay solely by complementing A and B while keeping every carry polarity unchanged."],"ans":2,"why":["Polarity alternation reduces stages per bit but does not create a prefix network.","The chosen polarity convention must still deliver the intended sum bits.","Complemented inputs and outputs can make successive carry polarities compatible.","All required input/output polarity relations must be honored to preserve arithmetic."],"ex":"The benefit comes from implementing the same arithmetic with compatible alternating carry polarity, reducing serial logic stages.","oral":"How does the inversion property speed up a ripple adder?"},{"id":"V161","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-a-sparse-tree-adder","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Sparse prefix adders","q":"What makes a sparse prefix adder sparse?","a":"A prefix tree that only computes every s-th carry, plus pairs of s-bit adders (Cin = 0/1) and a mux per group; fewer cells and wires, used in 32-64-bit high-performance adders.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Ask which carries must be produced globally and which can be handled within a group.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It computes selected group carries with a prefix tree and completes intervening bits locally","It removes every carry calculation and uses only XOR gates","It serializes all N carries through a single ripple chain","It computes every carry with a full dense tree and deletes the sum outputs"],"ans":0,"why":["Computing every s-th carry reduces tree wiring and cells; local adders or select logic finish each group.","Multi-bit addition still needs carry information.","A pure ripple chain does not retain a sparse global prefix structure.","A full carry tree is not sparse merely because outputs are omitted."],"ex":"Computing every s-th carry reduces tree wiring and cells; local adders or select logic finish each group.","oral":"What is a sparse tree adder?"},{"id":"V162","k":"md-eecs427-discussion-4-how-does-the-alu-compute-sub-cmp-with-an-adder-and-how-is-overflow-detected","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Subtraction with an adder","q":"For an n-bit two’s-complement ALU implemented with an adder, which operation and overflow test implement A−B?","a":"A + ¬B + 1 (carry-in = 1). Overflow F = Cn ⊕ Cn−1; zero Z = NOR of all result bits; negative N = Cn ⊕ Cn−1 ⊕ Sn−1 for signed results.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Recall the extra increment required by two’s-complement negation.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Add A + NOT(B) with carry-in 0; overflow is always the final carry","Add NOT(A) + B + 1; overflow is true only when the result is zero","Add A + NOT(B) + 1; overflow is carry into the sign bit XOR carry out","Add A + B + 1; overflow is the result sign bit"],"ans":2,"why":["Without the added one, the result is A−B−1; final carry alone is not signed overflow.","This computes B−A and uses an unrelated zero test.","Two’s-complement negation adds one after bitwise inversion, and sign-bit carry disagreement signals signed overflow.","This does not negate B and the sign bit alone is not overflow."],"ex":"A−B = A+(~B+1) modulo 2ⁿ. Signed overflow is distinct from unsigned carry/borrow and from the raw sign bit.","oral":"(Discussion 4) How does the ALU compute SUB/CMP with an adder, and how is overflow detected?"},{"id":"V163","k":"md-eecs427-what-does-a-hi-skew-inverter-p-3-n-1-cost-in-logical-effort-for-each-edge","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Edge-specific effort in a skewed inverter","q":"A HI-skew inverter has Wp = 3 and Wn = 1. Relative to a 2:1 balanced inverter of equal drive for each edge, what are its rising and falling logical efforts?","a":"Compared with the 2:1 reference of equal strength: g_u = 4/4.5 = 8/9 (favored), g_d = 4/3 (penalized). Skewing is limited by noise margin and the reset/precharge edge.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Use a separate equal-drive reference for each output transition.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["4/3 rising; 8/9 falling.","8/9 rising; 4/3 falling.","1 rising; 1 falling.","3/2 rising; 1/2 falling."],"ans":1,"why":["This reverses the edge favored by the stronger PMOS.","Cin = 4; equal-rise reference Cin = 4.5 and equal-fall reference Cin = 3.","Equal input capacitance does not imply equal drive for both edges.","These values do not normalize input capacitance to equal-drive references."],"ex":"A wider PMOS favors output rise at the cost of additional capacitance for the falling arc.","oral":"What does a HI-skew inverter (P = 3, N = 1) cost in logical effort for each edge?"},{"id":"V164","k":"md-eecs427-in-dcvs-which-output-switches-first-and-why-is-dcvs-called-ratioed","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"DCVS evaluation contention","q":"In a DCVS transition, why must the NMOS evaluation network be sized against the cross-coupled PMOS?","a":"The falling output first; the NMOS tree must overpower the still-on cross-coupled PMOS before the other side rises. Statically there is no current path, but the transient fight makes sizing ratioed.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Start from the previous state and find which device still resists the first transition.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The first falling node must overcome a still-on PMOS before feedback raises the other node","The first rising node must discharge through the PMOS to ground","Both PMOS are guaranteed off before evaluation begins","Ratioing is needed only to eliminate a permanent static short in every valid state"],"ans":0,"why":["The transient contention creates a strength requirement even though a settled valid state need not draw DC through-current.","PMOS devices provide pull-up; this reverses the initiating transition.","The old state keeps an opposing PMOS on until the evaluation node moves.","The relevant ratioed fight can occur during switching without a permanent static short."],"ex":"The transient contention creates a strength requirement even though a settled valid state need not draw DC through-current.","oral":"In DCVS, which output switches first, and why is DCVS called ratioed?"},{"id":"V165","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-an-nmos-pass-gate-deliver-only-vdd-vt-and-why-is-it-worse-than-vt0","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"NMOS weak high and body effect","q":"An NMOS pass device has its gate at VDD and body at ground while charging an output high. Why can the useful strong-conduction endpoint be lower than VDD−Vt0?","a":"The NMOS turns off when Vgs falls to Vt; the source is the rising node, so body effect raises Vt above Vt0.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Write VGS and VSB using the charging node as the source.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["As output rises, VGS falls and VSB rises, increasing threshold through body effect","Its body follows the output automatically, eliminating all threshold effects","As output rises, VGS rises and the threshold becomes negative","The output loses a fixed Vt0 at every elapsed clock cycle"],"ans":0,"why":["The rising output is the lower-potential source, so both effects weaken charging.","A grounded body does not track the output source potential.","VGS=VDD−Vout decreases, not increases.","Threshold degradation is a bias condition, not a per-cycle subtraction."],"ex":"The approximate endpoint solves Vout = VDD−Vt(Vout). Subthreshold charging can continue slowly, so the practical result also depends on time and load.","oral":"Why does an NMOS pass gate deliver only Vdd − Vt, and why is it worse than Vt0?"},{"id":"V166","k":"md-eecs427-delay-of-n-unbuffered-pass-gates-in-series","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Unbuffered pass-chain scaling","q":"For n identical series resistances R, each followed by capacitance C, what is the far-end Elmore delay moment?","a":"Elmore: RC·n(n+1)/2, i.e. O(n²); buffering every few stages makes it linear.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Sum the downstream capacitors seen by each series resistor.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["RC/n","RC n(n + 1)/2","RC n²/2 exactly for every n","RC n"],"ans":1,"why":["Adding series stages does not reduce the delay.","The resistances see progressively different downstream capacitance, summing 1 through n.","This is the large-n leading term but misses the n/2 term.","This omits the shared upstream resistances charging later nodes."],"ex":"The moment grows quadratically for long unbuffered chains; periodic buffering changes the scaling and introduces its own cost.","oral":"Delay of n unbuffered pass gates in series?"},{"id":"V167","k":"md-eecs427-what-does-the-leap-keeper-fix-and-what-is-the-sizing-trade-off","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Keeper strength and delay","q":"A LEAP-style level-restoring keeper is made stronger. Which tradeoff is expected?","a":"Restores full swing and noise tolerance and stops inverter leakage. Strong keeper: faster 0→1 completion, slower (or failed) 1→0. Weak keeper: the opposite.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish helping the retained state from allowing the opposite state to be written.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["No change to transition behavior because a keeper only affects DC","Faster restoration of a weak high, but more opposition to a legitimate high-to-low transition","Lower input capacitance solely because keeper current increased","Faster high-to-low transitions because the keeper pulls toward ground"],"ans":1,"why":["It participates during transitions and can even prevent a weak pull-down from winning.","A stronger restorative pull-up completes the high level sooner but increases contention during a low write.","Strength increases do not inherently remove capacitance.","The restoring keeper supports the high state rather than discharging it."],"ex":"A stronger restorative pull-up completes the high level sooner but increases contention during a low write.","oral":"What does the LEAP keeper fix, and what is the sizing trade-off?"},{"id":"V168","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-a-low-p-n-output-inverter-help-in-leap","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Low-trip receiver after a pass node","q":"A slowly rising degraded pass-node voltage drives the restoring output inverter in the source’s LEAP example. Why use a lower P/N strength ratio there?","a":"It has a low switching threshold, so it flips earlier on the slowly rising Vdd − Vt node and turns on the keeper sooner.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Consider where the receiver’s transfer curve should switch for a weak rising input.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It raises the inverter trip point above VDD to reject all degraded levels","It eliminates the need to verify the keeper’s write-0 contention","It directly removes the pass device’s body effect","It lowers the inverter trip point so the rising node is recognized sooner and restoration starts earlier"],"ans":3,"why":["A lower trip point, not a higher one, helps detect a weak rising high.","Keeper sizing still trades restoration against overwriting.","Changing the receiver ratio does not change the pass transistor’s body bias.","A weaker pull-up relative to pulldown moves the switching point down."],"ex":"The receiver threshold is chosen to respond to the available weak-high voltage, then feedback restoration pulls the node toward the rail.","oral":"Why does a low P/N output inverter help in LEAP?"},{"id":"V169","k":"md-eecs427-how-does-cpl-differ-from-dcvs-in-terms-of-functional-risk","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Recognize DCVS contention risk","q":"In a differential cascode-voltage-switch gate, why can oversized cross-coupled PMOS loads cause functional failure?","a":"In CPL the pass network drives the node directly, so PMOS size only affects speed and noise margin; in DCVS an oversized PMOS can stop the gate from switching.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"A stronger restorative load can resist the desired transition.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A stronger PMOS load always improves both output directions, independent of the NMOS network.","Only capacitive delay changes with PMOS size; the DC regenerative contest is unaffected.","Sizing can be checked with isolated inverter drive ratios without analyzing the cross-coupled state.","The NMOS evaluation network may be unable to overcome the restoring PMOS contention."],"ans":3,"why":["The PMOS helps restoration but can oppose the desired pull-down transition.","The pull-down must overcome the load electrically to switch at all.","Cross-coupled state and contention are essential to the operation.","The circuit must win a regenerative current fight to change state."],"ex":"Electrical ratio and regeneration constraints can make a logically correct DCVS topology fail to switch.","oral":"How does CPL differ from DCVS in terms of functional risk?"},{"id":"V170","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-opl-predict-1s-and-why-does-it-need-per-stage-clocks","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Output-prediction logic timing","q":"Why does output-prediction logic need evaluation sequencing after predicting high outputs?","a":"In an inverting chain half the gates are right and need no transition; but 1-in/1-out is unstable for an inverting gate, so each gate is held off until its inputs are ready.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Consider an inverter whose input and output are both initially predicted high.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The predicted high state permanently disables every PMOS","Per-stage clocks convert all inverting functions into non-inverting functions","Sequencing eliminates the need for valid inputs altogether","An inverting stage must wait until its inputs are valid so an initially predicted high does not propagate an incorrect evaluation"],"ans":3,"why":["The prediction is an initial condition, not permanent device disable.","Clocking changes when evaluation occurs, not the Boolean polarity of the stage.","Correct evaluation still requires correct input conditions.","A chain of inverting stages cannot safely evaluate all initial high predictions simultaneously without controlling readiness."],"ex":"A chain of inverting stages cannot safely evaluate all initial high predictions simultaneously without controlling readiness.","oral":"Why does OPL predict 1s, and why does it need per-stage clocks?"},{"id":"V171","k":"md-eecs427-why-must-domino-inputs-be-monotonically-rising-during-evaluate","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Domino input monotonicity","q":"During evaluate, a domino pulldown input rises long enough to discharge the dynamic node and then falls. What is the central risk?","a":"The dynamic node can only be discharged in evaluate; an input that is briefly high then falls discharges it irreversibly until the next precharge. The output inverter guarantees each domino output only rises, so chains stay monotonic.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Ask which enabled path can restore the lost charge after the pulse.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The dynamic node may remain discharged until precharge, leaving an incorrect asserted output","The node is guaranteed to recharge immediately through the disabled precharge device","The glitch is harmless because only the final input values matter","The static output inverter automatically restores the original dynamic-node charge"],"ans":0,"why":["Evaluation cannot freely undo a completed discharge within the cycle.","The precharge device is off in evaluate.","Dynamic logic depends on transition history during evaluate.","The output inverter senses the dynamic node; it is not a strong recharge path."],"ex":"Monotonic inputs prevent a transient true condition from irreversibly discharging the dynamic node during evaluation.","oral":"Why must domino inputs be monotonically rising during evaluate?"},{"id":"V172","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-the-switching-threshold-of-a-dynamic-gate-in-evaluate-and-what-does-it-imply","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Dynamic input noise sensitivity","q":"During evaluate, an NMOS dynamic pull-down can begin discharging when its input exceeds roughly Vtn. What is the implication compared with a balanced static inverter near VDD/2?","a":"About Vtn (vs ~VDD/2 for static, 0.6 V at 1.2 V): early switch point (fast) but low-side noise margin of only about Vt.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Compare the noise excursion needed to turn on the evaluation path.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Any partial discharge is immediately restored by the precharge PMOS.","A lower input noise excursion may initiate a harmful discharge.","The dynamic threshold is fixed at VDD/2 regardless of the NMOS threshold.","The dynamic node has a larger guaranteed low-input noise margin."],"ans":1,"why":["The strong precharge device is off during evaluate.","The early pull-down onset gives speed but weaker low-side noise tolerance.","The NMOS conduction threshold matters to the discharge path.","A lower effective triggering level reduces this margin."],"ex":"The exact transient failure depends on glitch amplitude, duration and keeper strength; a dynamic node cannot be treated as a static VTC alone.","oral":"What is the switching threshold of a dynamic gate in evaluate, and what does it imply?"},{"id":"V173","k":"md-eecs427-state-the-keeper-sizing-inequality-and-what-it-limits","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Keeper-current design window","q":"Which keeper-current window is required for a dynamic node with n off leakage paths and one possible selected pull-down?","a":"n·I_pd,off < I_keeper < I_pd,on (with corner margins). It limits OR fan-in - for a ROM, the number of cells per bitline.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Write one condition for retention and a separate condition for successful discharge.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Ikeeper = n·Ion regardless of leakage","Ikeeper > n·Ioff and Ikeeper > Ion","Ikeeper < n·Ioff and Ikeeper < Ion","n·Ioff < Ikeeper < Ion, with margins at the appropriate corners"],"ans":3,"why":["Scaling keeper strength by all possible on paths ignores the single-path worst case.","A keeper stronger than the selected pull-down can prevent evaluation.","A keeper below aggregate leakage cannot reliably retain a high node.","The keeper must replenish aggregate leakage yet lose to the intended selected discharge."],"ex":"The keeper must replenish aggregate leakage yet lose to the intended selected discharge.","oral":"State the keeper sizing inequality and what it limits."},{"id":"V174","k":"md-eecs427-derive-the-charge-sharing-voltage-when-cl-ca","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Pass-limited charge sharing","q":"A dynamic node initially at VDD=0.9 V has CL=10 fF and connects to an initially discharged CA=1 fF. Assume the pass device stops charging CA at VDD−VT, with VT=0.35 V. Neglect leakage and keeper current. What final dynamic-node voltage follows from charge conservation?","a":"CL·VDD = CL·VF + CA(VDD − VT) → VF = VDD − (CA/CL)(VDD − VT). If CL ~ CA: VF = CL·VDD/(CL + CA).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Track charge gained by CA and charge lost by CL separately.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["0.845 V","0.550 V","0.090 V","0.818 V"],"ans":0,"why":["The transferred charge is 1 fF×0.55 V, so CL loses 0.055 V.","0.55 V is the internal capacitor’s cutoff voltage, not the dynamic-node voltage.","0.09 V confuses the capacitance ratio with retained voltage.","0.818 V assumes both capacitors fully equalize rather than the stated pass cutoff."],"ex":"VF=VDD−(CA/CL)(VDD−VT)=0.9−0.1×0.55=0.845 V. The 55 mV droop is below VT, consistent with the assumed cutoff case.","oral":"Derive the charge-sharing voltage when CL ≫ CA."},{"id":"V175","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-footless-domino-still-need-a-footer-in-the-first-stage","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Why the first domino stage needs isolation","q":"Why may the first stage need a clocked footer even when later stages use footless domino?","a":"Its inputs come from a latch and are not guaranteed low during precharge, which would short the precharge PMOS to ground. Later stages get inputs that precharge low - provided their own precharge is delayed (Φ1, Φ2).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Ask where the first stage’s inputs originate during precharge.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Later-stage monotonicity automatically guarantees the first stage’s precharge safety.","Any stage driven by a latch is guaranteed to receive zero throughout precharge.","Its external inputs may remain high during precharge and otherwise create a discharge path against precharge.","A sufficiently large precharge PMOS eliminates contention rather than merely overpowering it."],"ans":2,"why":["First-stage input conditions must be checked separately.","A latch can hold a one; it is not inherently a precharge-low source.","The footer breaks a possible DC path during precharge.","Overpowering an active pull-down still burns current and may impair restoration."],"ex":"Footless operation relies on appropriate input and precharge sequencing. Check the first-stage boundary conditions explicitly.","oral":"Why does footless domino still need a footer in the first stage?"},{"id":"V176","k":"md-eecs427-why-use-dual-rail-domino-and-what-is-unsafe-about-cross-coupled-dual-rail-keepers","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Dual-rail startup state","q":"What is the risk of a cross-coupled dual-rail keeper scheme if both dynamic rails initially have no active keeper before data arrives?","a":"Domino is non-inverting, so complements must be computed explicitly. With cross-coupled keepers, both rails float with no keeper on until an input arrives.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Analyze the initial state before assuming one rail has already selected the other’s keeper.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Both rails can temporarily float and become vulnerable to leakage or noise","Both rails are guaranteed actively restored by the cross-coupling alone","The floating interval removes charge-sharing sensitivity","The circuit no longer needs complementary functions"],"ans":0,"why":["Cross-coupling does not guarantee a sustaining path in every pre-evaluation state; the unheld interval is the risk.","The premise states no keeper is active, so active restoration cannot be assumed.","Floating charge is vulnerable to disturbance; the interval does not remove that mechanism.","Dual-rail logic still represents true and complement functions."],"ex":"Cross-coupling does not guarantee a sustaining path in every pre-evaluation state; the unheld interval is the risk.","oral":"Why use dual-rail domino, and what is unsafe about cross-coupled dual-rail keepers?"},{"id":"V177","k":"md-eecs427-what-are-the-self-resetting-domino-timing-constraints","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Self-resetting domino pulse timing","q":"A self-resetting domino pulse chain has no isolation from an active input pulldown during reset. Which timing contract avoids re-discharge and prepares the next evaluation?","a":"f must fall after the inputs go low; f must rise again before the next input; all inputs must line up as pulses.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Consider whether an input can still discharge the node during recharge.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Stagger required input pulses so they never overlap at the evaluating gate","Complete reset while an evaluating input is still high, without checking contention","Start the next input pulse before the dynamic node has recharged","Align required input pulses for evaluation, let them return low before reset, and finish rearming before the next event"],"ans":3,"why":["A multi-input pulldown may never observe the simultaneous condition needed to evaluate.","A conducting pulldown can fight or undo precharge.","Incomplete recharge leaves the next evaluation without its required initial condition.","Reset/rearm fits between active input pulses, and required inputs coincide during evaluation."],"ex":"The essential requirement is an ordered evaluate/reset/rearm window and appropriately aligned input pulses. Signal polarity must be defined for a concrete implementation.","oral":"What are the self-resetting domino timing constraints?"},{"id":"V178","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-back-gate-coupling","d":"cmos","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"A dynamic node drives a static stack","q":"A dynamic node drives one input of a static gate. Switching another input changes the receiving transistor’s channel charge. What risk should be evaluated?","a":"A static gate driven by a dynamic node: when its other input turns its stack on, the driven transistor's gate capacitance rises and pulls charge off the dynamic node (i = C·dV/dt), slowing or corrupting it.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Look for a changing capacitance or charge distribution attached to a floating node.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A propagation-delay change solely inside the receiver, with no effect on its input voltage.","Only DC gate-oxide leakage, with no transient coupling because the driven terminal is a gate.","Full restoration by the downstream static gate’s output driver directly back into the dynamic input.","Capacitive charge removal from the weakly held dynamic node."],"ans":3,"why":["Receiver terminal charge can couple to a floating input source.","Insulated gates still have capacitance and can exchange transient displacement charge.","A static output driver does not directly clamp the gate input that drives it.","State-dependent channel coupling can disturb a charge-storing driver node."],"ex":"A nominal gate input is not an ideal isolated capacitor; changing downstream device state can couple back into a floating or weakly held source node.","oral":"What is back-gate coupling?"},{"id":"V179","k":"md-eecs427-a-full-output-010-cycle-draws-how-much-energy-from-vdd-and-where-is-it-dissipated","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Energy in a CMOS switching cycle","q":"For an ideal full-swing 0→1→0 charge/discharge cycle of capacitance C from a constant VDD, ignoring leakage and short-circuit current, what energy is drawn from VDD?","a":"C·VDD². ½CV² in the pMOS during the rise; ½CV² stored then dissipated in the nMOS during the fall. Independent of R.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish source energy from energy temporarily stored on the capacitor.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["2CVDD², because each edge draws the same energy from VDD","CVDD², with half dissipated charging and half dissipated discharging","½CVDD², because only the stored energy is drawn from VDD","Zero net energy, because the capacitor ends at its initial voltage"],"ans":1,"why":["The falling edge dissipates stored charge rather than drawing another CV² from the ideal supply.","Charging draws CV²: half becomes stored energy and half heat; the stored half is dissipated on discharge.","The source supplies both stored energy and charging-path dissipation.","Returning to the initial stored energy does not undo irreversible resistor/device dissipation."],"ex":"Charging draws CV²: half becomes stored energy and half heat; the stored half is dissipated on discharge.","oral":"A full output 0→1→0 cycle draws how much energy from VDD, and where is it dissipated?"},{"id":"V180","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-for-a-clock-a-signal-that-toggles-every-cycle-and-random-data","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Activity-factor convention","q":"Define α as expected 0→1 events per system-clock period. A clock rises once per period; a data bit toggles once per period; independent random data is 0 or 1 with equal probability each period. What are their α values respectively?","a":"1, ½, and 0.25 (= P0·P1 with P = 0.5) in this course's 0→1 convention.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Separate rising transitions from all transitions.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["1, 1/2, 1/4","2, 1, 1/4","1/2, 1/2, 1/2","1, 1, 1/2"],"ans":0,"why":["The clock has one rise; alternating data rises every second period; random data has probability P0P1=1/4.","The two clock edges are not both 0→1 events.","A clock has a rise every period and random data does not always change.","This counts every data transition rather than only charging events."],"ex":"Activity factors must state their event convention. Counting charging events gives these values for αCV²f.","oral":"What is α for a clock, a signal that toggles every cycle, and random data?"},{"id":"V181","k":"md-eecs427-4-input-and-as-nand2-nand2-nor2-with-p-0-5-inputs-at-the-nand-outputs-and-at-y","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Compute AND switching activity","q":"Four mutually independent inputs are each 1 with probability 1/2, with independent values between cycles. For their four-input AND, what is the probability of a 0→1 output transition per cycle?","a":"NAND outputs P1 = 0.75, α = 3/16; Y = NOR: P1 = 1/16, α = 15/256.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Find the output probability, then require zero in the old cycle and one in the new cycle.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["3/16","1/16","15/256","30/256"],"ans":2,"why":["This is α01 for a two-input NAND with P1 = 3/4.","This is the probability of output one, not of a transition.","P1 = 1/16 and P0 = 15/16, so α01 = P0P1.","This counts transitions in both directions."],"ex":"Activity convention matters: α here counts only 0→1 events, as used in αCV²f.","oral":"4-input AND as NAND2-NAND2-NOR2 with P = 0.5 inputs: α at the NAND outputs and at Y?"},{"id":"V182","k":"md-eecs427-estimate-the-1-b-transistor-65-nm-chip-s-dynamic-power","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Activity-weighted dynamic power","q":"Using charging-event activities αlogic=0.10 and αmem=0.02, Clogic=27 nF, Cmem=171 nF, VDD=1 V and f=1 GHz, what is switching power?","a":"C_logic = 27 nF (α 0.1), C_mem = 171 nF (α 0.02), 1 V, 1 GHz → ~6.1 W.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Weight each capacitance by its own activity before summing.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["0.612 W","6.12 W","198 W","3.06 W"],"ans":1,"why":["This is a factor-of-ten arithmetic or units error.","(0.10×27+0.02×171)nF×1²×1GHz=6.12 W.","198 W ignores the stated activities and switches all capacitance each cycle.","The activity definition already counts charging events; an extra factor of one-half is incorrect."],"ex":"(0.10×27+0.02×171)nF×1²×1GHz=6.12 W.","oral":"Estimate the 1 B-transistor 65 nm chip's dynamic power."},{"id":"V183","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-a-latch-with-14c-on-data-and-2c-on-clock-beat-one-with-8c-8c","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Clock-load energy tradeoff","q":"Two latch options have (data capacitance, clock capacitance) of (14C,2C) and (8C,8C). With data activity 0.1 and clock activity 1 at equal VDD and frequency, which has lower weighted switching capacitance?","a":"Weighted by activity (0.1 and 1): 3.4C vs 8.8C switched per cycle.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Weight each capacitance by its own activity factor.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["They tie because each has total capacitance 16C","(14C,2C): 14.2C versus 8.8C","(8C,8C): 1.6C versus 16C","(14C,2C): 3.4C versus 8.8C"],"ans":3,"why":["Total unweighted capacitance ignores different toggle rates.","14.2C applies the data/clock weighting incorrectly to the first option.","This assigns inappropriate activities and fails to weight the clock separately.","0.1×14+1×2=3.4; 0.1×8+1×8=8.8."],"ex":"Clock capacitance is expensive because it switches every cycle. Move capacitance to a quieter input only when timing and functionality remain acceptable.","oral":"Why does a latch with 14C on data and 2C on clock beat one with 8C/8C?"},{"id":"V184","k":"md-eecs427-pipelined-vs-parallel-datapath-power-and-area","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Compare voltage-scaled architectures","q":"At equal throughput, suppose pipelining gives C′ = 1.15C, V′ = V/1.7, f′ = f; parallelism gives C′ = 2.15C, the same V′, and f′ = f/2. With equal activity, what are the dynamic-power ratios?","a":"Pipelined: V/1.7, C ×1.15, P = 0.39, area 1.3. Parallel: V/1.7, C ×2.15, f/2, P = 0.36, area 3.4. Both: 2.0 V, 0.2, area 3.7.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Keep capacitance, squared voltage and frequency factors separate.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 0.40 for pipelining and 0.74 for parallelism.","About 0.40 for pipelining and 0.37 for parallelism.","About 1.15 for pipelining and 2.15 for parallelism.","About 0.68 for pipelining and 0.63 for parallelism."],"ans":1,"why":["The parallel result omits its half-frequency operation.","Use C′/C × (V′/V)² × f′/f for each case.","These include capacitance but omit voltage and frequency changes.","These use voltage linearly instead of quadratically."],"ex":"1.15/1.7² = 0.398 and 2.15/(2 × 1.7²) = 0.372. Area and register overhead still matter to the architecture decision.","oral":"Pipelined vs parallel datapath power and area?"},{"id":"V185","k":"md-eecs427-which-direction-needs-a-level-converter-and-why","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Low-to-high level shifting","q":"A low-voltage logic high VDDL drives an ordinary inverter powered at VDDH. Assume VDDH−VDDL is enough to keep its PMOS conducting. What is the concern?","a":"VDDL→VDDH: the receiving pMOS sees |Vgs| = VDDH − VDDL and does not turn off. Step-down needs none.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Reference the receiver PMOS gate voltage to its own source supply.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The PMOS sees exactly zero VSG whenever the input is logically high","The low-domain output automatically rises to VDDH through the receiver gate","Only the low-to-high interconnect capacitance matters; DC levels are irrelevant","The receiving PMOS may not turn fully off, causing contention and requiring suitable level translation"],"ans":3,"why":["Its source is at VDDH, so VSG is VDDH−VDDL, not zero.","An insulated gate is not a level-boosting connection.","DC device bias determines whether both pull-up and pull-down can conduct.","A high recognized in one domain may leave pull-up conduction in the higher-voltage receiver."],"ex":"A high recognized in one domain may leave pull-up conduction in the higher-voltage receiver.","oral":"Which direction needs a level converter, and why?"},{"id":"V186","k":"md-eecs427-a-swing-reduced-by-vt-drop-devices-from-a-single-vdd-supply-saves-how-much","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Reduced swing from one supply","q":"A capacitor switches through ΔV using charge drawn from a fixed VDD supply through threshold-drop devices. Ignore other losses. Which supply-energy expression applies to one charging event?","a":"P = α·C·VDD·(VDD − 2Vt)·f - linear in swing, not quadratic.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Use source energy = supply voltage × delivered charge.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["E = C·VDD² independent of ΔV","E = C·VDD·ΔV","E = (C/VDD)·ΔV","E = C·(ΔV)² regardless of supply arrangement"],"ans":1,"why":["Reduced swing reduces the amount of charge drawn.","The supplied charge is CΔV and each unit of charge is drawn at VDD.","This expression has incorrect dimensions for energy.","Quadratic swing scaling requires a correspondingly reduced charging supply, not merely a drop from fixed VDD."],"ex":"For the source’s swing ΔV=VDD−2Vt, P=αC·VDD·(VDD−2Vt)f. The saving is linear in swing at fixed supply.","oral":"A swing reduced by Vt-drop devices from a single VDD supply saves how much?"},{"id":"V187","k":"md-eecs427-name-the-four-leakage-mechanisms-and-the-dominant-one","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Identify leakage through an off channel","q":"Which leakage component is conduction between source and drain when VGS is below threshold?","a":"I1 reverse-biased junction (with BTBT), I2 subthreshold (dominant), I3 GIDL, I4 gate-oxide tunneling.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish channel conduction from dielectric and junction paths.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Gate-oxide tunneling.","Subthreshold leakage.","Gate-induced drain leakage.","Reverse-biased junction leakage."],"ans":1,"why":["This is current through the gate dielectric.","Weak-inversion channel current continues below the nominal threshold.","GIDL involves high-field effects near the gate/drain region, not ordinary weak-inversion channel conduction.","This flows at a reverse-biased junction."],"ex":"Leakage comprises different physical paths. Their relative importance depends on the device technology and bias.","oral":"Name the four leakage mechanisms and the dominant one."},{"id":"V188","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-s-and-why-100-mv-decade","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Subthreshold swing","q":"For subthreshold factor n=1.5 and thermal voltage UT=26 mV, use S=n·UT·ln(10). What swing is predicted?","a":"S = n·vT·ln10 ≈ 1.5 × 26 mV × 2.3 ≈ 90-100 mV per decade of current; 10× leakage per 100 mV of Vt.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"The logarithm base matters when converting an exponential slope to decades.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 60 mV per decade","About 90 mV per decade","About 39 mV per decade","About 150 mV per decade"],"ans":1,"why":["Approximately 60 mV corresponds to the ideal n≈1 room-temperature limit, not n=1.5.","1.5×26×ln(10)=89.8 mV for a tenfold current change.","39 mV omits conversion from natural exponential to decades.","150 mV is not the product of the supplied factors."],"ex":"1.5×26×ln(10)=89.8 mV for a tenfold current change.","oral":"What is S, and why ~100 mV/decade?"},{"id":"V189","k":"md-eecs427-from-the-roundup-plot-how-much-does-dibl-change-ioff-between-vds-0-1-v-and-1-0-v","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Leakage ratio from measured plot values","q":"A particular plotted device has Ioff=2.0 nA/µm at VDS=0.1 V and 27 nA/µm at VDS=1.0 V. What is the leakage increase factor?","a":"2.0 → 27 nA/µm, about 13× (η = 0.1 with S = 100 mV/dec predicts 10^0.9 ≈ 8×; the plotted device has stronger DIBL).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"A factor compares final current to initial current, not their difference.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["1.35×","0.074×","13.5×","25×"],"ans":2,"why":["This loses a factor of ten.","0.074 is the inverse ratio.","27/2.0=13.5.","25 is the difference in nA/µm, not the multiplicative ratio."],"ex":"Use the plotted values for this device: the increase is 13.5×. A simplified DIBL model need not exactly reproduce the plotted ratio.","oral":"From the roundup plot, how much does DIBL change Ioff between Vds = 0.1 V and 1.0 V?"},{"id":"V190","k":"md-eecs427-derive-the-2-stack-leakage","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Explain off-stack leakage reduction","q":"Two series NMOS devices are both off. Why can their combined leakage be lower than a single off device?","a":"Equate currents: Vx = ηVDD/(1 + 2η + kγ); I ≈ Ioff·10^(−ηVDD/S) ≈ 0.1 Ioff for η = 0.1, VDD = 1 V. Mechanisms: negative Vgs, reverse body bias, reduced DIBL on the top device.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Compute the top device’s gate-to-source voltage when its source rises.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The intermediate node rises, reducing top-device VGS and VDS and, in bulk devices, increasing reverse body bias.","The intermediate node forces both devices into strong inversion.","The top device acquires a positive gate overdrive equal to VDD.","Series stacking doubles every device’s VDS."],"ans":0,"why":["These bias shifts weaken subthreshold conduction and reduce DIBL.","The devices remain off; the stack alters their off-state biases.","With the gate low and source elevated, top-device VGS becomes negative.","The supply is shared rather than doubled across devices."],"ex":"Stack benefit comes from changed terminal voltages, not simply from adding two fixed resistances.","oral":"Derive the 2-stack leakage."},{"id":"V191","k":"md-eecs427-which-nand3-input-state-leaks-most-and-why","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Leakage characterization and stack effects","q":"For a static NAND3 with inputs 111 and output low, which off-network leakage path is present?","a":"111: three OFF pMOS in parallel, 192 pA. Least: 000 (three-stack), 10.5 pA. Dominant states have only one OFF device in a path.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Determine which network is on and which network must block the supply.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Three off NMOS devices stacked between output and ground","No off-device voltage because every device has zero VDS","Three off PMOS paths in parallel between VDD and the low output","One off PMOS in series with two on NMOS as the only supply path"],"ans":2,"why":["The NMOS gates are all high, so they are not an off stack.","Each off PMOS has substantial rail-to-output bias.","All NMOS are on and all PMOS are off; each PMOS can leak from VDD to the low output.","The pull-up topology is parallel PMOS, not the stated mixed series chain."],"ex":"All NMOS are on and all PMOS are off; each PMOS can leak from VDD to the low output.","oral":"Which NAND3 input state leaks most and why?"},{"id":"V192","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-block-leakage-vary-only-1-21-8-with-input-vector-while-a-nand4-varies-101","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Cell leakage versus block leakage spread","q":"Why can a NAND4 show a much larger input-state leakage ratio than a large block’s ratio across external input vectors?","a":"Averaging over many gates whose states are correlated and mixed; still 30-40% is worth recovering by forcing a min-leakage vector in sleep.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish one gate’s extreme states from a whole block’s simultaneously reachable states.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Leakage cancels algebraically between positive and negative gate currents","A block’s transistors lose their state dependence once connected together","The block must always equal the maximum leakage ratio of its worst individual gate","The block sums many gates in different correlated states, so their leakage extrema need not align"],"ans":3,"why":["Supply leakage contributions do not cancel as signed logic values.","State-dependent device leakage remains present.","A ratio of sums is not generally the maximum individual ratio.","Mixed internal states dilute the alignment of individual-gate best and worst cases."],"ex":"Circuit-state correlations and averaging reduce the observed block-level spread without making vector-dependent leakage irrelevant.","oral":"Why does block leakage vary only 1.2-1.8× with input vector while a NAND4 varies 101×?"},{"id":"V193","k":"md-eecs427-mtcmos-trade-offs","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Decide when power gating saves energy","q":"A high-Vt sleep switch reduces standby leakage. Which additional requirement determines whether a short idle interval actually saves net energy?","a":"High-Vt switches cut standby leakage by orders of magnitude, but cost IR drop (size wide), area, wake-up energy/time (break-even sleep time), isolation cells, and lost state unless retention latches are used.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Compare joules saved while asleep with joules spent changing state.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Clock gating makes the sleep switch’s leakage savings unnecessary because clock gating removes static leakage too.","A lower standby current alone proves that every idle interval saves energy.","A fast nominal wake-up waveform proves that retention and rail-droop checks can be omitted.","Idle leakage savings must exceed sleep-entry and wake-up energy."],"ans":3,"why":["Clock gating mainly removes clock-driven activity, not all leakage paths.","Short intervals may not repay the switching and restoration overhead.","Wake-up speed does not establish state preservation or safe rail behavior.","A transition overhead creates an energy break-even duration."],"ex":"Power gating trades leakage against transition energy, wake-up time, rail drop, area and state handling.","oral":"MTCMOS trade-offs?"},{"id":"V194","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-dual-vt-easier-to-deploy-than-multi-vdd","d":"pwr","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Dual-threshold design","q":"Why is swapping noncritical cells to a higher-Vt flavor often simpler than introducing a new supply-voltage island?","a":"No level converters and no clustering; cells can be swapped individually on non-critical paths.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Compare a device-flavor change with a change in the voltage used to represent logic.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A new voltage island never requires physical clustering","A Vt swap always has zero timing cost","Compatible Vt swaps can preserve supply and interface levels without new level-shifter boundaries","Higher Vt changes logic-high voltage to a new power domain"],"ans":2,"why":["Voltage islands require deliberate supply distribution and physical/domain organization.","Higher Vt can slow paths, so available slack must be checked.","A footprint-compatible same-supply swap avoids the additional voltage-domain interface and distribution problem.","Threshold flavor changes device behavior, not the cell’s supply rail by itself."],"ex":"A footprint-compatible same-supply swap avoids the additional voltage-domain interface and distribution problem.","oral":"Why is dual-Vt easier to deploy than multi-VDD?"},{"id":"V195","k":"md-eecs427-define-the-cell-ratio-and-the-pull-up-ratio-and-say-which-operation-each-protects","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"SRAM strength ratios","q":"For a conventional 6T SRAM, which qualitative strength requirements protect read stability and write-0 ability?","a":"CR = (W/L)pull-down / (W/L)access protects **read stability**. It keeps the read bump on the 0 node below the opposite inverter's trip point (N1 > N2). PR = (W/L)pull-up / (W/L)access protects **writability**. The access device must pull the 1 node low against the PMOS (N4 > P2). The sizing order is pull-down > access > pull-up.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Trace current into the stored-zero node during read and out of the stored-one node during write.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["All six devices must have identical strength for read and write margins","Access must dominate pulldown during read; pull-up must dominate access during write","Pulldown must resist access-induced read bump; access must overcome the opposing pull-up during write","Pull-up must dominate every other device in both operations"],"ans":2,"why":["Equal widths or strengths are not a universal SRAM margin solution.","This favors read disturb and failed writes.","Read and write impose opposing ratio constraints on the access device.","An excessively strong pull-up can prevent writing the high node low."],"ex":"Read stability compares pulldown to access; writeability compares access to pull-up. Actual ratios require device models and corner checks.","oral":"Define the cell ratio and the pull-up ratio, and say which operation each protects."},{"id":"V196","k":"md-eecs427-walk-through-a-read-of-a-0-which-bitline-moves-and-what-is-the-danger","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Trace an SRAM read-zero disturbance","q":"In a 6T cell storing A = 0 and Abar = 1, both bitlines are initially high. When WL rises, what occurs on the A side?","a":"Both bitlines are precharged to VDD, then WL rises. bit discharges through the access N2 and pull-down N1, and bit_b stays high. Node A rises slightly (the divider between N2 and N1). If it crosses the trip point of the A_b inverter, the cell flips and the read is destructive.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Follow current from the precharged bitline to ground.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Abar is forced low directly by a write driver during the read.","Its bitline rises above VDD while A remains exactly zero.","Its bitline discharges through access and pull-down devices while A rises slightly.","Both bitlines discharge equally regardless of stored data."],"ans":2,"why":["A read has no intended write-driver forcing of the opposite node.","There is no ideal mechanism driving the bitline above precharge, and A is disturbed.","The access/pull-down path removes bitline charge and creates a storage-node divider.","The stored state makes the two read paths asymmetric."],"ex":"The read-zero side provides the discharge path. Excessive rise of A can trigger the opposite inverter and upset the cell.","oral":"Walk through a read of A = 0. Which bitline moves, and what is the danger?"},{"id":"V197","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-a-6t-cell-written-by-pulling-the-1-node-low-rather-than-pushing-the-0-node-high","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"SRAM read and write strength ratios","q":"Why does a conventional 6T SRAM write-zero primarily rely on pulling the stored-high node down?","a":"The NMOS access device passes a weak 1. More importantly, the cell ratio is designed so the access device cannot raise the 0 node, since that is read stability. So the write must come from the bitline driven to 0 overpowering the weak PMOS pull-up.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Compare which cell device opposes writing low with which device opposes read disturb.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The access NMOS can pass a strong zero and overpower the pull-up, while read sizing resists raising the opposite low node","The access NMOS passes a stronger one than zero","The sense amplifier first disables both storage inverters","The cell pull-down is intentionally weaker than access during every read"],"ans":0,"why":["Write uses the access-versus-pull-up contest; trying to raise the low node fights the pull-down designed for read stability.","An NMOS pass device transmits zero strongly and a threshold-limited high.","Ordinary 6T writing does not require a sense amplifier to disable the inverters.","Read stability usually requires the pull-down to resist access-induced low-node rise."],"ex":"Write uses the access-versus-pull-up contest; trying to raise the low node fights the pull-down designed for read stability.","oral":"Why is a 6T cell written by pulling the 1 node low rather than pushing the 0 node high?"},{"id":"V198","k":"md-eecs427-how-is-the-static-noise-margin-defined-from-the-butterfly-curve-and-what-was-it-on-the-slide","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Butterfly static noise margin","q":"How is SRAM static noise margin obtained from a butterfly plot of two cross-coupled inverter transfer characteristics?","a":"Overlay one inverter's VTC with the other's mirrored VTC. The SNM is the side of the largest square that fits inside the smaller lobe, which equals the series noise voltage that eliminates one stable state. On the slide it was 0.32 V at VDD = 1.0 V. Read SNM, with WL on, is smaller than hold SNM.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Look for a voltage margin rather than an area or gain.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The horizontal distance between the two rail-voltage endpoints","The maximum small-signal slope of either inverter alone","The total area enclosed by both lobes","The side of the largest square that fits in the smaller lobe"],"ans":3,"why":["Rail separation ignores the inner stability boundary.","Gain contributes to regeneration but is not the butterfly SNM construction.","Area has units of voltage squared and is not the SNM voltage.","This construction measures the limiting tolerable static series-noise voltage."],"ex":"Overlay one VTC with the mirrored other VTC and use the limiting lobe. Read SNM must be evaluated with access loading present.","oral":"How is the static noise margin defined from the butterfly curve, and what was it on the slide?"},{"id":"V199","k":"md-eecs427-for-the-65-nm-cell-pull-down-130-65-pull-up-115-65-access-100-75-compute-cr-and-pr-why-is-the-access-l-75-nm","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Compute SRAM geometric ratios","q":"Define CR = (W/L)pull-down/(W/L)access and PR = (W/L)pull-up/(W/L)access. With dimensions 130/65, 115/65 and 100/75 nm respectively, what are CR and PR?","a":"CR = 2.0/1.33 ≈ 1.5 and PR = 1.77/1.33 ≈ 1.33. The longer access L weakens the pass gate without going below minimum width, which raises CR for read stability.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Include channel lengths before comparing widths.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["CR = 1.30; PR = 1.15.","CR = 2.00; PR ≈ 1.77.","CR = 1.50; PR ≈ 1.33.","CR ≈ 0.67; PR ≈ 0.75."],"ans":2,"why":["These use widths alone and miss the access device’s longer channel.","These are individual W/L values, not ratios to access.","Divide each W/L ratio by access W/L = 4/3.","These invert the specified ratios."],"ex":"CR = 2/(4/3) = 1.5; PR = (23/13)/(4/3) = 69/52 ≈ 1.327. These are geometric ratios, not mobility-adjusted current ratios.","oral":"For the 65 nm cell (pull-down 130/65, pull-up 115/65, access 100/75), compute CR and PR. Why is the access L 75 nm?"},{"id":"V200","k":"md-eecs427-why-do-memories-use-sense-amplifiers-and-why-is-the-sense-amp-clocked-with-isolation-devices","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Small-swing bitline sensing","q":"Why can a clocked sense amplifier with bitline isolation read faster than waiting for a full bitline swing?","a":"tpd ∝ C·ΔV/I. A bitline with 128 cells has big C (64C of shared diffusion) and is discharged by a tiny cell current, so the only knob is a small ΔV. A clocked sense amp burns power only when fired. It must fire after enough swing, and the isolation transistors disconnect the heavy bitlines so the regenerative latch resolves quickly.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Separate bitline signal development from the subsequent regenerative decision.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It makes the bitcell pull-down current infinite during sensing","It proves the sense-amplifier offset is exactly zero","It eliminates the need to develop any differential before enable","It regenerates a sufficient small differential after disconnecting much of the heavy bitline load"],"ans":3,"why":["Sensing does not remove the finite selected-cell current.","Mismatch can create offset even in a symmetric nominal schematic.","Offset and noise still require a sufficient decision margin before sensing.","Small ΔV reduces CΔV/I development time; isolation lets the regenerative nodes resolve with lower attached capacitance."],"ex":"Small ΔV reduces CΔV/I development time; isolation lets the regenerative nodes resolve with lower attached capacitance.","oral":"Why do memories use sense amplifiers, and why is the sense amp clocked with isolation devices?"},{"id":"V201","k":"md-eecs427-name-two-read-assists-and-two-write-assists-why-can-t-they-be-on-all-the-time","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Read and write assist conflict","q":"Why is lowering the wordline during a 6T SRAM read generally not applied as a permanent write assist too?","a":"Read: lower the WL voltage, or raise the cell VDD during read. Write: a negative bitline, raising the WL, or lowering or floating the cell VDD/GND. They push the same knobs in opposite directions (WL level, cell supply), so each is applied only during its own operation.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Compare the role of the access device when preserving a bit and when changing it.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It strengthens the access device in both read and write","It weakens access-induced read disturb but also weakens the access path needed to overwrite the cell","It permanently removes the cell’s stored charge","Read and write require the access transistor to remain off throughout"],"ans":1,"why":["Lower gate voltage weakens an NMOS access device.","The same access strength has opposite implications for stability and writeability.","An appropriately timed assist need not destroy retained state.","Both operations require a conducting access path during the access interval."],"ex":"Assist timing matters because read and write optimize different current competitions. Apply operation-specific bias changes rather than one permanent setting.","oral":"Name two read assists and two write assists. Why can't they be on all the time?"},{"id":"V202","k":"md-eecs427-a-2k-16-memory-is-folded-into-256-128-how-many-column-muxes-what-size-and-what-are-the-benefits-of-a-hierarchical-organization","d":"mem","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Fold a memory array","q":"A 2,048-word × 16-bit memory is organized physically as 256 rows × 128 columns. What column selection is needed for each 16-bit output?","a":"128/16 = 8, so sixteen 8:1 muxes (3 column-address bits, 8 row bits). Hierarchy shortens the wires inside each block and activates only the addressed block, which saves power. The mux can be an NMOS-only tree with precharged outputs, since it only has to pass a discharging 0.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Preserve both the total number of stored bits and the output width.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Sixteen 8:1 muxes, using 3 column-address bits.","128 8:1 muxes, using 8 column-address bits.","Sixteen 4:1 muxes, using 2 column-address bits.","Eight 16:1 muxes, using 4 column-address bits."],"ans":0,"why":["128 physical columns / 16 output bits = 8 choices per output.","There are sixteen output bits; 8 is the row-address width, not column width.","Four choices would cover only 64 physical columns.","This produces only eight outputs and the wrong grouping."],"ex":"Eight row-address bits choose one of 256 rows and three column-address bits choose one of eight words within that row.","oral":"A 2k × 16 memory is folded into 256 × 128. How many column muxes, what size, and what are the benefits of a hierarchical organization?"},{"id":"V203","k":"md-eecs427-derive-the-setup-and-hold-constraints-for-r1-logic-r2-with-skew-tclk2-tclk1","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Setup and hold inequalities","q":"With skew δ=tCLK,capture−tCLK,launch and no uncertainty, which pair of timing conditions is correct?","a":"Setup: data launched at 0 arrives at tc-q + tlogic,max and must precede R2's next edge at T + δ by tsu, so T ≥ tc-q + tlogic + tsu − δ. Hold: the same edge reaches R2 at δ, so the fastest new data must arrive after δ + thold, giving tc-q,cd + tlogic,cd > thold + δ. With cycle-to-cycle jitter, add 2tj to the right-hand side of each (per the annotation).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Draw the launch edge, same-cycle capture edge and next-cycle capture edge separately.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["tcq,max+tlogic,max+tsu ≤ T+δ; tcq,min+tlogic,min ≥ T+thold+δ","tcq,max+tlogic,max+tsu ≤ T+δ; tcq,min+tlogic,min ≥ thold+δ","tcq,max+tlogic,max+tsu ≤ δ; tcq,min+tlogic,min ≥ T+thold","tcq,max+tlogic,max+tsu ≤ T−δ; tcq,min+tlogic,min ≥ thold−δ"],"ans":1,"why":["Period does not enter the same-edge hold race.","Setup targets the next capture edge at T+δ; hold protects the same-edge window through δ+thold.","Setup needs the next-cycle period, while the hold check does not gain one.","This reverses the stated skew convention in both checks."],"ex":"Setup targets the next capture edge at T+δ; hold protects the same-edge window through δ+thold.","oral":"Derive the setup and hold constraints for R1 → logic → R2 with skew δ = tCLK2 − tCLK1."},{"id":"V204","k":"md-eecs427-a-chip-comes-back-from-the-fab-with-a-hold-violation-can-you-ship-it-at-a-lower-frequency","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Can a slower clock repair hold?","q":"For a same-edge flip-flop hold violation with unchanged clock skew and data delays, does lowering frequency repair the violation?","a":"No. T does not appear in the hold inequality; the race is between one clock edge reaching R2 and the fastest data launched by that same edge. Hold must be fixed in design: add delay on the short path, reduce δ, or use a flop with smaller hold or larger tc-q,cd.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Write the hold inequality and check whether T appears.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Yes; a longer period directly adds hold slack","Yes; setup and hold share exactly the same period term","Only if the new period is an integer multiple of the old period","No; add minimum data-path delay or change the relevant skew/hold requirement"],"ans":3,"why":["The period appears in next-edge setup, not this hold inequality.","Setup and hold protect different timing boundaries.","An integer ratio does not change the same-edge race.","Hold compares events launched and captured around the same clock edge."],"ex":"Hold slack depends on minimum arrival time, capture skew, hold requirement and uncertainty. Frequency reduction alone leaves those unchanged.","oral":"A chip comes back from the fab with a hold violation. Can you ship it at a lower frequency?"},{"id":"V205","k":"md-eecs427-is-positive-skew-good-or-bad","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"State the skew convention","q":"Define skew S = capture clock arrival − launch clock arrival. With all other terms fixed, increasing S by 20 ps does what?","a":"Both. It gives the setup path δ of extra time (good for performance) and makes hold δ harder. Negative skew does the opposite. Unpredictable skew (uncertainty) is bad for both.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Draw both clock arrival times before assigning a sign.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Improves setup slack by 20 ps and worsens hold slack by 20 ps.","Worsens setup slack by 20 ps and improves hold slack by 20 ps.","Leaves hold slack unchanged while improving setup.","Improves both setup and hold slack by 20 ps."],"ans":0,"why":["Later capture gives the maximum-delay path time but lets new data threaten the same capture edge.","This corresponds to reducing S under the stated convention.","Same-edge hold explicitly depends on capture-minus-launch skew.","The two checks have opposite signs for S."],"ex":"Setup slack contains +S; hold slack contains −S. Always state the skew sign convention.","oral":"Is positive skew good or bad?"},{"id":"V206","k":"md-eecs427-how-do-you-measure-setup-time-in-spice-and-why-not-just-find-the-pass-fail-point","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Clock-to-Q pushout calculation","q":"A course defines setup by 5% clock-to-Q pushout. If nominal tcq is 60 ps, what maximum tcq meets that criterion, provided the output is correct?","a":"Sweep the D transition toward the clock edge and watch tc-q. Setup is the D-to-CLK time at which tc-q has grown about 5% over nominal (the CAD2 / Discussion 2 definition). Hold is measured the same way with a second D toggle swept back toward the edge. At the pass/fail point tc-q is so large that the next stage fails, so the pushout criterion trades a bit of setup for a bounded tc-q. Measure rise and fall separately.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Convert the percentage into a fraction of the nominal delay.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["65 ps","60.05 ps","Any tcq before the next clock period","63 ps"],"ans":3,"why":["Adding a literal 5 ps confuses percentage with absolute time.","A 5% increase is 3 ps, not 0.05 ps.","The stated characterization bound is stricter than eventual capture before a later edge.","1.05×60 ps=63 ps is the specified delay bound."],"ex":"1.05×60 ps=63 ps is the specified delay bound.","oral":"How do you measure setup time in SPICE, and why not just find the pass/fail point?"},{"id":"V207","k":"md-eecs427-what-does-a-negative-hold-time-mean-and-how-can-it-happen","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Meaning of negative hold time","q":"A characterized flip-flop has hold time −10 ps under specified conditions. What does this permit at its external data pin, while other timing requirements remain met?","a":"D may change slightly before the clock edge and the old value is still captured. It happens when the internal data path (for example an input inverter before the master TG) is slower than the clock path to the device that closes the master, so the change is still in flight when the master closes. The CAD2 FAQ notes it is legitimately possible.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish external pin timing from arrival at the internal storage node.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Data must remain stable for 10 ps after the clock edge","Data may start changing as early as 10 ps before the reference clock edge without corrupting the old captured value","The device has no setup requirement","Clock-to-Q propagation must be negative"],"ans":1,"why":["That describes positive 10 ps hold.","A delayed internal data path can keep the new value from reaching storage before the internal gate closes.","Setup is a separate constraint and still applies.","An external hold constraint is not clock-to-Q propagation time."],"ex":"Negative external hold can result from relative internal clock and data delays; it does not imply causality violation or unrestricted input timing.","oral":"What does a negative hold time mean, and how can it happen?"},{"id":"V208","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-the-c2mos-register-insensitive-to-clk-clk-overlap-while-the-tg-flop-is-not","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Understand C²MOS overlap tolerance","q":"With sharp clock edges, why can a C²MOS register resist the simple race-through seen in a TG flop during clock/complement overlap?","a":"During 0-0 overlap only the PMOS clock devices conduct, and during 1-1 only the NMOS ones. Getting through two inverting stages would need a pull-up in one stage and a pull-down in the other, so data cannot race through, provided clock edges are sharp. In a TG flop, 1-1 overlap turns on both NMOS halves and data can race straight through.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Trace the device polarity required by consecutive inverting stages.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The immunity comes solely from making the data path faster than the clock overlap.","The same overlap immunity holds for arbitrarily slow clock edges because logic truth tables are unchanged.","Overlap enables only pull-ups or only pull-downs, while propagation through two inverting stages needs opposite types in succession.","Local generation guarantees zero clock/complement overlap regardless of slew."],"ans":2,"why":["A very fast transparent path can increase race risk; topology provides the stated protection.","Slow-edge analog conduction can invalidate the sharp-edge protection argument.","A single-polarity enabled path cannot perform both required inversions.","Local generation has finite delay and slew."],"ex":"The protection depends on the clocked-inverter structure and suitable edge slew; it should not be generalized to arbitrary slow waveforms.","oral":"Why is the C²MOS register insensitive to clk/!clk overlap, while the TG flop is not?"},{"id":"V209","k":"md-eecs427-for-a-pulsed-latch-with-pulse-width-tpw-what-happens-to-setup-and-hold-and-what-is-the-main-design-risk","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Pulsed-latch setup and hold","q":"What tradeoff follows from widening the transparency pulse of a pulsed latch?","a":"Setup can become negative (data may arrive during the pulse), hold rises to about tpw, and tc-q is one latch delay. The risk is the pulse width across PVT: too narrow and the latch is not written at the slow corner, too wide and hold races at the fast corner.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Reason from how long the storage element remains transparent.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["More time to capture late data, but a longer interval vulnerable to short-path races","Guaranteed improvement in both setup and hold for every path","Removal of all PVT sensitivity from latch capture","A shorter hold requirement and less time for slow data to enter"],"ans":0,"why":["A longer open interval can ease setup but allows new data to race through for longer, tightening hold protection.","The setup benefit competes with the hold cost.","Pulse width and latch behavior still vary across PVT.","This reverses the effects of keeping the latch open longer."],"ex":"A longer open interval can ease setup but allows new data to race through for longer, tightening hold protection.","oral":"For a pulsed latch with pulse width tpw, what happens to setup and hold, and what is the main design risk?"},{"id":"V210","k":"md-eecs427-list-the-flip-flop-robustness-rules-from-the-lecture","d":"seq","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Storage-node robustness","q":"Which change most directly reduces capacitive interference at a flip-flop’s sensitive internal storage node?","a":"Buffer the input (no pass transistor directly at D); no floating nodes (use pseudo-static storage); give storage nodes enough capacitance for noise immunity, but not so much that the flop slows down; keep storage-node wires short to limit coupling. Also keep the feedback path weak so the flop is writable.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Identify which geometry controls unwanted injected charge.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Keep its interconnect short and limit coupling to neighboring switching nets","Route it beside a long high-activity clock wire to maximize shared capacitance","Increase storage capacitance without limit, regardless of write delay","Remove every restoring feedback path so injected charge cannot circulate"],"ans":0,"why":["Short, carefully routed storage nets reduce coupled charge and exposure.","More coupling to an aggressor increases disturbance.","Capacitance can improve charge margin but unbounded loading prevents timely writing.","Removing restoration leaves state more vulnerable to charge loss and noise."],"ex":"Storage robustness balances noise margin, restoration and write delay; it is not achieved by maximizing one parameter indiscriminately.","oral":"List the flip-flop robustness rules from the lecture."},{"id":"V211","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-a-carry-save-adder-s-delay-independent-of-word-width","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Carry-save width scaling","q":"Why does one carry-save reduction level avoid a carry-propagation delay proportional to word width?","a":"It is N full adders with no carry chain between them; each bit's sum and carry depend only on that bit's three inputs, so one FA delay per level.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish a reduction level from the final binary addition.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Each bit computes local sum and carry outputs without waiting for a carry from its neighbor.","A carry-save level has logarithmic delay because it contains an internal parallel-prefix carry tree.","It removes carry storage and reconstructs discarded carries from the sum bits later.","Its final binary output is already resolved, so no later carry-propagate conversion is needed."],"ans":0,"why":["The two-vector representation avoids a word-wide propagation dependency within the level.","A local carry-save reduction level is not a prefix carry computation.","The carry vector is retained, shifted by its significance.","A conventional final sum usually still requires carry propagation."],"ex":"Carry-save arithmetic trades a redundant representation for local per-level computation.","oral":"Why is a carry-save adder's delay independent of word width?"},{"id":"V212","k":"md-eecs427-delay-of-adding-k-n-bit-numbers-with-chained-cpas-vs-a-csa-tree-plus-one-cpa","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Carry-save accumulation","q":"Why does carry-save reduction help when adding many operands?","a":"Chained: about (k − 1)·N; CSA approach: about (k − 2) + N (annotation: N + k).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Identify which operation is postponed instead of repeated for every operand.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It avoids propagating carry across the full word at every reduction step, leaving one final carry-propagate addition","It makes addition delay independent of both word width and operand count","It eliminates the final carry-propagate adder for every output format","It reduces all partial sums to a single vector after each compressor"],"ans":0,"why":["Compressors preserve value as sum and carry vectors, postponing the long carry propagation to the end.","Tree depth and final-adder width still affect delay.","A conventional binary result still needs final carry propagation or equivalent conversion.","A carry-save representation retains multiple weighted vectors rather than one ordinary binary sum."],"ex":"Compressors preserve value as sum and carry vectors, postponing the long carry propagation to the end.","oral":"Delay of adding k N-bit numbers with chained CPAs vs a CSA tree plus one CPA?"},{"id":"V213","k":"md-eecs427-why-does-radix-4-multiplication-need-booth-encoding","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Radix-4 Booth partial products","q":"What implementation benefit does radix-4 Booth recoding provide over unsigned digits 0,1,2,3?","a":"Plain radix-4 needs 0, Y, 2Y, 3Y, and 3Y needs an adder. Booth rewrites 3Y = 4Y − Y and 2Y = 4Y − 2Y, pushing the 4Y into the next digit, so only 0, ±Y, ±2Y (shifts and inversions) are needed.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Ask which conventional radix-4 multiple cannot be made by a shift alone.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It replaces a required 3Y multiple with signed digits 0, ±Y and ±2Y, obtainable by selection, shifts and negation","It removes all carries from the final product sum","It guarantees every partial product is nonnegative","It requires a dedicated 3Y adder at every digit"],"ans":0,"why":["Recoding carries weight into the next digit so an explicit 3Y generator is unnecessary.","Partial products still need summation and final carry propagation.","Negative partial products are central to Booth recoding.","Avoiding explicit 3Y generation is the benefit."],"ex":"The allowed signed multiples are simple to generate; the recoding and sign handling preserve the original product.","oral":"Why does radix-4 multiplication need Booth encoding?"},{"id":"V214","k":"md-eecs427-give-the-booth-digit-for-multiplier-bits-x2i-1-x2i-x2i1-011-and-101","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Radix-4 Booth recoding","q":"For radix-4 modified Booth recoding with triples ordered (x2i+1, x2i, x2i−1), which multiples of Y correspond to 011 and 101?","a":"011 → +2Y (DOUBLE); 101 → −Y (SINGLE, NEG).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Apply the weights −2, +1 and +1 to the triple.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["−2Y and +Y","+Y and −2Y","+2Y and −Y","+2Y and +Y"],"ans":2,"why":["Both signs are reversed.","Both magnitudes are swapped.","Using digit = −2x2i+1 + x2i + x2i−1 gives +2 and −1.","The leading one in 101 contributes −2, giving a negative digit."],"ex":"Evaluate the signed recoding digit for each overlapping three-bit group; the leading bit carries weight −2.","oral":"Give the Booth digit for multiplier bits (x2i+1, x2i, x2i−1) = 011 and 101."},{"id":"V215","k":"md-eecs427-how-is-a-negative-partial-product-formed-in-hardware","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Negative Booth partial products","q":"How is a negative Booth partial product formed in a fixed-width two’s-complement implementation?","a":"Invert the selected bits (XOR with NEG) and add 1 in the LSB position of the next row.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Recall the arithmetic identity for −x before applying the partial-product shift.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Complement the selected magnitude bits and add the correctly aligned correction one","Negate the final product without accounting for each shifted partial product","Set only the most-significant bit and leave every other bit unchanged","Complement the bits and omit the correction one"],"ans":0,"why":["Two’s-complement negation is bitwise complement plus one, with shift and sign-extension alignment preserved.","Independent negative partial products need their own correctly weighted correction.","Changing a sign bit is not two’s-complement arithmetic negation.","Complement alone gives one’s complement and is one count off at that alignment."],"ex":"Two’s-complement negation is bitwise complement plus one, with shift and sign-extension alignment preserved.","oral":"How is a negative partial product formed in hardware?"},{"id":"V216","k":"md-eecs427-what-is-the-sign-extension-trick","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Sign-extension reduction","q":"What is the purpose of the partial-product sign-extension trick described in the lecture?","a":"An all-0s or all-1s extension equals all 1s plus 1 in the right column; replace the extension with constant 1s and s̄, then precompute the sum of all constants so only a few sign bits per row remain.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Separate removing redundant hardware from removing signed information.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Discard high sign bits without compensating the represented value","Convert signed multiplication into unsigned multiplication without any recoding","Force all negative partial products to zero before summation","Replace long repeated sign fields with equivalent constant corrections and a few sign-dependent bits"],"ans":3,"why":["Dropping extension bits alone changes a negative operand’s numeric value.","Signed information still has to be represented in the corrected partial products.","Zeroing negative partial products changes the product.","Algebraic corrections preserve the value while reducing repeated sign-bit additions."],"ex":"The optimization changes how sign-extension contributions are encoded and summed, not the mathematical value of each product row.","oral":"What is the sign-extension trick?"},{"id":"V217","k":"md-eecs427-why-do-4-2-compressor-trees-often-beat-wallace-3-2-trees-in-practice","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Why compressor layout matters","q":"Why can a well-designed 4:2 compressor tree outperform an irregular 3:2 Wallace tree in a placed-and-routed multiplier?","a":"The lateral carry does not ripple, the tree is binary (more regular layout and wiring), and each level halves the height.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Consider routing regularity in addition to gate count.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Its final product never requires a carry-propagate conversion.","A 4:2 tree is faster solely because every transistor count is lower than in any 3:2 implementation.","Regular reduction and wiring can reduce physical overhead without a word-wide lateral carry ripple.","Its carry outputs may ripple across the full word without affecting delay."],"ans":2,"why":["Final binary result conversion generally remains necessary.","Cell implementation and wiring must be compared; transistor-count superiority is not guaranteed.","Wire regularity and controlled carry structure can improve real timing.","A word-wide serial carry would add a width-dependent delay."],"ex":"A theoretically shallow tree can lose its advantage to wiring and placement; compare physical implementations rather than labels alone.","oral":"Why do 4:2 compressor trees often beat Wallace 3:2 trees in practice?"},{"id":"V218","k":"md-eecs427-why-should-the-final-cpa-be-designed-knowing-the-tree-s-arrival-profile","d":"arith","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Arrival-aware final addition","q":"A multiplier reduction tree produces early outer columns and late middle columns. Why should the final carry-propagate adder use that arrival profile?","a":"The middle columns arrive last; the CPA can ripple through early-arriving bits and spend its fast prefix logic only where bits arrive late.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"The critical path includes input arrival time as well as the delay inside the final adder.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Arrival time affects only power and cannot affect final-adder delay","All bits must be treated as simultaneous or the arithmetic changes","The latest input can be ignored if enough outer bits arrive early","It can spend fast carry resources where inputs arrive late and let earlier bits use slower local propagation"],"ans":3,"why":["Output timing includes when each input reaches the carry/sum network.","Arithmetic correctness does not require identical arrival times.","Every relevant late bit must still reach the final result correctly.","Nonuniform arrival times change the useful allocation of carry architecture and timing budget."],"ex":"Nonuniform arrival times change the useful allocation of carry architecture and timing budget.","oral":"Why should the final CPA be designed knowing the tree's arrival profile?"},{"id":"V219","k":"md-eecs427-why-do-modern-wires-have-ar-2-and-what-is-the-side-effect","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Tall narrow interconnect","q":"Why can increasing metal height relative to width help routing density yet worsen coupling concerns?","a":"Tall narrow wires pack more tracks at acceptable resistance; the large sidewall makes coupling capacitance a big fraction of total C.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Think separately about current-carrying area and electric-field-facing area.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Height preserves cross-sectional area while narrow width packs tracks; larger sidewalls increase neighboring-wire capacitance","Narrower wires always reduce both resistance and coupling regardless of spacing","Tall wires remove the need for ground-referenced capacitance","Height reduces cross-sectional area and eliminates sidewall capacitance"],"ans":0,"why":["Resistance benefits from conductor area while lateral electrostatic coupling depends on facing surfaces and spacing.","Width reduction alone raises resistance and coupling depends on geometry.","Capacitance to other conductors remains present.","Increasing height does not eliminate sidewalls."],"ex":"Wire geometry must balance resistance, track pitch and the relative importance of sidewall coupling.","oral":"Why do modern wires have AR ≈ 2, and what is the side effect?"},{"id":"V220","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-the-l-model-crossed-out-and-the-model-boxed","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Choose a wire model with the right moment","q":"For a uniform wire with total R and C, driven by an ideal source with no extra load, which simple model matches the far-end Elmore moment RC/2?","a":"The L-model puts all C at the far end, giving Elmore = RC (2× pessimistic); the π-model gives RC/2, matching the distributed line.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Weight each capacitance by the resistance shared with the observation path.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["An L model with all C at the far end.","A model with all C at the source end and no far-end capacitance.","A π model with C at each end and the same R.","A π model with C/2 at each end and R between them."],"ans":3,"why":["Putting all capacitance behind R gives RC, twice the distributed moment.","An ideal source directly drives the source capacitance, leaving zero far-end RC moment.","This doubles the total capacitance and gives RC.","Only the far half-capacitance lies behind R in the one-section π model."],"ex":"Elmore moment is not itself an exact 50% crossing time; preserve that distinction when choosing lumped models.","oral":"Why is the L-model crossed out and the π-model boxed?"},{"id":"V221","k":"md-eecs427-ceff-of-a-wire-whose-neighbor-switches-in-the-opposite-direction","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Opposite-switching coupling load","q":"For matched simultaneous opposite-direction swings on adjacent wires, what effective coupling load appears in the simple Miller model?","a":"Cgnd + 2Cadj (MCF = 2); same direction: Cgnd (MCF = 0); quiet: Cgnd + Cadj.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Calculate the voltage change across the coupling capacitor, not just to ground.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["2Cgnd+Cadj","Cgnd+2Cadj","Cgnd+Cadj","Cgnd"],"ans":1,"why":["The doubled term is the interwire coupling capacitance, not the ground capacitance.","Opposite swings double the voltage change across the coupling capacitor, giving factor two on Cadj.","Factor one corresponds to a quiet fixed neighbor.","That is the ideal same-direction simultaneous case."],"ex":"Opposite swings double the voltage change across the coupling capacitor, giving factor two on Cadj.","oral":"Ceff of a wire whose neighbor switches in the opposite direction?"},{"id":"V222","k":"md-eecs427-floating-victim-noise-with-cadj-cgnd-v","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Floating-victim coupling step","q":"A floating victim has coupling capacitance Cadj to an aggressor and capacitance Cgnd to ground. If Cadj=Cgnd and the aggressor makes a voltage step ΔV, what ideal capacitive-divider step appears on the victim?","a":"ΔV = Cadj/(Cgnd + Cadj)·ΔVagg = 50%. With an equal-size driver: 8%; half-size: 16%; double: 4%.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Treat the victim as a floating capacitive divider.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["ΔV/4","Zero","ΔV","ΔV/2"],"ans":3,"why":["A quarter requires a different capacitance ratio.","A floating node has no ideal voltage source clamping it.","Full tracking would require negligible ground capacitance.","Cadj/(Cadj+Cgnd)=1/2."],"ex":"Charge conservation gives ΔVvictim = Cadj·ΔV/(Cadj+Cgnd). A driven victim requires its driver impedance and transition timing too.","oral":"Floating victim noise with Cadj = Cgnd-v?"},{"id":"V223","k":"md-eecs427-why-is-a-glitch-fatal-in-a-domino-node-or-precharged-bitline-but-not-in-static-cmos","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Why dynamic glitches can persist","q":"A dynamic node loses enough charge during evaluate to cross its receiver threshold. Why may it remain wrong after the triggering glitch ends?","a":"Static CMOS restores the level actively; a precharged/dynamic node that discharges has no pull-up during evaluation and cannot recover.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Ask which device is available to restore the lost charge during evaluate.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A static receiver restores the dynamic input through its own pull-up network.","Lowering clock frequency alone guarantees restoration during evaluate.","The strong precharge path is off, so there may be no adequate restoring pull-up until the next precharge.","A keeper always restores any discharged node before it is sampled, regardless of strength or feedback state."],"ans":2,"why":["The receiver drives its output; its insulated input does not directly restore the upstream node.","A longer evaluate interval does not turn the precharge path on and can increase leakage exposure.","Charge-based evaluation can make a transient disturbance persist through the phase.","Keeper strength, remaining voltage and feedback state determine whether recovery is possible."],"ex":"Dynamic-node vulnerability depends on lost charge, receiver threshold and keeper strength. Static restoration is helpful but not immunity to all glitches.","oral":"Why is a glitch fatal in a domino node or precharged bitline but not in static CMOS?"},{"id":"V224","k":"md-eecs427-optimal-repeater-spacing-and-size","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Repeater spacing and wire resistance","q":"In the continuous-size repeater model, optimal spacing is proportional to 1/√(rwire·cwire) with repeater parameters fixed. If wire resistance per length quadruples and capacitance per length is unchanged, how does spacing change?","a":"l/N = √(2RC′/(RwCw)), W = √(RCw/(RwC′)), C′ = C(1 + pinv) - derived from minimizing Elmore delay over N and W.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Apply the scaling law without changing the repeater parameters.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It falls to one-quarter","It halves","It doubles","It stays unchanged"],"ans":1,"why":["The dependence is inverse square root, not inverse linear.","The square root of a fourfold RC-per-length product is two, so its inverse halves spacing.","This applies the square-root dependence in the wrong direction.","Wire RC is explicitly in the spacing optimization."],"ex":"The square root of a fourfold RC-per-length product is two, so its inverse halves spacing.","oral":"Optimal repeater spacing and size?"},{"id":"V225","k":"md-eecs427-energy-cost-of-delay-optimal-repeaters-and-how-to-reduce-it","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Repeater energy-delay tradeoff","q":"In the lecture’s stated repeater example, delay-optimal repeaters cost 1.87E0, while an EDP-oriented choice costs 1.30E0 with 14% extra delay. Which interpretation is correct?","a":"1.87·Cw·VDD² per length (87% premium); size for min EDP to cut the premium to 30% for +14% delay.","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Keep energy and delay ratios separate before multiplying them.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Both consume the same energy because the physical wire is unchanged","The latter sacrifices some speed to cut energy; minimum delay is not minimum energy-delay product","The latter increases energy because 14% delay always implies more charging energy","The latter is 87% slower because the first energy premium was 87%"],"ans":1,"why":["Repeater sizing changes gate and diffusion capacitance.","Energy falls from 1.87E0 to 1.30E0 while delay rises by a smaller fraction.","Energy depends on switched capacitance and supply, not just elapsed time.","An energy percentage does not transfer to a delay percentage."],"ex":"The example illustrates distinct objectives. Its EDP ratio is (1.30×1.14)/1.87≈0.793 relative to the delay-optimal choice.","oral":"Energy cost of delay-optimal repeaters and how to reduce it?"},{"id":"V226","k":"md-eecs427-define-average-rms-and-peak-current-for-em-and-which-matters-where","d":"int","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Match current metrics to mechanisms","q":"Which pairing best distinguishes the three waveform metrics used in interconnect reliability analysis?","a":"I_avg = mean current (EM wear-out, power rails); I_rms = root-mean-square (Joule heating, signal wires); I_peak = max |i| (short-pulse damage).","src":"EECS 427 lecture book","hint":"Relate heating to i²R and charge transport to the current integral.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Average, RMS and peak are interchangeable whenever the current is periodic.","Average current: maximum instantaneous stress; RMS current: signed charge transfer; peak current: temperature average.","Average current: directional EM stress; RMS current: Joule heating; peak current: pulse limits.","Average current: Joule heating; RMS current: only logic activity; peak current: DC leakage."],"ans":2,"why":["A pulsed waveform can have very different values for these metrics.","This swaps the mathematical meanings and physical uses.","They characterize net transport, mean-square heating and extrema respectively, subject to foundry rules.","Heating scales with mean-square current, and peaks are not DC leakage."],"ex":"Use the PDK’s directionality, duty-cycle and pulse-duration rules; no single current statistic replaces all reliability checks.","oral":"Define average, RMS, and peak current for EM, and which matters where."},{"id":"V227","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p7-1-an-aggressor-net-transitions-next-to-a-victim-that-is-not-switching-if-you-reduce-the-width-of-the-victim-driver-does-the-aggressor-transition-get-faster-or-slower","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Coupled noise with a weak victim driver","q":"An aggressor switches beside a nominally quiet victim. In the stated coupled-RC model, weakening the victim’s holding driver can make the aggressor faster. Why?","a":"Faster. The victim's holding resistance is larger, so the victim node follows the aggressor and less voltage develops across Cc. The aggressor sees a smaller effective load. The cost is a larger noise peak on the victim.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compare the voltage difference across Cc for a fixed victim and a moving victim.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The aggressor receives an extra DC drive transistor from the victim","The victim follows the aggressor more, reducing voltage developed across the coupling capacitor","The physical coupling capacitance disappears when the victim driver is weak","The victim is clamped more strongly to its original rail"],"ans":1,"why":["Capacitive interaction does not add a direct DC transistor path.","A less rigid victim reduces effective coupling load on the aggressor, at the cost of larger victim noise.","The geometry and physical capacitance remain; the voltage across it changes.","A weaker driver gives a less effective clamp, not a stronger one."],"ex":"A less rigid victim reduces effective coupling load on the aggressor, at the cost of larger victim noise.","oral":"(Exam 1 P7.1) An aggressor net transitions next to a victim that is not switching. If you reduce the width of the victim driver, does the aggressor transition get faster or slower?"},{"id":"V228","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p7-2-as-technology-scales-the-collection-area-shrinks-why-does-that-improve-ser-robustness-and-how-does-it-make-the-design-less-robust-how-is-that-mitigated","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Multi-cell upset mitigation","q":"One particle strike can disturb several physically neighboring SRAM bits. Why does bit interleaving help an ECC that corrects only one erroneous bit per word?","a":"It improves robustness because the ion's charge is diluted across neighboring cells, so less reaches any one node. It hurts because one strike now reaches several cells, so multiple simultaneous failures become more likely. The circuit fix is **SRAM bit interleaving**, so the upset bits land in different ECC words.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Match the physical extent of a strike to the logical extent of a codeword.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It makes all upset bits share one word so correction occurs together","It maps neighboring physical cells into different ECC words, reducing multiple errors within one protected word","It increases the correction capability of the same ECC code without changing the code","It prevents the particle from depositing charge in any cell"],"ans":1,"why":["Concentrating errors into one single-error-correcting word is the problem.","Physical separation of bits belonging to the same word reduces the chance of exceeding that word’s correction capacity.","It does not change the code’s mathematical correction strength.","Interleaving changes mapping, not charge deposition."],"ex":"Interleaving addresses spatially correlated upsets by distributing them across independently protected words.","oral":"(Exam 1 P7.2) As technology scales, the collection area shrinks. Why does that improve SER robustness, and how does it make the design less robust? How is that mitigated?"},{"id":"V229","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p7-4b-you-own-300-processors-50-at-10-fit-and-250-at-100-fit-what-is-the-chance-of-a-failure-in-one-day","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Convert a fleet FIT rate","q":"Assume independent constant Poisson failure rates. A fleet has 50 processors at 10 FIT and 250 at 100 FIT; 1 FIT = 1 failure per 10⁹ device-hours. What is the approximate probability of at least one failure in 24 hours?","a":"(50·10/10⁹ + 250·100/10⁹)·24 = 612,000/10⁹ ≈ **6.1 × 10⁻⁴** per day.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Aggregate device-hour rates before applying the observation time.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["2.55 × 10⁻⁵","6.12 × 10⁻⁴","2.40 × 10⁻⁶","6.12 × 10⁻¹"],"ans":1,"why":["This is the aggregate hourly rate, before multiplying by 24.","The fleet rate is 25,500/10⁹ per hour; multiply by 24 for the small-probability approximation.","This corresponds to one 100-FIT processor for one day, not the fleet.","This introduces a factor-of-1,000 unit error."],"ex":"λt = (50 × 10 + 250 × 100) × 24 / 10⁹ = 0.000612. Exact probability 1 − exp(−λt) ≈ 0.000611813.","oral":"(Exam 1 P7.4B) You own 300 processors: 50 at 10 FIT and 250 at 100 FIT. What is the chance of a failure in one day?"},{"id":"V230","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p7-4a-digital-noise-is-deterministic-why-can-t-we-predict-it-well-and-just-do-a-worst-case-analysis","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Feasible worst-case digital noise","q":"Why can blindly summing every aggressor’s worst digital-noise contribution be too pessimistic?","a":"A real chip has an enormous number of transition combinations, and in each one the noise may or may not be masked temporally or logically. Simulating every scenario is impossible, and summing all aggressors as a worst case is far too pessimistic.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Ask whether the assumed worst events can coexist and reach the sampling point.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Logic masking guarantees that digital noise can never cause failure","Every aggressor contribution is statistically independent by definition","The worst transitions may not be simultaneously feasible or aligned with an unmasked victim window","Deterministic noise has no relationship to logic state"],"ans":2,"why":["Masking applies to particular scenarios, not every possible disturbance.","Shared logic and timing can correlate aggressors.","Timing and logic constraints can prevent simultaneous sensitization, while exhaustive combinations are impractical.","Logic state and transition timing are central to deterministic noise behavior."],"ex":"Timing and logic constraints can prevent simultaneous sensitization, while exhaustive combinations are impractical.","oral":"(Exam 1 P7.4A) Digital noise is deterministic. Why can't we predict it well and just do a worst-case analysis?"},{"id":"V231","k":"md-eecs627-aggressor-and-victim-each-have-cg-4-ff-with-cc-2-ff-the-aggressor-switches-opposite-to-the-victim-and-twice-as-fast-what-grounded-caps-model-each-net","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Coupling with unequal opposite slews","q":"During a linear-ramp interval, two wires each have 4 fF to ground and 2 fF mutual coupling. The aggressor voltage changes opposite to the victim at twice the victim’s rate. Using coupling current Cc(dV1/dt−dV2/dt), what effective grounded capacitances do they see?","a":"The victim sees 4 + 3·2 = **10 fF** and the aggressor sees 4 + 1.5·2 = **7 fF** (slide 70). At equal slew both see 8 fF. If both switch the same way, both see 4 fF.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compute the relative slew ratio from each wire’s perspective.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Victim 8 fF; aggressor 8 fF","Victim 7 fF; aggressor 10 fF","Victim 10 fF; aggressor 7 fF","Victim 6 fF; aggressor 6 fF"],"ans":2,"why":["Both become 8 fF for equal-magnitude opposite slew.","This swaps the slew ratios seen from the two wires.","Victim coupling multiplier is 1−(−2)=3; aggressor multiplier is 1−(−1/2)=1.5.","A multiplier of one ignores the other node’s simultaneous motion."],"ex":"Ceff,v=4+3×2=10 fF and Ceff,a=4+1.5×2=7 fF during the stated ramp overlap.","oral":"Aggressor and victim each have Cg = 4 fF with Cc = 2 fF. The aggressor switches opposite to the victim and twice as fast. What grounded caps model each net?"},{"id":"V232","k":"md-eecs627-why-does-the-lecture-recommend-staying-below-the-12-gain-point-instead-of-using-the-unity-gain-points-for-vil-vih","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Noise gain near a feedback limit","q":"In the lecture’s simplified positive-feedback noise model Gnoise = k/(1 − k), what are the gain at k = 1/2 and its limit as k approaches 1 from below?","a":"Loop noise gain is k/(1−k). At k = 1 it is infinite, so any extra noise tips the stage. At k = ½ it is 1, so noise is not amplified. The unity-gain points only maximize NML + NMH.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Examine both numerator and denominator as k changes.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["1/2, then 1.","1, then unbounded growth.","0, then −1.","2, then 0."],"ans":1,"why":["This confuses the local parameter k with the closed-loop noise gain.","Substitution gives (1/2)/(1/2) = 1; the denominator tends to zero near one.","The gain is positive for 0 < k < 1.","Neither value follows the stated expression."],"ex":"A boundary defined by unity local gain need not offer useful additional noise headroom; state the model before comparing noise-margin conventions.","oral":"Why does the lecture recommend staying below the ½-gain point instead of using the unity-gain points for VIL/VIH?"},{"id":"V233","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p7-3-seu-hardened-latch","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Transient evidence and state retention","q":"In a hardened latch, a transient fault disables both strong paths to Q while Q’s stored charge initially preserves the correct value. What would be an unjustified robustness claim?","a":"TG1 writes D onto Q and TG2 writes D onto INT2. INT2 and INT3 form a cross-coupled inverter pair (I2: INT2→INT3, I1: INT3→INT2). INT1a is driven by MP3 (gate INT2) and MN3 (gate Q). INT1b is driven by MP4 (gate Q) and MN4 (gate INT2). Q is driven by the stack MP1 (gate INT1a) + MP2 (gate INT3) to Vdd and the stack MN2 (gate INT3) + MN1 (gate INT1b) to ground. PMOS and NMOS have equal on currents and equal off currents. The posted solutions for this part are blank, so all answers below are (derived).\n    - **(A) Q = 0 is stored and a strike drives INT3 1 → 0 for several FO4. Sketch INT2, INT1a, INT1b and Q. Does the latch survive?** Stored state: INT2 = 0, INT3 = 1, INT1a = 1, INT1b = 1, Q = 0. The strike sets INT3 = 0. I1 then drives INT2 0 → 1, which turns MP2 on and MN2 off. Q loses both paths (MP1 is off because INT1a = 1, MN2 is off), so **Q floats at 0**. INT2 = 1 turns MP3 off while MN3 stays off (Q = 0), so **INT1a floats at 1**. MN4 turns on while MP4 stays on, so **INT1b fights to about ½**. Q stays at 0 because pulling it up needs INT1a and INT3 to both go low, and INT1a can only fall if Q first rises. The output stage acts like a C-element. **Yes, the latch resists** at Q. The INT2/INT3 pair stays flipped until the next transparent phase rewrites it through TG2.\n    - **(B) Same case, but the strike holds INT3 at 0 indefinitely. Can MP1-4 and MN1-4 be sized so Q is unaffected?** Yes, through leakage ratios. Q is floating between MP1 (off) and MN2 (off), so make the pull-down leakage win: **MN2 (and MN1) wider than MP1**. INT1a is floating between MP3 (off) and MN3 (off), so make **MP3 wider than MN3** to keep INT1a high and MP1 off.\n    - **(C) Can it be sized so it also survives the mirror case: Q = 1 and a held 0 → 1 strike on INT3?** In the mirror case INT2 falls, so MP2 and MN1 are off, Q floats at 1, INT1b floats at 0 and INT1a fights. Holding Q = 1 needs **MP2 wider than MN1** and **MN4 wider than MP4**. These are different devices from case B, so on paper both sets of ratios can be met at once. In practice the retention is purely leakage-based, sensitive to PVT and to TG1 leakage from D, and holds only for a limited time. Hardening is meant for transient strikes.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish transient fault tolerance from a permanently forced internal node.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The immediate response can depend on stored capacitance","The initial correct Q value proves retention for an arbitrarily long fault at every PVT corner","Leakage ratios can affect how long the isolated node stays valid","A later transparent phase may be needed to restore corrupted internal state"],"ans":1,"why":["A floating node can temporarily retain its previous charge, so this is a valid mechanism.","Initial charge retention is not indefinite active restoration; leakage, coupling and PVT can eventually move the node.","Unbalanced leakage changes the floating voltage with time, making this an essential check.","The described redundant structure can preserve Q while an internal pair remains corrupted until rewritten."],"ex":"Initial charge retention is not indefinite active restoration; leakage, coupling and PVT can eventually move the node.","oral":"(Exam 1 P7.3) SEU-hardened latch."},{"id":"V234","k":"md-eecs627-why-can-t-a-standard-cross-coupled-latch-recover-from-a-particle-strike-and-how-does-dice-fix-this","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"DICE recovery mechanism","q":"Why can a properly implemented DICE storage cell recover from a single-node upset that can defeat an ordinary two-node cross-coupled latch?","a":"A strike can turn either (L, H) or (H, L) into (H, H), so with only two nodes the original state is ambiguous. DICE stores the bit on four interlocked nodes, and each node needs both neighbors to agree before it changes. A single strike only floats or half-drives its neighbors, and the two untouched nodes restore the struck one.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Ask which untouched state remains available after one node is disturbed.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It guarantees recovery from any number of simultaneously corrupted nodes","Four interlocked nodes preserve redundant state so unaffected nodes can restore the disturbed node","Its storage devices have zero capacitance, so a strike deposits no voltage","It disables all feedback after every clock edge"],"ans":1,"why":["The stated protection is for a single-node upset; correlated multi-node events require separate analysis.","The additional interlocked state retains information lost when both nodes of a simple latch become ambiguous.","Real storage nodes have capacitance and can collect charge.","Recovery uses controlled redundant feedback, not its complete removal."],"ex":"DICE provides redundant state and interlocking feedback. Its single-node robustness must not be generalized to arbitrary multi-node strikes.","oral":"Why can't a standard cross-coupled latch recover from a particle strike, and how does DICE fix this?"},{"id":"V235","k":"md-eecs627-derive-r-for-a-cross-coupled-latch-near-its-metastable-point","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Derive a latch regeneration constant","q":"Neglect output conductance. If a latch’s differential mode obeys C d(ΔV)/dt = gm ΔV, what is the regeneration time constant?","a":"→ ΔI = gm·ΔV charges Cd: dΔV/dt = gm·ΔV/Cd, so ΔV(t) = ΔV0·e^(t/τr) with τr = Cd/gm.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Check dimensions and match the coefficient of time in the exponent.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["τ = Cgm","τ = C/gm","τ = 1/(Cgm)","τ = gm/C"],"ans":1,"why":["This product does not have units of time.","The exponential rate is gm/C, whose reciprocal is the time constant.","This inverse product also does not have the required dimensions.","This has units of inverse seconds, the growth rate."],"ex":"ΔV(t) = ΔV(0) exp(gm t/C) = ΔV(0) exp(t/τ). With output conductance included, use the net regenerative conductance.","oral":"Derive τr for a cross-coupled latch near its metastable point."},{"id":"V236","k":"md-eecs627-why-does-td-go-to-infinity-as-t-goes-to-0-and-what-saves-real-circuits","d":"seq","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Metastability resolution time","q":"In the metastability model td=τr·ln(ta/Δt), what happens as the initial timing offset Δt approaches zero?","a":"→ td = τr·ln(ta/Δt): the initial imbalance ΔV0 = Δt/ta goes to zero, so exponential growth needs unbounded time to reach 1 V. Noise breaks the tie in practice, but the resolve time is still unbounded in probability.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Examine the logarithm as its denominator tends toward zero.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Resolution time is bounded by exactly one clock period","Noise guarantees every event resolves before the next stage samples","Resolution time grows without a finite deterministic bound, although noise usually breaks exact symmetry","Resolution time approaches zero because both branches become equal"],"ans":2,"why":["The model contains no one-cycle upper bound.","Noise makes eventual resolution likely but cannot guarantee an imposed deadline for every event.","A vanishing initial imbalance needs arbitrarily long exponential amplification; noise does not create a hard maximum resolution time.","An almost balanced state is harder to resolve, not faster."],"ex":"A vanishing initial imbalance needs arbitrarily long exponential amplification; noise does not create a hard maximum resolution time.","oral":"Why does td go to infinity as Δt goes to 0, and what saves real circuits?"},{"id":"V237","k":"md-eecs627-recompute-the-slide-15-example-fa-50-mhz-fb-300-mhz-ta-100-ps-r-200-ps-td-10-ns","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Metastability MTBF calculation","q":"Use MTBF = exp(td/τ)/(ta·fA·fB), with fA=50 MHz, fB=300 MHz, ta=100 ps, τ=200 ps and td=10 ns. What is MTBF approximately?","a":"→ P = 100 ps·300 MHz·e^(−50) = 5.786×10⁻²⁴. MTBF = 1/(P·fA) = 3.45×10¹⁵ s ≈ 1.1×10⁸ years.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Convert all times and frequencies to seconds and hertz before evaluating the exponential.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["6.67×10⁻⁷ s","3.46×10¹⁵ s","3.46×10⁶ s","1.73×10²³ s"],"ans":1,"why":["This omits the exponential benefit of resolution time.","td/τ=50 and ta·fA·fB=1.5×10⁶ s⁻¹, giving exp(50)/(1.5×10⁶).","This introduces a factor-of-10⁹ unit error.","This omits the final factor of fA after computing a per-sampling-event probability."],"ex":"The stated model yields about 3.46×10¹⁵ seconds, or 1.1×10⁸ years. This is a model-based single-crossing estimate, not a fleet guarantee.","oral":"Recompute the slide 15 example: fA = 50 MHz, fB = 300 MHz, ta = 100 ps, τr = 200 ps, td = 10 ns."},{"id":"V238","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p4-a-b-a-two-flop-synchronizer-has-tcq-tsetup-50-ps-fa-1-ghz-sending-fb-1-5-ghz-receiving-tlogic-200-ps-after-the-first-flop-ta-20-ps-what-r-gives-pfailure-6-21011-and-what-is-the-mtbf","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Convert synchronizer failure probability to MTBF","q":"A synchronizer model gives failure probability 6.2 × 10⁻¹¹ per asynchronous data event. Events occur at 1 GHz. What is the model’s MTBF?","a":"→ td = Tperiod − (Tlogic + Tsetup) = 670 ps − 250 ps = 420 ps. Pfailure = ta·fB·e^(−td/τr) gives 0.03·e^(−420/τr) = 6.2×10⁻¹¹, so 420/τr = ln(4.84×10⁸) ≈ 20.0 and **τr ≈ 21 ps** (derived; the solution leaves it as X). MTBF = 1/(fA·Pfailure) = 1/(10⁹ × 6.2×10⁻¹¹) ≈ **16 s**.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"First convert the per-event probability to failures per second.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 16.1 seconds","About 16.1 nanoseconds","About 1.61 × 10¹⁰ seconds","About 62 seconds"],"ans":0,"why":["Failure rate is 10⁹ × 6.2 × 10⁻¹¹ = 0.062 per second, so MTBF = 1/0.062.","The event-rate factor is applied incorrectly.","This is approximately the reciprocal per-event probability, before converting events to time.","The reciprocal of 0.062 is 16.1, not 62."],"ex":"Keep probability per event separate from event rate. This calculation does not depend on an ambiguous definition of the internal resolution interval.","oral":"(Exam 2 P4 A/B) A two-flop synchronizer has TCQ = Tsetup = 50 ps. fA = 1 GHz (sending), fB = 1.5 GHz (receiving), Tlogic = 200 ps after the first flop, ta = 20 ps. What τr gives Pfailure = 6.2×10⁻¹¹, and what is the MTBF?"},{"id":"V239","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p4-c-d-in-a-latch-used-as-a-synchronizer-how-do-ta-and-r-change-if-you-upsize-c-the-forward-inverter-or-d-the-input-tristate","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Input-device sizing and metastability","q":"In the stated latch synchronizer model, upsizing only the input tristate strengthens input drive but adds storage-node capacitance without adding regenerative-loop gm. What tradeoff follows?","a":"→ (C) ta increases, because the bigger inverter loads Ni and slows the input tristate, so the chance of metastability goes up. τr is unchanged to first order (gm and Cd scale together) and decreases slightly in practice because some capacitance is fixed, which helps. (D) ta decreases, because the stronger input drive moves Ni faster, which helps. τr increases, because there is extra capacitance at Ni with no extra loop gm, which hurts.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Separate the initial sampling slope from the later feedback regeneration.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["ta increases while τr decreases because the feedback inverter was upsized","The aperture parameter ta can decrease while regeneration time constant τr increases","Both ta and τr must decrease by the same factor","Neither quantity can change because the latch Boolean function is unchanged"],"ans":1,"why":["That reasoning concerns a different modification; the question changes the input device only.","Stronger input drive creates imbalance faster, but added node capacitance slows regeneration with unchanged loop gm.","Input acquisition and regeneration depend on different circuit ratios and need not improve together.","Analog settling and capacitance matter even when the logical function is unchanged."],"ex":"Stronger input drive creates imbalance faster, but added node capacitance slows regeneration with unchanged loop gm.","oral":"(Exam 2 P4 C/D) In a latch used as a synchronizer, how do ta and τr change if you upsize (C) the forward inverter or (D) the input tristate?"},{"id":"V240","k":"md-eecs627-why-is-a-108-year-mtbf-not-good-enough-for-a-data-center-product","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Fleet-level synchronizer failures","q":"Assume each synchronizer has an independent constant failure rate corresponding to MTBF=10⁸ years. A fleet has 10⁵ synchronizers per chip and 10⁴ chips. What is the expected aggregate failure count per year?","a":"→ 10⁵ synchronizers per chip × 10⁴ parts = 10⁹ synchronizers, so about 10 failures per year across the fleet.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Add component failure rates rather than their MTBF values.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["10","0.1","10⁹","10⁻⁸"],"ans":0,"why":["There are 10⁹ synchronizers, each contributing 10⁻⁸ failures/year.","This is smaller by a factor of 100.","This is the number of synchronizers, not their annual expected failures.","This is the rate of only one synchronizer."],"ex":"Expected failure rates add: 10⁵×10⁴/10⁸ = 10 per year. An impressive individual MTBF can still be insufficient at fleet scale.","oral":"Why is a 10⁸-year MTBF not good enough for a data-center product?"},{"id":"V241","k":"md-eecs627-why-is-a-two-flop-synchronizer-better-than-one-flop-followed-by-logic","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Protect synchronizer resolution time","q":"With other parameters fixed, removing combinational logic delay tlogic from between synchronizer stages increases resolution time by tlogic. What happens to MTBF in the exponential model?","a":"→ td goes from TA − tlogic − tsu to TA − tsu. Because td is in the exponent, MTBF improves by e^(tlogic/τr).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Subtract the old and new exponents.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It is unchanged because the number of flops is unchanged.","It is multiplied only by 1 + tlogic/τ.","It is divided by exp(tlogic/τ).","It is multiplied by exp(tlogic/τ)."],"ans":3,"why":["Available resolution time matters even with the same stage count.","This is only a small-argument approximation, not the general relation.","More resolution time improves, rather than degrades, MTBF.","MTBF is proportional to exp(tresolution/τ)."],"ex":"Keep the first-stage output path short and dedicated so metastability has time to resolve before the next capture.","oral":"Why is a two-flop synchronizer better than one flop followed by logic?"},{"id":"V242","k":"md-eecs627-why-not-synchronize-each-bit-of-a-bus-or-synchronize-one-signal-in-two-places","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Synchronizer reconvergence","q":"Why should one asynchronous control signal generally be synchronized once and then distributed within its destination domain, rather than synchronized independently twice?","a":"→ The copies can resolve to different cycles, so the receiver sees inconsistent values or a bus value that was never sent. Use a single synchronized REQ/ACK handshake (or Gray-coded pointers), and synchronize once then fan out.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Consider a transition arriving near the sampling aperture of both chains.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A second synchronizer makes the source clock synchronous with the destination","Two synchronizers always cancel each other’s metastability","Independent synchronizers can report the transition on different cycles and create inconsistent control states","The copies are guaranteed identical because their RTL is identical"],"ans":2,"why":["Adding registers does not change the clocks’ asynchronous relationship.","Separate chains do not communicate a shared resolution decision.","Different resolution and sampling histories can disagree about the cycle of the event, even with identical topology.","Identical logic structure does not guarantee identical analog sampling outcomes."],"ex":"Different resolution and sampling histories can disagree about the cycle of the event, even with identical topology.","oral":"Why not synchronize each bit of a bus, or synchronize one signal in two places?"},{"id":"V243","k":"md-eecs627-the-energy-drawn-from-vdd-to-charge-c-to-vdd-is-cvdd2-where-does-it-go-and-does-pmos-size-matter-practice-exam-2-p1-a","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Where charging energy goes","q":"An ideal fixed VDD supply charges a initially uncharged capacitor C through a resistive PMOS path to VDD. Ignore leakage, short-circuit overlap and size-dependent parasitics. Where does the supplied CVDD² energy go?","a":"Half (½CV²) is dissipated in the PMOS and half is stored on C, later burned in the NMOS on discharge. ∫Ip·Vp dt is the same for any PMOS resistance, so no sizing of P1 minimizes the energy supplied by VDD.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compare source work VQ with the capacitor energy formula.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["All is stored on C","The split changes to zero dissipation if PMOS resistance is halved","All is dissipated and none is stored","Half is stored on C and half dissipated in the charging path"],"ans":3,"why":["The capacitor stores only half the delivered source energy.","Changing resistance changes duration, not this ideal energy split.","The final charged capacitor retains energy.","Stored capacitor energy is CVDD²/2; the remainder is lost during ordinary resistive charging."],"ex":"Source work is VDD·Q = CVDD², while stored energy is CVDD²/2. Device sizing cannot change that split under the stated assumptions.","oral":"The energy drawn from VDD to charge C to VDD is C·VDD². Where does it go, and does PMOS size matter? (Practice Exam 2, P1(A))"},{"id":"V244","k":"md-eecs627-practice-exam-2-p1-c-the-load-is-split-into-two-cl-2-capacitors-one-charged-through-p1-and-one-through-a-further-device-n2-can-any-sizing-make-vdd-supply-less-energy-than-in-the-single-cap-case","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Energy when a load is split","q":"Two capacitors CL/2 start at zero and both finish at VDD, charged from an ideal constant VDD supply through resistive devices. Ignoring other currents, what total energy does the supply deliver?","a":"No. VDD still delivers charge VDD·2(CL/2) = CL·VDD, so energy CL·VDD². Sizing only changes which transistor dissipates it.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Add the charge delivered to both half-capacitors.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["CL VDD²","(1/2) CL VDD²","An arbitrarily small value if the devices are wide enough","2CL VDD²"],"ans":0,"why":["Total supplied charge is CL VDD; multiplying by supply voltage gives CL VDD².","This is the final stored energy, not the energy supplied.","Resistance changes the transient, not the required charge from this constant-voltage source.","Splitting CL into two halves does not double total capacitance."],"ex":"Half the supplied energy is stored and half dissipated for charging ideal initially uncharged capacitors from a fixed voltage through resistance.","oral":"(Practice Exam 2, P1(C)) The load is split into two CL/2 capacitors, one charged through P1 and one through a further device N2. Can any sizing make VDD supply less energy than in the single-cap case?"},{"id":"V245","k":"md-eecs627-why-does-a-clock-gating-cell-use-a-latch-rather-than-a-plain-and-gate","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Clock-gating enable timing","q":"For a positive-pulse AND-based clock-gating cell, what does the low-level-transparent latch accomplish?","a":"The latch is transparent only while clk is low, so `en` is frozen during the high phase, and a late or glitchy enable cannot truncate or create a pulse on `g_clk`.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Identify the phase in which an enable transition could directly alter the output clock.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It holds enable constant throughout CLK’s high phase","It divides clock frequency by two regardless of enable","It intentionally permits enable changes during the clock high phase","It guarantees an asynchronous enable has no metastability risk"],"ans":0,"why":["Freezing enable while the AND passes CLK prevents enable-driven pulse truncation or creation under valid timing conditions.","The gate passes or suppresses pulses according to enable rather than always dividing by two.","That would allow the very partial-pulse behavior the latch is intended to prevent.","Clock-gating stability checks remain necessary; a latch is not a universal asynchronous synchronizer."],"ex":"Freezing enable while the AND passes CLK prevents enable-driven pulse truncation or creation under valid timing conditions.","oral":"Why does a clock-gating cell use a latch rather than a plain AND gate?"},{"id":"V246","k":"md-eecs627-a-design-is-pipelined-with-10-register-overhead-and-vdd-scaled-to-0-66-what-is-the-new-power","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Pipelining and voltage-scaled power","q":"Assume pipelining increases switched capacitance by 10%, supply becomes 0.66 times its original value, and activity and frequency stay fixed. What is the dynamic-power ratio?","a":"P = 0.66²·1.1·Pref = 0.48·Pref.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Apply the capacitance multiplier and voltage-squared multiplier separately.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Approximately 1.67","Approximately 0.726","Approximately 0.436","Approximately 0.479"],"ans":3,"why":["1.67 divides by voltage rather than applying CV²f.","0.726 uses linear rather than squared voltage scaling.","0.436 omits the added switched capacitance.","1.10×0.66²=0.47916."],"ex":"Pnew/Pold = 1.10×0.4356 = 0.47916. The result is conditional on the unchanged activity/frequency assumptions.","oral":"A design is pipelined with 10% register overhead and VDD scaled to 0.66×. What is the new power?"},{"id":"V247","k":"md-eecs627-what-is-the-minimum-energy-point-and-why-does-ntv-back-off-from-it","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Why voltage cannot fall indefinitely for energy","q":"Why can lowering VDD below the minimum-energy operating point increase energy per completed operation?","a":"It is the VDD where leakage energy per operation (I_leak·V·t_delay) becomes comparable to active energy; below it, exponential delay makes leakage energy explode. NTV costs about 2× the minimum energy but runs about 100× faster than at Vmin, with much less variability, and it is still about 10× more energy-efficient than superthreshold.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Consider joules per operation, not only watts or switching energy.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Execution time becomes independent of voltage in weak inversion.","Switching energy necessarily increases as VDD² when VDD decreases.","The longer operation time accumulates enough leakage energy to outweigh further switching-energy savings.","Every transistor ceases all conduction exactly at its threshold voltage."],"ans":2,"why":["Weak-inversion current and delay are strongly voltage dependent.","The switching term decreases with decreasing voltage.","Total energy includes leakage power integrated over a voltage-dependent delay.","Subthreshold current exists below the nominal threshold."],"ex":"Minimum-energy voltage balances active energy against time-integrated leakage. Near-threshold operation may trade some energy for throughput and variability benefits.","oral":"What is the minimum-energy point, and why does NTV back off from it?"},{"id":"V248","k":"md-eecs627-state-the-two-dcvs-level-converter-constraints-and-why-they-get-harder-at-low-vddl","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"DCVS device-strength window","q":"In the simplified DCVS level-converter current window, which ordering supports both switching and retention?","a":"IN-ON > IP-ON (the NMOS must win the fight) and IP-ON > IN-OFF (the PMOS must beat leakage). At low VDDL the NMOS goes subthreshold, so its ION/IOFF falls from about 10⁶ to about 10³, and the margin per side drops from about 1000× to about 30×.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Write one inequality for overcoming contention and another for opposing leakage.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["IN,on > IN,off > IP,on","IN,off > IP,on > IN,on","IN,on > IP,on > IN,off","IP,on > IN,on > IN,off"],"ans":2,"why":["A PMOS weaker than off-leakage cannot reliably retain the high state.","This fails both switching and retention conditions.","The NMOS on-current must overcome the opposing PMOS, while the PMOS must still beat NMOS off-leakage.","A PMOS stronger than the switching NMOS can prevent the state from changing."],"ex":"The NMOS on-current must overcome the opposing PMOS, while the PMOS must still beat NMOS off-leakage.","oral":"State the two DCVS level-converter constraints and why they get harder at low VDDL."},{"id":"V249","k":"md-eecs627-practice-exam-1-p2-a-regular-dcvs-level-converter-vt-400-mv-vddl-0-3-v-vddh-0-8-v-corners-ff-fs-sf-ss-first-letter-pmos-at-20-c-or-85-c-which-corner-checks-that-the-nmos-is-sized-large-enough-relative-to-the-pmos","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Skewed corner for a DCVS shifter","q":"Use the exam convention in which the first corner letter denotes PMOS. A DCVS shifter has VDDL=0.3 V below |Vt|=0.4 V and VDDH=0.8 V. Which stated corner most stresses NMOS ability to overcome the PMOS: FF, FS, SF or SS, at −20°C or 85°C?","a":"**FS at −20 °C.** The NMOS (gate at 0.3 V < Vt) is subthreshold, so it is weakest when slow (S) and cold. The PMOS (|VGS| = 0.8 V) is superthreshold, so it is strongest when fast (F) and cold (higher mobility). Both worst cases coincide at −20 °C.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Decode the corner letters before comparing device strengths.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["FS at −20°C: fast PMOS, slow NMOS, cold","FS at 85°C because subthreshold NMOS is always weakest hot","SF at −20°C: slow PMOS, fast NMOS, cold","FF at 85°C because equal corner letters maximize contention"],"ans":0,"why":["The subthreshold writer weakens cold while the fast, strongly biased PMOS remains a strong opponent.","Under the stated model, cold reduces the near-threshold NMOS drive.","This makes the writer fast and the opposing PMOS slow.","Equal letter speed alone does not identify the worst strength ratio."],"ex":"Name the physics: weak low-domain NMOS versus strong high-domain PMOS. FS here means PMOS-fast/NMOS-slow; other documents may reverse letter order.","oral":"(Practice Exam 1, P2(A)) Regular DCVS level converter, |Vt| = 400 mV, VDDL = 0.3 V, VDDH = 0.8 V. Corners FF/FS/SF/SS (first letter PMOS) at −20 °C or 85 °C. Which corner checks that the NMOS is sized large enough relative to the PMOS?"},{"id":"V250","k":"md-eecs627-practice-exam-1-p2-b-now-vddl-0-6-v-vddh-0-8-v-which-corner-checks-that-the-pmos-is-strong-enough-relative-to-the-nmos","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Name a skewed-device corner explicitly","q":"To test whether a PMOS can overcome a competing NMOS in a circuit, which relative device corner is most directly stressful?","a":"**SF at both 85 °C and −20 °C (and possibly 25 °C).** Weak PMOS (S) against strong NMOS (F). Both devices are now superthreshold, so temperature makes both stronger or both weaker together, and it is not obvious which temperature is worst, so all must be simulated.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Identify which device must win the current contest.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Both devices typical, because relative strengths cannot vary.","Weak PMOS with strong NMOS, checked over the required temperatures.","Both devices fast, with no need to consider skewed strengths.","Strong PMOS with weak NMOS, checked only at room temperature."],"ans":1,"why":["NMOS and PMOS can have different process shifts.","It weakens the device that must win while strengthening its opponent.","A common fast corner does not necessarily bound an opposing-device ratio.","This makes the intended pull-up fight easier, not harder."],"ex":"Name the strengths explicitly because SF/FS label ordering can vary. Temperature must be checked rather than guessed from a single mobility argument.","oral":"(Practice Exam 1, P2(B)) Now VDDL = 0.6 V, VDDH = 0.8 V. Which corner checks that the PMOS is strong enough relative to the NMOS?"},{"id":"V251","k":"md-eecs627-derive-the-impedance-target-and-evaluate-it-for-100-w-1-v-10-ripple","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Power-grid target impedance","q":"A 100 W block operates at 1 V and permits 10% supply ripple. Using Ztarget=allowed ΔV/current, what target impedance results?","a":"I = P/VDD and ΔV = I·Z ≤ r·VDD, so Z ≤ r·VDD²/P = 0.1·1/100 = 1 mΩ.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Convert power into current before dividing the allowed ripple by it.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["100 mΩ","10 mΩ","1 mΩ","1 Ω"],"ans":2,"why":["100 mΩ would produce 10 V drop in this calculation.","10 mΩ would allow 1 V ripple at 100 A.","Current is 100 A and allowed ripple is 0.1 V, giving 0.1/100=0.001 Ω.","1 Ω is three orders of magnitude above the calculated target."],"ex":"Current is 100 A and allowed ripple is 0.1 V, giving 0.1/100=0.001 Ω.","oral":"Derive the impedance target and evaluate it for 100 W, 1 V, 10% ripple."},{"id":"V252","k":"md-eecs627-going-from-15-w-at-1-2-v-and-200-mhz-to-150-w-at-0-8-v-and-3-ghz-how-do-ir-and-ldi-dt-drop-scale","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Power-grid scaling assumptions","q":"Power changes from 15 W at 1.2 V/200 MHz to 150 W at 0.8 V/3 GHz. Assume fixed R and L, waveform amplitude proportional to average current, and current-transition time inversely proportional to frequency. How do resistive and inductive drops scale?","a":"Current grows 10/0.66 = 15×, so IR drop grows 15× at fixed R. dI/dt grows 15× (current) × 15× (frequency) = 225×, so L·di/dt drop grows 225×.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"First compute current from power and voltage; then account for the assumed transition duration.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["IR: 225×; L·di/dt: 15×","IR: 15×; L·di/dt: 15×","IR: 10×; L·di/dt: 150×","IR: 15×; L·di/dt: 225×"],"ans":3,"why":["This applies the time-scaling factor to the wrong mechanism.","This ignores the explicitly shorter transition time.","The lower supply makes current grow more than the 10× power ratio.","Current is P/V, giving 15×; the transition rate adds the 15× frequency factor for di/dt."],"ex":"Iold=12.5 A and Inew=187.5 A. Inductive scaling is 15×15 only under the stated waveform/timescale assumptions; frequency alone does not determine di/dt.","oral":"Going from 15 W at 1.2 V and 200 MHz to 150 W at 0.8 V and 3 GHz, how do IR and L·di/dt drop scale?"},{"id":"V253","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p5-a-why-are-power-grid-wires-more-susceptible-to-electromigration-than-signal-nets","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Why power rails deserve EM attention","q":"Compared with many alternating signal nets, what makes power-grid interconnect especially exposed to electromigration?","a":"High current density; unidirectional (DC) current, so no recovery as in bidirectional signal nets; and changes in wire width/thickness (vias, transitions) where voids form.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Look for where the current converges into a smaller cross section.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A wide upper rail guarantees that the same current is safe in all of its smaller vias.","High sustained directional current density and current crowding at vias or geometry changes.","Power rails need only average-current checks, so pulse and heating limits cannot constrain them.","Their average current density is always greater than every signal net’s peak density."],"ans":1,"why":["Via area and current sharing can remain limiting despite a wide rail.","Both sustained transport and local bottlenecks contribute to risk.","Foundry rules may separately constrain average, RMS and pulse behavior.","The actual waveform and geometry must be compared; this universal ordering is unsupported."],"ex":"Check local segments and vias, the waveform and temperature using foundry rules; rail width alone is not a reliability signoff.","oral":"(Exam 2, P5(A)) Why are power-grid wires more susceptible to electromigration than signal nets?"},{"id":"V254","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p5-b-implicit-decap-of-a-1-2-w-chip-130-nm-s-15-clock-1-vdd-1-2-v-f-1-ghz","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Decoupling-capacitance estimate","q":"Use the exam’s stated model: P=s·Ctotal·V²·f and implicit decap=0.85·Ctotal. For P=1.2 W, s=0.15, V=1.2 V and f=1 GHz, what implicit decap follows?","a":"C_total·1.2²·10⁹·0.15 = 1.2 → C_total = 5.56 nF. Official answer: C_decap = 0.85·C_total = 4.72 nF. (With the lecture's \"half of non-switching\" rule, 2.36 nF.)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Use the explicitly supplied decap convention; the course discusses another convention separately.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["2.36 nF","4.72 nF","0.833 nF","5.56 nF"],"ans":1,"why":["2.36 nF additionally applies a half-of-nonswitching convention that this question did not specify.","Ctotal=1.2/(0.15×1.2²×1GHz)=5.556 nF; taking 85% gives 4.722 nF.","0.833 nF omits the activity factor when estimating total capacitance.","5.56 nF is total modeled capacitance before the specified 85% fraction."],"ex":"Ctotal=1.2/(0.15×1.2²×1GHz)=5.556 nF; taking 85% gives 4.722 nF.","oral":"(Exam 2, P5(B)) Implicit decap of a 1.2 W chip, 130 nm, s = 15% (clock = 1), VDD = 1.2 V, f = 1 GHz."},{"id":"V255","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p5-c-c2-2-nf-core-decap-c1-12c2-1-nf-extra-on-chip-cap-l1-l2-1-nh-r1-r2-100-m-i-0-initially-vx-1-v-c1-on-the-1-v-supply-and-vy-0-5-v-c2-on-the-0-5-v-supply-at-t1-s2-opens-and-s1-closes-find-vx-vy-just-after-t1-and-at-t","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Initial decap charge sharing","q":"An ideal switch directly joins an isolated 1 nF capacitor initially at 1 V to a 2 nF capacitor initially at 0.5 V. Before an external supply contributes appreciable charge, what shared voltage results?","a":"Charge sharing: C1·1 V + C2·0.5 V = C2·1 V = 1.5C2·V_new, so VX = VY ≈ 0.67 V just after t1 (the inductor current cannot change instantly, so it does not help at first). At t = ∞ all currents and drops are zero, so VX = VY = 1 V. Between them, L1 recharges C1 + C2 with an underdamped ring (Q = (1/0.1)·√(1 nH/3 nF) ≈ 5.8), so expect overshoot above 1 V before settling (added).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Use capacitance-weighted charge conservation rather than averaging voltages.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["1.0 V","0.5 V","0.75 V","2/3 V, approximately 0.667 V"],"ans":3,"why":["Reaching 1 V requires additional charge from the supply.","The initially higher-voltage capacitor transfers nonzero charge.","0.75 V is an unweighted mean despite unequal capacitances.","The total 2 nC charge is shared by 3 nF."],"ex":"V=(C1V1+C2V2)/(C1+C2)=(1×1+2×0.5)/3 = 2/3 V. This is the ideal charge-redistribution limit; the subsequent supply transient is a different stage.","oral":"(Exam 2, P5(C)) C2 = 2 nF (core decap), C1 = ½C2 = 1 nF (extra on-chip cap), L1 = L2 = 1 nH, R1 = R2 = 100 mΩ, I = 0. Initially VX = 1 V (C1 on the 1 V supply) and VY = 0.5 V (C2 on the 0.5 V supply). At t1, S2 opens and S1 closes. Find VX, VY just after t1 and at t = ∞."},{"id":"V256","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p5-d-resonant-frequency-after-t1","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Separate angular from cyclic resonance","q":"For an ideal LC resonance with L = 1 nH and C = 3 nF, what is the cyclic frequency f0?","a":"L = 1 nH, C = C1 + C2 = 3 nF: 1/√(LC) = 5.77·10⁸ rad/s, i.e. **f ≈ 92 MHz** (the official solution writes \"Wr = 92 MHz\"; numerically that is ωr/2π).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compute ω0 first, then divide by 2π.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 91.9 MHz","About 5.77 GHz","About 577 MHz","About 15.3 MHz"],"ans":0,"why":["f0 = 1/(2π√LC); angular frequency is about 5.77 × 10⁸ rad/s.","This has both a scale and angular-versus-cyclic conversion error.","This reports the angular-frequency number as cycles per second.","This divides by an extra factor near six."],"ex":"√LC = √(3 × 10⁻¹⁸) s. Keep rad/s and Hz labels distinct.","oral":"(Exam 2, P5(D)) Resonant frequency after t1?"},{"id":"V257","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p5-e-s1-closed-long-enough-that-both-caps-are-at-1-v-the-core-current-steps-0-500-ma-resonant-frequency-and-worst-case-undershoot-at-vy","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Approximate RLC supply droop","q":"Use the stated approximate droop estimate: Vmin=1−IR−I√(L/C)exp(−Rtpeak/(2L)), with I=0.5 A, R=0.1 Ω, L=1 nH, C=3 nF and tpeak=2.72 ns. Which Vmin is closest?","a":"Resonance is unchanged, ~92 MHz. IR drop = 0.5 A·100 mΩ = 50 mV. t_peak = ¼·T_r ≈ 2.72 ns (official: 2.75 ns). L·di/dt term = I·√(L/C)·e^(−R·t_peak/2L) = 0.5·√(1 nH/3 nF)·e^(−0.1·2.72 ns/2 nH) = 0.5·0.577·e^(−0.136) = 0.289·0.873 ≈ **0.25 V**. The official solution states **275 mV**, giving 50 + 275 = 325 mV and VY_min = 675 mV; recomputing with the same formula gives ~252 mV, so a total of ~300 mV and **VY_min ≈ 0.70 V**. Treat the 275 mV as an arithmetic slip; the method (IR + damped ΔI·√(L/C) at a quarter period) is the point.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Calculate the damping exponent with ns and nH converted consistently; this is an approximation, not an exact RLC minimum.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["0.675 V","0.75 V","0.70 V","0.95 V"],"ans":2,"why":["0.675 V uses a 0.275 V dynamic term that does not follow from the supplied arithmetic.","0.75 V roughly omits the separate 0.05 V static drop.","IR=0.05 V and the damped term is about 0.252 V, giving Vmin≈0.698 V under this approximation.","0.95 V includes only the static IR term and omits dynamic droop."],"ex":"IR=0.05 V and the damped term is about 0.252 V, giving Vmin≈0.698 V under this approximation.","oral":"(Exam 2, P5(E)) S1 closed long enough that both caps are at 1 V; the core current steps 0 → 500 mA. Resonant frequency and worst-case undershoot at VY?"},{"id":"V258","k":"md-eecs627-why-does-adding-more-decap-help-ldi-dt-droop-less-than-you-might-hope-and-what-else-can-you-do","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Decap diminishing returns","q":"Under the approximation peak droop ≈ ΔI√(L/C), with ΔI and L fixed, how much capacitance is needed to halve peak droop?","a":"Peak droop ∝ √(L/C)·ΔI, so 4× the decap only halves the droop, at area (and leakage) cost. Alternatives: lower L (flip-chip, more bumps), ramp current slowly (t_ramp ≫ 2L/R, e.g. 50-100 cycles), or add damping (lower Q).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Solve the droop ratio before solving for C.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["16× the original capacitance","4× the original capacitance","1/2 of the original capacitance","2× the original capacitance"],"ans":1,"why":["16× C would reduce this model’s droop to one quarter.","The square root of 1/4 is 1/2.","Reducing capacitance increases droop.","Doubling C gives only a 1/√2 reduction."],"ex":"The square-root law produces diminishing returns. Lower inductance, slower current ramps or suitable damping can complement added decap.","oral":"Why does adding more decap help L·di/dt droop less than you might hope, and what else can you do?"},{"id":"V259","k":"md-eecs627-why-did-soi-stay-restricted-to-space-applications-for-many-years","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Economic adoption of an SOI technology","q":"In the course’s historical bonded-SOI example, why could the technology be attractive for radiation-sensitive systems yet less competitive for general products?","a":"→ Bonded SOI needed two wafers per SOI wafer, so cost was not competitive for terrestrial products; the radiation tolerance still made it worth it for space. (V2 practice exam 2, Problem 6A)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Separate a technology’s physical benefit from its manufacturing economics.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The manufacturing premium was offset automatically by a guaranteed proportional clock-frequency gain.","Radiation tolerance was incidental; low wafer cost was the reason it fit specialized systems.","The application choice followed transistor count alone, without weighing radiation behavior against cost.","Radiation benefits could justify its more expensive wafer manufacturing."],"ans":3,"why":["A process benefit does not guarantee a proportional speed improvement or economic offset.","The cited example describes a cost premium justified by radiation requirements.","The central tradeoff is application-specific robustness versus manufacturing cost.","Application value can outweigh a manufacturing premium in a specialized market."],"ex":"This is the tradeoff in the cited historical example, not a claim that all SOI technologies or markets share one adoption history.","oral":"Why did SOI stay restricted to space applications for many years?"},{"id":"V260","k":"md-eecs627-what-technique-made-terrestrial-soi-practical","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Smart Cut wafer transfer","q":"What manufacturing benefit is associated with the Smart Cut SOI technique described in the course?","a":"→ Smart Cut: H+ implant defines a cleave plane so the donor wafer is reused, avoiding two wafers per SOI wafer. (V2 practice exam 2, Problem 6B)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Focus on how the thin silicon layer is separated from the donor wafer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It creates SOI entirely through metal deposition after transistor fabrication","It makes body potential irrelevant by eliminating all source/drain junctions","An implanted cleave plane permits transfer of a thin silicon layer while enabling donor-wafer reuse","It removes the buried oxide so the wafer becomes ordinary bulk silicon"],"ans":2,"why":["The technique is a substrate/layer-transfer process rather than post-transistor metal deposition.","SOI devices still have terminal and body electrostatics.","The hydrogen-implant cleave plane supports thin-layer transfer and reuse of the remaining donor substrate.","SOI retains an insulating buried layer; removing it is not the defining technique."],"ex":"The hydrogen-implant cleave plane supports thin-layer transfer and reuse of the remaining donor substrate.","oral":"What technique made terrestrial SOI practical?"},{"id":"V261","k":"md-eecs627-list-the-dc-body-charging-mechanisms-of-an-nmos-in-pd-soi-and-which-direction-each-pushes-vb","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Floating-body charging in PD-SOI","q":"In the source’s partially depleted SOI NMOS model, which pairing correctly identifies a body-charging mechanism and a body-voltage discharge/clamp mechanism?","a":"→ In: DB reverse leakage, impact ionization (holes), gate tunneling; out: BS forward diode (fast clamp); plus slow thermal generation/recombination.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Track hole inflow versus a forward-biased body-source escape path.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Forward body-to-source conduction charges the body; impact ionization removes all body holes","Impact-ionization holes charge the body; forward body-to-source diode conduction discharges/clamps it","Only gate capacitance sets the DC body voltage, regardless of leakage currents","Drain reverse leakage makes body charge identically zero; gate tunneling cannot affect it"],"ans":1,"why":["This reverses the principal directions.","Hole generation raises the floating body potential until current out balances current in.","Capacitance governs transients, while DC equilibrium also requires current balance.","Leakage and tunneling can contribute to the body current balance."],"ex":"A floating-body voltage is determined by competing charging and discharge mechanisms, not a fixed ground connection.","oral":"List the DC body-charging mechanisms of an NMOS in PD-SOI and which direction each pushes Vb."},{"id":"V262","k":"md-eecs627-which-is-slower-the-first-or-the-second-switch-of-an-nmos-pull-down-and-why","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Floating-body switching history","q":"In a floating-body SOI NMOS example, the body is more positively biased before the second pull-down event than before the first. If that lowers Vth, which event is faster, all else equal?","a":"→ The first: the body starts at 0.35 V and Cdb coupling drives it to about -0.1 V; in the second switch the body starts at 0.45 V (coupled up during the previous edge), so Vth is lower.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Use the stated threshold change rather than assuming identical initial conditions.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The first, because body history affects only leakage and never on-current.","The second, because lower Vth gives stronger pull-down drive.","They are identical because identical gate waveforms force identical effective thresholds.","The second only if its output load is also reduced; threshold shift alone cannot change delay."],"ans":1,"why":["Threshold and effective overdrive affect on-current as well.","Body history changes effective overdrive and hence current.","A floating body can retain a different voltage despite identical gate stimulation.","With the load fixed, stronger current can still reduce delay."],"ex":"Floating-body circuits can exhibit history-dependent delay; compare terminal voltages at the start of each event.","oral":"Which is slower, the first or the second switch of an NMOS pull-down, and why?"},{"id":"V263","k":"md-eecs627-describe-the-parasitic-bipolar-failure","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Floating-body SOI effects","q":"In a floating-body SOI pass device, why can lowering the source after the body has charged cause conduction even with the MOS gate off?","a":"→ Source and drain both high for a long time charge the floating body; when the source is pulled low (e.g., pass-transistor logic) the B-S junction forward biases and the lateral n-p-n conducts drain current with the gate off - it can discharge a held dynamic node.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Use the body as the base of the parasitic bipolar device.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Lowering the source guarantees the body voltage falls instantly by the same amount","The body-source junction can forward-bias and activate the parasitic lateral bipolar transistor","The buried oxide shorts source to drain during every voltage transition","The MOS gate oxide becomes a deliberate DC wire to the drain"],"ans":1,"why":["A floating body has capacitance and stored charge, so instantaneous equal tracking is not guaranteed.","Stored body charge can provide bipolar base drive when source potential drops, creating a gate-independent drain-current path.","The insulating buried oxide is not a switch that shorts source and drain.","Normal gate-off parasitic bipolar conduction does not require an oxide short."],"ex":"Stored body charge can provide bipolar base drive when source potential drops, creating a gate-independent drain-current path.","oral":"Describe the parasitic bipolar failure."},{"id":"V264","k":"md-eecs627-why-does-fd-soi-avoid-the-bipolar-and-most-hysteresis","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Fully depleted SOI and body history","q":"Why does fully depleting a sufficiently thin SOI film reduce the floating-body history effects emphasized in partially depleted SOI?","a":"→ The film (<50 nm) fully depletes, so there is no neutral body to store charge; side benefits are ~70 mV/dec swing and lower Vth at equal Ioff.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Focus on the reservoir of charge that remembers earlier bias conditions.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It removes every source of transistor leakage and variation","It removes the neutral body volume that stores substantial excess charge","It forces the device to operate only in strong inversion","It connects the gate directly to the drain"],"ans":1,"why":["It mitigates particular effects rather than eliminating all nonidealities.","Less neutral charge storage reduces the mechanism behind the cited hysteresis and parasitic bipolar effects.","Fully depleted devices can operate in different inversion regimes.","Full depletion is an electrostatic/body condition, not a gate-drain short."],"ex":"The relevant distinction is whether a neutral floating body can accumulate charge and alter subsequent behavior.","oral":"Why does FD-SOI avoid the bipolar and most hysteresis?"},{"id":"V265","k":"md-eecs627-what-do-body-contacts-cost-and-what-does-a-body-to-gate-tie-buy","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"What dynamic-threshold bias buys","q":"In a body-to-gate-tied DTMOS concept operated within safe junction-bias limits, what is the intended threshold behavior?","a":"→ Grounded body: slower than bulk but no history variation and no bipolar. Body-to-gate (DTMOS): low Vth when on, high Vth when off, but Vdd < 0.6 V, more gate and Miller capacitance, and back-gate RC delay.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Relate the body voltage to the input’s on and off states.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Stronger active drive with unchanged gate loading and no junction-bias limit.","Higher threshold while on and lower threshold while off.","Lower threshold in both states with no change in standby leakage.","Lower threshold while on and higher threshold while off."],"ans":3,"why":["Body connection can add capacitance and imposes safe-bias constraints.","This reverses the intended active/standby benefit.","The body bias changes with the gate, and off leakage remains a design constraint.","The bias seeks stronger active drive while limiting off leakage."],"ex":"Dynamic body bias has supply, junction-bias and capacitance constraints; it is not a free increase in drive.","oral":"What do body contacts cost, and what does a body-to-gate tie buy?"},{"id":"V266","k":"md-eecs627-two-utbb-soi-advantages-and-two-disadvantages","d":"dev","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Fully depleted SOI body control","q":"Which pair of advantages is associated with the UTBB-SOI approach described in the course?","a":"→ Strong back-bias effect and an undoped channel (low RDF); hard to manufacture and swing penalized by the thin BOX.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Identify the variability mechanism directly connected to intentional channel dopants.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Elimination of all process variation and all manufacturing complexity","Unlimited signal swing regardless of buried-oxide or device constraints","No electrostatic influence from the back gate and increased channel doping","Strong back-bias control and reduced random-dopant variability from an undoped channel"],"ans":3,"why":["Suppressing one variability mechanism does not eliminate all variation or fabrication difficulty.","Voltage swing remains subject to the actual device and oxide constraints.","This reverses the cited back-bias and doping advantages.","The thin-body/BOX structure supports back-bias control, and an undoped channel removes a source of random-dopant variation."],"ex":"The thin-body/BOX structure supports back-bias control, and an undoped channel removes a source of random-dopant variation.","oral":"Two UTBB-SOI advantages and two disadvantages?"},{"id":"V267","k":"md-eecs627-in-a-cmos-inverter-switched-by-an-instant-input-step-is-there-a-pull-up-size-that-minimizes-the-energy-drawn-from-vdd","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Can PMOS resistance minimize ideal charging energy?","q":"For an ideal step-driven CMOS inverter charging a fixed capacitor fully from a fixed VDD, neglect leakage, overlap current and device parasitics. What happens to energy drawn from VDD when PMOS resistance is reduced?","a":"→ No. The energy lost in the pull-up is ∫I·V dt = ½CV² regardless of its resistance; size only changes how fast it is burned. (V2 practice exam 2, Problem 1A)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Integrate current over the completed transition rather than comparing instantaneous power.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It stays CVDD² per charge event; charging becomes faster","It grows in direct proportion to resistance reduction","It halves whenever resistance halves","It becomes zero as resistance decreases"],"ans":0,"why":["Total delivered charge remains CVDD even though its time profile changes.","The stated ideal model supplies no such inverse-resistance energy law.","Energy is not proportional to resistance in full fixed-supply charging.","The same final stored charge still requires source work."],"ex":"The source supplies VDD∫I dt=VDD·CVDD. Real resizing can alter parasitic capacitance and overlap current, excluded here.","oral":"In a CMOS inverter switched by an instant input step, is there a pull-up size that minimizes the energy drawn from VDD?"},{"id":"V268","k":"md-eecs627-an-nmos-pass-device-n2-gate-at-vhigh-vdd-charges-cl-from-a-source-ramping-linearly-over-t-does-n2-sizing-matter","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Sizing for a slow power-clock ramp","q":"In the slow-ramp regime T ≫ RC, an NMOS with ample gate overdrive charges CL from a linear 0-to-VDD ramp. Neglect its added capacitance. What does reducing its resistance do to resistive loss?","a":"→ Yes, larger is better: the current is fixed at about CL·VDD/T, and a wider N2 lowers the voltage across it, so E = I²RT drops. (Problem 1B)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Determine current from the desired capacitor-voltage slope.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Increases loss because the ramp fixes a larger final charge.","Reduces loss approximately in proportion to R.","Makes the required average load current proportional to 1/R.","Leaves loss exactly at (1/2)CL VDD² for every ramp duration."],"ans":1,"why":["The final charge is unchanged; less resistance lowers dissipation.","The approximate current is CL VDD/T, giving loss I²RT.","In the tracking approximation, ramp slope and capacitance set the current.","That fixed loss applies to abrupt constant-voltage charging, not slow ramping."],"ex":"Adiabatic-style charging loss is approximately (RC/T)CL VDD² when the load follows the ramp; real sizing also changes capacitance.","oral":"An NMOS pass device N2 (gate at Vhigh ≫ VDD) charges CL from a source ramping linearly over T. Does N2 sizing matter?"},{"id":"V269","k":"md-eecs627-splitting-cl-into-cl-2-cl-2-behind-p1-and-an-n2-pass-device-with-an-instant-step-can-sizing-beat-circuit-a","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Split-capacitor switching energy","q":"An initially discharged total capacitance CL is split into two CL/2 capacitors and fully charged to VDD from an ideal constant supply through switches, without energy recovery. Can switch sizing reduce the total source energy below CL·VDD²?","a":"→ No. VDD supplies charge CL·VDD and energy CL·VDD² either way; sizing only changes which transistor dissipates the energy. (Problem 1C)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compare total initial/final capacitor charge while keeping the charging-source model fixed.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Yes; increasing switch resistance returns energy to the supply","No; it can redistribute dissipation and change timing, but total delivered charge remains CL·VDD","No; the source energy is instead always ½CL·VDD²","Yes; equal splitting halves the charge required for the final state"],"ans":1,"why":["A resistive charging path dissipates energy; energy recovery requires a different supply/charging scheme.","The supply delivers QVDD=CLVDD² for the same initial and final capacitor voltages; resistance changes the waveform and heat distribution.","One-half CV² is the final stored energy, not the full energy drawn from a step supply.","Two half-capacitances charged to the same voltage still require CLVDD total charge."],"ex":"The supply delivers QVDD=CLVDD² for the same initial and final capacitor voltages; resistance changes the waveform and heat distribution.","oral":"Splitting CL into CL/2 + CL/2 behind P1 and an N2 pass device, with an instant step: can sizing beat circuit (a)?"},{"id":"V270","k":"md-eecs627-give-three-difficulties-of-adiabatic-circuits-in-a-processor","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Adiabatic processor implementation costs","q":"Which set captures the source’s practical difficulties for resonant/adiabatic processor logic?","a":"→ Need an inductor with good Q on chip; resonant frequency not easily tunable (C load sets it); speed penalty (one gate per clock phase); poor scaling of size/complexity; dummy paths to align cascaded logic at multi-input gates. (Problem 1D)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Consider what circuitry returns stored charge to the power clock.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["High-Q energy-recovery hardware, load-dependent resonant timing, and phase-alignment overhead","An energy cost fixed entirely by transistor count, independent of power-clock waveform","Guaranteed full-speed operation at arbitrary load with no synchronization","Elimination of clock phases, arbitrary path timing, and no reactive components"],"ans":0,"why":["Energy recovery imposes physical resonator and timing constraints.","Waveform rate and circuit loading are central to recovered versus dissipated energy.","Changing load changes timing and phase requirements.","The approach depends on power-clock phases and reactive energy storage."],"ex":"Low dissipation is conditional on carefully shaped, phased power delivery; this can complicate frequency tuning and processor-scale composition.","oral":"Give three difficulties of adiabatic circuits in a processor."},{"id":"V271","k":"md-eecs627-energy-lost-when-ramping-over-t-300-ps-with-rc-30-ps","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Slow-ramp energy arithmetic","q":"Using Eres ≈ (RC/T)CV² for slow linear charging, what loss follows from RC = 30 ps and T = 300 ps?","a":"→ E = (RC/T)·CV² = 0.1·CV², versus 0.5·CV² for a step (derived).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Use the dimensionless ratio of the two times.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["0.10 CV²","0.50 CV²","10 CV²","0.01 CV²"],"ans":0,"why":["The ratio RC/T is 0.1.","This is the abrupt-step resistor loss.","This reverses the ratio.","This squares the ratio without justification."],"ex":"The stated approximation gives one-fifth of the 0.5CV² step-charging loss. It excludes power-clock generation and recovery overhead.","oral":"Energy lost when ramping over T = 300 ps with RC = 30 ps?"},{"id":"V272","k":"md-eecs627-why-use-a-dual-rail-adiabatic-gate-instead-of-the-single-rail-or","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Dual-rail adiabatic loading","q":"Why can a dual-rail adiabatic gate improve on a single-rail pass-gate OR in the described scheme?","a":"→ Full-rail outputs (PMOS restoration removes the Vt drop) and a data-independent load on the resonant clock.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Separate logic-level restoration from the load seen by the energy-recovery clock.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It creates adiabatic behavior even from an instantaneous fixed-voltage step","It can restore full output levels and present a more data-independent load to the resonant clock","It guarantees zero loss at any ramp rate and resistance","It eliminates the need for complementary signal representation"],"ans":1,"why":["Energy recovery depends on the power-clock waveform and switching conditions, not only the logic rails.","Complementary operation and PMOS restoration address weak levels and data-dependent clock loading in that scheme.","Finite resistance and nonideal switching still dissipate energy.","Dual-rail operation explicitly uses complementary representations."],"ex":"Complementary operation and PMOS restoration address weak levels and data-dependent clock loading in that scheme.","oral":"Why use a dual-rail adiabatic gate instead of the single-rail OR?"},{"id":"V273","k":"md-eecs627-why-can-t-dual-rail-adiabatic-gates-be-wired-arbitrarily","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Phase alignment in adiabatic logic","q":"In a four-phase dual-rail adiabatic family where each stage evaluates on the next 90° power-clock phase, why may a shorter logic path require dummy stages before a multi-input gate?","a":"→ Each gate evaluates on its own 90°-shifted phase; inputs must come from the previous phase, so unequal path depths need dummy buffer stages.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Track both signal value and power-clock phase through each path.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Its data must arrive with the same required preceding phase as the other inputs","The power clock makes arrival phase irrelevant","Dummy stages convert every Boolean function into an inverter","The dummy stages are required only to increase transistor count for symmetry"],"ans":0,"why":["Logic correctness depends on phase-qualified inputs, not only final Boolean values.","Evaluation follows a phase schedule.","Buffers preserve the intended function while aligning stages.","The requirement is timing alignment, not aesthetic device symmetry."],"ex":"Unequal path depth can produce mismatched phases; balancing stages restores the required temporal relationship.","oral":"Why can't dual-rail adiabatic gates be wired arbitrarily?"},{"id":"V274","k":"md-eecs627-where-did-energy-recovery-actually-ship-and-how-much-did-it-save","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Infer the clock share from an energy saving","q":"An illustrative resonant-clock report attributes a 10% reduction in total chip power entirely to a 24% reduction in clock power. With other power unchanged, what was the original clock share of total power?","a":"→ AMD Piledriver's resonant clock mesh: 24% of clock power, 10% of total.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Write total saving as subsystem share times subsystem reduction.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 2.4%","About 24%","About 10%","About 41.7%"],"ans":3,"why":["Multiplying the percentages answers a different question.","The clock reduction percentage is not its original share.","The total reduction is not the clock’s original share.","If clock share is x, 0.24x = 0.10, so x = 0.4167."],"ex":"A subsystem saving must be weighted by its original contribution before converting it to a whole-chip saving.","oral":"Where did energy recovery actually ship, and how much did it save?"},{"id":"V275","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p3a-what-does-cmp-stand-for-which-chip-elements-does-it-impact-and-how-and-which-foundry-design-rule-results-from-it","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"CMP density and layout effects","q":"Why do CMP effects motivate windowed metal-density rules and fill?","a":"Chemical Mechanical Polishing. It changes the metal dimensions (thickness, through dishing of wide lines and erosion of dense regions), which changes the electric field and therefore the **coupling capacitance and ground capacitance** (and wire resistance). The resulting rule is the **metal density** rule (min/max density per window, enforced with fill).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Connect local pattern density to post-polish metal geometry.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Density fill is solely a method for changing RTL fanout","CMP sets only transistor logical polarity and cannot alter wires","A DRC density check directly certifies timing for every extracted net","Pattern-dependent polishing changes thickness through dishing/erosion, affecting resistance and capacitance"],"ans":3,"why":["Fill is a manufacturing/layout feature and can itself affect parasitics.","CMP changes physical topography and metal dimensions, not Boolean polarity.","Density compliance is a geometry/process check, not complete electrical timing signoff.","Controlling local pattern density reduces polishing nonuniformity that changes physical interconnect dimensions and parasitics."],"ex":"Controlling local pattern density reduces polishing nonuniformity that changes physical interconnect dimensions and parasitics.","oral":"(Exam 1, P3A) What does CMP stand for, which chip elements does it impact and how, and which foundry design rule results from it?"},{"id":"V276","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p3b-list-three-manufacturing-uncertainties-that-cause-ioff-variation-and-how","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Why process variation strongly affects leakage","q":"Which explanation best connects manufacturing uncertainty to a large spread in off-state current?","a":"(1) **RDF and RTA** change Vth. (2) Lithography-induced uncertainty (**OPE/OPC, phase shift masks, double patterning**) changes the channel length. (3) **Temperature fluctuation during oxide growth** changes oxide thickness. Each enters Ioff exponentially through Vth, which is why leakage spreads 20X while frequency spreads 30%.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compare polynomial on-state behavior with exponential subthreshold dependence.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Every manufacturing variation changes only metal resistance and leaves transistor bias behavior fixed","Frequency and leakage must have identical percentage variation because they use the same transistors","Leakage is independent of threshold because the transistor is nominally off","Dopant/anneal, channel-length and oxide variations alter device electrostatics and threshold, which enter subthreshold current exponentially"],"ans":3,"why":["Several manufacturing steps directly affect transistor properties.","On-current delay and off-current leakage have different sensitivities.","An off transistor still conducts subthreshold current sensitive to threshold.","Small electrostatic shifts can produce large multiplicative leakage changes."],"ex":"The source names dopant/thermal processing, lithographic length and oxide growth as contributors; their electrical effects need a device model, not one universal percentage.","oral":"(Exam 1, P3B) List three manufacturing uncertainties that cause Ioff variation and how."},{"id":"V277","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p4a-b-a-6t-sram-sees-rdf-and-reticle-to-reticle-laser-intensity-variation-which-moves-gate-length-which-causes-bitcell-failure-with-single-patterning-and-with-double-patterning","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Patterning variation becomes mismatch","q":"In an SRAM exam model, one mask’s dose shift moves all six devices together; two separate masks can shift different devices differently. Why can the second case create an additional cell-stability risk?","a":"Single patterning: **RDF**, because laser intensity shifts all six transistors the same way and the cell fails on mismatch. Double patterning: **both**, because laser intensity now differs for the odd and even (mask 1 vs mask 2) transistors, so it creates mismatch inside the cell.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Ask whether both sides of a current contest move together.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It creates relative device mismatch inside the cell rather than only a common shift.","It creates only a common threshold shift, so all device strength ratios must remain unchanged.","It affects wire delay only; pattern-dependent gate-length changes cannot affect cell ratios.","It replaces random dopant fluctuation, making local random mismatch irrelevant."],"ans":0,"why":["Cell stability depends on relative strengths, not only average speed.","Separately patterned device groups can shift differently under the stated model.","Unequal device lengths can change the competing transistor strengths.","Different sources of mismatch can coexist."],"ex":"Distinguish common-mode process variation from differential changes among devices that must maintain a strength ratio.","oral":"(Exam 1, P4A/B) A 6T SRAM sees RDF and reticle-to-reticle laser intensity variation (which moves gate length). Which causes bitcell failure with single patterning, and with double patterning?"},{"id":"V278","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p4c-if-only-nmos-vary-does-single-double-patterning-hurt-read-or-write-more","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"SRAM mismatch and correlation","q":"Assume NMOS devices sharing one patterning step vary together, while splitting access and pull-down NMOS across steps reduces their correlation; PMOS do not vary in this model. Which SRAM margin gains an extra mismatch mechanism?","a":"**Read.** Read is set by access + pull-down, which sit on separate patterning steps, so they now mismatch. Write is set by access + pull-up, and the PMOS has no variation, so write only sees the NMOS's own variation.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Identify the two devices whose relative strength sets each operation’s margin.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Neither margin, because device variation cancels regardless of correlation","Read margin improves automatically because independent variation averages exactly to zero","Write margin only, because it is set by two PMOS devices","Read margin, because it depends on access-to-pull-down strength matching"],"ans":3,"why":["Ratio variability depends on covariance, so correlation cannot be ignored.","Independent random deviations do not cancel in every cell and can worsen the tail.","Writing involves access NMOS versus pull-up PMOS, not two varying PMOS here.","Read disturb depends on the ratio of two NMOS strengths; reduced correlation increases ratio variation under the stated assumptions."],"ex":"Read disturb depends on the ratio of two NMOS strengths; reduced correlation increases ratio variation under the stated assumptions.","oral":"(Exam 1, P4C) If only NMOS vary, does single → double patterning hurt read or write more?"},{"id":"V279","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p5a-clock-network-launch-flop-clocked-directly-capture-flop-through-two-clock-inverters-logic-10-inverters-each-inverter-50-ps-g-10-ps-l-5-ps-tc-q-100-setup-15-hold-100-ps-no-variation-with-die-to-die-only-give-the-3-skew-min-clock-period-and-min-logic-delay-for-hold","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Correlated global variation in setup timing","q":"A setup-period requirement is T = 100 ps + 15 ps + 10D − 2D, where every inverter shares the same die-to-die random delay D with mean 50 ps and σ=10 ps. Ignore local variation. What is mean(T)+3σ(T)?","a":"Skew = 2Dnom + 2Dd2d: µ = 100, σ = 20 → **160 ps**. Tclk = tC-Q + tsu + 10Dinv − 2Dinv: µ = 100 + 15 + 8·50 = 515, σ = 8·10 = 80 → **755 ps**. Hold: Dlogic = thold + 2Dinv − tC-Q: µ = 100, σ = 20 → **160 ps**. Global cancels between clock and data paths, leaving 8 net inverters.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Simplify the expression using the shared random variable before computing variance.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["915 ps","515 ps","755 ps","575 ps"],"ans":2,"why":["This fails to cancel the shared global variation in the subtracted clock path.","515 ps is the mean alone, with no 3σ allowance.","T=115+8D has mean 515 ps and σ=80 ps; adding 240 ps gives 755 ps.","This treats the correlated eight-delay contribution as much less variable."],"ex":"Perfectly shared global variation combines algebraically: ten data inverters minus two capture-clock inverters leave eight net copies of D.","oral":"(Exam 1, P5A) Clock network: launch flop clocked directly, capture flop through two clock inverters; logic = 10 inverters; each inverter µ = 50 ps, σG = 10 ps, σL = 5 ps; tC-Q = 100, setup = 15, hold = 100 ps (no variation). With die-to-die only, give the 3σ skew, min clock period and min logic delay for hold."},{"id":"V280","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p5b-and-b-now-add-l-5-ps-b-local-mc-at-the-3-die-to-die-corner-b-full-mc-with-both-random","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Combine independent timing variation","q":"A delay has independent zero-mean global and local random contributions with standard deviations 80 ps and √(12 × 5²) ps. What is the combined standard deviation?","a":"(B): skew µ = 160, σ = √(2·25) ≈ 7 → **181 ps**; Tclk µ = 515 + 24·10 = 755, σ = √(12·25) = 17.3 → **806.9 ps**; hold µ = 160, σ ≈ 7 → **181 ps**. (B+): skew σ = √(20² + 2·5²) = 21.2 → **163.6 ps**; Tclk σ = √(80² + 12·25) = 81.9 → 515 + 3·81.9 = **760.5 ps**; hold → **163.6 ps**. Local variation does not cancel (all 12 inverters count, in quadrature), and stacking a 3σ global corner on a 3σ local excursion (B) is pessimistic versus the true joint 3σ point (B+).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Add variances for independent random quantities.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 140 ps","About 81.9 ps","About 17.3 ps","About 97.3 ps"],"ans":1,"why":["This sums twelve local sigma values linearly with the global term.","Independent variances add: √(80² + 12 × 25) = √6700.","This includes only the local contribution.","This adds standard deviations directly rather than variances."],"ex":"For independent Gaussian contributions, a joint distribution is not the same claim as stacking separate worst-case sigma excursions.","oral":"(Exam 1, P5B and B+) Now add σL = 5 ps: (B) local MC at the +3σ die-to-die corner; (B+) full MC with both random."},{"id":"V281","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p5c-add-a-second-identical-logic-path-how-do-the-mean-and-of-fmax-change-under-die-to-die-only-and-under-within-die-only","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Shared variation across identical paths","q":"Two identical paths have exactly the same delay on each die because all variation is a perfectly shared die-to-die shift. What happens to chip fmax distribution when the second path is added?","a":"D2D only: mean **same**, σ **same** (the two paths are identical, so they are the same path). WID only: mean fmax **reduces** (fmax = min of the two path frequencies, the max of two delays), and σ **reduces** (the max of two partially correlated distributions is narrower).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Ask whether the extra path adds an independent possible worst case.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It is unchanged; taking the slower of two identical realized delays adds no new variation","Its mean halves because the number of paths doubles","Its standard deviation falls by √2 because the delays are averaged","Its standard deviation doubles because there are two paths"],"ans":0,"why":["Perfectly correlated identical paths have the same realized delay, so their maximum equals either one.","Parallel path count does not sum path delays.","Chip timing takes a maximum delay, not an average of independent samples.","A duplicated identical random value does not widen the maximum distribution."],"ex":"Perfectly correlated identical paths have the same realized delay, so their maximum equals either one.","oral":"(Exam 1, P5C) Add a second identical logic path. How do the mean and σ of fmax change under die-to-die only, and under within-die only?"},{"id":"V282","k":"md-eecs627-contrast-nbti-hci-and-em-and-say-why-power-grid-wires-are-more-em-prone-than-signal-wires","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"EM mechanism versus transistor aging","q":"Which pairing identifies electromigration rather than a transistor threshold-aging mechanism?","a":"NBTI: PMOS under negative gate bias at high T, Si-H bond breaking, set by duty cycle, partially recovers; ΔVth in mV falls with scaling but ΔIon(%) rises. HCI: mostly NMOS, only during switching, scales with activity × frequency. Power grid EM: high current density, **unidirectional DC** current with no healing, and width/thickness/via changes that create flux divergence.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Identify whether the damaged object is an interconnect or a transistor interface.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["PMOS negative-bias stress shifting threshold through interface damage","Current-driven metal transport producing voids/hillocks, especially under sustained unidirectional stress","A capacitive coupling pulse temporarily shifting a victim signal voltage","Hot-carrier stress changing transistor characteristics during operation"],"ans":1,"why":["This describes the source’s NBTI category.","This is interconnect material degradation associated with EM.","Coupled voltage noise is not accumulated EM damage.","This describes hot-carrier degradation, not metal atom transport."],"ex":"EM concerns wires and vias. NBTI and HCI concern transistor degradation; they must not be substituted for one another in reliability checks.","oral":"Contrast NBTI, HCI and EM, and say why power grid wires are more EM-prone than signal wires."},{"id":"V283","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p6a-what-is-the-difference-between-dvfs-and-avfs","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Separate workload and silicon adaptation","q":"Which description captures the distinction used here between conventional DVFS and AVFS?","a":"DVFS is **workload adaptation** (design-time V/f table, same for all dies). AVFS is **process and environmental adaptation** (V/f set per die and over time, using sensors, canaries, in-situ monitors or Razor).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Separate demanded performance from the voltage a particular die needs.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["DVFS chooses operating points for workload; AVFS additionally adapts to the die and environment using feedback or characterization.","AVFS adapts workload priority but deliberately keeps voltage identical across process and temperature.","AVFS removes the need for all safety margin because any canary exactly matches every critical path.","DVFS necessarily measures each die’s true timing continuously; AVFS only chooses from one universal workload table."],"ans":0,"why":["The adaptive scheme can recover per-die and environmental margin.","That omits the silicon/environmental adaptation being described.","Monitor mistracking and response latency can still require margin.","This reverses the distinction in the question."],"ex":"Real implementations may combine both concepts: workload policy selects performance while silicon feedback adjusts the voltage needed to deliver it.","oral":"(Exam 1, P6A) What is the difference between DVFS and AVFS?"},{"id":"V284","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p6b-i-a-chip-has-three-tester-filled-v-f-tables-at-20-25-and-85-c-and-a-temperature-sensor-it-reads-40-c-which-table","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Temperature selection under a validated model","q":"A temperature lookup controller must conservatively choose between characterized 25°C and 85°C tables at 40°C. Assuming validated monotonic hot-slow behavior at the current voltage, which table should it use?","a":"Above Vt, use the **higher-temperature** table (85 °C), since hot is the worst case. Below Vt, use the **lower-temperature** table (25 °C), because of temperature inversion (cold is slow when Vt dominates).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Choose based on the validated trend at this operating voltage, not a universal temperature rule.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Whichever table permits the higher frequency","The 25°C table regardless of temperature-inversion behavior","The 85°C table","The 25°C table"],"ans":2,"why":["Choosing performance alone discards the conservatism requirement.","The safe direction must change if the validated voltage regime exhibits cold-slow temperature inversion.","Under the explicitly assumed hot-slow trend, the hotter bracketing table is conservative.","The cooler table could allow a setting unsafe at the measured temperature."],"ex":"Under the explicitly assumed hot-slow trend, the hotter bracketing table is conservative.","oral":"(Exam 1, P6B i) A chip has three tester-filled V/f tables at −20, 25 and 85 °C and a temperature sensor. It reads 40 °C. Which table?"},{"id":"V285","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p6b-ii-which-variations-does-this-table-approach-address","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Limits of static calibration tables","q":"Consider the source’s calibration table characterized for die-to-die/within-die process spread and ambient temperature, without time-resolved sensing. Which additional effect should not be assumed covered?","a":"Addresses **both process variations (D2D and WID)** and **ambient temperature**. Does not address lifetime degradation, package effects, hot spots, or any fast-changing variation (IR drop, droop, coupling, jitter).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish slow calibrated conditions from transient events during operation.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The characterized ambient-temperature dependence","The characterized within-die process dependence","Fast supply droop and clock jitter","The characterized die-to-die process dependence"],"ans":2,"why":["Ambient temperature is explicitly part of the assumed table coverage.","Within-die variation is explicitly included.","Rapid operating disturbances are not captured merely by static calibration conditions.","Die-to-die variation is explicitly included."],"ex":"Coverage is limited by what the calibration observes. Aging, local hot spots, package effects and rapid disturbances require additional treatment.","oral":"(Exam 1, P6B ii) Which variations does this table approach address?"},{"id":"V286","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p6b-iiiiv-compare-with-a-canary-circuit-which-approach-wins","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Evaluate a canary fairly","q":"A canary tracks some aging and temperature changes but has voltage/temperature mistracking and different local devices from the real path. What is the defensible conclusion?","a":"A canary also tracks **degradation (aging)** and slow package variation, and can track temperature at finer grain. But it **mistracks across temperature and voltage** and **does not track intra-die variation**. Overall there is no firm answer, but the tables probably win: finer temperature tracking is offset by V/T mistracking, canaries miss WID, and degradation is typically small.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"List what the sensor shares with the real path and what it does not.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A precomputed table cannot be useful under any conditions.","Compare tracking error, coverage and response overhead against alternatives; neither method wins universally.","The canary always captures the exact worst path on every instruction.","The canary never needs guardband because it is on the same die."],"ans":1,"why":["A calibrated table can be competitive when its assumptions and margins are appropriate.","Benefits and errors must be quantified for the actual design.","Critical paths and their scaling can change with workload and conditions.","Local mismatch and response delay remain."],"ex":"Adaptive monitoring replaces some uncertainty with measurement, but the monitor’s relationship to the protected path must itself be bounded.","oral":"(Exam 1, P6B iii-iv) Compare with a canary circuit. Which approach wins?"},{"id":"V287","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p6c-i-in-razor-ii-what-sets-the-required-hold-time","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Razor short-path constraints","q":"In the described Razor II detection scheme, why must a short path avoid changing the latch node during the detection window?","a":"The time from the **detection clock going high to the main clock going low** (the latch becoming opaque). A short path launched by the next edge must not toggle the latch node during that window, or it is flagged as an error.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish the delayed intended sample from a newly launched value arriving too soon.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The required hold interval is set only by the previous cycle’s longest path","Short paths can never affect a timing-error detector","The detector measures only static leakage and ignores data transitions","A legitimate next-data transition during that window can be mistaken for a timing-error event"],"ans":3,"why":["The short-path requirement follows the detection and latch-closing window, not only the max path.","Hold constraints specifically protect against fast paths.","Razor-style timing detection relies on observed data transitions in relation to clock phases.","The detector watches transitions over a defined open/detection interval, so fast new data must be excluded from it."],"ex":"The detector watches transitions over a defined open/detection interval, so fast new data must be excluded from it.","oral":"(Exam 1, P6C i) In Razor II, what sets the required hold time?"},{"id":"V288","k":"md-eecs627-exam-1-p6c-ii-a-razorii-chip-comes-back-with-one-hold-violation-how-does-it-behave","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Razor replay and hold errors","q":"Suppose a replay-based timing-error scheme retries the same instruction at a lower frequency after an error. The exercised path has a deterministic same-edge hold violation and all other delays/skew stay fixed. What happens?","a":"When an instruction exercises that path, Razor flags an error and recovery replays the instruction at lower frequency. A hold failure does not depend on frequency, so it fails again every time: the processor hangs, makes no forward progress, and the error rate spikes to **100%**. It cannot execute that code.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Check whether replay changes any term in the hold inequality.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["The retry must succeed because every timing constraint improves with period","The retry can fail again because lowering frequency does not repair the hold race","The error detector changes the physical path delay merely by reporting the error","Only the first retry can fail; a second retry removes the short path"],"ans":1,"why":["Hold lacks the setup period term.","The faulty same-edge timing condition is reproduced on each retry.","Detection alone does not alter the failing circuit.","Repeating an operation does not inherently add minimum delay."],"ex":"Recovery by frequency reduction is suited to certain setup failures, not a fixed hold violation; the described loop can lose forward progress.","oral":"(Exam 1, P6C ii) A RazorII chip comes back with one hold violation. How does it behave?"},{"id":"V289","k":"md-eecs627-why-does-a-canary-need-margin-even-for-slow-variations-and-how-large-is-the-local-variation-part","d":"var","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Why a slow environment still needs canary margin","q":"Even if supply and temperature change slowly, why does a separate canary still require margin?","a":"Three reasons: mistracking (the real critical path changes with instruction and scales differently with V/T), local variation (canary and path are different devices: about 3σ on each side, with replica mistracking σ = 3% at 1.2 V and 8% at 0.7 V), and response time (detection + response delay times the environment's rate of change). Multiple canaries reduce the first two.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Separate static mismatch from the rate of environmental change.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Its topology and local device mismatch can make it track the real critical path imperfectly.","A slow environment makes critical-path selection independent of instructions.","Slow changes force every transistor to have the same mismatch.","Sensor response delay is the only possible source of margin."],"ans":0,"why":["Quasistatic operation does not remove systematic or local tracking error.","Workload can still select different paths.","Local mismatch does not vanish when the environment changes slowly.","Mistracking remains even if response delay is negligible."],"ex":"Guardband can cover tracking differences, local variation and response latency; removing one component does not eliminate the others.","oral":"Why does a canary need margin even for slow variations, and how large is the local-variation part?"},{"id":"V290","k":"md-eecs627-give-the-key-razor-arm-silicon-numbers","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Percentage power reduction","q":"A reported tuning example reduces an outlier’s power from 100 mW to 48 mW. What reduction relative to the original power is this?","a":"65 nm, 88 dies (30 FF / 37 TT / 21 SS), sign-off 724 MHz at 0.9 V / SS / 125 °C. TT9 at 1 GHz: typical workload at 0.97 V vs 1.1 V (3% margin) = **32% power saving**; power-virus PoFF 1.07 V. Razor-tuned VDD 906 mV (FF5), 963 mV (TT9), 1.063 V (SS6) vs 1.2 V: outlier power 100 → 48 mW (**52% saving**); both µ and σ of power improve.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Use the original power as the percentage denominator.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["52%","48%","2.08%","108.3%"],"ans":0,"why":["The saving is (100−48)/100=0.52, or 52% of the original power.","48% is the fraction of original power remaining.","The ratio 100/48 is about 2.08 times, not a 2.08% saving.","Dividing the saving by the final value uses the wrong baseline."],"ex":"The saving is (100−48)/100=0.52, or 52% of the original power.","oral":"Give the key Razor ARM silicon numbers."},{"id":"V291","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p2a-an-inverter-nmos-m1-and-pmos-m4-both-1-m-has-an-nmos-footer-m2-1-m-between-virtual-ground-vgnd-and-gnd-nmos-vth-300-mv-pmos-vth-200-mv-i0-300-a-m-1-vt-25-mv-0-vdd-1-v-input-vdd-sleep-bar-0-what-is-the-steady-state-leakage","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Footer-limited leakage estimate","q":"In the stated sleep model, a 1 µm NMOS footer limits steady leakage, with I0=300 µA/µm, Vth=300 mV, n=1 and thermal voltage=25 mV. Its drain voltage is large enough to neglect the reverse exponential factor. What is Ioff?","a":"The off PMOS (Vth = 200 mV) leaks far more than the footer (Vth = 300 mV), so VGND rises close to VDD and the footer has the full VDD across it, (1 − e^(−Vds/VT)) ≈ 1. Leakage is set by M2: I = 300 µA·e^(−300/25) = 300 µA·e^(−12) ≈ **1.8 nA**.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Evaluate the dimensionless threshold-to-thermal-voltage ratio first.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 1.84 µA","About 1.84 nA","About 25 µA","About 100.6 nA"],"ans":1,"why":["This is a factor-of-1,000 unit error.","300 µA×exp(−300/25)=300 µA×exp(−12)≈1.843 nA.","Dividing by 12 is not the exponential dependence.","About 100.6 nA would use a 200 mV threshold in this formula."],"ex":"Use the threshold of the limiting footer, not the off PMOS, under the explicitly stated current-limiting approximation.","oral":"(Exam 2, P2A) An inverter (NMOS M1 and PMOS M4, both 1 µm) has an NMOS footer M2 (1 µm) between virtual ground VGND and GND. NMOS Vth = 300 mV, PMOS Vth = 200 mV, I0 = 300 µA/µm, η = 1, VT = 25 mV, γ = β = 0, VDD = 1 V. Input = VDD, sleep_bar = 0. What is the steady-state leakage?"},{"id":"V292","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p2b-to-make-vgnd-reach-steady-state-instantly-a-small-transistor-m3-shorts-vgnd-to-vdd-when-sleep-is-asserted-with-cg-cj-1-ff-m-what-is-the-minimum-sleep-time-for-an-energy-saving","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Power-gating break-even time","q":"Assume sleep-entry/wake-up overhead is 5 fJ, supply is 1 V, ungated leakage is 100 nA and gated leakage is 1.8 nA immediately after entry. What sleep time breaks even?","a":"Overhead: switching the sleep transistor gate (Cg·1 V²) plus charging the junction capacitances of M1, M2, M4 to VDD; the solution totals E_overhead = **5 fJ**. Without gating the leakage is through the PMOS: 300 µA·e^(−200/25) ≈ **100 nA**. With gating: **1.8 nA**. Power saved = 1 V·(100 − 1.8) nA = **98.2 nW**. Break-even: t = 5 fJ / 98.2 nW ≈ **51 ns** (assuming leakage stops instantly).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Use the difference between ungated and gated power.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["About 50.9 ns","About 5.09 ns","About 98.2 ns","About 2.78 µs"],"ans":0,"why":["Saved power is 98.2 nW; 5 fJ / 98.2 nW = 50.9 ns.","This is a factor-of-ten error.","98.2 is the saved-power value in nW, not a time.","This divides by residual gated power instead of the saved power."],"ex":"Energy savings become positive only when saved leakage energy exceeds transition overhead under the stated instantaneous-settling assumption.","oral":"(Exam 2, P2B) To make VGND reach steady state instantly, a small transistor M3 shorts VGND to VDD when sleep is asserted. With Cg = Cj = 1 fF/µm, what is the minimum sleep time for an energy saving?"},{"id":"V293","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p2c-was-the-shorting-transistor-a-good-idea-with-the-simplified-model-0-does-steady-state-sleep-leakage-increase-decrease-or-stay-the-same-and-with-a-real-model","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Footer leakage and virtual ground","q":"A sleep modification holds an off NMOS footer’s drain at a higher virtual-ground voltage while its source and gate remain at ground. With DIBL included, why can its leakage increase?","a":"Simplified model: **increase**. M2 starts leaking at its full steady-state value immediately rather than after VGND drifts up, and VGND sits slightly higher (at VDD), so M2 leaks very slightly more. Real model: **increase**. With β ≠ 0 (**DIBL**), VGND held at VDD puts the maximum VDS across M2, which lowers its effective Vth and raises its leakage.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Identify which terminal voltage changed and the sign of its DIBL effect.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Higher drain voltage always raises threshold through DIBL","The gate necessarily acquires the same voltage as the drain","A device with VGS=0 has exactly zero current in every model","Higher VDS can lower the source-channel barrier and increase off current"],"ans":3,"why":["DIBL lowers the barrier/effective threshold rather than raising it.","The gate voltage is explicitly held at ground and is not shorted to the drain.","Subthreshold and other leakage mechanisms remain at zero gate-source bias.","Drain-induced barrier lowering reduces the effective off-state barrier as drain bias rises."],"ex":"Drain-induced barrier lowering reduces the effective off-state barrier as drain bias rises.","oral":"(Exam 2, P2C) Was the shorting transistor a good idea? With the simplified model (γ = β = 0), does steady-state sleep leakage increase, decrease or stay the same? And with a real model?"},{"id":"V294","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p2d-super-cutoff-lowers-an-nmos-switch-s-vgs-below-0-v-to-cut-subthreshold-leakage-why-does-total-leakage-eventually-rise-again-as-vgs-keeps-decreasing","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Excessively negative gate bias","q":"Why can making an off NMOS gate increasingly negative eventually increase total leakage when its drain stays high?","a":"**GIDL.** With VGS strongly negative and VDS large, the gate-drain field causes band-to-band tunneling in the overlap region, and current flows from drain to body. It grows with VDG, so it eventually outweighs the falling subthreshold current.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Consider the drain-to-gate voltage as the gate moves farther below ground.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Subthreshold current necessarily grows as VGS becomes more negative","Negative gate bias removes the drain electric field entirely","The gate always forward-biases a source-body diode regardless of body voltage","Gate-induced drain leakage grows with the strong gate-drain field and can exceed the reduced subthreshold current"],"ans":3,"why":["The usual subthreshold component falls with decreasing VGS.","The gate-drain potential difference increases.","The stated mechanism does not follow from gate voltage alone.","Band-to-band tunneling near the gate-drain overlap can become dominant."],"ex":"Total leakage is a sum of mechanisms. Suppressing subthreshold current can expose an increasing GIDL contribution.","oral":"(Exam 2, P2D) Super-cutoff lowers an NMOS switch's VGS below 0 V to cut subthreshold leakage. Why does total leakage eventually rise again as VGS keeps decreasing?"},{"id":"V295","k":"md-eecs627-why-does-a-2-high-nmos-off-stack-leak-9x-less-than-a-single-device-in-planar-90-nm-but-only-3x-less-in-a-16-nm-finfet","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Why stack benefit varies by device","q":"In the supplied off-stack model, a device platform has a smaller DIBL coefficient and a smaller rise of the intermediate node. Why can its leakage-reduction factor be smaller?","a":"The intermediate node rises to VM ≈ λd·VDD/(1+2λd), which gives the top device a negative VGS and a smaller VDS. Both act through the DIBL coefficient. Planar λd ≈ 0.1 gives VM ≈ 100 mV and Istack/Iinv ≈ 0.12; FinFET λd ≈ 0.05 gives VM ≈ 35 mV and ≈ 0.33. Less DIBL means less stack benefit.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish absolute leakage from the ratio between stacked and unstacked leakage.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["A lower stack-reduction factor proves that the device must have higher absolute leakage.","The same two-device topology guarantees the same reduction factor at every node.","The reduction factor can be copied between platforms using supply ratio alone, without their DIBL parameters.","Stacking removes less drain-induced leakage enhancement and produces less negative top-device VGS."],"ans":3,"why":["A relative improvement does not determine the unstacked baseline.","Different device electrostatics produce different bias and leakage responses.","Supply matters, but so do the device’s electrostatic coefficients and resulting node voltage.","The benefit depends on the bias shift and device sensitivity to it."],"ex":"A weaker stack-effect multiplier does not imply a worse absolute leakage device; compare the baseline and the relative improvement separately.","oral":"Why does a 2-high NMOS off stack leak ~9X less than a single device in planar 90 nm, but only ~3X less in a 16 nm FinFET?"},{"id":"V296","k":"md-eecs627-for-a-nand3-pull-down-which-input-state-minimizes-subthreshold-leakage-and-what-rule-governs-gate-leakage","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"NAND off-stack input selection","q":"For a NAND3 pull-down in a subthreshold-dominated model, which input state most strongly exploits the off-stack effect?","a":"000 (all three off, 1 pA vs 11 pA for 011). Isub depends mainly on the number of off devices, then on VDS across the off stack. Igate depends on the position of on devices: an on device whose channel is at 0 V leaks most (~2 pA); one with source/drain at VDD leaks ~0. Off devices with the drain at VDD show a small reverse current.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Count off devices in series; do not assume the same state minimizes every other leakage mechanism.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["000","011","101","110"],"ans":0,"why":["All three NMOS are off, allowing intermediate-node biases to suppress stack leakage.","Only the top NMOS is off, leaving no three-device off stack.","Only the middle NMOS is off.","Only the bottom NMOS is off."],"ex":"All three NMOS are off, allowing intermediate-node biases to suppress stack leakage.","oral":"For a NAND3 pull-down, which input state minimizes subthreshold leakage, and what rule governs gate leakage?"},{"id":"V297","k":"md-eecs627-in-a-finfet-process-going-from-ulvt-to-hvt-cuts-leakage-to-0-003-at-0-8-v-and-0-001-at-0-5-v-why-is-hvt-acceptable-at-0-8-v-but-not-at-0-5-v","d":"pwr","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"HVT penalty near threshold","q":"Why may an HVT device offer attractive leakage reduction at a high supply yet impose an unacceptable delay penalty when VDD approaches its threshold?","a":"Delay ∝ VDD/(VDD − Vth)^α. At 0.8 V the HVT delay penalty is 2.28X; at 0.5 V, VDD is close to the HVT threshold and the penalty is 13.81X. Low-VDD designs need low-Vt devices and must control leakage with gating instead.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compare the remaining gate overdrive, not just the off-current ratio.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["HVT leakage necessarily becomes greater than ULVT leakage near threshold","Threshold voltage stops influencing current at low VDD","The load capacitance becomes exactly zero near threshold","Its available overdrive becomes small, sharply reducing drive current and increasing delay"],"ans":3,"why":["The issue described is a drive penalty despite leakage savings.","Low-voltage current is particularly sensitive to threshold.","Capacitance does not vanish at threshold.","Delay scales roughly as CV/I, and near-threshold drive is highly sensitive to VDD−Vth."],"ex":"Device selection must satisfy both leakage and timing. The source’s numerical penalty is model-specific; HVT suitability depends on the path budget and operating supply.","oral":"In a FinFET process, going from ULVT to HVT cuts leakage to 0.003 at 0.8 V and 0.001 at 0.5 V. Why is HVT acceptable at 0.8 V but not at 0.5 V?"},{"id":"V298","k":"md-eecs627-what-are-the-trade-offs-of-putting-decap-on-the-virtual-rail-instead-of-the-real-supply-rail-of-a-power-gated-block","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Place decap on a virtual rail","q":"What tradeoff arises when decap is placed on the power-gated virtual rail?","a":"Virtual-rail decap gives better active performance (it suppresses the noise the switch adds) and its oxide leakage is gated off in sleep. But it lengthens convergence (wake-up) time and creates large in-rush current when the header turns on, so the header must be turned on slowly (staged), or the in-rush causes IR drop and ground bounce on the always-on rails.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Follow the capacitor’s charge from sleep to active operation.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It supports the local active rail but adds wake-up charge and potential inrush-current stress.","More virtual-rail capacitance necessarily reduces the charge required during wake-up.","It provides the same active support as an always-on decap without any extra recharge work.","Its active droop benefit proves that the sleep switch can be turned on abruptly at every corner."],"ans":0,"why":["The capacitor helps transient support while active and must be recharged on wake-up.","Charge demand scales with capacitance for a fixed voltage change.","The virtual node changes during sleep, so state-dependent charging matters.","Abrupt turn-on can create inrush and disturb the always-on grid."],"ex":"Stage the switch turn-on when needed and verify rail recovery, inrush and always-on-grid disturbance.","oral":"What are the trade-offs of putting decap on the virtual rail instead of the real supply rail of a power-gated block?"},{"id":"V299","k":"md-eecs627-why-is-on-chip-inductance-frequency-dependent-and-in-which-direction-do-r-and-l-move-with-frequency","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"High-frequency return-current distribution","q":"As frequency rises and return current crowds closer to an on-chip signal path, what trend does the course’s impedance model predict?","a":"Current takes the path of least impedance R + jωL. At low frequency the return spreads (low R, high L); at high frequency it crowds close to the signal by the proximity effect (higher R, lower L); at very high frequency skin effect raises R further (R ~ √f).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Separate the size of the current loop from the cross-sectional area carrying current.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Larger loop inductance and lower resistance because the return spreads farther","No change in either quantity because metal geometry is fixed","Zero resistance once inductive reactance dominates","Smaller loop inductance but higher effective resistance"],"ans":3,"why":["This describes the opposite of the stated return-current crowding.","Current distribution within fixed geometry can change with frequency.","Reactive dominance does not eliminate conductor loss.","A tighter return loop lowers inductance; concentrated current and skin/proximity effects raise effective resistance."],"ex":"A tighter return loop lowers inductance; concentrated current and skin/proximity effects raise effective resistance.","oral":"Why is on-chip inductance frequency dependent, and in which direction do R and L move with frequency?"},{"id":"V300","k":"md-eecs627-skin-depth-of-copper-at-1-ghz-and-the-formula","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Skin-depth frequency scaling","q":"For fixed conductor resistivity and permeability, skin depth is δ=√(2ρ/(ωμ)). If δ≈2.06 µm at 1 GHz, what is it approximately at 100 MHz?","a":"δ = √(2ρ/(ωµ)) = 2.06 µm (6.52 µm at 100 MHz, 8.42 mm at 60 Hz).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Use the square root of the frequency ratio.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["0.652 µm","6.52 µm","20.6 µm","2.06 µm"],"ans":1,"why":["This applies the scaling in the wrong direction.","Reducing frequency by ten increases δ by √10.","This assumes inverse-linear rather than inverse-square-root frequency dependence.","Skin depth changes with frequency even if material properties are fixed."],"ex":"δ scales as f⁻¹/², so 2.06√10≈6.52 µm.","oral":"Skin depth of copper at 1 GHz, and the formula?"},{"id":"V301","k":"md-eecs627-a-3-ghz-chip-has-10-ps-edges-what-frequency-should-you-use-to-judge-inductive-effects","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Use edge rate for interconnect bandwidth","q":"Using the heuristic fedge ≈ 1/(πtr), a 3 GHz circuit has tr = 10 ps. What frequency scale should guide high-frequency R/L evaluation?","a":"The edge rate sets it: the spectrum corner is about 1/(π·tr) ≈ 30 GHz, so evaluate R and L in the tens-of-GHz (proximity) regime, not at 3 GHz.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Convert picoseconds to seconds before taking the reciprocal.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["3 GHz","About 0.318 GHz","About 31.8 GHz","100 MHz"],"ans":2,"why":["Clock repetition alone misses fast-edge spectral content.","This has a factor-of-100 time/frequency conversion error.","The edge bandwidth is 1/(π × 10 ps), much higher than the repetition rate.","This is far below the stated edge bandwidth."],"ex":"The spectral heuristic depends on edge shape, but the key distinction is fast transition content versus clock repetition.","oral":"A 3 GHz chip has 10 ps edges. What frequency should you use to judge inductive effects?"},{"id":"V302","k":"md-eecs627-what-does-inductive-coupling-do-to-a-quiet-victim-and-how-does-it-differ-from-capacitive-coupling","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Mutual inductance and induced voltage","q":"Which expression captures the basic induced voltage from mutual inductive coupling to a victim loop?","a":"It induces Vy = m·dIx/dt, a bipolar glitch. In the 3 mm bus example it is about 0.18 V and opposite in sign to the small capacitive dip of the RC model, and it couples through loop overlap, so it reaches beyond nearest neighbours.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Check units and whether the coupling is driven by changing current or changing voltage.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["v=Cc·diaggressor/dt","v=M·diaggressor/dt","v=R·dvaggressor/dt","v=M·vaggressor"],"ans":1,"why":["Coupling capacitance relates current to voltage slew, not voltage to current slew.","Mutual inductance converts changing aggressor current into induced loop voltage; geometry and return paths determine M.","Resistance times voltage derivative has incorrect units and does not describe magnetic coupling.","Multiplying inductance by voltage has incorrect units for induced voltage."],"ex":"Mutual inductance converts changing aggressor current into induced loop voltage; geometry and return paths determine M.","oral":"What does inductive coupling do to a quiet victim, and how does it differ from capacitive coupling?"},{"id":"V303","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p3-a-two-single-layer-layouts-layout-1-has-fewer-more-widely-spaced-power-ground-stripes-layout-2-has-more-stripes-spaced-closer-but-its-nearest-stripe-is-farther-from-the-signal-than-layout-1-s-which-has-lower-resistance-and-lower-inductance-at-low-khz-and-high-10-ghz-frequency","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"High-frequency return-path choice","q":"In the lecture’s high-frequency approximation, return current is concentrated in the nearest same-size ground stripe. Layout 1 has a closer nearest stripe than layout 2. What follows within that approximation?","a":"Lower R: low f → **2** (more stripes in parallel); high f → **same** (current flows only in the nearest wire, and widths/thicknesses match). Lower L: low f → **2** (current spreads over all stripes, and layout 2's stripes are closer together, giving a smaller loop); high f → **1** (current takes the nearest wire, which is closer to the signal in layout 1).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Distinguish conductor cross-section from signal-return loop area.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Layout 2 must have lower inductance because it has more distant parallel stripes","Similar conductor resistance, but layout 1 has lower loop inductance","Layout 1 has zero resistance because the return path is closer","Both inductances are identical regardless of signal-return separation"],"ans":1,"why":["The premise says distant stripes do not carry appreciable high-frequency return current.","The selected stripe geometry sets similar resistance, while closer return reduces loop area.","Closer return changes loop geometry, not conductor resistance to zero.","Signal-return spacing affects magnetic field and loop inductance."],"ex":"This is a stated nearest-return model, not a universal layout ranking. Low-frequency current spreading can produce a different comparison.","oral":"(Exam 2, P3(A)) Two single-layer layouts: layout 1 has fewer, more widely spaced power/ground stripes; layout 2 has more stripes, spaced closer, but its nearest stripe is farther from the signal than layout 1's. Which has lower resistance and lower inductance at low (kHz) and high (10 GHz) frequency?"},{"id":"V304","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p3-b-an-explicit-decap-cd-is-added-between-the-vdd-and-gnd-grids-layout-3-signal-centred-between-the-grids-layout-4-signal-three-times-closer-to-gnd-than-to-vdd-signal-rising-all-vddgnd-current-through-the-decap-effect-on-low-frequency-inductance","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Common-mode motion across a decap","q":"An ideal decap joins two supply-grid nodes. In a symmetric excitation, both terminals have exactly the same voltage waveform. What current flows through that decap?","a":"Layout 3: equal signal-to-grid capacitances inject equal currents, both Cd terminals move together, no current flows through Cd, so **inductance stays the same**. Layout 4: more current enters the GND grid, Cd conducts some of it into the VDD grid, which is farther from the signal (larger loop), so **inductance increases slightly** (only slightly, because at low frequency the current spreads and the loops are large anyway).","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Write the capacitor voltage as one terminal minus the other.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["C times the slew of either terminal relative to ground.","Zero, because its differential voltage does not change.","A current determined by the absolute common-mode voltage rather than its differential change.","Twice C times the common-mode slew because both terminals contribute."],"ans":1,"why":["This incorrectly treats the opposite terminal as fixed ground.","i = C d(V1 − V2)/dt, which is zero for identical waveforms.","An ideal capacitor responds to differential voltage change, not common-mode level.","The two equal terminal changes subtract in capacitor voltage, rather than add."],"ex":"Return-path effects depend on differential excitation. Symmetry can make an added grid-to-grid capacitor inactive for a particular mode.","oral":"(Exam 2, P3(B)) An explicit decap Cd is added between the VDD and GND grids. Layout 3: signal centred between the grids. Layout 4: signal three times closer to GND than to VDD. Signal rising, all VDD-GND current through the decap. Effect on low-frequency inductance?"},{"id":"V305","k":"md-eecs627-exam-2-p3-c-same-layouts-high-frequency","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Decoupling and return-path inductance","q":"At high frequency, decoupling lets return current transfer from a distant supply grid to a closer ground grid. If this reduces loop area, what can happen to the signal loop inductance?","a":"Cd is now a short between grids and current follows the smallest loop. Layout 3: VDD and GND return loops are equal, so jumping grids gains nothing; **no (little) change**. Layout 4: current injected into the VDD grid from the far half of the signal can now jump through Cd to the closer GND grid mid-way, shrinking its loop, so **inductance decreases**, but only by a limited fraction (the solution suggests maybe 25% or less), since only the smaller signal-to-VDD current from the second half of the line is affected.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Follow the high-frequency return path rather than only the forward signal trace.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It decreases, by an amount depending on which portions of current actually change path","It is unchanged even if the current-return geometry changes substantially","It must increase because the number of named grids increased","It becomes exactly zero for any nonzero decoupling capacitor"],"ans":0,"why":["A tighter magnetic loop can lower inductance, but only the affected return-current portions contribute to the improvement.","Changing return distribution can change inductance even with the same signal wire.","Inductance depends on current geometry, not the count of supply-net names.","Finite geometry and nonideal decoupling do not imply zero loop inductance."],"ex":"A tighter magnetic loop can lower inductance, but only the affected return-current portions contribute to the improvement.","oral":"(Exam 2, P3(C)) Same layouts, high frequency?"},{"id":"V306","k":"md-eecs627-why-can-a-repeated-wire-be-faster-with-some-inductance-and-what-limits-it","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Inductance in a repeated wire","q":"Why is “less inductance always means less delay” not sufficient for the source’s repeated-wire example?","a":"Inductance sharpens the edge at each repeater input, and faster input slopes cascade down the chain: slew improves about 5× and total delay drops to about 0.87× of RC near 1.4 nH. Beyond the inflection point overshoot rises rapidly (to about 28% of VDD at 3 nH) and delay increases.","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Account for both edge shape and underdamped ringing.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Inductance removes all wire resistance and therefore always improves both delay and integrity","Overshoot cannot propagate through a chain of repeaters","Moderate inductance can sharpen repeater input edges, but excessive inductance adds overshoot/ringing and can increase delay","The optimum is independent of driver strength, capacitance and damping"],"ans":2,"why":["Resistance remains and excessive ringing is a cost.","Repeater behavior depends on the incoming waveform.","The transient shape affects successive gate delays as well as signal integrity.","RLC and driver conditions determine the balance."],"ex":"Evaluate the complete transient and receiver thresholds. A faster edge in one regime does not justify unrestricted inductance.","oral":"Why can a repeated wire be faster with some inductance, and what limits it?"},{"id":"V307","k":"md-eecs627-using-the-slide-s-energy-numbers-how-much-more-expensive-is-fetching-a-bit-from-off-chip-memory-than-computing-with-it-in-a-pe-and-what-does-that-imply","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Compare movement with compute energy","q":"In an illustrative system, off-chip fetch costs 1,000 fJ/bit, an SRAM access 50 fJ/bit and local computation 6 fJ/bit. Which comparison is correct?","a":"~1000 fJ/bit vs 6 fJ/bit, about 167X (and 20X an on-chip SRAM access at 50 fJ/bit). Data movement, not arithmetic, dominates energy, so the goal is to reduce weight and activation movement: near-memory or in-memory compute. (derived)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compare each cost per bit using consistent units.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Off-chip fetch is about 20× compute and 167× SRAM access.","Off-chip fetch is about 6× compute and 50× SRAM access.","Off-chip fetch is about 167× compute and 20× SRAM access.","Off-chip fetch is about 0.006× compute and 0.05× SRAM access."],"ans":2,"why":["This swaps the denominators.","The raw energy values are not the required ratios.","The ratios are 1000/6 and 1000/50.","These are the inverse ratios."],"ex":"Under these stated numbers, reducing repeated data movement can save more energy than optimizing an already cheap arithmetic operation.","oral":"Using the slide's energy numbers, how much more expensive is fetching a bit from off-chip memory than computing with it in a PE, and what does that imply?"},{"id":"V308","k":"md-eecs627-two-cells-in-the-same-column-are-read-at-once-on-a-precharged-bl-blb-pair-what-does-each-line-compute-and-which-input-case-is-hardest-to-sense","d":"arith","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Bitline computation truth tables","q":"In the stated ideal two-row SRAM compute scheme, BL remains high only when both stored bits are 1, while BLB remains high only when both are 0. Which Boolean functions do the high-level indicators represent?","a":"BL stays high only if both cells hold 1: **AND**. BLB stays high only if both hold 0: **NOR**. XOR = NOR(AND, NOR). The **01/10** case has the smallest margin, since it lies between the no-discharge and two-cell-discharge levels. Multi-row activation also raises read-disturb risk in a 6T cell. (derived)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Build the four-entry truth table using the given high-level conditions.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["BL: OR; BLB: NAND","BL: XOR; BLB: XNOR","BL: NAND; BLB: OR","BL: AND; BLB: NOR"],"ans":3,"why":["OR is high for either one and NAND is high except for 11, which contradicts the given conditions.","XOR and XNOR distinguish equality, not the two specified rail-high cases.","This reverses the logic implied by the stated precharge/discharge convention.","High only for 11 defines AND, and high only for 00 defines NOR."],"ex":"High only for 11 defines AND, and high only for 00 defines NOR.","oral":"Two cells in the same column are read at once on a precharged BL/BLB pair. What does each line compute, and which input case is hardest to sense?"},{"id":"V309","k":"md-eecs627-why-do-many-cim-designs-use-an-8t-cell-rather-than-a-6t-cell","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"8T read-port advantage for CIM","q":"Why does a decoupled 8T SRAM read port help computations that activate multiple read wordlines?","a":"The 8T read port (RWL-gated 2T stack onto RBL) is decoupled from the storage nodes. RBL discharges only when RWL·Q = 1, which is a 1-bit multiply, and many rows can be active at once without disturbing the stored data. (derived)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Compare which node the read current flows through in 6T and decoupled-read cells.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["It removes the need to sense the read bitline","Read-bitline discharge is controlled by stored data without directly exposing storage nodes to the shared read bitline","It makes read-bitline capacitance independent of array size","It writes all selected storage nodes to zero before computing"],"ans":1,"why":["An output quantity still must be sensed or converted.","Separating read and storage paths reduces read-disturb coupling during multirow activation.","Read wiring and device loading still scale with implementation.","Destructive overwriting is not the intended decoupled-read mechanism."],"ex":"The source’s read stack discharges for RWL·Q=1, enabling a one-bit product while isolating stored state from the read path.","oral":"Why do many CIM designs use an 8T cell rather than a 6T cell?"},{"id":"V310","k":"md-eecs627-compare-current-domain-and-charge-domain-accumulation","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Current versus charge accumulation","q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes idealized current-domain and charge-domain accumulation?","a":"Current domain: cell currents sum on a clamped BL (KCL), read by a current ADC. Simple cells, but nonlinear and burns static current. Charge domain: each cell adds ΔQ (I_ON·t_pulse or C0·V_WL) and the BL voltage accumulates ΔV = ΣΔQ/C_BL, read by a voltage ADC. Linear (especially capacitor-based), but larger cells and a range limited by VDD. (derived)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Choose the conserved quantity before writing the output relation.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Both modes ideally produce an output voltage independent of bitline capacitance for fixed input pulse widths.","Current mode sums cell currents by KCL; charge mode sums delivered charge and converts it to voltage through capacitance.","Charge mode provides unlimited output range as more cells contribute charge.","Current mode inherently avoids device I-V nonlinearity because KCL is linear."],"ans":1,"why":["Charge-mode voltage depends explicitly on accumulated charge divided by capacitance.","The conserved quantities are current at a node and charge on a capacitor.","Finite supply and sensing range limit the allowable accumulated voltage.","KCL sums currents, but each device’s current generation can be nonlinear."],"ex":"Current summation faces device nonlinearity and current power; charge accumulation faces capacitance, swing and conversion constraints.","oral":"Compare current-domain and charge-domain accumulation."},{"id":"V311","k":"md-eecs627-define-cim-signal-margin-and-the-adc-requirement-why-does-margin-fall-as-more-rows-are-accumulated","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Compute-in-memory decision margin","q":"Adjacent CIM output distributions leave a usable gap m. Ignoring every other error, what ADC offset bound keeps the threshold inside a midpoint decision gap?","a":"Signal margin is the minimum gap between adjacent MAC-level distributions ≈ (output range / number of levels) − variation, and ADC offset must be < margin/2. More rows means more levels in the same range (gap ∝ 1/N), while each level's spread grows with the number of contributing cells. Throughput rises and margin collapses (slide 37: from ~0.75 a.u. at 1 row to ~0 at 128). (derived)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"A centered threshold can move only to the nearest gap boundary.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["No offset bound if the ADC has enough nominal codes","Offset equal to the entire output range","Magnitude of offset less than m/2","Magnitude of offset less than 2m"],"ans":2,"why":["Resolution alone does not remove an input-referred threshold offset.","Full-scale range says nothing about separation of neighboring levels.","A midpoint threshold has half the remaining gap available in either direction before reaching a distribution boundary.","Twice the gap can move the threshold across an adjacent distribution."],"ex":"A midpoint threshold has half the remaining gap available in either direction before reaching a distribution boundary.","oral":"Define CIM signal margin and the ADC requirement. Why does margin fall as more rows are accumulated?"},{"id":"V312","k":"md-eecs627-in-a-current-domain-sram-cim-with-n-rows-active-what-sets-the-zero-level-and-the-maximum-useful-n","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"CIM zero-level leakage","q":"In a current-domain SRAM CIM model, each of N active rows contributes Ioff when its product is zero and Ion when its product is one. What is the all-zero current and the current for k ones?","a":"Off cells still conduct I_off, so MAC = 0 gives N·I_off and every level is offset by the off-current of the cells not contributing. Useful N is limited by the I_on/I_off ratio and its variation at the hot, leaky corner. This is the same limit as cells per bitline in SRAM/ROM. (derived)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Sum contributions from the k conducting rows and the remaining N−k rows.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Zero: Ioff; k ones: k·Ion + Ioff","Zero: N·Ion; k ones: N·Ion − k·Ioff","Zero: N·Ioff; k ones: k·Ion + (N−k)·Ioff","Zero: 0; k ones: k·Ion"],"ans":2,"why":["The baseline scales with the number of off contributors, not one cell.","This interchanges the zero and conducting states.","All rows contribute leakage except those contributing their on-current instead.","This neglects leakage from the zero-contributing cells."],"ex":"The level spacing is Ion−Ioff around an N·Ioff baseline. Leakage and variation limit useful row count and distinguishable levels.","oral":"In a current-domain SRAM CIM with N rows active, what sets the zero level and the maximum useful N?"},{"id":"V313","k":"md-eecs627-what-does-a-mux-based-local-compute-cell-replace-and-what-are-the-two-variants-benefits","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Understand mux-based local compute","q":"Two one-bit inputs select contributions W0 and W1. Which four values must a mux select to replace their multiplications and first addition?","a":"It replaces the 1-bit multipliers plus the first adder stage. With two input bits the output is 0, W1, W0 or W0+W1. With a static adder generating W0+W1: 7% fewer devices and 5% faster (ISSCC'24). With W0+W1 precomputed and stored in the array: the static adder's area and power are removed as well. (derived)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Enumerate the four pairs of one-bit inputs.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["0, W1, W0, W0 − W1.","W0, W1, W0W1, W0/W1.","0, W1, W0, W0 + W1.","0, 1, W0, W1."],"ans":2,"why":["When both input bits are one, the contributions add rather than subtract.","Binary gating produces sums of selected weights, not products or ratios of weights.","The four input combinations select neither, one, or both weights.","The both-active case must include both weights."],"ex":"Precomputing or storing W0 + W1 trades storage and selection logic against repeated local addition.","oral":"What does a mux-based local compute cell replace, and what are the two variants' benefits?"},{"id":"V314","k":"md-eecs627-why-does-a-digital-cim-macro-use-a-dual-rail-supply-and-which-devices-go-where","d":"mem","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Separate supplies in compute-in-memory","q":"For the described dual-supply digital-CIM policy, which assignment reflects the intended speed/leakage tradeoff?","a":"MAC/adder logic sits on a higher-VDD, low-Vt domain for performance. SRAM storage and decode sit on a VDDM domain with high-Vt devices for low standby current. Level shifters cross between them. This applies the dual-Vt leakage trade-off from L09 at the macro level. (derived)","src":"EECS 627 lecture book","hint":"Separate the many retained bits from the high-activity arithmetic path.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Performance-critical MAC logic on a faster higher-voltage domain; storage on a leakage-oriented domain with appropriate interfaces","All devices must use the highest voltage and lowest Vt because storage never leaks","Storage on a separate supply automatically eliminates all level-translation needs","The two domains must have identical voltage, Vt and timing to gain any benefit"],"ans":0,"why":["The policy spends speed/power where computation needs it and saves standby power in the large stored-state array.","Stored arrays can dominate leakage precisely because of their device count.","Signals crossing different voltage domains need validated electrical interfaces.","The intended benefit comes from different operating tradeoffs, not mandatory identical domains."],"ex":"The policy spends speed/power where computation needs it and saves standby power in the large stored-state array.","oral":"Why does a digital CIM macro use a dual-rail supply, and which devices go where?"},{"id":"V315","k":"md-scripting-how-did-you-run-16-pe-variants","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Parameterized parallel implementation runs","q":"Which flow matches the source’s method for running indexed PE variants through Make and Tcl?","a":"- **\"How did you run 16 PE variants?\"** A Make pattern rule; `$*` passed the index as an env var; Tcl read it with `::env` and elaborated with `-parameters`; `make -j` ran them in parallel.","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"Trace how the target-specific index reaches the HDL elaboration command.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Put the variant number only in a comment so the elaborator can infer it","A Make pattern-rule stem selects a variant; pass it through an environment variable read by Tcl and elaborate with that parameter","Treat make -j as a substitute for passing each variant’s index","Use one fixed Tcl parameter for every target and rename outputs afterward"],"ans":1,"why":["Comments do not establish the elaboration parameter contract.","The index becomes an explicit elaboration input while Make schedules independent targets.","Parallel execution controls scheduling, not design parameter values.","Renaming files does not change the elaborated hardware."],"ex":"Keep the variant index explicit from build target through environment to Tcl elaboration. Parallel jobs also need separate output artifacts.","oral":"\"How did you run 16 PE variants?\""},{"id":"V316","k":"md-scripting-what-does-upvar-do","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Tcl caller-variable alias","q":"Inside a Tcl procedure, what does `upvar 1 counter localCounter` do?","a":"- **\"What does `upvar` do?\"** It links a local name to a variable in the caller's scope: pass by reference. I used it for a running pin-slot counter.","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"Distinguish aliasing a variable from copying its current value.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Makes localCounter refer to counter in the caller’s scope.","Copies counter once into an independent local variable.","Creates a new global variable regardless of call depth.","Schedules counter to be updated after the procedure returns."],"ans":0,"why":["upvar creates an alias to a variable at the specified stack level.","Subsequent updates through the alias affect the linked variable.","Level 1 means the caller, not always the global scope.","Updates through the alias happen when executed."],"ex":"Use scope levels deliberately; aliasing permits a procedure to update caller-owned state.","oral":"\"What does upvar do?\""},{"id":"V317","k":"md-scripting-vs-in-tcl","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Tcl braces and substitution","q":"In Tcl, after `set x 7`, how do `set a {$x}` and `set b \"$x\"` differ? Ignore the special backslash-newline processing case.","a":"- **\"`{}` vs `\"\"` in Tcl?\"** Braces: no substitution (used for code bodies and `expr`). Quotes: variable and command substitution.","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"Compare word grouping with whether substitution is performed inside the group.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["a contains literal $x; b contains 7","a contains 7; b contains literal $x","Both contain literal $x","Both contain 7"],"ans":0,"why":["Braces suppress ordinary variable substitution during this parsing step; double quotes allow it.","This reverses brace and quote substitution behavior.","The quoted word substitutes the current value of x.","The braced word preserves the dollar expression literally."],"ex":"Braces suppress ordinary variable substitution during this parsing step; double quotes allow it.","oral":"\"{} vs \"\" in Tcl?\""},{"id":"V318","k":"md-scripting-why-escape-brackets-in-pin-names","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Literal brackets in Tcl","q":"Why must a literal pin name bus[3] be braced or have its brackets escaped in Tcl?","a":"- **\"Why escape brackets in pin names?\"** In Tcl, `[ ]` runs a command, so `bus[3]` must be written as `bus\\[3\\]` or inside braces.","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"Separate the design tool’s pin-name grammar from Tcl’s command grammar.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Unprotected square brackets request command substitution, so Tcl may try to execute 3","Square brackets always denote a Tcl list index without substitution","Braces cause command substitution while square brackets disable it","The bracket characters are forbidden in all legal pin names"],"ans":0,"why":["Tcl evaluates bracketed text as a command unless protected by appropriate quoting.","Tcl list indexing uses commands such as lindex; literal brackets are not indexing syntax here.","This reverses their substitution roles.","The issue is Tcl interpretation, not the legality of the netlist pin name."],"ex":"Use {bus[3]} for a literal word, or escape brackets where substitution of other text is needed.","oral":"\"Why escape brackets in pin names?\""},{"id":"V319","k":"md-scripting-vs-vs","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Make automatic variables","q":"In a Make pattern-rule recipe, which meanings correspond to `$@`, `$<` and `$*`?","a":"- **\"`$@` vs `$<` vs `$*`?\"** Target name, first prerequisite, pattern stem.","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"Identify the output, the first input and the pattern match separately.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Target name; first prerequisite; matched stem.","First prerequisite; target name; all prerequisites.","Shell exit status; process ID; command arguments.","Matched stem; all prerequisites; target name."],"ans":0,"why":["These are the rule-specific automatic variables used to construct commands.","This reverses the first two and confuses the stem with all prerequisites.","Those are shell-style concepts, not these Make automatic variables.","All prerequisites are not represented by these mappings."],"ex":"The pattern stem is the portion matched by `%`; `$<` is one prerequisite rather than the complete dependency list.","oral":"\"$@ vs $< vs $*?\""},{"id":"V320","k":"md-scripting-why-does-each-make-recipe-line-need-or","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Make recipe shell scope","q":"In ordinary Make recipes without `.ONESHELL`, why does a `cd work` command on one recipe line not change the directory for a command on the next line?","a":"- **\"Why does each Make recipe line need `;` or `\\`?\"** Each line runs in a separate shell, so `cd` and `export` don't carry over.","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"Ask which process owns the working-directory state.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Only environment variables can cross lines, so directory changes are automatically exported","Each logical recipe line normally executes in a separate shell","The second line executes before the first by definition","Make forbids changing directory in recipes"],"ans":1,"why":["A child-shell directory change does not alter the next shell or Make’s own working directory.","The directory change belongs to the first shell process; combine dependent commands in one shell or use an appropriate Make mechanism.","The issue is shell lifetime, not mandatory reverse ordering.","A recipe can change directory within its shell."],"ex":"The directory change belongs to the first shell process; combine dependent commands in one shell or use an appropriate Make mechanism.","oral":"\"Why does each Make recipe line need ; or \\?\""},{"id":"V321","k":"md-scripting-how-would-you-find-the-worst-slack-across-1-000-reports","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Worst slack across many reports","q":"You must find the worst slack across 1,000 reports. Which procedure prevents an unreadable report from silently becoming a good result?","a":"- **\"How would you find the worst slack across 1,000 reports?\"** grep/awk or a Python parser into a table, sorted; flag unparsed files; check the format on one report first.","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"Decide how the summary represents a report that did not yield a valid number.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Select the largest slack because positive numbers indicate the worst violation","Sort raw text strings without normalizing numbers or units","Validate the format on a sample, parse comparable slack records with provenance, report unparsed files, then take the minimum valid slack","Treat files with no parsed slack as zero and include them in the ranking"],"ans":2,"why":["The most negative comparable slack is the worst.","Lexical sorting and mixed units can misorder numerical results.","Explicit parse/coverage failures preserve the distinction between valid timing and missing evidence.","Zero may hide a missing severe violation and is not a measured value."],"ex":"A ranking is valid only for successfully parsed, comparable records; it does not excuse incomplete coverage.","oral":"\"How would you find the worst slack across 1,000 reports?\""},{"id":"V322","k":"md-scripting-how-do-you-make-a-flow-script-robust","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Fail safely in a flow script","q":"A required timing report is absent when a flow script starts. What is the most robust behavior?","a":"- **\"How do you make a flow script robust?\"** Fail fast on missing inputs, no hard-coded paths (use `info script`), one config file for shared constants, log everything, and a summary at the end.","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"What conclusion is justified when the evidence does not exist?","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."],"opts":["Treat the report as empty and emit zero violations.","Reuse a same-named report from an unknown older run.","Stop with a clear missing-input error and record the failure in the run summary.","Silently skip the check and mark the overall run passed."],"ans":2,"why":["No data is not evidence of zero violations.","Untracked stale data can misrepresent the current design.","The script preserves the distinction between unavailable evidence and a passing check.","Skipping a required check must not become a pass."],"ex":"Robust automation validates inputs, centralizes configuration and logs decisions so every summary can be traced to an actual run.","oral":"\"How do you make a flow script robust?\""},{"id":"V323","k":"md-scripting-perl","d":"code","lvl":2,"f":"short","title":"\"Perl?\"","q":"\"Perl?\"","a":"- **\"Perl?\"** \"I read and modify it; my go-to for new scripts is Python. Regex and file processing carry over directly, and I'd ramp up on the team's Perl quickly.\"","src":"Scripting Companion","hint":"Work the source prompt on paper or aloud before opening the answer.","trap":"Reading a worked answer is not the same as reconstructing it unaided.","fu":["Use the source’s follow-up questions, changed assumptions and worked examples to test your reasoning."]},{"id":"V324","k":"platform-analog-gm-ro-gain","d":"ana","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Loaded common-source voltage gain","q":"At valid saturation bias, gm=1 mS, ro=100 kΩ, RD=8 kΩ and AC-coupled RL=100 kΩ. Neglect body effect. What is low-frequency common-source gain?","a":"The gain is **−6.897 V/V**. Output-node KCL places all three resistances in parallel:\n\n```latex\nR_{out}=(100\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})\\parallel(8\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})\\parallel(100\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})=6.897\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}\n```\n\nMultiplying by −1 mS gives the result. Using gm·ro alone would predict −100 and ignore the resistor that dominates the output conductance.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Write output-node KCL and combine all output conductances.","trap":"Intrinsic gain is not the gain after loading.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["−100 V/V","−6.897 V/V","−8 V/V","+6.897 V/V"],"ans":1,"why":["−100 uses intrinsic gmro and ignores both loading resistances.","The parallel resistance is 1/(1/100k+1/8k+1/100k)=6.897 kΩ; gain is −gmRout.","−8 ignores finite ro and the 100 kΩ external load.","The magnitude is right but a common-source drain output inverts the small signal."],"ex":"The parallel resistance is 1/(1/100k+1/8k+1/100k)=6.897 kΩ; gain is −gmRout.","oral":"At a valid saturation bias, gm = 1 mS, ro = 100 kΩ, RD = 8 kΩ and AC-coupled RL = 100 kΩ. What is the low-frequency common-source gain in V/V? Neglect body effect."},{"id":"V325","k":"platform-analog-gm-ro-bias","d":"ana","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Diagnose gain loss after increasing bias","q":"A common-source amplifier’s measured gain falls after bias current doubles at fixed geometry and supply. Which measurement plan best separates output-resistance loss, reduced saturation margin and a shifted pole?","a":"First, output resistance may fall faster than transconductance improves; compare gm and gds at both operating points. Second, the larger resistor drop may push the device out of saturation or reduce swing; inspect VDS, VOV and a small DC input sweep. Third, the measured frequency may now lie near a changed pole because bias and capacitance changed; compare DC gain with the full AC response. Also keep the external load identical. These mechanisms require different fixes; gain loss alone does not identify the cause.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Separate DC operating point, low-frequency gain and frequency dependence.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which change helps bandwidth while potentially reducing intrinsic gain?"],"opts":["Compare only one high-frequency magnitude point with a different load","Compare gm/gds, VDS versus overdrive, and DC gain versus full AC response at the same external load","Measure bias current alone and assume gm determines gain","Check only that the supply voltage is unchanged"],"ans":1,"why":["Changing load and observing one point cannot isolate the cause.","These measurements distinguish device gain, operating-region/headroom and frequency-response mechanisms.","Gain also depends on output conductance, load and frequency.","Fixed supply does not imply fixed drain bias, swing or poles."],"ex":"A larger gm can coexist with lower ro, less saturation margin or different bandwidth. Gain loss alone does not identify a unique repair.","oral":"You double the bias current of a common-source stage at fixed geometry and supply, and its gain falls. Give three physically distinct explanations and the measurements that separate them."},{"id":"V326","k":"platform-analog-differential-pair-polarity","d":"ana","lvl":2,"f":"short","title":"Which gain belongs to which output?","q":"A matched NMOS pair has ideal tail bias, gm = 1 mS per device and RD = 5 kΩ per drain. Define vid = v1 − v2 and vod = vd2 − vd1. Which small-signal gain pair is correct?","a":"**Choose B.** Each branch changes by ±gm·vid/2. Drain 1 falls by gm·RD·vid/2 while drain 2 rises by the same amount. Subtracting drain 1 from drain 2 doubles that single-ended change, giving +5 V/V differential gain and −2.5 V/V at drain 1. Reversing the differential output definition reverses only its sign.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start by stating the model, the operating condition and the quantity being measured.","trap":"A fixed total current still permits differential current steering.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"]},{"id":"V327","k":"platform-analog-differential-pair-headroom","d":"ana","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Find differential-pair common-mode floor","q":"An NMOS differential pair needs VGS = 0.60 V at its bias current. Its grounded tail source requires VDS ≥ 0.15 V. What first-order input common-mode floor follows from this tail-side constraint?","a":"The common-source node must remain at least 0.15 V above ground. Each input gate must sit 0.60 V above that node, so the first-order minimum is **0.75 V**. This checks the tail-source side only. A complete input range also checks the input devices, load compliance and available output swing.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Stack the gate-source voltage above the tail source’s required drain voltage.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["0.15 V","0.45 V","0.75 V","0.60 V"],"ans":2,"why":["This is the source-node floor, not the input gate voltage.","This subtracts compliance instead of stacking required headroom.","The source node must be at least 0.15 V, and the gate is 0.60 V above it.","This leaves no voltage across the tail source."],"ex":"VCM,min ≈ VGS,input + VDS,min,tail = 0.75 V. Load and input-device saturation impose additional range constraints.","oral":"An NMOS differential pair needs VGS = 0.60 V at its operating current. Its tail source requires VDS ≥ 0.15 V and is tied to ground. Estimate the minimum input common-mode voltage."},{"id":"V328","k":"platform-analog-current-mirrors-ratio","d":"ana","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Mirror error from channel-length modulation","q":"For the saturation mirror model, Iref=50 µA, width ratio=4, λ=0.10 V⁻¹, VDS,ref=0.60 V and VDS,out=1.20 V. What Iout follows?","a":"The geometry-only answer is 200 µA. Channel-length modulation scales it by (1 + 0.10×1.20)/(1 + 0.10×0.60) = 1.12/1.06. Therefore **Iout ≈ 211.32 µA**, about 5.66% high. This is a deterministic bias error under the given model; it should not be labeled random mismatch.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Apply the geometry ratio and then the ratio of drain-voltage correction factors.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["224 µA","200 µA","189.29 µA","211.32 µA"],"ans":3,"why":["224 µA includes the output modulation but fails to normalize by the reference modulation.","200 µA ignores unequal-drain-voltage channel-length modulation.","189.29 µA reverses the output/reference modulation ratio.","Iout=50×4×(1+0.10×1.20)/(1+0.10×0.60)=211.32 µA."],"ex":"Iout=50×4×(1+0.10×1.20)/(1+0.10×0.60)=211.32 µA.","oral":"Use the stated saturation model with Iref = 50 µA, width ratio 4, lambda = 0.10 V⁻¹, VDS,ref = 0.60 V and VDS,out = 1.20 V. Estimate Iout in µA."},{"id":"V329","k":"platform-analog-current-mirrors-diagnosis","d":"ana","lvl":3,"f":"short","title":"A mirror fails only at low output voltage","q":"A mirror ratio looks accurate above 0.35 V but collapses as Vout approaches ground. Which first diagnostic is strongest?","a":"**Choose C.** The voltage-dependent knee points first to output compliance: the transistor may leave saturation and cannot sustain the intended current. Inspect VDS and overdrive at the failing operating points. Random mismatch can shift the curve, but it does not by itself explain why nominal operation recovers above a consistent voltage boundary. A cascode can increase output resistance while worsening the minimum voltage requirement.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start by stating the model, the operating condition and the quantity being measured.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"]},{"id":"V330","k":"platform-analog-feedback-stability-phase","d":"ana","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Phase margin at unity loop gain","q":"For a conventional single-loop amplifier with stable open-loop behavior and one relevant unity-loop-gain crossing, loop-gain phase at that crossing is −125°. What phase margin does this indicate?","a":"**55°**. Phase margin is 180° + (−125°). The measurement is of loop gain Aβ, not just the closed-loop signal transfer. It does not establish large-signal settling or guarantee adequate margin at every load and PVT corner.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Measure angular distance to −180° at the relevant loop-gain crossing.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["125°","55°","−55°","305°"],"ans":1,"why":["125° is the magnitude of the measured phase, not the distance from −180°.","Phase margin is 180°−125°=55°.","The phase is 55° above −180°, not below it.","Adding the absolute phase magnitude to 180° uses the wrong sign."],"ex":"Use the phase of loop gain Aβ at its unity magnitude. This single result does not establish large-signal settling or all-corner/load stability.","oral":"For a conventional single-loop amplifier with a stable open-loop response and one relevant unity-loop-gain crossing, the phase at that crossing is −125°. What phase margin does this indicate?"},{"id":"V331","k":"platform-analog-feedback-stability-settling","d":"ana","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Distinguish slew limiting from poor damping","q":"An amplifier has adequate small-signal phase margin but is slow on large steps. Which observation most directly supports slew limiting?","a":"The amplifier may be slew limited: finite current charges a compensation or output capacitance, limiting dV/dt. Compare a small step with progressively larger steps. A roughly constant slope during a portion of large-step response and settling time that grows with step amplitude support slew limiting. Ringing on small steps instead points toward insufficient damping or another small-signal issue. Check output saturation and overload recovery too; stable linear analysis does not cover recovery from every large-signal state.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"A capacitor charged by a current limit produces a characteristic waveform shape.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["Settling time is identical for every step amplitude and output level.","Small and large steps show the same normalized damped ringing envelope.","Only a DC gain measurement changes when input amplitude is swept slowly.","Large steps show an approximately constant-slope interval whose duration grows with step amplitude."],"ans":3,"why":["Strong amplitude dependence is expected when a slew interval grows.","Amplitude-independent normalized dynamics suggest a linear response rather than slew limiting.","DC gain alone does not identify a transient slew-current limit.","A finite charging current limits dV/dt independently of step size over that interval."],"ex":"Compare progressively larger steps while checking output saturation and recovery. Small-signal stability does not bound every large-signal settling condition.","oral":"A feedback amplifier has adequate small-signal phase margin but settles much too slowly after a large step. Give a mechanism and a test that distinguishes it from poor phase margin."},{"id":"V332","k":"platform-analog-noise-offset-bandwidth","d":"ana","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Integrated white-noise voltage","q":"Flat voltage-noise density is 8 nV/√Hz across an ideal 100 kHz brick-wall band. Ignore other noise. What rms input noise follows?","a":"The density is multiplied by the square root of bandwidth: 8 nV/√Hz × √100000 Hz = **2.530 µV rms**. Multiplying directly by bandwidth gives incorrect units. With a real filter, use equivalent noise bandwidth or integrate the squared transfer function.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"For flat uncorrelated noise, rms grows with the square root of bandwidth.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["8 µV rms","2.530 µV rms","800 µV rms","0.0253 µV rms"],"ans":1,"why":["The supplied density and bandwidth do not give 8 µV; integrate noise power before taking its square root.","8 nV/√Hz×√100000 Hz=2529.8 nV=2.530 µV rms.","Multiplying density directly by bandwidth gives wrong units and the wrong value.","This is a factor-of-100 conversion error."],"ex":"8 nV/√Hz×√100000 Hz=2529.8 nV=2.530 µV rms.","oral":"An amplifier has flat input voltage-noise density 8 nV/√Hz. Ignore other noise and integrate over a 100 kHz brick-wall band. What is its input noise in µV rms?"},{"id":"V333","k":"platform-analog-noise-offset-offset","d":"ana","lvl":3,"f":"short","title":"What averaging removes","q":"Repeated readings show random scatter plus a constant input-equivalent 0.5 mV bias. Which statement is strongest?","a":"**Choose A.** Averaging independent zero-mean samples reduces the random standard error as 1/√N. A constant offset remains. Correlation, drift and low-frequency noise can also prevent the ideal averaging benefit, so independence must be checked rather than assumed. Calibration or a suitable offset-cancellation technique targets the constant bias.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start by stating the model, the operating condition and the quantity being measured.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"]},{"id":"V334","k":"platform-analog-sampling-adc-acquisition","d":"ana","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"ADC acquisition to half an LSB","q":"A 10-bit converter with 1 V span acquires a full-scale step through an ideal 1 kΩ-2 pF RC. How long to reduce the remaining error to half an LSB?","a":"The RC is 2 ns. Half an LSB relative to a full-scale step is 1/2048, so exp(−t/RC) ≤ 1/2048. Therefore t ≥ RC·ln(2048) = **15.25 ns**. This is an ideal settling floor; extra time may be required for nonlinear switch resistance and driver recovery.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Convert the error target into a fraction of the full-scale step.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["About 1.39 ns","About 13.86 ns","About 2 ns","About 15.25 ns"],"ans":3,"why":["0.693RC only halves the initial error.","13.86 ns corresponds to 1/1024, or one LSB, rather than half an LSB.","One time constant leaves about 37% error.","Half an LSB is 1/2048 of full scale, so t=2 ns×ln(2048)."],"ex":"RC=2 ns and exp(−t/RC)≤1/2048 requires t≥15.249 ns. This excludes nonlinear switch resistance and driver recovery.","oral":"A 10-bit, 1 V-span converter acquires through an ideal 1 kΩ-2 pF RC. Starting one full-scale step away, how many ns are needed to settle to half an LSB?"},{"id":"V335","k":"platform-analog-sampling-adc-alias","d":"ana","lvl":2,"f":"short","title":"Aliasing versus resolution","q":"A 70 MHz interferer enters an ideal 100 MS/s sampler. Its alias at 30 MHz overlaps your desired input band. Which change directly prevents this folding?","a":"**Choose C.** Reject the 70 MHz component in the analog path before sampling, or change the frequency plan/sample rate so its alias no longer overlaps the signal band. Increasing resolution preserves the ambiguous samples more precisely. Digital filtering after sampling cannot separate two original frequencies that produced the same sampled sequence in the occupied band.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start by stating the model, the operating condition and the quantity being measured.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"]},{"id":"V336","k":"platform-rtl-sequential-semantics-pipeline","d":"rtl","lvl":2,"f":"short","title":"Read the old state","q":"Initially a = 0 and b = 0. At a rising edge with din = 1, the block executes a <= din; b <= a;. What are a and b after the nonblocking updates?","a":"**Choose B.** Both right-hand sides are evaluated from the pre-update state: a gets din = 1 and b gets old a = 0. The updates do not turn the source-code sequence into combinational propagation through both registers at the same edge.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start by stating the model, the operating condition and the quantity being measured.","trap":"Source-code order is not a substitute for a clock-cycle model.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"]},{"id":"V337","k":"platform-rtl-sequential-semantics-latch","d":"rtl","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Does a missing else imply a latch?","q":"Compare `if (en) q <= d;` inside `always_ff @(posedge clk)` with a path that leaves y unassigned inside `always_comb`. Which interpretation is correct?","a":"In the edge-triggered process, q updates only when en is true and otherwise holds its registered value; this describes a flip-flop with enable behavior. In an intended combinational process, failing to assign y along a path requires its previous value to be retained, which implies level-sensitive storage or triggers a tool diagnostic because always_comb promises combinational behavior. Use complete assignments/defaults for combinational outputs. Whether hardware includes storage is determined by behavior and event control, not by the mere presence of an else keyword.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start with the process’s event control, then examine unassigned paths.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["The first describes a flip-flop enable; the second requests retention in a combinational process and can infer a latch or cause a diagnostic.","Both are purely combinational because the statements use if.","The first is an unconditional flip-flop; the second implicitly assigns y to zero.","Both necessarily describe level-sensitive latches because both omit else."],"ans":0,"why":["Event control and assignment completeness determine storage behavior.","The clock event and missing combinational assignment both matter.","No assignment means retain behavior, not an implicit zero or unconditional update.","An edge-triggered process retains its registered value between qualifying updates."],"ex":"Do not diagnose storage from the presence of `else` alone. Use complete combinational assignments and intentional sequential enables.","oral":"Compare if (en) q <= d; inside an always_ff @(posedge clk) block with an incomplete assignment to y inside always_comb. Explain the intended or inferred storage."},{"id":"V338","k":"platform-rtl-cdc-handshake-coherence","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Multi-bit clock-domain crossing","q":"Eight binary counter bits each cross through a separate two-flop synchronizer. What failure remains possible even if each chain has acceptable individual metastability MTBF?","a":"Different bits can be observed from different source updates, especially when several bits change together. The destination may see a word the source never produced. MTBF for individual resolution does not establish bus coherency. Use a suitable transaction handshake, an asynchronous FIFO, or a deliberately constrained Gray-coded transfer where appropriate. State the information-rate and physical timing assumptions for the chosen scheme.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Individual bit reliability and word-level transaction correctness are different properties.","trap":"Eight acceptable single-bit synchronizers do not imply one coherent eight-bit transaction.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["The destination clock must become frequency-locked to the source","All counter words become coherent once the chains have identical RTL","A mixed word composed of bits from different source updates","Every output bit is forced to the same value by the synchronizers"],"ans":2,"why":["Synchronizer chains do not lock clocks.","Structural identity does not establish coherent sampling across multi-bit transitions.","Different bits can be observed on different cycles, creating a value the source never issued.","They independently carry bit values and do not force a shared logic level."],"ex":"Different bits can be observed on different cycles, creating a value the source never issued.","oral":"An 8-bit binary count passes through eight separate two-flop synchronizers. What remains unsafe even if each individual synchronizer has acceptable metastability MTBF?"},{"id":"V339","k":"platform-rtl-cdc-handshake-reuse","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"short","title":"When can the source change the payload?","q":"In a bundled-data request/acknowledge crossing, when may the source overwrite the current payload holding register?","a":"**Choose C.** The source must wait until the acknowledgement has safely returned and the particular protocol permits the next transfer. Capturing a request in the first destination synchronizer is not confirmation that the destination consumed the payload. Holding the data until acknowledgement is part of the coherency contract.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start by stating the model, the operating condition and the quantity being measured.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"]},{"id":"V340","k":"platform-rtl-reset-verification-release","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Asynchronous reset release","q":"Why does a design commonly synchronize deassertion of an asynchronously asserted reset within each clock domain?","a":"Assertion directly forces the reset state according to the cell behavior. Release near a clock edge can violate recovery/removal requirements, leaving registers metastable or leaving related state elements on different effective release cycles. A domain-specific release synchronizer reduces this risk and makes initialization sequencing deliberate. Also satisfy the cell minimum reset pulse width and the system sequencing contract; asynchronous assertion is not permission to ignore all electrical timing.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Distinguish forcing a state from allowing normal clocked operation to resume.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["Asynchronous assertion makes deassertion electrically independent of every clock edge","Release near an active edge can violate recovery/removal or release related registers on inconsistent cycles","The release synchronizer eliminates the cell’s minimum reset pulse-width requirement","One release clock can safely serve unrelated clock domains without separate timing analysis"],"ans":1,"why":["Recovery/removal requirements still couple release to clock timing.","A controlled release reduces metastability and inconsistent initialization timing.","Minimum pulse width and sequencing remain separate requirements.","Each domain needs release behavior appropriate to its own clock."],"ex":"Asynchronous forcing of state does not imply unrestricted release timing. Respect recovery/removal, pulse width and domain-specific sequencing.","oral":"Why can an asynchronous reset assert safely while its deassertion still needs synchronization and timing analysis?"},{"id":"V341","k":"platform-rtl-reset-verification-valid","d":"cdc","lvl":3,"f":"short","title":"Can data registers be left unreset?","q":"A datapath register is intentionally not reset. Which condition is necessary for a safe design?","a":"**Choose D.** A reset, well-defined valid/control path must prevent unknown data from influencing any meaningful action until the datapath is initialized. This can reduce reset routing and implementation cost, but requires verification. Merely ignoring a displayed output is insufficient if uninitialized bits can reach an address or write enable.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start by stating the model, the operating condition and the quantity being measured.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"]},{"id":"V342","k":"platform-physical-timing-repair-repair","d":"flow","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"A hold fix that breaks setup","q":"A path has setup slack +20 ps and hold slack −20 ps. A cell adds 30 ps minimum and 45 ps maximum delay; all other delays and clocks are fixed. What are the new slacks?","a":"The maximum data delay grows by 45 ps, so setup slack falls from +20 to **−25 ps**. Hold improves by 30 ps to +10 ps. The cell therefore does not produce a fully timing-clean solution; placement or sizing must preserve enough max-delay margin as well.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Use the maximum increment for setup and minimum increment for hold.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["Setup −25 ps; hold +10 ps.","Setup +65 ps; hold +10 ps.","Setup −10 ps; hold +25 ps.","Setup +20 ps; hold +10 ps."],"ans":0,"why":["Setup loses the 45 ps maximum increment, while hold gains the 30 ps minimum increment.","Added maximum data delay consumes setup slack, not adds it.","This swaps minimum and maximum increments.","A hold cell on the shared path also changes maximum delay."],"ex":"20 − 45 = −25 ps; −20 + 30 = +10 ps. The repair fixes the stated hold check but does not make the path timing-clean.","oral":"A shared path has +20 ps setup slack and −20 ps hold slack. A proposed cell adds 30 ps minimum delay and 45 ps maximum delay. What is the new setup slack? Assume clocks and all other delays stay fixed."},{"id":"V343","k":"platform-physical-timing-repair-skew","d":"flow","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Clock-skew setup and hold tradeoff","q":"Define skew as capture-clock arrival minus launch-clock arrival. With all other delays fixed, adding +10 ps skew changes setup and same-edge hold slack how?","a":"A later capture edge gives the max-delay path more time before the next capture deadline, adding to setup slack. The same late capture clock also keeps the receiver vulnerable longer to the new fastest data launched on that cycle, subtracting from hold slack. Under this convention every additional picosecond of skew adds one picosecond to setup slack and removes one from hold slack, before other analysis changes. Check both equations rather than calling all skew either good or bad.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Place both the next capture edge and the same-cycle capture edge on the timing axis.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["Setup +10 ps; hold −10 ps","Both −10 ps","Setup −10 ps; hold +10 ps","Both +10 ps"],"ans":0,"why":["A later capture edge extends the setup deadline but also extends the same-edge hold protection window.","Setup receives more available time under the stated convention.","This uses the opposite skew sign convention.","The same late clock cannot simultaneously lend time to setup and shorten hold vulnerability."],"ex":"A later capture edge extends the setup deadline but also extends the same-edge hold protection window.","oral":"With skew defined as capture-clock arrival minus launch-clock arrival, explain why increasing positive skew helps setup but hurts same-edge hold."},{"id":"V344","k":"platform-physical-parasitics-congestion-wire","d":"int","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Driver plus distributed-wire Elmore moment","q":"Use τE = Rd(Cw+CL) + Rw(Cw/2+CL). Given Rd=1 kΩ, Rw=200 Ω, Cw=80 fF and CL=20 fF, what is τE?","a":"The driver term is 1000×100 fF = 100 ps. The distributed-wire term is 200×(40+20) fF = 12 ps. The total is **112 ps**. This is the stated Elmore moment, not automatically a measured 50% propagation delay.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Keep the driver’s total capacitive load separate from the distributed wire’s weighted load.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["120 ps","112 ps","77.28 ps","60 ps"],"ans":1,"why":["This assigns full Cw rather than Cw/2 to the wire-resistance term.","The driver term is 100 ps and the wire term is 12 ps.","This multiplies 112 ps by 0.69, which the requested Elmore expression does not specify.","This incorrectly halves the capacitance seen by the driver as well."],"ex":"τE=1000×100 fF + 200×60 fF = 112 ps. This is the stated Elmore moment, not automatically the measured 50% delay.","oral":"Use τE = Rd(Cw+CL) + Rw(Cw/2+CL). Given Rd = 1 kΩ, Rw = 200 Ω, Cw = 80 fF and CL = 20 fF, calculate τE in ps."},{"id":"V345","k":"platform-physical-parasitics-congestion-route","d":"flow","lvl":3,"f":"mcq","title":"Investigate a routed timing regression","q":"Cells and placement are unchanged, but a critical path becomes slower after detailed routing. What should be inspected before deciding to upsize?","a":"Compare estimated and extracted net resistance/capacitance, actual detours, routing layers, via counts, coupling environment and endpoint load. Check whether input slew changed and how the library delay calculation responded. Inspect congestion and pin-access restrictions near the bad nets. Resizing may be useful for a driver-limited path, but it cannot be assumed to fix a long detour or an inaccessible pin, and its added input capacitance can worsen the preceding stage.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Unchanged placement does not imply unchanged electrical interconnect.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["Only endpoint load, treating distributed resistance and aggressor switching as negligible by default.","Only total wire length, since different layers and via counts cannot change resistance at fixed length.","Extracted net R/C, detours, layers, vias, coupling and resulting input slew along the path.","Only cell arc tables, because unchanged cells imply the same input slew after routing."],"ans":2,"why":["Distributed RC and coupling may be exactly what created the regression.","Layers, cross sections and vias affect resistance and capacitance.","Routing changes parasitic loads and transitions even with identical cells and placement.","Routed loading can change input slews and therefore cell delays."],"ex":"Find whether the new delay is wire-, driver- or slew-limited. Upsizing may help one segment while loading its predecessor more heavily.","oral":"A critical path regresses after detailed routing even though its cells and placement are unchanged. What evidence would you inspect before resizing?"},{"id":"V346","k":"platform-physical-scan-test-shift","d":"flow","lvl":2,"f":"mcq","title":"Parallel scan-chain loading time","q":"There are 1200 scan registers in four equal parallel chains, shifted at 20 MHz. Ignoring capture and unload, how long to load one scan state?","a":"Each chain contains 300 registers. All four chains shift in parallel, so loading requires 300 cycles, not 1200. At 20 MHz each cycle is 50 ns; total time is **15 µs**. If chains are unbalanced, the longest chain determines the shift length.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Use the longest chain length, since all four chains shift together.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"],"opts":["60 µs","30 µs","15 µs","3.75 µs"],"ans":2,"why":["60 µs treats all 1200 bits as one serial chain.","30 µs adds an unload despite the stated load-only calculation.","Each chain is 300 bits and one cycle is 50 ns, so 300×50 ns=15 µs.","Dividing by four again double-counts the parallel-chain benefit."],"ex":"Each chain is 300 bits and one cycle is 50 ns, so 300×50 ns=15 µs.","oral":"There are 1200 scan registers in four equal parallel chains. Shift clock is 20 MHz. How many µs are required to load one complete scan state? Ignore capture and unload."},{"id":"V347","k":"platform-physical-scan-test-coverage","d":"flow","lvl":2,"f":"short","title":"What does 99% stuck-at coverage establish?","q":"An ATPG report states 99% stuck-at fault coverage. Which conclusion is defensible?","a":"**Choose B.** The report describes coverage of its specified stuck-at model under its assumptions and exclusions. It does not prove functional correctness, at-speed operation or coverage of every physical defect. Inspect the definition of the denominator and treatment of untestable or excluded faults before comparing percentages across flows.","src":"Earlier studio extension","hint":"Start by stating the model, the operating condition and the quantity being measured.","trap":"A correct equation is useful only within its stated operating conditions.","fu":["Which assumption would you verify first in simulation, and why?"]}];
